window.EF_ENGLISH_ORIGINAL_PRACTICE_SNIPPET_INDEX = [{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=1", "t": "Parts of Speech", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["Identify the role of the highlighted word. I read the newspaper daily. Answer: Verb Hinglish Explanation: read action batata hai—newspaper padhne ka kaam. Isliye yahan ye verb hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Verb”. Action/state showing word = verb. Quick Rule: Action/state showing word = verb.","Identify the role of the highlighted word. She is a teacher. Answer: Verb Hinglish Explanation: is subject She ko complement a teacher se link karta hai; yahan linking/main verb hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Verb”. Forms of be can work as linking verbs. Quick Rule: Forms of be can work as linking verbs.","What part of speech is the highlighted name? Rahul stays in Mumbai. Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: Rahul ek person ka naam hai, isliye proper noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. A naming word is a noun. Quick Rule: A naming word is a noun.","What does the highlighted word do? Tanu is a bibliophile. She carries a book with her everywhere. Answer: Pronoun Hinglish Explanation: She pehle sentence ke noun Tanu ko replace karta hai, hence pronoun . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Pronoun”. A pronoun replaces/refers back to a noun. Quick Rule: A pronoun replaces/refers back to a noun.","Identify the highlighted word. She has a red hat. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: red noun hat ki quality/colour batata hai, so adjective. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. A word modifying a noun/pronoun is an adjective. Quick Rule: A word modifying a noun/pronoun is an adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. The southern lights are beautiful. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: southern noun lights ko specify/describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. Noun modifier = adjective function. Quick Rule: Noun modifier = adjective function.","Identify the highlighted word. He drives carefully . Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: carefully batata hai he drives how ; verb ko modify kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. A word modifying a verb is an adverb. Quick Rule: A word modifying a verb is an adverb.","What is the role of the highlighted word? I am extremely sorry for the delay. Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: extremely adjective sorry ki degree modify karta hai, hence adverb. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. Adverbs can modify adjectives. Quick Rule: Adverbs can modify adjectives.","Identify the highlighted word. She is narrating the incident very slowly . Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: slowly narrating ka manner batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. Manner word modifying an action = adverb. Quick Rule: Manner word modifying an action = adverb.","Identify the highlighted word. I live in India. Answer: Preposition Hinglish Explanation: in live aur India ke beech place relation dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Preposition”. A preposition shows relationship with a noun/pronoun. Quick Rule: A preposition shows relationship with a noun/pronoun.","Identify the highlighted word. There is a box under the table. Answer: Preposition Hinglish Explanation: under box ki position table ke relation me batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Preposition”. Relationship/position word before noun phrase = preposition. Quick Rule: Relationship/position word before noun phrase = preposition.","Identify the highlighted word. I am fond of coffee. Answer: Preposition Hinglish Explanation: of adjective fond ko noun coffee se relate karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Preposition”. Prepositions connect a noun/pronoun to another element. Quick Rule: Prepositions connect a noun/pronoun to another element.","Identify the joining word. Rohan and Sahil are friends. Answer: Conjunction Hinglish Explanation: and do nouns ko join karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Conjunction”. A conjunction joins words/phrases/clauses. Quick Rule: A conjunction joins words/phrases/clauses.","Identify the joining word. She and I visited Goa last weekend. Answer: Conjunction Hinglish Explanation: and do subjects ko join karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Conjunction”. Joining word = conjunction. Quick Rule: Joining word = conjunction.","Identify the highlighted word. Do you have a rough notebook or at least a rough sheet of paper? Answer: Conjunction Hinglish Explanation: or alternatives ko join karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Conjunction”. or is a coordinating conjunction. Quick Rule: or is a coordinating conjunction.","Identify the highlighted connector. I did not go to work today because I was not feeling well. Answer: Conjunction Hinglish Explanation: because reason clause ko main clause se join karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Conjunction”. because introduces a reason clause and functions as a conjunction. Quick Rule: because introduces a reason clause and functions as a conjunction.","Identify the expressive word. Ouch! That hurt. Answer: Interjection Hinglish Explanation: Ouch! sudden pain/emotion express karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Interjection”. An interjection expresses sudden feeling/reaction. Quick Rule: An interjection expresses sudden feeling/reaction.","Identify the expressive word. Yo! I love to listen rap music. Answer: Interjection Hinglish Explanation: Yo! exclamatory/expressive call hai, so interjection. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Interjection”. Expressive standalone word = interjection. Quick Rule: Expressive standalone word = interjection.","In this sentence, what part of speech is the highlighted word? The fast car drove around the curve. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: fast noun car ko describe kar raha hai—kaisi car? fast car . Isliye yahan fast = adjective . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. A word that describes a noun/pronoun works as an adjective. Quick Rule: A word that describes a noun/pronoun works as an adjective.","Identify the part of speech of the highlighted word. The car drove fast around the curve. Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: Yahan fast verb drove ko modify karta hai—car kaise drove? fast . Isliye fast = adverb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. When a word tells how an action happens, it commonly functions as an adverb. Quick Rule: When a word tells how an action happens, it commonly functions as an adverb.","What is the grammatical role of the highlighted word? An advance of five hundred rupees was given. Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: advance ke pehle article an hai aur poora phrase ek amount/thing ko name kar raha hai. Isliye advance = noun . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. Article + naming word is a strong noun signal. Quick Rule: Article + naming word is a strong noun signal.","Identify the part of speech of the highlighted word. The advance money has been returned. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: advance noun money ko qualify kar raha hai—kaunsa money? advance money . Isliye yahan adjective . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. A word placed before a noun to qualify it can function as an adjective. Quick Rule: A word placed before a noun to qualify it can function as an adjective.","In this sentence, what is express ? An express runs from Amritsar to Delhi. Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: Yahan an express ek train/service ka naam hai aur sentence ka subject hai. Isliye express = noun . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Noun”. A word can change its part of speech according to its job in the sentence. Quick Rule: A word can change its part of speech according to its job in the sentence.","Identify the part of speech of the highlighted word. I have expressed my views. Answer: Verb Hinglish Explanation: expressed action batata hai aur have ke saath verb phrase bana raha hai. Isliye expressed = verb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Verb”. In a verb phrase such as have + V3, the participle is the main verb. Quick Rule: In a verb phrase such as have + V3, the participle is the main verb.","What is the role of express here? I will catch the express train. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: express noun train ko specify kar raha hai—kis type ki train? express train . Isliye adjective . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Adjective”. If a word qualifies a following noun, it functions adjectivally. Quick Rule: If a word qualifies a following noun, it functions adjectivally.","Identify the part of speech of the highlighted word. She works for a multinational company. Answer: Verb Hinglish Explanation: works yahan action/main verb hai: She works . Isliye verb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Verb”. A finite word showing the subject's action/state is a verb. Quick Rule: A finite word showing the subject's action/state is a verb.","What part of speech is works in this sentence? Several works of art were stolen from the town museum yesterday. Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: Several works me works plural naming word hai, yani pieces/creations of art. Isliye noun . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. Determiners such as several commonly introduce plural nouns. Quick Rule: Determiners such as several commonly introduce plural nouns.","Identify the part of speech of the highlighted word. The object of the game is to score the most points. Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: The object me object ek thing/aim ko name kar raha hai aur subject phrase ka head hai. Isliye noun . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. The + naming word is commonly a noun phrase. Quick Rule: The + naming word is commonly a noun phrase.","What is objects in this sentence? If nobody objects , we will postpone the meeting till next week. Answer: Verb Hinglish Explanation: objects ka meaning yahan 'opposes/raises an objection' hai aur nobody ke baad finite action word hai. Isliye verb . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Verb”. The same root can be a noun in one sentence and a verb in another. Quick Rule: The same root can be a noun in one sentence and a verb in another."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=2", "t": "Verb Roles & Forms", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["Which analysis of the highlighted verb is correct? She writes letters. Answer: Main verb, V5 Hinglish Explanation: writes khud action carry karta hai aur singular subject She ke saath -s form me hai. So it is main verb (V5) . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Main verb, V5”. V5 is the -s/-es form used with a third-person singular subject in the simple present. Quick Rule: V5 is the -s/-es form used with a third-person singular subject in the simple present.","Which analysis is correct? He played hockey. Answer: Main verb, V2 Hinglish Explanation: played sentence ka main action hai aur past form hai. So main verb, V2 . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Main verb, V2”. V2 is the simple past form. Quick Rule: V2 is the simple past form.","Choose the correct analysis of the verb phrase. I am going to Shimla. Answer: am = helping verb; going = main verb (V4) Hinglish Explanation: Continuous structure me am tense/aspect support karta hai, jabki actual action going hai. So am = helping verb , going = main verb (V4/ing) . English Explanation: The correct answer is “am = helping verb; going = main verb (V4)”. Be + V-ing: form of be acts as helping verb and V-ing carries the main action. Quick Rule: Be + V-ing: form of be acts as helping verb and V-ing carries the main action.","What is the role of the highlighted word? He is going to Shimla. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: is akela meaning complete nahi kar raha; going main action hai. Isliye is = helping verb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. In is/am/are + V-ing, the be-form is auxiliary/helping. Quick Rule: In is/am/are + V-ing, the be-form is auxiliary/helping.","What is the form and role of gone ? They have gone to Shimla. Answer: Main verb, V3 Hinglish Explanation: have helping verb hai aur gone verb go ka past participle V3 hai; wahi main lexical action deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb, V3”. Have/has + V3 forms a perfect construction. Quick Rule: Have/has + V3 forms a perfect construction.","In this sentence, what is am ? I am an architect. Answer: Main/linking verb Hinglish Explanation: Yahan am ke baad koi doosra main verb nahi hai. am subject I ko complement an architect se link karta hai, isliye yahi main/linking verb hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Main/linking verb”. A form of be can be a main/linking verb when no other main verb follows. Quick Rule: A form of be can be a main/linking verb when no other main verb follows.","Choose the correct analysis. I am teaching . Answer: am = helping verb; teaching = main verb (V4) Hinglish Explanation: am auxiliary hai; teaching present participle V4 hai aur actual action batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “am = helping verb; teaching = main verb (V4)”. Present continuous = am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present continuous = am/is/are + V-ing.","What is the role of was here? She was a dancer. Answer: Main/linking verb Hinglish Explanation: was ke baad noun complement a dancer hai, koi V-ing/V3 main verb nahi. So was = main/linking verb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main/linking verb”. Be can link the subject to a noun/adjective complement. Quick Rule: Be can link the subject to a noun/adjective complement.","Which analysis is correct? She was dancing . Answer: was = helping verb; dancing = main verb (V4) Hinglish Explanation: Past continuous me was helping verb hai aur dancing main action V4 hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was = helping verb; dancing = main verb (V4)”. Past continuous = was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Past continuous = was/were + V-ing.","In this sentence, what is has ? She has a beautiful home. Answer: Main verb meaning possession Hinglish Explanation: Yahan has possession batata hai— she possesses a home . Koi V3 follow nahi kar raha, so has = main verb . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Main verb meaning possession”. Have/has can be a main verb when it means possession. Quick Rule: Have/has can be a main verb when it means possession.","Choose the correct analysis. She has eaten food. Answer: has = helping verb; eaten = main verb (V3) Hinglish Explanation: Perfect structure me has auxiliary hai aur eaten main verb ka V3 form hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has = helping verb; eaten = main verb (V3)”. Present perfect = has/have + V3. Quick Rule: Present perfect = has/have + V3.","What is the role of have ? They have an adventurous life. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: have yahan possession/experience ko express kar raha hai aur uske baad noun phrase hai, V3 nahi. So it is a main verb . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb”. Check what follows have/has: a noun phrase often signals main-verb use; V3 often signals auxiliary use. Quick Rule: Check what follows have/has: a noun phrase often signals main-verb use; V3 often signals auxiliary use.","Which analysis is correct? They have made a prototype. Answer: have = helping verb; made = main verb (V3) Hinglish Explanation: have + made perfect verb phrase hai. have helping verb aur made V3 main verb hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have = helping verb; made = main verb (V3)”. Perfect aspect uses have/has/had + past participle (V3). Quick Rule: Perfect aspect uses have/has/had + past participle (V3).","In this sentence, what is did ? She did her job successfully. Answer: Main verb, V2 of do Hinglish Explanation: Yahan did khud action 'performed' ka meaning deta hai aur uske baad doosra base verb nahi hai. So did = main verb (V2) . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Main verb, V2 of do”. Do/did can itself be the main verb when it means perform/carry out. Quick Rule: Do/did can itself be the main verb when it means perform/carry out.","Choose the correct analysis of did ... do . She did not do her work. Answer: did = helping verb; do = main verb (V1) Hinglish Explanation: Negative simple past me did auxiliary hota hai, isliye main verb base form do (V1) me aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “did = helping verb; do = main verb (V1)”. After did/didn't, the main verb stays in the base form (V1). Quick Rule: After did/didn't, the main verb stays in the base form (V1)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=3", "t": "Object, Subject & Complement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["What is the direct object of the verb plays ? She plays football . Answer: football Hinglish Explanation: Question karo: She plays what? → football . Isliye football direct object hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “football”. Verb + what/whom? can help locate a direct object. Quick Rule: Verb + what/whom? can help locate a direct object.","Identify the direct object. Karan threw the ball . Answer: the ball Hinglish Explanation: Karan threw what? → the ball . Hence it is the direct object. English Explanation: Karan threw what? → the ball. Hence it is the direct object. Rule: The direct object receives the action of a transitive verb. Quick Rule: The direct object receives the action of a transitive verb.","In this question, which word is the object of call ? Could you call the neighbours ? Answer: the neighbours Hinglish Explanation: Call whom? → the neighbours . So it is the object; Could is helping verb and call main verb. English Explanation: Call whom? → the neighbours. So it is the object; Could is helping verb and call main verb. Rule: Ask whom/what after the main verb to test for an object. Quick Rule: Ask whom/what after the main verb to test for an object.","What is the grammatical role of you ? I like you . Answer: Object pronoun / object Hinglish Explanation: I like whom? → you . Yahan you pronoun hote hue object ka kaam kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Object pronoun / object”. An object may be a noun or a pronoun. Quick Rule: An object may be a noun or a pronoun.","What kind of verb use is shown? The dog was running . Answer: Intransitive — no object Hinglish Explanation: was running ke baad action ka direct object nahi hai. Dog khud running kar raha hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Intransitive — no object”. An intransitive verb does not take a direct object. Quick Rule: An intransitive verb does not take a direct object.","Identify the prepositional object. My mother writes for a weekly magazine in Delhi . Answer: Delhi Hinglish Explanation: Preposition in ka object Delhi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Delhi”. Object of a preposition comes after the preposition. Quick Rule: Object of a preposition comes after the preposition.","What is hours in this sentence? We talked for hours . Answer: Object of the preposition for Hinglish Explanation: hours directly talked ka object nahi; yeh preposition for ke baad aata hai. So it is object of the preposition . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Object of the preposition for”. Preposition + noun/pronoun forms a prepositional phrase; the noun/pronoun is the preposition's object. Quick Rule: Preposition + noun/pronoun forms a prepositional phrase; the noun/pronoun is the preposition's object.","What is the role of Shimla ? I am going to Shimla . Answer: Object of the preposition to Hinglish Explanation: Shimla preposition to ka object hai: to Shimla . It is not a direct object of going . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Object of the preposition to”. A noun after a preposition is the object of that preposition. Quick Rule: A noun after a preposition is the object of that preposition.","Which pair correctly identifies the two prepositional objects? I work for a large firm in Paris . Answer: firm and Paris Hinglish Explanation: firm is object of for ; Paris is object of in . Dono prepositional phrases hain: for a large firm , in Paris . English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “firm and Paris”. Each preposition can have its own object. Quick Rule: Each preposition can have its own object.","Choose the correct analysis. The girls carry water to their village . Answer: water = direct object; village = object of to Hinglish Explanation: Carry what? → water direct object. to their village me village preposition to ka object hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “water = direct object; village = object of to”. Separate the verb's object from nouns inside prepositional phrases. Quick Rule: Separate the verb's object from nouns inside prepositional phrases.","Does the verb have an object here? They laughed uncontrollably . Answer: No—the sentence has no object Hinglish Explanation: uncontrollably adverb hai; laughed what/whom? ka koi answer nahi milta. So laughed is used without an object. English Explanation: The correct answer is “No—the sentence has no object”. Not every verb takes an object; adverbs are not objects. Quick Rule: Not every verb takes an object; adverbs are not objects.","Which word is the direct object? I saw a sick man . Answer: man Hinglish Explanation: I saw whom? → man . sick adjective hai jo man ko describe karta hai. English Explanation: The required word or phrase is “man”. In a noun phrase, the head noun—not its adjective—is the object. Quick Rule: In a noun phrase, the head noun—not its adjective—is the object.","Choose the correct analysis. The little girl gave money to the sick old man . Answer: money = direct object; man = object of to Hinglish Explanation: gave what? → money direct object. to ... man me man preposition to ka object hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “money = direct object; man = object of to”. Direct object answers what/whom after the verb; a noun after to is normally a prepositional object. Quick Rule: Direct object answers what/whom after the verb; a noun after to is normally a prepositional object.","What is the direct object of wants ? Karan wants a smile from Kajal. Answer: a smile Hinglish Explanation: Karan wants what? → a smile . Kajal is object of preposition from . English Explanation: Karan wants what? → a smile. Kajal is object of preposition from. Rule: One sentence can contain a direct object and a separate prepositional object. Quick Rule: One sentence can contain a direct object and a separate prepositional object.","Which word is the direct object of kicked ? Kajal kicked Karan under the table. Answer: Karan Hinglish Explanation: Kajal kicked whom? → Karan . table is object of the preposition under . English Explanation: Kajal kicked whom? → Karan. table is object of the preposition under. Rule: The noun immediately receiving the action is the direct object. Quick Rule: The noun immediately receiving the action is the direct object.","Which phrase is the subject complement? She became a teacher . Answer: a teacher Hinglish Explanation: became linking verb hai aur a teacher subject She ko rename karta hai. English Explanation: The required word or phrase is “a teacher”. Subject complement follows a linking verb and describes/renames the subject. Quick Rule: Subject complement follows a linking verb and describes/renames the subject.","What is a teacher in this sentence? I am a teacher . Answer: Subject complement Hinglish Explanation: am linking verb hai; a teacher subject I ko rename karta hai. Isliye yeh subject complement hai, object nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Subject complement”. Subject + linking verb + subject complement: the complement refers back to the subject. Quick Rule: Subject + linking verb + subject complement: the complement refers back to the subject.","What is the grammatical role of friends ? We are friends . Answer: Subject complement Hinglish Explanation: friends subject We ko rename/identify karta hai after linking verb are . So it is a subject complement. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Subject complement”. A noun after a linking verb may be a subject complement. Quick Rule: A noun after a linking verb may be a subject complement.","What is beautiful here? She is beautiful . Answer: Subject complement (adjective) Hinglish Explanation: beautiful subject She ki quality batata hai after linking verb is . So it is an adjective subject complement. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Subject complement (adjective)”. A subject complement may be a noun, pronoun, or adjective. Quick Rule: A subject complement may be a noun, pronoun, or adjective.","Choose the correct role of a great mother . She will become a great mother . Answer: Subject complement Hinglish Explanation: become linking verb ki tarah subject ko new identity/state se connect karta hai. a great mother subject complement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Subject complement”. Become is a common linking verb when it links subject to identity/state. Quick Rule: Become is a common linking verb when it links subject to identity/state.","Why is a teacher different here? She married a teacher . Answer: It is the object of married Hinglish Explanation: She married whom? → a teacher . Yahan married action/transitive verb hai, linking verb nahi; so a teacher = object . English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is the object of married”. Do not call every noun after a verb a complement; first check whether the verb is linking or transitive. Quick Rule: Do not call every noun after a verb a complement; first check whether the verb is linking or transitive.","Identify the subject complement. She is a brilliant writer . Answer: brilliant writer Hinglish Explanation: brilliant writer subject She ko rename/describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “brilliant writer”. Linking verb + noun/adjective complement = subject complement. Quick Rule: Linking verb + noun/adjective complement = subject complement.","Which word is the subject? A wise man knows his skills. Answer: man Hinglish Explanation: Main verb knows ka doer/head noun man hai. English Explanation: The required word or phrase is “man”. Ask who/what performs the main verb to find the subject. Quick Rule: Ask who/what performs the main verb to find the subject.","Identify the subject phrase. The student from the classrooms at the end of the hall were very noisy. Answer: The student from the classrooms at the end of the hall Hinglish Explanation: Long modifiers ke bawajood poora highlighted noun phrase subject position me hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “The student from the classrooms at the end of the hall”. Subject can be a long noun phrase, not only one word. Quick Rule: Subject can be a long noun phrase, not only one word.","What is the subject of the main clause? She is my friend who can dance, whistle, and hum a tune at the same time. Answer: She Hinglish Explanation: Main clause She is my friend ka subject She hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “She”. Relative clause inside a sentence does not change the main-clause subject. Quick Rule: Relative clause inside a sentence does not change the main-clause subject.","Identify the subject phrase. The most concentrated area of mapped data was in the middle space. Answer: The most concentrated area of mapped data Hinglish Explanation: Main verb was se pehle poora noun phrase subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “The most concentrated area of mapped data”. The complete noun phrase before the main finite verb can function as subject. Quick Rule: The complete noun phrase before the main finite verb can function as subject.","Identify the subject. The baby is crying. Answer: The baby Hinglish Explanation: Kaun crying kar raha hai? The baby . So it is the subject; is crying is the verb phrase. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “The baby”. The subject is the person/thing the sentence is about and usually performs or experiences the verb. Quick Rule: The subject is the person/thing the sentence is about and usually performs or experiences the verb.","What is the subject of this sentence? Beauty lies in the eyes of the beholder. Answer: Beauty Hinglish Explanation: Sentence Beauty ke baare me statement karta hai aur verb lies isi subject se agree karta hai. So Beauty is subject. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Beauty”. Ignore nouns inside prepositional phrases when locating the main subject. Quick Rule: Ignore nouns inside prepositional phrases when locating the main subject.","Choose the correct subject-object pair. Dead men tell no tales . Answer: Dead men = subject; no tales = object Hinglish Explanation: Dead men do the action tell ; tell what? → no tales . So subject = Dead men, object = no tales. English Explanation: Dead men do the action tell; tell what? → no tales. So subject = Dead men, object = no tales. Rule: Find who/what performs the verb, then ask verb + what/whom for the object. Quick Rule: Find who/what performs the verb, then ask verb + what/whom for the object.","What is the main subject? The intensity of her hard work shows passion. Answer: The intensity Hinglish Explanation: Main verb shows ka subject The intensity hai. of her hard work sirf prepositional phrase hai jo intensity ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The intensity”. The subject is not automatically the nearest noun before the verb; nouns inside of-phrases can be modifiers. Quick Rule: The subject is not automatically the nearest noun before the verb; nouns inside of-phrases can be modifiers.","What is the subject of the main clause? At the conference, the participant who is known for his trivial questions stood up again and interrupted the discussion. Answer: the participant who is known for his trivial questions Hinglish Explanation: participant head noun hai; relative clause who is known... usko modify karta hai. Poora phrase subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “the participant who is known for his trivial questions”. Relative clause may be embedded inside the subject noun phrase. Quick Rule: Relative clause may be embedded inside the subject noun phrase.","In the command, what is the direct object? Bring a glass of water. Answer: a glass Hinglish Explanation: Command ka implied subject you hota hai. Bring what? → a glass . water is object of preposition of . English Explanation: The correct answer is “a glass”. Imperatives usually omit the subject you; the verb can still take a direct object. Quick Rule: Imperatives usually omit the subject you; the verb can still take a direct object.","What is the direct object? Shut the door . Answer: the door Hinglish Explanation: Command me implied subject you hai. Shut what? → the door , so it is the direct object. English Explanation: The correct answer is “the door”. Imperative sentences can have an unstated subject and an explicit object. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences can have an unstated subject and an explicit object.","Is the highlighted verb transitive here? She ate mangoes. Answer: Yes — mangoes is its object Hinglish Explanation: ate what? → mangoes . Direct object mil raha hai, so transitive. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Yes — mangoes is its object”. Transitive verb answers what/whom with a direct object. Quick Rule: Transitive verb answers what/whom with a direct object.","Is the highlighted verb transitive here? I like you. Answer: Yes — you is its object Hinglish Explanation: like whom? → you . Hence transitive use. English Explanation: like whom? → you. Hence transitive use. Rule: Verb + direct object = transitive use. Quick Rule: Verb + direct object = transitive use.","Classify the highlighted verb use. She cried loudly. Answer: Intransitive Hinglish Explanation: loudly adverb hai, object nahi. cried direct object nahi leta here. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Intransitive”. Adverb after a verb is not a direct object. Quick Rule: Adverb after a verb is not a direct object.","Classify the highlighted main verb. I am going to Shimla. Answer: Intransitive Hinglish Explanation: to Shimla prepositional phrase hai; going ka direct object nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Intransitive”. A prepositional complement does not make the verb transitive. Quick Rule: A prepositional complement does not make the verb transitive.","Does this group form a complete sentence? Rahul is going to Shimla . Answer: Yes — complete sentence Hinglish Explanation: Isme subject Rahul aur finite verb phrase is going ke saath complete sense hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Yes — complete sentence”. A sentence gives complete sense and has a complete clause structure. Quick Rule: A sentence gives complete sense and has a complete clause structure.","Classify the sentence. Karan has completed the work. Answer: Assertive affirmative Hinglish Explanation: Statement information de raha hai aur negative marker nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive affirmative”. Positive statement = affirmative assertive sentence. Quick Rule: Positive statement = affirmative assertive sentence.","Classify the sentence. Karan has not completed the work. Answer: Assertive negative Hinglish Explanation: not negative statement banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive negative”. Statement with not/negative meaning = negative assertive. Quick Rule: Statement with not/negative meaning = negative assertive.","Classify the sentence. They are playing chess. Answer: Assertive affirmative Hinglish Explanation: Ye simple positive statement hai, question/order nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive affirmative”. A declarative information sentence is assertive. Quick Rule: A declarative information sentence is assertive.","Classify the sentence. They are not playing chess. Answer: Assertive negative Hinglish Explanation: not ke saath negative statement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive negative”. Negative declarative = negative assertive. Quick Rule: Negative declarative = negative assertive."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=4", "t": "Indirect Interrogatives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect form of the direct question. Direct: What are your hobbies? → Indirect: I want to know _____ . Answer: what your hobbies are Hinglish Explanation: Indirect question me statement order hota hai: your hobbies are , aur end me question mark zaroori nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “what your hobbies are”. Indirect WH-question: WH-word + subject + verb. Quick Rule: Indirect WH-question: WH-word + subject + verb.","What type of question is this? Are you playing football? Answer: Direct yes/no interrogative Hinglish Explanation: Helping verb Are subject you se pehle hai aur direct question mark hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Direct yes/no interrogative”. Direct yes/no question uses auxiliary + subject order. Quick Rule: Direct yes/no question uses auxiliary + subject order.","What type of question is this? Where are you playing football? Answer: Direct WH-interrogative Hinglish Explanation: WH-word Where ke baad direct-question inversion are you hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Direct WH-interrogative”. Direct WH-question = WH-word + auxiliary + subject + main verb. Quick Rule: Direct WH-question = WH-word + auxiliary + subject + main verb.","Choose the correct indirect-question word order. May I ask where are you planning to go for summer vacation? Answer: where you are planning Hinglish Explanation: May I ask... ke baad embedded/indirect question hai, so inversion hat jaata hai: where you are planning . English Explanation: The correct answer is “where you are planning”. Indirect questions use statement word order. Quick Rule: Indirect questions use statement word order.","Choose the correct indirect-question word order. Could you tell me why did you not confess the truth? Answer: why you did not confess Hinglish Explanation: Could you tell me... ke baad direct inversion nahi; why you did not confess . English Explanation: The correct answer is “why you did not confess”. Embedded WH-question: WH + subject + auxiliary + main verb. Quick Rule: Embedded WH-question: WH + subject + auxiliary + main verb.","Choose the grammatically correct indirect question. Could you tell me where is the school principal ? Answer: Could you tell me where the school principal is? Hinglish Explanation: Indirect question ke embedded part me normal statement order hota hai: the school principal + is , not is + subject . Correct: where the school principal is . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Could you tell me where the school principal is?”. After an introductory phrase like Could you tell me..., the embedded wh-clause normally uses subject-before-verb order. Quick Rule: After an introductory phrase like Could you tell me..., the embedded wh-clause normally uses subject-before-verb order.","Choose the correct form. Could you tell me whom do you want to visit? Answer: Could you tell me whom you want to visit? Hinglish Explanation: Embedded clause direct-question order do you want use nahi karta. Correct is whom you want to visit . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Could you tell me whom you want to visit?”. Indirect interrogatives do not normally use do/does/did inversion inside the embedded clause. Quick Rule: Indirect interrogatives do not normally use do/does/did inversion inside the embedded clause.","Which correction is right? May I know what do you want to talk about? Answer: May I know what you want to talk about? Hinglish Explanation: May I know ke baad clause assertive order me hota hai: you want , not do you want . English Explanation: The correct answer is “May I know what you want to talk about?”. Embedded wh-clause: wh-word + subject + verb. Quick Rule: Embedded wh-clause: wh-word + subject + verb.","Choose the corrected indirect question. Can you tell me where does the organizer sell offline tickets? Answer: Can you tell me where the organizer sells offline tickets? Hinglish Explanation: Indirect form me does hata kar normal subject-verb order use hota hai, aur singular subject organizer ke saath sells aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Can you tell me where the organizer sells offline tickets?”. Remove do-support from the embedded clause and restore normal agreement. Quick Rule: Remove do-support from the embedded clause and restore normal agreement.","Choose the correct embedded question. I had never seen him in this locality before; that's why I inquired who was he . Answer: who he was Hinglish Explanation: I inquired ke baad embedded clause me statement order: he was . So who he was . English Explanation: The correct answer is “who he was”. In indirect questions, subject comes before the finite verb. Quick Rule: In indirect questions, subject comes before the finite verb.","Which sentence is correct? I did not understand why did you not ask for help. Answer: I did not understand why you did not ask for help. Hinglish Explanation: Yeh direct question nahi hai; why clause sentence ka object hai. Isliye normal order: you did not ask . English Explanation: The correct answer is “I did not understand why you did not ask for help.”. Indirect wh-clause keeps statement word order even when its meaning is a question. Quick Rule: Indirect wh-clause keeps statement word order even when its meaning is a question.","Select the correct indirect question. I want to know where does this road go . Answer: I want to know where this road goes. Hinglish Explanation: this road singular subject hai, so statement order me this road goes . Direct-question inversion does this road go yahan nahi aayega. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I want to know where this road goes.”. Embedded clause = where + subject + finite verb. Quick Rule: Embedded clause = where + subject + finite verb.","What is the correct embedded form? He did not say anything about how did he get into the fight . Answer: how he got into the fight Hinglish Explanation: Preposition about ke baad yeh embedded question hai. Normal order use hoga: how he got into the fight . English Explanation: The correct answer is “how he got into the fight”. Inside an indirect question, do/did inversion disappears and the lexical verb carries the tense. Quick Rule: Inside an indirect question, do/did inversion disappears and the lexical verb carries the tense.","Choose the corrected indirect form inside the passage. I tried to persuade them that I am like his family. But they still did not open their mouth about why did he call into the principal office. Answer: why he called Hinglish Explanation: about why... embedded question hai; subject he auxiliary inversion ke bina aata hai: why he called . English Explanation: The correct answer is “why he called”. Indirect questions do not use direct-question inversion. Quick Rule: Indirect questions do not use direct-question inversion."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=5", "t": "Sentence Types", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["What kind of imperative emphasis is used? Do work hard . Answer: Emphatic affirmative imperative Hinglish Explanation: Imperative me Do + V1 command/advice ko emphasis deta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Emphatic affirmative imperative”. Do + base verb can emphasize an affirmative imperative. Quick Rule: Do + base verb can emphasize an affirmative imperative.","Classify this sentence. Please, finish the work . Answer: Polite imperative/request Hinglish Explanation: Please ke saath base verb finish request/imperative banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Polite imperative/request”. Imperative sentences express order, advice or request. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences express order, advice or request.","What type of sentence is this? Work hard. Answer: Imperative Hinglish Explanation: Sentence kisi ko command/advice deta hai aur base verb Work se start hota hai. So it is imperative . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Imperative”. Imperatives express order, advice, request, or instruction; the subject you is usually understood. Quick Rule: Imperatives express order, advice, request, or instruction; the subject you is usually understood.","Identify the sentence type. Do not talk to her. Answer: Imperative Hinglish Explanation: Yeh negative command hai: Do not + V1 . So it is an imperative sentence . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Imperative”. Negative imperative commonly uses do not/don't + base verb. Quick Rule: Negative imperative commonly uses do not/don't + base verb.","Classify this sentence. May you rot in hell! Answer: Optative sentence Hinglish Explanation: May + subject + V1 yahan curse/wish express karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Optative sentence”. Optative sentences express wish, blessing or curse. Quick Rule: Optative sentences express wish, blessing or curse.","Identify the sentence type. Alas! He lost the race. Answer: Exclamatory Hinglish Explanation: Alas! strong sorrow/emotion express karta hai, so sentence exclamatory hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory”. Interjections such as Alas!, Hurrah!, Wow! often introduce exclamatory sentences. Quick Rule: Interjections such as Alas!, Hurrah!, Wow! often introduce exclamatory sentences.","What type of sentence is this? Hurrah! We won. Answer: Exclamatory Hinglish Explanation: Hurrah! joy/celebration ka strong reaction hai. Isliye exclamatory . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Exclamatory”. Exclamatory sentences express strong feeling or reaction. Quick Rule: Exclamatory sentences express strong feeling or reaction.","Identify the sentence type. What an amazing movie! Answer: Exclamatory Hinglish Explanation: Yahan What question nahi puch raha; poora pattern admiration/strong reaction express karta hai. So it is exclamatory . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory”. What/How can introduce exclamations when they express strong feeling rather than ask for information. Quick Rule: What/How can introduce exclamations when they express strong feeling rather than ask for information.","What type of sentence is this? May God bless you! Answer: Optative Hinglish Explanation: Sentence ek blessing/wish express karta hai. Isliye yeh optative sentence hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Optative”. Optative sentences express wish, blessing, curse, or prayer, often with May. Quick Rule: Optative sentences express wish, blessing, curse, or prayer, often with May."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=01.+Basics&section=6", "t": "Phrase & Clause", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 01. Basics", "x": ["Classify the highlighted group. This information age Answer: Phrase Hinglish Explanation: Is group me subject-verb combination nahi hai aur complete proposition nahi ban raha. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Phrase”. A phrase has no complete subject + finite-verb combination. Quick Rule: A phrase has no complete subject + finite-verb combination.","Which label best fits the highlighted words? In the beginning Answer: Phrase Hinglish Explanation: In the beginning me subject + finite verb combination nahi hai, isliye yeh complete clause nahi; it is a phrase . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Phrase”. A phrase is a group of words without its own subject-finite-verb combination. Quick Rule: A phrase is a group of words without its own subject-finite-verb combination.","Identify the highlighted group. Really fast Answer: Phrase Hinglish Explanation: Really fast meaningful group hai but subject + finite verb nahi hai. So it is a phrase . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Phrase”. No subject-verb combination → not a clause. Quick Rule: No subject-verb combination → not a clause.","What is the highlighted group? After a rainy day Answer: Phrase Hinglish Explanation: Preposition After se start hone wala yeh group complete statement nahi deta aur isme subject-finite verb pair nahi. So phrase . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Phrase”. Prepositional groups commonly function as phrases. Quick Rule: Prepositional groups commonly function as phrases.","In the sentence, what is very fast ? He drove the car very fast . Answer: A phrase functioning adverbially Hinglish Explanation: very fast verb drove ko modify karta hai but khud subject + finite verb nahi rakhta. So it is an adverbial phrase . English Explanation: The correct answer is “A phrase functioning adverbially”. A phrase can function like an adverb by modifying a verb. Quick Rule: A phrase can function like an adverb by modifying a verb.","Classify this example. She is pretty . Answer: Independent clause / simple sentence Hinglish Explanation: Subject She + finite verb is aur complete meaning hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Independent clause / simple sentence”. A simple sentence can consist of one independent clause. Quick Rule: A simple sentence can consist of one independent clause.","Classify this example. He is going to a party today . Answer: Independent clause / simple sentence Hinglish Explanation: Subject He aur finite verb phrase is going se complete thought banta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Independent clause / simple sentence”. Complete subject-verb unit that can stand alone = independent clause. Quick Rule: Complete subject-verb unit that can stand alone = independent clause.","How many clauses are in this sentence? The girl hugged the boy, and the boy hugged the cat. Answer: 2 Hinglish Explanation: Do clear subject-verb pairs hain: the girl hugged and the boy hugged . So total 2 clauses . English Explanation: The correct answer is “2”. Count clause cores by locating separate subject + finite verb combinations. Quick Rule: Count clause cores by locating separate subject + finite verb combinations.","How many clauses are present? If you subscribe to this online service, you will need a password. Answer: 2 Hinglish Explanation: 1) you subscribe (dependent, introduced by if) + 2) you will need (main). Total 2 clauses . English Explanation: 1) you subscribe (dependent, introduced by if) + 2) you will need (main). Total 2 clauses. Rule: A dependent clause still has a subject and verb; it simply cannot stand alone in that context. Quick Rule: A dependent clause still has a subject and verb; it simply cannot stand alone in that context.","How many clauses are there? While Tom reads novels, Jack reads comics, but Sam reads only magazines. Answer: 3 Hinglish Explanation: Clause cores: Tom reads , Jack reads , Sam reads . Total 3 . English Explanation: Clause cores: Tom reads, Jack reads, Sam reads. Total 3. Rule: Conjunctions can connect several clauses; count each subject-finite-verb unit. Quick Rule: Conjunctions can connect several clauses; count each subject-finite-verb unit.","How many clauses are in the sentence? Even though John likes golf, he played cricket at the weekend, and he enjoyed it very much. Answer: 3 Hinglish Explanation: Subject-verb pairs: John likes , he played , he enjoyed . Total 3 clauses . English Explanation: Subject-verb pairs: John likes, he played, he enjoyed. Total 3 clauses. Rule: Even though introduces a dependent clause; and joins another clause. Quick Rule: Even though introduces a dependent clause; and joins another clause.","Classify the highlighted group. He ate dinner . Answer: Independent clause Hinglish Explanation: Ye akela complete sentence ban sakta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Independent clause”. Independent clause can stand alone. Quick Rule: Independent clause can stand alone.","Classify the highlighted group. I was working . Answer: Independent clause Hinglish Explanation: Subject + finite verb aur complete sense, so independent. English Explanation: Subject + finite verb aur complete sense, so independent. Rule: Independent clause is complete by itself. Quick Rule: Independent clause is complete by itself.","Classify this group. When Rahul got home Answer: Dependent clause Hinglish Explanation: Subject Rahul + verb got hai, lekin when isko dependent banata hai; thought incomplete hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent clause”. Subordinator + subject + verb can form a dependent clause. Quick Rule: Subordinator + subject + verb can form a dependent clause.","How many clauses are present? Yesterday, the weather was terrible, yet today it's lovely, so we'll stay out all day. Answer: 3 Hinglish Explanation: Cores: weather was , it is , we will stay . Therefore 3 clauses . English Explanation: Cores: weather was, it is, we will stay. Therefore 3 clauses. Rule: Each finite subject-verb core contributes a clause, even when shortened by contractions such as it's/we'll. Quick Rule: Each finite subject-verb core contributes a clause, even when shortened by contractions such as it's/we'll.","How many clauses are in this sentence? I could not decide where I should work or what I should do. Answer: 3 Hinglish Explanation: Cores: I could not decide , I should work , I should do . Hence 3 clauses . English Explanation: Cores: I could not decide, I should work, I should do. Hence 3 clauses. Rule: Embedded wh-groups can be clauses when they contain their own subject + finite verb. Quick Rule: Embedded wh-groups can be clauses when they contain their own subject + finite verb.","What kind of clause is the highlighted group? When Rahul got home , he ate dinner. Answer: Dependent/subordinate clause Hinglish Explanation: When Rahul got home has subject + verb, so it is a clause; but when makes it dependent on the main clause for completion. English Explanation: When Rahul got home has subject + verb, so it is a clause; but when makes it dependent on the main clause for completion. Rule: Subordinators such as when can make a clause dependent. Quick Rule: Subordinators such as when can make a clause dependent.","Which part is the independent clause? When Rahul got home, he ate dinner . Answer: he ate dinner Hinglish Explanation: he ate dinner apne aap complete sentence ban sakta hai, so it is the independent/main clause . English Explanation: The correct answer is “he ate dinner”. An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence. Quick Rule: An independent clause can stand alone as a complete sentence.","Classify this group. while he was playing Answer: Dependent clause Hinglish Explanation: while subordinate marker hai; clause ko main clause chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent clause”. while commonly introduces a dependent time clause. Quick Rule: while commonly introduces a dependent time clause.","Classify this group. If you come here Answer: Dependent clause Hinglish Explanation: If condition marker clause ko dependent banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent clause”. if introduces a dependent condition clause. Quick Rule: if introduces a dependent condition clause.","Classify this group. Since the sun will shine today Answer: Dependent clause Hinglish Explanation: Since ke saath reason/time clause apne aap complete main statement nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent clause”. since can introduce a dependent clause. Quick Rule: since can introduce a dependent clause.","Classify this group. Because mom said so Answer: Dependent clause Hinglish Explanation: Because reason clause ko subordinate/dependent banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent clause”. because introduces a dependent reason clause. Quick Rule: because introduces a dependent reason clause.","Choose the correct clause analysis. While he was playing , I was working. Answer: Highlighted part is dependent Hinglish Explanation: while highlighted clause ko subordinate banata hai. I was working main/independent clause hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Highlighted part is dependent”. While commonly introduces a dependent time/contrast clause. Quick Rule: While commonly introduces a dependent time/contrast clause.","What kind of clause is If you come here ? If you come here , I shall help you. Answer: Dependent/subordinate clause Hinglish Explanation: If condition introduce karta hai; clause me subject you + verb come hai but it depends on the main clause. So dependent clause . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Dependent/subordinate clause”. If commonly introduces a dependent conditional clause. Quick Rule: If commonly introduces a dependent conditional clause.","Identify the highlighted clause. Since the sun will shine today , we will go to the beach. Answer: Dependent/subordinate clause Hinglish Explanation: Since se introduced group me subject the sun + verb phrase will shine hai, so it is a clause; it depends on the main clause we will go... . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent/subordinate clause”. Since can introduce a dependent reason/time clause. Quick Rule: Since can introduce a dependent reason/time clause.","What is the role of the highlighted group? Because mom said so , I apologized to Radha. Answer: Dependent/subordinate clause Hinglish Explanation: Because subordinator hai. mom said subject-verb pair hone ki wajah se yeh clause hai, but because ke saath standalone complete statement nahi—so dependent clause . English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Dependent/subordinate clause”. Because normally introduces a dependent reason clause. Quick Rule: Because normally introduces a dependent reason clause."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=1", "t": "Simple Present & Habit", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Identify the tense in the source example. Karan plays cricket. Answer: Simple Present Hinglish Explanation: plays V5 form present action/habit structure dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Present”. Simple Present affirmative: V1/V5. Quick Rule: Simple Present affirmative: V1/V5.","Choose the best correction. The downhearted mother is praying for the well-being of her son every day. Answer: prays Hinglish Explanation: Every day repeated/habitual action dikhata hai. Isliye ongoing-looking is praying ki jagah prays aayega. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “prays”. Habit/repeated action -> Simple Present (V1/V5). Quick Rule: Habit/repeated action -> Simple Present (V1/V5).","Choose the best correction. Books are her best friends. She is generally carrying them around wherever she goes. Answer: generally carries Hinglish Explanation: Generally frequency word hai; yahan regular habit bat rahi hai. So she generally carries . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “generally carries”. usually/generally/often + habitual action -> Simple Present. Quick Rule: usually/generally/often + habitual action -> Simple Present.","Which verb phrase correctly completes the sentence? The typical design of books for readers _____ engaging and colourful illustrations. Answer: often includes Hinglish Explanation: Subject design singular hai aur statement general fact hai, so includes (V5) chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “often includes”. Singular subject + Simple Present affirmative -> V1 + s/es. Quick Rule: Singular subject + Simple Present affirmative -> V1 + s/es.","Why is Simple Present appropriate here? The newspaper boy comes daily at 6 am. Answer: Habitual/repeated action Hinglish Explanation: Time/frequency cue ke saath comes repeated routine batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Habitual/repeated action”. Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present.","Why is Simple Present appropriate here? She usually sleeps late at night. Answer: Habitual/repeated action Hinglish Explanation: Time/frequency cue ke saath sleeps repeated routine batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Habitual/repeated action”. Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present.","Why is Simple Present appropriate here? They go to Chicago every summer. Answer: Habitual/repeated action Hinglish Explanation: Time/frequency cue ke saath go repeated routine batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Habitual/repeated action”. Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Habitual/repeated present actions use Simple Present.","Choose the correction for the routine meaning. I don't understand how Nick can't be here. He's always talking the same road to reach the university. Answer: takes Hinglish Explanation: Same road regularly use karna habitual action hai; source correction takes hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “takes”. Routine/habit -> Simple Present. Quick Rule: Routine/habit -> Simple Present.","Choose the standard habitual form. She is always speaking the truth. Answer: always speaks Hinglish Explanation: Yahan always simple habit/characteristic batata hai; special irritation sense nahi hai. So always speaks . English Explanation: The correct answer is “always speaks”. Plain habits with always normally use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Plain habits with always normally use Simple Present.","Which sentence is correct as a regular habit? The good shepherd is always going through the valley and spend some time in solitude. Answer: always goes through the valley and spends some time Hinglish Explanation: Regular routine ke liye dono coordinated verbs same Simple Present me honge: goes and spends . English Explanation: The correct answer is “always goes through the valley and spends some time”. Parallel habitual verbs should normally stay in the same Simple Present form. Quick Rule: Parallel habitual verbs should normally stay in the same Simple Present form.","Which sentence correctly expresses irritation about a repeated action? You always disturb me , and I am going to complain of it. Answer: You are always disturbing me Hinglish Explanation: Yahan speaker complaint/irritation dikha raha hai. are always disturbing repeated annoying behaviour ko emphatically show karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “You are always disturbing me”. always + Present Continuous can express repeated irritating behaviour. Quick Rule: always + Present Continuous can express repeated irritating behaviour.","Choose the tense used for this universal fact. Plants give us oxygen. Answer: Simple Present Hinglish Explanation: General scientific fact ko Simple Present me state kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Present”. Universal truths/facts -> Simple Present. Quick Rule: Universal truths/facts -> Simple Present.","Choose the correct verb for a universal fact. The moon _____ the Earth. Answer: orbits Hinglish Explanation: Astronomical fact ko timeless/general truth ki tarah bola gaya hai, so orbits . English Explanation: The correct answer is “orbits”. Universal truths/facts -> Simple Present. Quick Rule: Universal truths/facts -> Simple Present.","Choose the correct verb for a scientific fact. Our planet Earth _____ around its axis from west to east. Answer: rotates Hinglish Explanation: Earth ka rotation general scientific fact hai; rotates sahi form hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “rotates”. General scientific facts are stated in Simple Present. Quick Rule: General scientific facts are stated in Simple Present.","Choose the best correction. Global warming is a human-caused problem. It caused the sea level to rise due to expansion of warmer seas and melting ice sheets. Answer: causes Hinglish Explanation: Statement ongoing general cause-effect explain kar raha hai, past event nahi. Isliye causes . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “causes”. General cause-effect statements -> Simple Present. Quick Rule: General cause-effect statements -> Simple Present.","Choose the correction used in the source example. The people in ancient times believed that the sun moves around the Earth. Answer: moved Hinglish Explanation: Main reporting verb believed past me hai aur source example historical belief ko backshift karta hai: moved . English Explanation: The correct answer is “moved”. A past reporting context can backshift a non-universal reported belief into past tense. Quick Rule: A past reporting context can backshift a non-universal reported belief into past tense.","Which part needs no change because it states a universal truth? The scientist believed that gravity pulls down everything . Answer: gravity pulls down everything Hinglish Explanation: Gravity wala statement universal truth hai, isliye past reporting verb ke baad bhi pulls reh sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “gravity pulls down everything”. Universal truths generally stay in Simple Present even after a past reporting verb. Quick Rule: Universal truths generally stay in Simple Present even after a past reporting verb.","Classify the source sentence. Does she go to the mall? Answer: Simple Present interrogative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka question form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present interrogative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Classify the source sentence. She does not go to the mall. Answer: Simple Present negative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka negative form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present negative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Classify the source sentence. Does she talk to anyone? Answer: Simple Present interrogative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka question form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present interrogative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Classify the source sentence. She does not talk to anyone. Answer: Simple Present negative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka negative form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present negative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Classify the source sentence. Do you read novels? Answer: Simple Present interrogative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka question form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present interrogative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Classify the source sentence. You do not read novels. Answer: Simple Present negative Hinglish Explanation: Do/does based structure Simple Present ka negative form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present negative”. Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Present interrogative/negative uses do/does + V1.","Choose the correction. How often is he visiting this place to meet his family and friends? Answer: does he visit Hinglish Explanation: How often habitual frequency pooch raha hai; does he visit chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “does he visit”. Habitual questions -> do/does + subject + V1. Quick Rule: Habitual questions -> do/does + subject + V1.","Choose the correction. Why don't you told him about the issue? Answer: tell Hinglish Explanation: Auxiliary do ke baad base form tell aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “tell”. After do/does/did, main verb stays V1. Quick Rule: After do/does/did, main verb stays V1."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=2", "t": "Simple Past & Past Habit", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Identify the tense in the source example. Karan played cricket. Answer: Simple Past Hinglish Explanation: played V2 form hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Past”. Simple Past = V2. Quick Rule: Simple Past = V2.","Choose the source correction in the historical-belief sentence. Many ancient cultures believed that the Earth resembles a flat disc. Answer: resembled Hinglish Explanation: Past reporting context me source example belief ko resembled me backshift karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “resembled”. Past reporting can backshift a non-universal reported belief. Quick Rule: Past reporting can backshift a non-universal reported belief.","Why is Simple Past used here? I met your father yesterday. Answer: Finished action in a definite past time Hinglish Explanation: Sentence completed past event ko definite past context me dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Finished action in a definite past time”. Finished/definite past -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Finished/definite past -> Simple Past.","Why is Simple Past used here? My father went to Manali last year. Answer: Finished action in a definite past time Hinglish Explanation: Sentence completed past event ko definite past context me dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Finished action in a definite past time”. Finished/definite past -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Finished/definite past -> Simple Past.","Why is Simple Past used here? I bought this pen in UK. Answer: Finished action in a definite past time Hinglish Explanation: Sentence completed past event ko definite past context me dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Finished action in a definite past time”. Finished/definite past -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Finished/definite past -> Simple Past.","Choose the correct sentence for a finished past event. A few years ago an accident _____ her family , but she survived. Answer: an accident destroyed her family Hinglish Explanation: A few years ago clear finished past time hai. Isliye destroyed . English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “an accident destroyed her family”. Finished action + definite past time -> Simple Past (V2). Quick Rule: Finished action + definite past time -> Simple Past (V2).","Choose the best correction. Science has given us many new inventions in the 19th century. Answer: gave Hinglish Explanation: In the 19th century completely finished time period hai; Present Perfect use nahi karte. So gave . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “gave”. Definite finished past time -> Simple Past, not Present Perfect. Quick Rule: Definite finished past time -> Simple Past, not Present Perfect.","Choose the best correction for the dated 2006 event. On October 12, 2006, the Indian Super League has gotten off to a splendid start. Answer: got off Hinglish Explanation: Exact past date diya hua hai, so action ko got off Simple Past me rakhna chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “got off”. Exact past date/time marker -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Exact past date/time marker -> Simple Past.","Choose the corrected form. It is time we work hard. Answer: worked Hinglish Explanation: It is time + subject + V2 source pattern: we worked . English Explanation: It is time + subject + V2 source pattern: we worked. Rule: It is time + subject + V2. Quick Rule: It is time + subject + V2.","Choose the corrected form. It is high time she leaves for the station. Answer: left Hinglish Explanation: It is high time ke baad conventional past form left . English Explanation: The correct answer is “left”. It is high time + subject + V2. Quick Rule: It is high time + subject + V2.","Choose the correct past form. The whole neighbourhood was in shock after they were hearing a piece of terrifying news about the murder. Answer: they heard a piece of terrifying news Hinglish Explanation: News receive/hear karna yahan completed event hai, prolonged background activity nahi. So they heard . English Explanation: The correct answer is “they heard a piece of terrifying news”. A completed past event is normally expressed with Simple Past. Quick Rule: A completed past event is normally expressed with Simple Past.","Complete the 'high time' expression correctly. It is high time we _____ for competitive exams. Answer: prepared Hinglish Explanation: It is high time ke baad conventional form subject + V2 hota hai: we prepared . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “prepared”. It is high time + subject + V2. Quick Rule: It is high time + subject + V2.","Choose the correct form after 'It is time'. It is time you _____ food. Answer: ate Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern me It is time + subject + V2 use hua hai, hence ate . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “ate”. It is time + subject + V2 (to mean the right time has arrived). Quick Rule: It is time + subject + V2 (to mean the right time has arrived).","Choose the best correction. It is an opportune time the state government had taken some strict actions against the wrongdoers. Answer: took Hinglish Explanation: Source example opportune-time construction ko past-form pattern ke saath correct karta hai: took . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “took”. In this source pattern, 'It is an opportune time + subject + V2' is used. Quick Rule: In this source pattern, 'It is an opportune time + subject + V2' is used.","Choose the correct sentence. It is time you studies well to pass the examination. Answer: studied well Hinglish Explanation: It is time ke baad V2 : you studied well . 'studies' subject 'you' ke saath bhi wrong hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “studied well”. It is time + subject + V2. Quick Rule: It is time + subject + V2.","Choose the correct form for a past habit. I _____ sand in childhood. Answer: used to eat Hinglish Explanation: Past repeated habit ke liye used to + V1 : used to eat . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “used to eat”. used to + base verb (V1) expresses a past habit/state no longer true. Quick Rule: used to + base verb (V1) expresses a past habit/state no longer true.","Choose the correct form for a past habit. He _____ football for the local team. Answer: used to play Hinglish Explanation: used to ke baad base form play aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “used to play”. used to + V1; never used to + V2. Quick Rule: used to + V1; never used to + V2.","Choose the best correction. He is gambling a lot. But when his wife and children left him, he is gambling heavily. Answer: used to gamble Hinglish Explanation: Past period ki repeated habit describe ho rahi hai, so used to gamble . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “used to gamble”. A repeated past habit can be expressed with used to + V1. Quick Rule: A repeated past habit can be expressed with used to + V1.","Choose the correct correction. When I was a kid, I used to going to the seaside every summer. Answer: used to go Hinglish Explanation: used to ke baad gerund nahi, base verb go chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “used to go”. used to + V1, not used to + V-ing. Quick Rule: used to + V1, not used to + V-ing.","Classify the source sentence. Did she eat food? Answer: Simple Past interrogative Hinglish Explanation: did + V1 ya did not + V1 Simple Past question/negative banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Past interrogative”. Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1.","Classify the source sentence. Did she go to college? Answer: Simple Past interrogative Hinglish Explanation: did + V1 ya did not + V1 Simple Past question/negative banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Past interrogative”. Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1.","Classify the source sentence. She did not eat food. Answer: Simple Past negative Hinglish Explanation: did + V1 ya did not + V1 Simple Past question/negative banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Past negative”. Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1.","Classify the source sentence. She did not go to college. Answer: Simple Past negative Hinglish Explanation: did + V1 ya did not + V1 Simple Past question/negative banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Past negative”. Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Past question/negative uses did + V1.","Choose the source correction. He was late for his presentation because the train had not arrived on time yesterday. Answer: did not arrive Hinglish Explanation: Yesterday definite finished past context hai; source correction did not arrive hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “did not arrive”. Simple Past negative = did not + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Past negative = did not + V1.","Choose the correction for the short completed action. She was trying to figure out which dress to choose when her mother had rung the doorbell. Answer: rang Hinglish Explanation: Ongoing was trying ko short event rang interrupt karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “rang”. Interrupting short action -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Interrupting short action -> Simple Past.","Choose the correction. He was strolling in the neighbourhood when the old lady has started arguing with him. Answer: started Hinglish Explanation: Narrative past frame me short event started Simple Past hoga. English Explanation: The correct answer is “started”. Past narrative short event -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Past narrative short event -> Simple Past.","Identify the sequence. When I went to meet Jatin, he went to Gym. Answer: Both actions are Simple Past sequence Hinglish Explanation: Dono actions V2 me hain; source simple past sequence dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Both actions are Simple Past sequence”. Simple sequence in past may use V2 for both actions. Quick Rule: Simple sequence in past may use V2 for both actions.","Choose the correction for the definite past time. For instance, when he had come to meet me last week, I had gone to Mumbai. Answer: came Hinglish Explanation: last week aur later past event ke liye source correction came Simple Past. English Explanation: The correct answer is “came”. Later/second past action often uses Simple Past while earlier one may use Past Perfect. Quick Rule: Later/second past action often uses Simple Past while earlier one may use Past Perfect."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=3", "t": "Future & Time-Condition Clauses", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Identify the tense in the source example. Karan will play cricket. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: will + play (V1) future structure hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Future”. Simple Future = will/shall + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Future = will/shall + V1.","Identify the tense. The college will re-open in November. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: will + V1 simple future structure hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Future”. will/shall + V1 = Simple Future. Quick Rule: will/shall + V1 = Simple Future.","Identify the tense. He will buy a flat soon. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: will buy non-fixed future action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Future”. Simple Future = will + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Future = will + V1.","Identify the tense. The P.M. will give a speech tomorrow. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: will give future event batata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Future”. will + V1 expresses simple future. Quick Rule: will + V1 expresses simple future.","Which form expresses the fixed future event in Simple Present? The P.M. gives a speech tomorrow. Answer: gives Hinglish Explanation: Fixed/scheduled event ko source gives Simple Present me bhi dikhata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “gives”. Fixed/timetabled future can use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Fixed/timetabled future can use Simple Present.","Identify the fixed-future form. The match will start at 7 in the evening. Answer: will start Hinglish Explanation: Future clock-time event ko will + V1 se express kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “will start”. Simple Future can express a fixed future event. Quick Rule: Simple Future can express a fixed future event.","Identify the alternative fixed-future form. The match starts at 7 in the evening. Answer: Simple Present used for schedule Hinglish Explanation: Schedule/timetable sense me starts present form future time ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Simple Present used for schedule”. Schedules often use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Schedules often use Simple Present.","Why is the present form acceptable? My grandfather retires on 28th November. Answer: Fixed/scheduled future action Hinglish Explanation: Specific scheduled future date hai; source me retires fixed future ke liye hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Fixed/scheduled future action”. Fixed future events may use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Fixed future events may use Simple Present.","Why is the present form acceptable? The school reopens in October. Answer: Fixed/scheduled future action Hinglish Explanation: Reopening scheduled/fixed event hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Fixed/scheduled future action”. Simple Present can describe timetabled future events. Quick Rule: Simple Present can describe timetabled future events.","Choose the correct form for a scheduled/fixed future event. The train _____ at 6:30 in the evening. Answer: leaves Hinglish Explanation: Timetable/schedule ko Simple Present me express kiya ja sakta hai: leaves . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “leaves”. Timetabled/fixed future events often use Simple Present. Quick Rule: Timetabled/fixed future events often use Simple Present.","Choose the best correction. Her brother returned tomorrow after three years. Answer: returns tomorrow Hinglish Explanation: Tomorrow future time hai; source fixed-action pattern me returns tomorrow use karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “returns tomorrow”. A fixed/scheduled future action may use Simple Present. Quick Rule: A fixed/scheduled future action may use Simple Present.","Identify the tense. She will arrive at 6 o'clock. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: will arrive = will + V1. English Explanation: will arrive = will + V1. Rule: Simple Future = will/shall + V1. Quick Rule: Simple Future = will/shall + V1.","Identify the tense. You will go to college tomorrow. Answer: Simple Future Hinglish Explanation: Tomorrow ke saath will go simple future hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Simple Future”. will + V1 expresses future action. Quick Rule: will + V1 expresses future action.","Choose the correct time-clause sentence. If you will call me, I will give you the address. Answer: call Hinglish Explanation: Future meaning hone ke bawajood if-clause me will nahi; Simple Present use hota hai: If you call... English Explanation: The correct answer is “call”. Future condition: If + Simple Present, main clause can use will/shall. Quick Rule: Future condition: If + Simple Present, main clause can use will/shall.","Choose the correct form after 'when' for future time. When it will rain , the plant will grow. Answer: rains Hinglish Explanation: Future time clause when ke baad will nahi; rains . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “rains”. Future time words (when, until, before, after, as soon as, etc.) normally take Simple Present in the subordinate clause. Quick Rule: Future time words (when, until, before, after, as soon as, etc.) normally take Simple Present in the subordinate clause.","Choose the best correction. Unless you will work hard, you will not succeed. Answer: work Hinglish Explanation: Unless condition introduce karta hai. Future result ke liye subordinate clause me work Simple Present. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “work”. Unless + Simple Present for future condition. Quick Rule: Unless + Simple Present for future condition.","Choose the correct form after 'provided that'. I shall help him provided that he will give me 60k. Answer: gives Hinglish Explanation: Provided that condition clause hai; yahan future reference ke liye gives . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “gives”. provided that + Simple Present for future condition. Quick Rule: provided that + Simple Present for future condition.","Choose the correct form after 'before'. All the flowers in his garden will die before the rain will fall . Answer: falls Hinglish Explanation: Main clause future me hai, but before -clause me falls Simple Present. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “falls”. before + Simple Present in a future time clause. Quick Rule: before + Simple Present in a future time clause.","Choose the correct form after 'as soon as'. He shall buy a motorcycle as soon as the price will come down. Answer: comes Hinglish Explanation: as soon as ke subordinate clause me future will avoid hota hai; comes . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “comes”. as soon as + Simple Present for future reference. Quick Rule: as soon as + Simple Present for future reference.","Choose the best correction. Clearing the exam was the first step towards her career. She goes for the training when she receives the call letter. Answer: will go Hinglish Explanation: Yahan main action future result hai, isliye main clause will go ; when she receives subordinate clause Simple Present me rehta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “will go”. Future time clause: main clause may use will; when-clause stays Simple Present. Quick Rule: Future time clause: main clause may use will; when-clause stays Simple Present.","Choose the correct form after 'unless'. Unless I am having enough time, I will not watch the film. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Possession/state sense me have stative hai aur future condition clause me Simple Present chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have”. Unless + Simple Present; stative 'have' normally does not use continuous for possession. Quick Rule: Unless + Simple Present; stative 'have' normally does not use continuous for possession.","Choose the correction in the unless-clause. You will need to maintain this pace of understanding and learning things unless you will get your dream job. Answer: get Hinglish Explanation: Future main clause ke saath unless clause me will nahi; get Simple Present. English Explanation: The correct answer is “get”. Future condition/time clause -> Simple Present inside unless/if/when etc. Quick Rule: Future condition/time clause -> Simple Present inside unless/if/when etc.","Choose the correction after provided that. I shall help you with this problem provided that you will sign my report card as a guardian. Answer: sign Hinglish Explanation: provided that condition clause me source sign Simple Present use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “sign”. Condition clause uses Simple Present for future reference. Quick Rule: Condition clause uses Simple Present for future reference."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=4", "t": "Continuous & Stative Verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Choose the form that expresses repeated irritation. He always babbles about trifle things. Answer: is always babbling Hinglish Explanation: Complaint/irritation sense me is always babbling continuous + always natural hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “is always babbling”. always + Present Continuous can show repeated irritating behaviour. Quick Rule: always + Present Continuous can show repeated irritating behaviour.","Identify the tense in the source example. I am teaching. Answer: Present Continuous Hinglish Explanation: am/is/are + V-ing present moment/ongoing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Present Continuous”. Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing.","Identify the tense in the source example. We are studying. Answer: Present Continuous Hinglish Explanation: am/is/are + V-ing present moment/ongoing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Present Continuous”. Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing.","Identify the tense in the source example. The boys are playing hockey in the field. Answer: Present Continuous Hinglish Explanation: am/is/are + V-ing present moment/ongoing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Present Continuous”. Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing.","Choose the correction for the stative meaning. She is owning a Scooty. Answer: owns Hinglish Explanation: Possession sense me own stative hai; source owns use karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “owns”. Stative possession verbs normally avoid continuous. Quick Rule: Stative possession verbs normally avoid continuous.","Choose the correction for the stative meaning. He is knowing me. Answer: knows Hinglish Explanation: know stative mental verb hai; knows chahiye. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “knows”. Stative know normally uses simple form. Quick Rule: Stative know normally uses simple form.","Why is continuous correct here? I am playing cricket. Answer: It is a physical action in progress Hinglish Explanation: Playing physical activity hai aur ongoing action show kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is a physical action in progress”. Dynamic physical actions readily take continuous forms. Quick Rule: Dynamic physical actions readily take continuous forms.","Why is continuous correct here? They are going to Manali tonight. Answer: It is a planned/arranged near-future action Hinglish Explanation: Present Continuous future arrangement ko bhi express kar sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is a planned/arranged near-future action”. Present Continuous can describe a planned near-future arrangement. Quick Rule: Present Continuous can describe a planned near-future arrangement.","Identify the tense used in the source example. I am teaching . Answer: Present Continuous Hinglish Explanation: am + teaching (V-ing) action ko present moment par continuing dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Present Continuous”. Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present Continuous = am/is/are + V-ing.","Choose the correction when see means visual perception. He is seeing me coming towards him. Answer: sees Hinglish Explanation: Visual perception sense me see stative; source correction sees . English Explanation: Visual perception sense me see stative; source correction sees. Rule: Perception sense of see normally uses simple tense. Quick Rule: Perception sense of see normally uses simple tense.","Choose the correct stative form of 'see' meaning perception. My father is seeing that you are not doing the work properly. Answer: sees Hinglish Explanation: Yahan see = perceive/notice , stative sense hai. Isliye sees , not continuous. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “sees”. Stative perception 'see' normally uses a simple tense. Quick Rule: Stative perception 'see' normally uses a simple tense.","Choose the correct active meaning of 'see'. I _____ the Chief Minister tomorrow. Answer: am seeing Hinglish Explanation: Yahan see = meet , planned activity hai. Is active meaning me Present Continuous possible hai: am seeing . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “am seeing”. Some stative verbs can use continuous when their meaning becomes an active action (see = meet). Quick Rule: Some stative verbs can use continuous when their meaning becomes an active action (see = meet).","Choose the correction when hear means passive perception. My father is hearing that you are going to Shimla. Answer: hears Hinglish Explanation: Passive hearing/perception me hears simple form natural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “hears”. Perception hear is normally stative; continuous is for special active uses. Quick Rule: Perception hear is normally stative; continuous is for special active uses.","Choose the correct perception form. She is hearing a noise. Answer: hears Hinglish Explanation: hear = perceive sound stative sense me hai, so hears . English Explanation: The correct answer is “hears”. Perception 'hear' is usually simple, not continuous. Quick Rule: Perception 'hear' is usually simple, not continuous.","Which sentence is correct because 'hear' means actively conduct/listen to a case? The judge _____ a case today. Answer: is hearing Hinglish Explanation: Court context me hear a case ek deliberate ongoing activity hai, so is hearing valid hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is hearing”. A normally stative verb may take continuous when used as a deliberate activity. Quick Rule: A normally stative verb may take continuous when used as a deliberate activity.","Choose the correct stative form of 'taste'. This stew is tasting fantastic. Answer: tastes Hinglish Explanation: Yahan taste = have a flavour state hai; tastes fantastic sahi hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “tastes”. Taste meaning 'have a flavour' is normally stative/simple. Quick Rule: Taste meaning 'have a flavour' is normally stative/simple.","Choose the correct active form of 'taste'. He _____ the food to see if it's okay. Answer: is tasting Hinglish Explanation: Yahan taste = intentionally sample action hai, so continuous is tasting . English Explanation: The correct answer is “is tasting”. Taste meaning 'sample/test deliberately' can be continuous. Quick Rule: Taste meaning 'sample/test deliberately' can be continuous.","Choose the correction when appear means seem. Joey is appearing happy this morning. He's smiling. Answer: appears Hinglish Explanation: Meaning seems hai, so stative appears . English Explanation: The correct answer is “appears”. appear = seem usually avoids continuous. Quick Rule: appear = seem usually avoids continuous.","Why is continuous acceptable here? Now, the new artist is appearing on the stage. Answer: appear means perform/come before an audience Hinglish Explanation: Yahan appear ka meaning stage par perform/come out hai—dynamic action. English Explanation: The correct answer is “appear means perform/come before an audience”. Dynamic meaning of appear can use continuous. Quick Rule: Dynamic meaning of appear can use continuous.","Choose the correct stative form of 'smell'. You are smelling nice. Answer: smell Hinglish Explanation: Yahan smell = have a scent state hai, so You smell nice . English Explanation: The correct answer is “smell”. Smell meaning 'have an odour' is normally simple. Quick Rule: Smell meaning 'have an odour' is normally simple.","Choose the source form used when 'smell' means an active physical action. The dog _____ the pillow. Answer: was smelling Hinglish Explanation: Dog actively pillow ko sniff/smell kar raha tha, isliye action sense me continuous was smelling valid hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was smelling”. Sense verbs can take continuous when they describe deliberate/physical activity. Quick Rule: Sense verbs can take continuous when they describe deliberate/physical activity.","Choose the correct stative form of 'appear' meaning 'seem'. The exam is appearing to be difficult. Answer: appears Hinglish Explanation: appear = seem stative meaning hai, so appears to be difficult . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “appears”. Appear meaning 'seem' normally uses a simple tense. Quick Rule: Appear meaning 'seem' normally uses a simple tense.","Choose the correct form when 'appear' means perform/be published. My new book _____ next month. Answer: is appearing Hinglish Explanation: Book ka appear yahan 'be published/come out' active scheduled meaning hai; is appearing valid hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is appearing”. Appear can be continuous when it means come out/be published/perform. Quick Rule: Appear can be continuous when it means come out/be published/perform.","Choose the correction when think means opinion. He is thinking you will succeed. Answer: thinks Hinglish Explanation: Opinion sense me think stative-like simple form leta hai: thinks . English Explanation: The correct answer is “thinks”. think = believe/opine normally uses simple form. Quick Rule: think = believe/opine normally uses simple form.","Why is continuous correct? He is thinking of going to Chandigarh. Answer: thinking means considering Hinglish Explanation: thinking of = considering, ek active mental process hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “thinking means considering”. think = consider can use continuous. Quick Rule: think = consider can use continuous.","Choose the correction for possession. I am having three children. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Children possess/relationship sense me have stative hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. have = possess/relationship normally uses simple tense. Quick Rule: have = possess/relationship normally uses simple tense.","Why is continuous correct? He is having a good time these days. Answer: have means experience/enjoy, not possession Hinglish Explanation: have a good time activity/experience sense hai, so continuous valid. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have means experience/enjoy, not possession”. have can be dynamic for activities/experiences. Quick Rule: have can be dynamic for activities/experiences.","Choose the correct opinion form. I am thinking that coffee is great. Answer: think Hinglish Explanation: Yahan think = hold an opinion stative sense hai, so I think . English Explanation: The correct answer is “think”. Think meaning 'believe/opine' is normally simple. Quick Rule: Think meaning 'believe/opine' is normally simple.","Choose the correct form when 'think' means consider. I _____ of opening a new business. Answer: am thinking Hinglish Explanation: Yahan thinking of = considering; active mental process hai, so continuous sahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “am thinking”. Think meaning 'consider' can use continuous. Quick Rule: Think meaning 'consider' can use continuous.","Choose the correct possession form. He is having a great family. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: have = possess stative hai. Present possession ke liye has . English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. Have meaning possession normally does not use continuous. Quick Rule: Have meaning possession normally does not use continuous.","Choose the correct activity form of 'have'. I _____ lunch right now. Answer: am having Hinglish Explanation: Yahan have lunch activity hai, possession nahi; action in progress ke liye am having . English Explanation: The correct answer is “am having”. Have can be continuous in activity expressions such as have lunch/have a good time. Quick Rule: Have can be continuous in activity expressions such as have lunch/have a good time.","Choose the correction. Are you hearing a noise? Answer: Do you hear Hinglish Explanation: Passive perception hear ke liye simple question Do you hear...? . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Do you hear”. Stative hear generally uses do/does in Simple Present questions. Quick Rule: Stative hear generally uses do/does in Simple Present questions.","Choose the correction. It is appearing to me that my friends are plotting against me. Answer: appears Hinglish Explanation: appears to me = seems; stative meaning me simple form. English Explanation: appears to me = seems; stative meaning me simple form. Rule: appear = seem -> simple tense. Quick Rule: appear = seem -> simple tense.","Choose the corrected verb phrase. You are a very lovable person but I am not loving you. Answer: do not love Hinglish Explanation: love emotion/state verb hai; neutral state ke liye do not love . English Explanation: The correct answer is “do not love”. Stative emotion verbs normally use simple tense. Quick Rule: Stative emotion verbs normally use simple tense.","Choose the source correction for the developing situation. After his last swim, he does not hear well. I think to take him to an ENT specialist as soon as possible. Answer: am thinking Hinglish Explanation: Yahan speaker current consideration/process batata hai; source correction am thinking . English Explanation: The correct answer is “am thinking”. think = consider can use Present Continuous. Quick Rule: think = consider can use Present Continuous.","Choose the corrected pair. Her dishes are smelling and tasting savourly. Answer: smell and taste Hinglish Explanation: Dishes ki inherent smell/taste describe ho rahi hai; stative linking sense me smell and taste . English Explanation: The correct answer is “smell and taste”. Sense/linking verbs describing a state usually use simple forms. Quick Rule: Sense/linking verbs describing a state usually use simple forms.","Choose the correction for an action happening at the moment. I don't think it is the right time to bring this topic up, since they have lunch at the moment. Answer: are having Hinglish Explanation: at the moment aur have lunch activity sense → are having . English Explanation: at the moment aur have lunch activity sense → are having. Rule: Present activity now -> Present Continuous. Quick Rule: Present activity now -> Present Continuous.","Identify the tense. I was teaching. Answer: Past Continuous Hinglish Explanation: was/were + V-ing past me continuing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Past Continuous”. Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing.","Identify the tense. We were writing. Answer: Past Continuous Hinglish Explanation: was/were + V-ing past me continuing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Past Continuous”. Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing.","Identify the tense. The boys were playing cricket. Answer: Past Continuous Hinglish Explanation: was/were + V-ing past me continuing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Past Continuous”. Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing.","Choose the correct parallel-action sentence. While my sister was singing, I had been sleeping . Answer: was sleeping Hinglish Explanation: Do parallel ongoing past actions ke liye source was singing / was sleeping use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was sleeping”. Parallel ongoing past actions commonly use Past Continuous in both clauses. Quick Rule: Parallel ongoing past actions commonly use Past Continuous in both clauses.","Choose the correction for the first ongoing action. While I had been writing this chapter, my friend was watching T.V. Answer: was writing Hinglish Explanation: Parallel actions same past frame me continuing hain: was writing + was watching . English Explanation: The correct answer is “was writing”. Parallel Past Continuous: while + was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Parallel Past Continuous: while + was/were + V-ing.","Which sentence uses the correct long-action + short-action pattern? I was reading a book when the delivery boy arrived. Answer: was reading Hinglish Explanation: Long background action was reading , short interrupting event arrived Simple Past. English Explanation: Long background action was reading, short interrupting event arrived Simple Past. Rule: Long past action: Past Continuous; interrupting short event: Simple Past. Quick Rule: Long past action: Past Continuous; interrupting short event: Simple Past.","Choose the correct background-action form. When the storm hit, they were sleeping . Answer: were sleeping Hinglish Explanation: Storm hit short event hai; sleeping us waqt ongoing background action. English Explanation: The correct answer is “were sleeping”. Past Continuous + Simple Past for interrupted action. Quick Rule: Past Continuous + Simple Past for interrupted action.","Choose the correction. Radhika was hurried to the station when she dashed against the truck. Answer: was hurrying Hinglish Explanation: Ongoing action ka active form was hurrying chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was hurrying”. Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing for ongoing action. Quick Rule: Past Continuous = was/were + V-ing for ongoing action.","Choose the correct Past Continuous form. He found the old coin as he cleans the floor. Answer: was cleaning Hinglish Explanation: Coin find hona short event hai; floor cleaning background action chal rahi thi. So was cleaning . English Explanation: The correct answer is “was cleaning”. Past Continuous often gives the longer/background action interrupted by a short Simple Past event. Quick Rule: Past Continuous often gives the longer/background action interrupted by a short Simple Past event.","Choose the correct form for the shorter interrupting action. We were still standing in the queue when the film was beginning . Answer: began Hinglish Explanation: Queue me standing ongoing background hai; film ka start point short event hai, so began Simple Past. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “began”. Long background -> Past Continuous; short interrupting event -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Long background -> Past Continuous; short interrupting event -> Simple Past.","Choose the correction. Rahul attempt to open the umbrella when her spectacles slipped off and fell down. Answer: was attempting Hinglish Explanation: Umbrella kholne ki process ongoing thi jab short event hua; was attempting . English Explanation: The correct answer is “was attempting”. Ongoing past action interrupted by short event -> Past Continuous. Quick Rule: Ongoing past action interrupted by short event -> Past Continuous.","Choose the correction. He used to walk along the road when a wild and ferocious dog knocked him down. Answer: was walking Hinglish Explanation: Specific moment par ongoing walk tha, habit nahi; was walking . English Explanation: The correct answer is “was walking”. Specific ongoing past scene -> Past Continuous, not used to. Quick Rule: Specific ongoing past scene -> Past Continuous, not used to.","Identify the tense. He will be playing cricket tomorrow morning. Answer: Future Continuous Hinglish Explanation: will/shall + be + V-ing future me ongoing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Future Continuous”. Future Continuous = will/shall + be + V-ing. Quick Rule: Future Continuous = will/shall + be + V-ing.","Identify the tense. At this time tomorrow, I shall be watching a film. Answer: Future Continuous Hinglish Explanation: will/shall + be + V-ing future me ongoing action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Future Continuous”. Future Continuous = will/shall + be + V-ing. Quick Rule: Future Continuous = will/shall + be + V-ing."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=5", "t": "Present Perfect", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Which form shows an action connected with the present? Tanu came to meet me / Tanu has come to meet me. Answer: has come Hinglish Explanation: Source timeline me present connection ke liye has come Present Perfect hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has come”. Present Perfect = has/have + V3. Quick Rule: Present Perfect = has/have + V3.","Identify the tense in the source contrast. Tanu has come to meet me. Answer: Present Perfect Hinglish Explanation: has + come (V3) = Present Perfect; situation present se connected hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Present Perfect”. Present Perfect = has/have + V3. Quick Rule: Present Perfect = has/have + V3.","Choose the correct sentence with a definite past-time expression. I have eaten lunch an hour ago. Answer: ate Hinglish Explanation: An hour ago finished past time hai; Present Perfect ke saath use nahi hota. So ate . English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “ate”. ago/yesterday/last... -> Simple Past, not Present Perfect. Quick Rule: ago/yesterday/last... -> Simple Past, not Present Perfect.","Choose the correct tense for the dated historical event. Man has reached the moon in 1969. Answer: reached Hinglish Explanation: In 1969 definite finished past time hai, so reached . English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “reached”. Finished past date -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Finished past date -> Simple Past.","Choose the correct form with an unfinished time span/count up to now. I asked this question from management three times. Answer: have asked Hinglish Explanation: Source example repeated experience/count ko present relevance ke saath have asked me rakhta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have asked”. Present Perfect can express repeated experiences/actions up to now when no finished past time is given. Quick Rule: Present Perfect can express repeated experiences/actions up to now when no finished past time is given.","Choose the best correction. She used four chapters of this book so far. Answer: has used Hinglish Explanation: So far present tak ka unfinished span dikhata hai, so has used . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has used”. so far/up to now -> Present Perfect. Quick Rule: so far/up to now -> Present Perfect.","Choose the correct form after 'This is the first time'. This is the first time I saw this movie. Answer: have seen Hinglish Explanation: This is the first time... present experience ko present tak connect karta hai, so have seen . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have seen”. This is the first/second/... time + Present Perfect. Quick Rule: This is the first/second/... time + Present Perfect.","Which phrase needs no correction? Everything we have so far looked at gives inconclusive evidence about the change. Answer: have so far looked at Hinglish Explanation: So far present tak ke span ko dikhata hai; have looked at suitable hai. English Explanation: The required word or phrase is “have so far looked at”. so far + Present Perfect when the period continues to the present. Quick Rule: so far + Present Perfect when the period continues to the present.","Choose the correction for a state continuing up to now. Have you heard the news? Karan and Himanshi are engaged. That is not news; I knew it for ages. Answer: have known Hinglish Explanation: for ages state past se present tak continue karta hai; have known . English Explanation: The correct answer is “have known”. Unfinished state up to now -> Present Perfect. Quick Rule: Unfinished state up to now -> Present Perfect.","Choose the best correction. The state government presented 18 new rape and murder cases in the last three days. No check-solid actions had been taken so far by the concerned authorities. Answer: have been taken so far Hinglish Explanation: So far present tak ka reference hai; passive form have been taken . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have been taken so far”. Present Perfect passive = have/has been + V3. Quick Rule: Present Perfect passive = have/has been + V3.","Choose the best correction. The meeting with clients continued for a very long time last night. We did not start with the new project yet . Answer: have not started yet Hinglish Explanation: Source yet ko present non-completion se link karta hai, so have not started yet . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have not started yet”. yet commonly appears with Present Perfect in negative/interrogative sentences about unfinished results. Quick Rule: yet commonly appears with Present Perfect in negative/interrogative sentences about unfinished results.","Choose the best correction. Although an official announcement was made last week about the submission, no reports were submitted so far . Answer: have been submitted so far Hinglish Explanation: So far present tak ka result hai; passive chahiye: have been submitted . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have been submitted so far”. so far + Present Perfect; passive = have/has been + V3. Quick Rule: so far + Present Perfect; passive = have/has been + V3.","Which form is also possible for the same one-month state/result? She has lived in London for a month. Answer: Present Perfect Hinglish Explanation: has lived Present Perfect state/result ko express karta hai; source dono patterns contrast karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Present Perfect”. Some verbs/situations allow Present Perfect as well as Present Perfect Continuous. Quick Rule: Some verbs/situations allow Present Perfect as well as Present Perfect Continuous.","Choose the correction for the stative verb. I have been loving her since we met. Answer: loved Hinglish Explanation: Neutral stative love ko source continuous se hata kar have loved karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “loved”. Stative verbs usually prefer Present Perfect, not Perfect Continuous. Quick Rule: Stative verbs usually prefer Present Perfect, not Perfect Continuous.","Choose the correction for the stative verb. She has been hating me since I broke her heart. Answer: hated Hinglish Explanation: hate state hai; source correction has hated . English Explanation: The correct answer is “hated”. Stative emotion verbs normally avoid continuous forms. Quick Rule: Stative emotion verbs normally avoid continuous forms.","Choose the correction for possession. He has been owing this car for a year. Answer: owned Hinglish Explanation: Source intended possession verb own ; continuous ko has owned me correct kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “owned”. Possession state -> Present Perfect simple, not continuous. Quick Rule: Possession state -> Present Perfect simple, not continuous.","Choose the correction for the stative emotion. He has been adoring her for more than six years. Answer: adored Hinglish Explanation: Long-term emotion/state ke liye source correction has adored . English Explanation: The correct answer is “adored”. Stative emotion verbs generally use perfect simple rather than perfect continuous. Quick Rule: Stative emotion verbs generally use perfect simple rather than perfect continuous."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=6", "t": "Past Perfect", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Identify the earlier action. When I went to meet Jatin, he had gone to Gym. Answer: He had gone to Gym Hinglish Explanation: had gone past of the past—mere arrival se pehle action complete ho chuka tha. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “He had gone to Gym”. Earlier of two past actions -> Past Perfect (had + V3). Quick Rule: Earlier of two past actions -> Past Perfect (had + V3).","Which action happened earlier? When he reached home yesterday, his mother had cooked lunch. Answer: Mother had cooked lunch Hinglish Explanation: had cooked indicates lunch preparation was already complete before he reached. English Explanation: had cooked indicates lunch preparation was already complete before he reached. Rule: Past Perfect marks the earlier completed past action. Quick Rule: Past Perfect marks the earlier completed past action.","Choose the better sequence when lunch was already ready before he reached home. When he reached home yesterday, his mother cooked lunch. Answer: had cooked Hinglish Explanation: Home pahunchne se pehle lunch cook ho chuka tha. Earlier past action -> had cooked . English Explanation: The correct answer is “had cooked”. Earlier of two past actions -> Past Perfect; later action -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: Earlier of two past actions -> Past Perfect; later action -> Simple Past.","Choose the correct earlier/later sequence. I went before he had gone. Answer: I had gone before he went Hinglish Explanation: Source case me before ke pehle wala action earlier hai: I had gone ; later action he went Simple Past. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I had gone before he went”. With two past actions, use Past Perfect for the action completed earlier when sequence needs emphasis. Quick Rule: With two past actions, use Past Perfect for the action completed earlier when sequence needs emphasis.","Choose the best correction. The culprit ran away from the house before the police had come. Answer: had run away; the police came Hinglish Explanation: Culprit ka escape police ke aane se earlier hai. So had run away + later came . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “had run away; the police came”. Earlier past action -> had + V3; later past action -> V2. Quick Rule: Earlier past action -> had + V3; later past action -> V2.","Choose the correct sequence. She submitted her papers to the concerned faculty before the college fair had started. Answer: had submitted; started Hinglish Explanation: Submission fair start hone se pehle complete hua; so had submitted , later action started . English Explanation: The correct answer is “had submitted; started”. Before: earlier action can take Past Perfect, later action Simple Past. Quick Rule: Before: earlier action can take Past Perfect, later action Simple Past.","Choose the correct correction. I reached the examination hall after the exam began . Answer: had begun Hinglish Explanation: Exam ka start mere reach karne se pehle hua; earlier past action -> had begun . English Explanation: The correct answer is “had begun”. After + two past actions: the action that happened first can take Past Perfect. Quick Rule: After + two past actions: the action that happened first can take Past Perfect.","Choose the correct sequence. The chief guest had reached the function after the inaugural dance started. Answer: reached; had started Hinglish Explanation: Dance pehle start hua, chief guest baad me reached. So earlier had started ; later reached . English Explanation: The correct answer is “reached; had started”. Past Perfect marks the earlier of two past events. Quick Rule: Past Perfect marks the earlier of two past events.","Choose the corrected tense sequence. Rahul has succeed in catching the ball before it had reached the boundary line. Answer: had succeeded; reached Hinglish Explanation: Earlier action catching/succeeding ko Past Perfect, later boundary event ko Simple Past: had succeeded ... reached . English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “had succeeded; reached”. With two past actions around before, earlier action can take had + V3 and later V2. Quick Rule: With two past actions around before, earlier action can take had + V3 and later V2.","Choose the best correction. By the time Manoj received the award, he worked with many famous actors. Answer: had worked Hinglish Explanation: Award receive karne ke point se pehle ka accumulated experience hai, so had worked . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “had worked”. By the time + past reference -> earlier completed action often Past Perfect. Quick Rule: By the time + past reference -> earlier completed action often Past Perfect.","Choose the correct sequence. By the time Harvard had sent its admission letter, she had joined Cambridge University. Answer: sent; had joined Hinglish Explanation: By the time wala reference event later Simple Past sent ; usse pehle joining complete: had joined . English Explanation: The correct answer is “sent; had joined”. In 'By the time + Simple Past', the earlier action commonly takes Past Perfect. Quick Rule: In 'By the time + Simple Past', the earlier action commonly takes Past Perfect.","Choose the best correction. I was trying to avoid meeting Karan. I had come after he went . Answer: came after he had gone Hinglish Explanation: Main baad me aaya; Karan pehle ja chuka tha. So later came , earlier had gone . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “came after he had gone”. With after, earlier past action -> Past Perfect; later past action -> Simple Past. Quick Rule: With after, earlier past action -> Past Perfect; later past action -> Simple Past.","Choose the best correction. The thief escaped before I opened the door. Answer: had escaped Hinglish Explanation: Door open karne se pehle escape complete ho chuka tha; source rule emphasis me had escaped . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “had escaped”. Past Perfect can emphasize completion before another past action. Quick Rule: Past Perfect can emphasize completion before another past action.","Choose the correct tense for the earlier dinner action. When she reached for the family reunion, the dinner started . Answer: had started Hinglish Explanation: Dinner uske reach karne se pehle start ho chuka tha, so had started . English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “had started”. Earlier completed past action -> Past Perfect. Quick Rule: Earlier completed past action -> Past Perfect."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=7", "t": "Future Perfect", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Choose the correct form for an action completed by a future deadline. We _____ our coaching by August. Answer: shall have completed Hinglish Explanation: By August future deadline hai aur action us deadline tak complete hoga: shall have completed . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “shall have completed”. Future Perfect = will/shall have + V3 for completion before/by a future time. Quick Rule: Future Perfect = will/shall have + V3 for completion before/by a future time.","Choose the correct tense. By 2050, the robots _____ control all over the world. Answer: will have taken Hinglish Explanation: By 2050 future deadline hai; us deadline tak completion ke liye will have taken . English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “will have taken”. by + future time -> Future Perfect when completion is intended. Quick Rule: by + future time -> Future Perfect when completion is intended.","Choose the best correction. By the end of this century, scientists surely must discover a cure for AIDS. Answer: will surely have discovered Hinglish Explanation: End-of-century deadline se pehle completion predict ho rahi hai, so will surely have discovered . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “will surely have discovered”. Future Perfect expresses an action expected to be complete by a stated future deadline. Quick Rule: Future Perfect expresses an action expected to be complete by a stated future deadline.","Is the highlighted future-perfect phrase correct? His father left him a fortune, but he is so extravagant that he will have spent it all before he is 30. Answer: Yes — no correction required Hinglish Explanation: before he is 30 future deadline hai; us deadline se pehle completion = will have spent . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Yes — no correction required”. Future Perfect expresses completion before a future time/deadline. Quick Rule: Future Perfect expresses completion before a future time/deadline.","Choose the best correction. He has read four plays written by Shakespeare by the end of his vacations. Answer: will have read Hinglish Explanation: By the end of his vacations future deadline hai; completion -> will have read . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “will have read”. Future deadline + completed amount/result -> Future Perfect. Quick Rule: Future deadline + completed amount/result -> Future Perfect."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=02.+Tenses&section=8", "t": "Perfect Continuous & Duration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 02. Tenses", "x": ["Choose the correct tense for an action that started in the past and is still continuing. Rahul _____ here for five years. Answer: has been working Hinglish Explanation: Five-year duration past se start hokar present tak continue ho rahi hai, so has been working . English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “has been working”. Present Perfect Continuous = has/have been + V-ing for continuing duration up to now. Quick Rule: Present Perfect Continuous = has/have been + V-ing for continuing duration up to now.","Choose the correct form with 'since 2015'. I _____ English since 2015. Answer: have been teaching Hinglish Explanation: Since 2015 starting point hai aur action ab tak continue hai; have been teaching . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have been teaching”. since + starting point; use Present Perfect Continuous for a continuing activity. Quick Rule: since + starting point; use Present Perfect Continuous for a continuing activity.","Choose the best correction. I am trying to finish this letter for one hour. Answer: have been trying Hinglish Explanation: For one hour duration past se now tak chal rahi hai, so have been trying . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have been trying”. for + duration continuing to now -> Present Perfect Continuous. Quick Rule: for + duration continuing to now -> Present Perfect Continuous.","Choose the source correction for an action continuing up to today. Society had been playing a crucial role in the upbringing of a child since his childhood. Answer: has been playing Hinglish Explanation: Context even today / up to present tak continuation dikhata hai, so has been playing . English Explanation: The correct answer is “has been playing”. Past-started action continuing to present -> Present Perfect Continuous. Quick Rule: Past-started action continuing to present -> Present Perfect Continuous.","Which form emphasizes duration/continuity? She has been living in London for a month. Answer: Present Perfect Continuous Hinglish Explanation: has been + living + for duration/ongoing activity ko emphasize karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Present Perfect Continuous”. Present Perfect Continuous = has/have been + V-ing + for/since. Quick Rule: Present Perfect Continuous = has/have been + V-ing + for/since.","Choose the correct correction for a stative verb. She has been knowing me since 2005. Answer: has known Hinglish Explanation: Know stative verb hai, normally continuous nahi hota. Since 2005 + state -> has known . English Explanation: The correct answer is “has known”. Stative verbs such as know often use Present Perfect simple, not Perfect Continuous. Quick Rule: Stative verbs such as know often use Present Perfect simple, not Perfect Continuous.","Identify the tense. I had been sleeping for six hours when my mother arrived. Answer: Past Perfect Continuous Hinglish Explanation: had been + sleeping + for six hours later past event se pehle duration dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Past Perfect Continuous”. Past Perfect Continuous = had been + V-ing + for/since. Quick Rule: Past Perfect Continuous = had been + V-ing + for/since.","Choose the correct Past Perfect Continuous sentence. When the dance teacher came, he _____ for three hours. Answer: had been dancing Hinglish Explanation: Dance teacher ke aane se pehle activity teen ghante se chal rahi thi: had been dancing . English Explanation: The correct answer is “had been dancing”. Past Perfect Continuous = had been + V-ing + duration before a past reference point. Quick Rule: Past Perfect Continuous = had been + V-ing + duration before a past reference point.","Identify the tense. By this time tomorrow, she will have been doing this work for three days. Answer: Future Perfect Continuous Hinglish Explanation: Future point tak activity ki duration three days hogi. English Explanation: The correct tense/form is “Future Perfect Continuous”. Future Perfect Continuous = will have been + V-ing + for/since. Quick Rule: Future Perfect Continuous = will have been + V-ing + for/since.","Choose the correct Future Perfect Continuous sentence. By this time next year, I _____ here for three years. Answer: shall have been working Hinglish Explanation: Future reference point tak continuous duration three years complete ho chuki hogi; shall have been working . English Explanation: The correct answer is “shall have been working”. Future Perfect Continuous = will/shall have been + V-ing + duration by a future point. Quick Rule: Future Perfect Continuous = will/shall have been + V-ing + duration by a future point."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=1", "t": "Conditional Types", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Identify the conditional type. If you come here, I will help you. Answer: First Conditional Hinglish Explanation: The if-clause uses Simple Present (come) and the result uses will + V1 (will help). This describes a real/possible future condition. English Explanation: The if-clause uses Simple Present (come) and the result uses will + V1 (will help). This describes a real/possible future condition. Rule: First Conditional: If + Simple Present, will + V1. Quick Rule: First Conditional: If + Simple Present, will + V1.","Identify the conditional type. If I win the lottery, I will buy all the famous super cars. Answer: First Conditional Hinglish Explanation: win is present form and will buy is future result. The situation is presented as a real/possible future possibility. English Explanation: win is present form and will buy is future result. The situation is presented as a real/possible future possibility. Rule: First Conditional = real/possible future condition. Quick Rule: First Conditional = real/possible future condition.","Identify the conditional type. If I won the lottery, I would buy all the famous super cars. Answer: Second Conditional Hinglish Explanation: won is past form used for an unreal/imaginary present or future condition; the result is would + V1 . English Explanation: won is past form used for an unreal/imaginary present or future condition; the result is would + V1. Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1. Quick Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1.","Identify the conditional type. If I had won the lottery, I would have bought all the famous super cars. Answer: Third Conditional Hinglish Explanation: had won (Past Perfect) + would have bought describes an unreal past situation—something that did not happen. English Explanation: had won (Past Perfect) + would have bought describes an unreal past situation—something that did not happen. Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=2", "t": "Zero Conditional", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Identify the conditional type. If you do not brush your teeth, you get cavities. Answer: Zero Conditional Hinglish Explanation: Both clauses use Simple Present and the sentence states a general cause-and-effect truth. English Explanation: Both clauses use Simple Present and the sentence states a general cause-and-effect truth. Rule: Zero Conditional: If + Simple Present, Simple Present. Quick Rule: Zero Conditional: If + Simple Present, Simple Present.","Identify the conditional type. If you freeze water, it becomes solid. Answer: Zero Conditional Hinglish Explanation: This is a general fact: whenever the condition happens, the result follows. Both verbs are in Simple Present. English Explanation: This is a general fact: whenever the condition happens, the result follows. Both verbs are in Simple Present. Rule: Zero Conditional is used for general truths and regular cause-effect facts. Quick Rule: Zero Conditional is used for general truths and regular cause-effect facts.","Identify the conditional type. If you do not water the plants, they die . Answer: Zero Conditional Hinglish Explanation: The sentence expresses a general result, not a one-time future prediction. Both clauses are in Simple Present. English Explanation: The sentence expresses a general result, not a one-time future prediction. Both clauses are in Simple Present. Rule: Zero Conditional: present condition + present result. Quick Rule: Zero Conditional: present condition + present result."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=3", "t": "First Conditional", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Choose the correct result clause. If he studies hard, he _____ well in the exam. Answer: will do Hinglish Explanation: The condition is possible in the future. With If + Simple Present , the normal result is will + V1 : “will do well.” English Explanation: The condition is possible in the future. With If + Simple Present, the normal result is will + V1: “will do well.” Rule: First Conditional: If + V1/V5, will + V1. Quick Rule: First Conditional: If + V1/V5, will + V1.","Choose the correct result clause. If I find her address, I _____ her an invitation. Answer: will send Hinglish Explanation: find is Simple Present in the if-clause, so a real future result takes will send . English Explanation: find is Simple Present in the if-clause, so a real future result takes will send. Rule: Do not normally use “will” inside the if-clause of a standard First Conditional. Quick Rule: Do not normally use “will” inside the if-clause of a standard First Conditional."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=4", "t": "Second Conditional & Unreal Present", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Identify the conditional type. If he studied hard, he would do well in the exam. Answer: Second Conditional Hinglish Explanation: Past form studied + would do presents an unreal or imagined present/future situation. English Explanation: Past form studied + would do presents an unreal or imagined present/future situation. Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1. Quick Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1.","Identify the conditional type. If I found her address, I would send her an invitation. Answer: Second Conditional Hinglish Explanation: found is V2 and would send is the hypothetical result. English Explanation: found is V2 and would send is the hypothetical result. Rule: Second Conditional expresses an unreal/less likely present or future situation. Quick Rule: Second Conditional expresses an unreal/less likely present or future situation.","Choose the best correction for the unreal present meaning. He talks as if he knows everything about the world. Answer: knew Hinglish Explanation: The speaker is not accepting the claim as real; as if for an unreal present situation takes a past form: knew . English Explanation: The speaker is not accepting the claim as real; as if for an unreal present situation takes a past form: knew. Rule: Unreal present after as if/as though: use V2; with be, “were” is preferred. Quick Rule: Unreal present after as if/as though: use V2; with be, “were” is preferred.","Choose the best correction for the unreal present meaning. He talks as though he is the Prime Minister of India. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: The comparison is unreal in the present. With be , formal exam grammar uses were for all persons in such hypothetical clauses. English Explanation: The comparison is unreal in the present. With be, formal exam grammar uses were for all persons in such hypothetical clauses. Rule: Unreal present: as if/as though + subject + were. Quick Rule: Unreal present: as if/as though + subject + were.","Choose the best correction. If I am a bird, I would fly. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: The result would fly shows an unreal present condition, so the if-clause should be If I were a bird . English Explanation: The result would fly shows an unreal present condition, so the if-clause should be If I were a bird. Rule: Hypothetical “be” in Second Conditional: If I/he/she were … Quick Rule: Hypothetical “be” in Second Conditional: If I/he/she were …"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=5", "t": "Third Conditional & Unreal Past", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Identify the conditional type. If he had studied hard, he would have done well in the exam. Answer: Third Conditional Hinglish Explanation: The condition refers to an unreal past: he did not study hard. Past Perfect in the if-clause pairs with would have + V3 . English Explanation: The condition refers to an unreal past: he did not study hard. Past Perfect in the if-clause pairs with would have + V3. Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3.","Identify the conditional type. If I had found her address, I would have sent her an invitation. Answer: Third Conditional Hinglish Explanation: Both parts look back at an unreal past possibility: had found → would have sent . English Explanation: Both parts look back at an unreal past possibility: had found → would have sent. Rule: Use Third Conditional for imagined alternatives to past events. Quick Rule: Use Third Conditional for imagined alternatives to past events.","Choose the best correction. I wish you were present here when I got the prize. Answer: had been present Hinglish Explanation: The wish is about a past occasion (“when I got the prize”), so the unreal past form is had been present . English Explanation: The wish is about a past occasion (“when I got the prize”), so the unreal past form is had been present. Rule: Past regret/wish: wish + had + V3. Quick Rule: Past regret/wish: wish + had + V3.","Choose the best correction. I wish I saw you when you had been living in England. Answer: had seen Hinglish Explanation: The wished-for meeting belongs to the past. For an unreal past wish, use had seen . English Explanation: The wished-for meeting belongs to the past. For an unreal past wish, use had seen. Rule: Wish about an unreal past event: wish + Past Perfect. Quick Rule: Wish about an unreal past event: wish + Past Perfect.","Choose the best correction. I wish I stopped you when you had been leaving for Paris. Answer: had stopped Hinglish Explanation: The stopping did not happen in the past, so the regret uses had stopped . English Explanation: The stopping did not happen in the past, so the regret uses had stopped. Rule: Past regret after wish: had + V3. Quick Rule: Past regret after wish: had + V3.","Choose the best correction for the unreal comparison. He boasts as if the Earth belongs to him. Answer: belonged Hinglish Explanation: The sentence presents the idea as unreal/exaggerated in the present, so belonged is used after as if . English Explanation: The sentence presents the idea as unreal/exaggerated in the present, so belonged is used after as if. Rule: Unreal present after as if/as though: use a past form. Quick Rule: Unreal present after as if/as though: use a past form."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=6", "t": "Conditional Inversion", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Choose the equivalent inverted form. If I were a bird , I would fly. Answer: Were I a bird, I would fly. Hinglish Explanation: In formal conditional inversion, remove if and move were before the subject: Were I a bird … . English Explanation: In formal conditional inversion, remove if and move were before the subject: Were I a bird …. Rule: If + subject + were … = Were + subject … Quick Rule: If + subject + were … = Were + subject …","Choose the equivalent if-clause form. Were I a bird , I would fly. Answer: If I were a bird, I would fly. Hinglish Explanation: Were I … is the inverted form of If I were … ; the meaning stays hypothetical. English Explanation: Were I … is the inverted form of If I were …; the meaning stays hypothetical. Rule: Were + subject … ↔ If + subject + were … Quick Rule: Were + subject … ↔ If + subject + were …","Choose the equivalent inverted form. If I had known the answer, I would have told you. Answer: Had I known the answer, I would have told you. Hinglish Explanation: For Third Conditional inversion, drop if and move had before the subject: Had I known … . English Explanation: For Third Conditional inversion, drop if and move had before the subject: Had I known …. Rule: If + subject + had + V3 … = Had + subject + V3 … Quick Rule: If + subject + had + V3 … = Had + subject + V3 …","Choose the equivalent if-clause form. Had I known the answer, I would have told you. Answer: If I had known the answer, I would have told you. Hinglish Explanation: Had I known … is simply the inverted Third Conditional form of If I had known … . English Explanation: Had I known … is simply the inverted Third Conditional form of If I had known …. Rule: Had + subject + V3 … ↔ If + subject + had + V3 … Quick Rule: Had + subject + V3 … ↔ If + subject + had + V3 …"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=7", "t": "But for & Mixed Conditionals", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Choose the correct result form. But for his help, the patient _____ . Answer: would have died Hinglish Explanation: But for his help means “if he had not helped.” That is an unreal past condition, so the result takes would have + V3 . English Explanation: But for his help means “if he had not helped.” That is an unreal past condition, so the result takes would have + V3. Rule: But for + past-saving cause → would have + V3 for the unreal past result. Quick Rule: But for + past-saving cause → would have + V3 for the unreal past result.","Identify the time reference of the result. If I were you, I would enjoy my vacation. Answer: Present/future hypothetical result Hinglish Explanation: would enjoy is a hypothetical present/future result following the unreal condition If I were you . English Explanation: would enjoy is a hypothetical present/future result following the unreal condition If I were you. Rule: If I were you + would + V1 = hypothetical advice/result. Quick Rule: If I were you + would + V1 = hypothetical advice/result.","Identify the structure. If I were you, I would have enjoyed my vacation. Answer: Mixed conditional: unreal present condition + past result Hinglish Explanation: The condition If I were you is unreal-present, while would have enjoyed refers to a past result. The time references are mixed. English Explanation: The condition If I were you is unreal-present, while would have enjoyed refers to a past result. The time references are mixed. Rule: A mixed conditional can combine an unreal present condition with a past hypothetical result. Quick Rule: A mixed conditional can combine an unreal present condition with a past hypothetical result."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=03.+Conditionals&section=8", "t": "Miscellaneous Practice", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 03. Conditionals", "x": ["Keeping the if-clause unchanged, choose the best correction. If he worked hard, he will surely get the job of his choice. Answer: would surely get Hinglish Explanation: With worked as the hypothetical past form, the matching result is would + V1 : “would surely get.” English Explanation: With worked as the hypothetical past form, the matching result is would + V1: “would surely get.” Rule: Second Conditional consistency: If + V2, would + V1. Quick Rule: Second Conditional consistency: If + V2, would + V1.","Keeping the if-clause unchanged, choose the best correction. If he does not respond to my appeal, I would dismiss him. Answer: will dismiss Hinglish Explanation: The if-clause is Simple Present and describes a real future possibility, so the result should be will dismiss . English Explanation: The if-clause is Simple Present and describes a real future possibility, so the result should be will dismiss. Rule: First Conditional consistency: If + Simple Present, will + V1. Quick Rule: First Conditional consistency: If + Simple Present, will + V1.","Choose the best correction. If you had not come on time, we would lose our lives. Answer: would have lost Hinglish Explanation: Past Perfect in the if-clause signals an unreal past condition; therefore the result must be would have lost . English Explanation: Past Perfect in the if-clause signals an unreal past condition; therefore the result must be would have lost. Rule: Third Conditional: had + V3 → would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third Conditional: had + V3 → would have + V3.","Choose the best correction. If I was you, I would not have committed this blunder. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: For the unreal identity comparison If I … you , formal conditional grammar uses were . English Explanation: For the unreal identity comparison If I … you, formal conditional grammar uses were. Rule: Use “If I were you” for hypothetical advice/comparison. Quick Rule: Use “If I were you” for hypothetical advice/comparison.","Does this sentence require a conditional correction? If I were in your position, I would have respected the wishes of my parents and made them happy. Answer: No correction required Hinglish Explanation: The sentence can be read as a mixed conditional: unreal present identity/position ( If I were … ) + a hypothetical past action ( would have respected … and made … ). English Explanation: The sentence can be read as a mixed conditional: unreal present identity/position (If I were …) + a hypothetical past action (would have respected … and made …). Rule: Mixed conditionals can combine different time references when the meaning requires it. Quick Rule: Mixed conditionals can combine different time references when the meaning requires it.","Choose the best replacement for the highlighted phrase. We had planned the trip, but rain stopped us. But for the rain, we would go to the woods. Answer: we would have gone Hinglish Explanation: The rain prevented a past trip. But for the rain means “if it had not rained,” so the missed past result is would have gone . English Explanation: The rain prevented a past trip. But for the rain means “if it had not rained,” so the missed past result is would have gone. Rule: Unreal past result: would have + V3. Quick Rule: Unreal past result: would have + V3.","Choose the best correction. But for your co-operation, the organization of this workshop would not be so smooth. Answer: would not have been Hinglish Explanation: The workshop organization is treated as a completed past outcome; without the cooperation, it would not have been so smooth. English Explanation: The workshop organization is treated as a completed past outcome; without the cooperation, it would not have been so smooth. Rule: But for + past cause often takes would have + V3 for the counterfactual result. Quick Rule: But for + past cause often takes would have + V3 for the counterfactual result.","Choose the best correction. Had I realized that it was such a long way, I would take a taxi. Answer: would have taken Hinglish Explanation: Had I realized … is inverted Third Conditional, so the past counterfactual result is would have taken . English Explanation: Had I realized … is inverted Third Conditional, so the past counterfactual result is would have taken. Rule: Had + subject + V3 → would have + V3. Quick Rule: Had + subject + V3 → would have + V3.","Choose the best correction. Were she I, she will not allow you to go anywhere. Answer: would not allow Hinglish Explanation: Were she I is an inverted unreal-present condition, so the result takes would + V1 . English Explanation: Were she I is an inverted unreal-present condition, so the result takes would + V1. Rule: Were + subject … → would + V1 for a Second Conditional result. Quick Rule: Were + subject … → would + V1 for a Second Conditional result.","Keeping the if-clause unchanged, choose the best correction. If he had enough money, he would have done some charity. Answer: would do Hinglish Explanation: Here had enough money is simple past “had” (possession), not Past Perfect. It forms a Second Conditional, so the result is would do . English Explanation: Here had enough money is simple past “had” (possession), not Past Perfect. It forms a Second Conditional, so the result is would do. Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1. Past Perfect would be “had had.” Quick Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1. Past Perfect would be “had had.”","Choose the best correction. I wish you were present there when your daughter got the prize. Answer: had been present Hinglish Explanation: The wish concerns a completed past occasion, so use the Past Perfect form had been present . English Explanation: The wish concerns a completed past occasion, so use the Past Perfect form had been present. Rule: Wish about the past: wish + had + V3. Quick Rule: Wish about the past: wish + had + V3.","Choose the best correction. Had he not reached in time, he will have missed a golden opportunity. Answer: would have missed Hinglish Explanation: Had he not reached … is inverted Third Conditional, so the result is would have missed . English Explanation: Had he not reached … is inverted Third Conditional, so the result is would have missed. Rule: Inverted Third Conditional: Had + subject + V3, would have + V3. Quick Rule: Inverted Third Conditional: Had + subject + V3, would have + V3.","Choose the best correction. If I was a millionaire, I would fight elections. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: This is an unreal present/future condition. With be , exam grammar prefers were : “If I were a millionaire …” English Explanation: This is an unreal present/future condition. With be, exam grammar prefers were: “If I were a millionaire …” Rule: Second Conditional with be: If I/he/she were … Quick Rule: Second Conditional with be: If I/he/she were …","Choose the best correction. But for her prompt action, many people would lose their lives. Answer: would have lost Hinglish Explanation: Her action prevented a past bad outcome. The unreal past result is would have lost . English Explanation: Her action prevented a past bad outcome. The unreal past result is would have lost. Rule: But for + past saving action → would have + V3. Quick Rule: But for + past saving action → would have + V3.","Choose the best correction to the if-clause. If he would have receive my letter in time, he would be able to change his plans. Answer: received Hinglish Explanation: The result would be able points to a Second Conditional. The if-clause therefore takes Simple Past: If he received … , not “would have …”. English Explanation: The result would be able points to a Second Conditional. The if-clause therefore takes Simple Past: If he received …, not “would have …”. Rule: Normally do not use would in the if-clause; Second Conditional uses If + V2. Quick Rule: Normally do not use would in the if-clause; Second Conditional uses If + V2.","Choose the best correction to the if-clause. If I would know what you wanted, I would help you. Answer: knew Hinglish Explanation: The result is would help , so the unreal condition should be Simple Past: If I knew … . English Explanation: The result is would help, so the unreal condition should be Simple Past: If I knew …. Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1. Quick Rule: Second Conditional: If + V2, would + V1.","Choose the best correction to the if-clause. If I would have realized what a bad person my friend was, I would have discarded his friendship. Answer: had realized Hinglish Explanation: The result would have discarded is a past counterfactual, so the condition needs Past Perfect: had realized . English Explanation: The result would have discarded is a past counterfactual, so the condition needs Past Perfect: had realized. Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third Conditional: If + had + V3, would have + V3.","Choose the best correction. You will fail in the examination if you will not work hard. Answer: do not work Hinglish Explanation: In a standard First Conditional, the if-clause uses Simple Present even though the meaning is future: if you do not work hard . English Explanation: In a standard First Conditional, the if-clause uses Simple Present even though the meaning is future: if you do not work hard. Rule: After if in a standard First Conditional, use Simple Present—not will. Quick Rule: After if in a standard First Conditional, use Simple Present—not will.","Because the sentence explicitly ends with “now,” choose the best standard-English result. If the bus had not broken down, we will be at home now. Answer: would be Hinglish Explanation: The condition is unreal past ( had not broken down ), but the result is about the present moment ( now ). Therefore a mixed conditional uses would be at home now . English Explanation: The condition is unreal past (had not broken down), but the result is about the present moment (now). Therefore a mixed conditional uses would be at home now. Rule: Mixed conditional: If + Past Perfect (past condition), would + V1/be (present result). Quick Rule: Mixed conditional: If + Past Perfect (past condition), would + V1/be (present result)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=1", "t": "Proper, Common & Collective Nouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the standard sentence. I went to a Delhi . Answer: I went to Delhi. Hinglish Explanation: Delhi is a proper noun naming a specific city, so ordinary reference does not take a/an . English Explanation: Delhi is a proper noun naming a specific city, so ordinary reference does not take a/an. Rule: Proper place names normally take no indefinite article. Quick Rule: Proper place names normally take no indefinite article.","Choose the standard sentence. I went to the Delhi . Answer: I went to Delhi. Hinglish Explanation: For the city name Delhi , the ordinary sentence is simply I went to Delhi . English Explanation: For the city name Delhi, the ordinary sentence is simply I went to Delhi. Rule: Most city names are used without an article. Quick Rule: Most city names are used without an article.","What does the article do in this sentence? You are a Salman Khan . Answer: It uses a proper name as a common noun meaning “a person like Salman Khan.” Hinglish Explanation: A famous proper name can be used like a common noun when it means a person of that type/quality . English Explanation: A famous proper name can be used like a common noun when it means a person of that type/quality. Rule: A/an may precede a proper name when the name is used figuratively as a common noun. Quick Rule: A/an may precede a proper name when the name is used figuratively as a common noun.","What is the intended grammar pattern? You are a Tendulkar . Answer: A proper name is being used as a common noun meaning “a person like Tendulkar.” Hinglish Explanation: Here a Tendulkar means someone comparable to Tendulkar, not the unique person himself. English Explanation: Here a Tendulkar means someone comparable to Tendulkar, not the unique person himself. Rule: Proper noun → common-noun use can take a/an. Quick Rule: Proper noun → common-noun use can take a/an.","Choose the verb that treats the collective noun as one unit. The team ___ very strong. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Team is viewed as one whole unit, so a singular verb is used. English Explanation: Team is viewed as one whole unit, so a singular verb is used. Rule: Collective noun as a single unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as a single unit → singular verb.","Choose the verb that focuses on the individual members. The team ___ quarrelling like children. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: The members are acting separately, so the collective noun takes a plural verb in this exam-style usage. English Explanation: The members are acting separately, so the collective noun takes a plural verb in this exam-style usage. Rule: Collective noun as separate members → plural verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as separate members → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb. The jury ___ of twelve persons. Answer: consists Hinglish Explanation: The jury is treated as one body when we state its composition. English Explanation: The jury is treated as one body when we state its composition. Rule: One collective unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: One collective unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb. The jury ___ divided in their opinions. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: The pronoun their and different opinions show the jurors are being considered individually. English Explanation: The pronoun their and different opinions show the jurors are being considered individually. Rule: Members acting separately → plural verb/pronoun. Quick Rule: Members acting separately → plural verb/pronoun.","Choose the correct verb. The committee ___ submitted its report. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Its report presents the committee as one unit. English Explanation: Its report presents the committee as one unit. Rule: Collective noun as a unit → singular verb and singular pronoun. Quick Rule: Collective noun as a unit → singular verb and singular pronoun.","Choose the correct verb. The committee ___ different opinions. Answer: hold Hinglish Explanation: Different members hold different opinions, so the group is treated as plural. English Explanation: Different members hold different opinions, so the group is treated as plural. Rule: Collective noun referring to members separately → plural verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun referring to members separately → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb. Your family ___ well reputed. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: The family is viewed as one household/group. English Explanation: The family is viewed as one household/group. Rule: Collective noun as a whole → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as a whole → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb when the opposition acts as one body. The opposition ___ opposed the new bill. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: The opposition is presented as one political body taking one action. English Explanation: The opposition is presented as one political body taking one action. Rule: Collective noun as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the form that emphasizes different members. The opposition ___ in disagreement over the new bill. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Being in disagreement implies members hold differing views, so plural agreement fits the intended sense. English Explanation: Being in disagreement implies members hold differing views, so plural agreement fits the intended sense. Rule: Collective noun as individuals → plural verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as individuals → plural verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=2", "t": "Compound & Irregular Plurals", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the correct plural. Plural of “garden chair” → ? Answer: garden chairs Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of garden chair is garden chairs . English Explanation: The correct plural of garden chair is garden chairs. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “table leg” → ? Answer: table legs Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of table leg is table legs . English Explanation: The correct plural of table leg is table legs. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “ghost story” → ? Answer: ghost stories Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of ghost story is ghost stories . English Explanation: The correct plural of ghost story is ghost stories. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “girl student” → ? Answer: girl students Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of girl student is girl students . English Explanation: The correct plural of girl student is girl students. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “village after village” → ? Answer: villages after villages Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of village after village is villages after villages . English Explanation: The correct plural of village after village is villages after villages. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “mother-in-law” → ? Answer: mothers-in-law Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of mother-in-law is mothers-in-law . English Explanation: The correct plural of mother-in-law is mothers-in-law. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “sister-in-law” → ? Answer: sisters-in-law Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of sister-in-law is sisters-in-law . English Explanation: The correct plural of sister-in-law is sisters-in-law. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “member of parliament” → ? Answer: members of parliament Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of member of parliament is members of parliament . English Explanation: The correct plural of member of parliament is members of parliament. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “commander-in-chief” → ? Answer: commanders-in-chief Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of commander-in-chief is commanders-in-chief . English Explanation: The correct plural of commander-in-chief is commanders-in-chief. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “passer-by” → ? Answer: passers-by Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of passer-by is passers-by . English Explanation: The correct plural of passer-by is passers-by. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “looker-on” → ? Answer: lookers-on Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of looker-on is lookers-on . English Explanation: The correct plural of looker-on is lookers-on. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “runner-up” → ? Answer: runners-up Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of runner-up is runners-up . English Explanation: The correct plural of runner-up is runners-up. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “criterion” → ? Answer: criteria Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of criterion is criteria . English Explanation: The correct plural of criterion is criteria. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “phenomenon” → ? Answer: phenomena Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of phenomenon is phenomena . English Explanation: The correct plural of phenomenon is phenomena. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “curriculum” → ? Answer: curricula Hinglish Explanation: curricula is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. English Explanation: curricula is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “emporium” → ? Answer: emporia Hinglish Explanation: emporia is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. English Explanation: emporia is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “memorandum” → ? Answer: memoranda Hinglish Explanation: memoranda is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. English Explanation: memoranda is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “millennium” → ? Answer: millennia Hinglish Explanation: millennia is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. English Explanation: millennia is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “stratum” → ? Answer: strata Hinglish Explanation: strata is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. English Explanation: strata is the classical plural shown in the source; modern English may also accept an anglicized -s plural for some of these words. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “analysis” → ? Answer: analyses Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of analysis is analyses . English Explanation: The correct plural of analysis is analyses. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “hypothesis” → ? Answer: hypotheses Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of hypothesis is hypotheses . English Explanation: The correct plural of hypothesis is hypotheses. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “axis” → ? Answer: axes Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of axis is axes . English Explanation: The correct plural of axis is axes. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “basis” → ? Answer: bases Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of basis is bases . English Explanation: The correct plural of basis is bases. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “crisis” → ? Answer: crises Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of crisis is crises . English Explanation: The correct plural of crisis is crises. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “synthesis” → ? Answer: syntheses Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of synthesis is syntheses . English Explanation: The correct plural of synthesis is syntheses. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the correct plural. Plural of “diagnosis” → ? Answer: diagnoses Hinglish Explanation: The correct plural of diagnosis is diagnoses . English Explanation: The correct plural of diagnosis is diagnoses. Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main/head element of a compound; some Greek/Latin nouns have irregular plurals.","Choose the form used in the repeated expression. hour after hour Answer: Keep the expression unchanged. Hinglish Explanation: hour after hour is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. English Explanation: hour after hour is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged. Quick Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged.","Choose the form used in the repeated expression. page after page Answer: Keep the expression unchanged. Hinglish Explanation: page after page is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. English Explanation: page after page is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged. Quick Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged.","Choose the form used in the repeated expression. word for word Answer: Keep the expression unchanged. Hinglish Explanation: word for word is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. English Explanation: word for word is used as a fixed repeated expression in the source and is not converted to a plural pattern. Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged. Quick Rule: Fixed repetition such as ‘hour after hour’ normally stays unchanged.","Choose the correct noun form. Children enjoy listening to ghosts stories especially on Halloween night. Answer: ghost stories Hinglish Explanation: In a noun+noun compound, the first noun normally stays singular: ghost stories . English Explanation: In a noun+noun compound, the first noun normally stays singular: ghost stories. Rule: Noun + noun: plural usually goes on the head noun. Quick Rule: Noun + noun: plural usually goes on the head noun.","Choose the best correction. Cities after cities were destroyed by the army. Answer: City after city was Hinglish Explanation: The distributive repeated pattern is city after city ; it is treated as singular here: was destroyed . English Explanation: The distributive repeated pattern is city after city; it is treated as singular here: was destroyed. Rule: ‘Noun after noun’ often stays singular when it means one after another. Quick Rule: ‘Noun after noun’ often stays singular when it means one after another.","Choose the correct sentence. All his sisters-in-law have been invited to the party. Answer: All his sisters-in-law have been invited to the party. Hinglish Explanation: The plural is sisters-in-law , and the plural subject takes have . English Explanation: The plural is sisters-in-law, and the plural subject takes have. Rule: Pluralize the main noun before the prepositional part: sisters-in-law. Quick Rule: Pluralize the main noun before the prepositional part: sisters-in-law.","Choose the corrected phrase. ... especially his brother-in-laws . Answer: brothers-in-law Hinglish Explanation: The head noun brother takes the plural, not law . English Explanation: The head noun brother takes the plural, not law. Rule: Compound with -in-law → pluralize the first/head noun. Quick Rule: Compound with -in-law → pluralize the first/head noun.","Choose the correct plural. He had drunk two silver cupful of brandy. Answer: cupfuls Hinglish Explanation: The ordinary plural is cupfuls : add -s to the whole word. English Explanation: The ordinary plural is cupfuls: add -s to the whole word. Rule: Measure compounds ending in -ful usually form the plural with -fuls. Quick Rule: Measure compounds ending in -ful usually form the plural with -fuls.","Choose the correct compound. All the boys visitors are advised to stay in the queue. Answer: boy visitors Hinglish Explanation: When one noun modifies another, the modifier generally stays singular: boy visitors . English Explanation: When one noun modifies another, the modifier generally stays singular: boy visitors. Rule: Noun used attributively normally remains singular. Quick Rule: Noun used attributively normally remains singular.","Choose the standard plural phrase. Having been one of the runner-up in the European Ballroom Championships many times. Answer: runners-up Hinglish Explanation: The head word is runner , so the plural is runners-up . English Explanation: The head word is runner, so the plural is runners-up. Rule: Hyphenated compound: pluralize the head noun. Quick Rule: Hyphenated compound: pluralize the head noun.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man doctor → ? Answer: men doctors Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is men doctors . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is men doctors. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man servant → ? Answer: men servants Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is men servants . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is men servants. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man teacher → ? Answer: men teachers Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is men teachers . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is men teachers. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. woman doctor → ? Answer: women doctors Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is women doctors . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is women doctors. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. woman teacher → ? Answer: women teachers Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is women teachers . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is women teachers. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man eater → ? Answer: man eaters Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is man eaters . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is man eaters. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man lover → ? Answer: man lovers Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is man lovers . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is man lovers. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the plural shown by the source. man hater → ? Answer: man haters Hinglish Explanation: The plural form used is man haters . Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters . English Explanation: The plural form used is man haters. Notice that man/woman changes when it means the gendered modifier (men doctors, women teachers), but stays singular in compounds like man eaters. Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound. Quick Rule: Check whether man/woman is itself a gendered modifier or part of a lexical compound.","Choose the corrected phrase. I called many women servant for the sake of my household work. Answer: women servants Hinglish Explanation: Both elements change here: women servants . English Explanation: Both elements change here: women servants. Rule: Woman/man as a gender modifier + plural head noun → both may show plural. Quick Rule: Woman/man as a gender modifier + plural head noun → both may show plural.","Choose the corrected phrase. There have been ten woman doctors in our country. Answer: women doctors Hinglish Explanation: The standard plural is women doctors . English Explanation: The standard plural is women doctors. Rule: Woman as gender modifier changes to women before a plural profession noun. Quick Rule: Woman as gender modifier changes to women before a plural profession noun.","Is the noun phrase correct? All the women teachers are agitating against the haughty attitude of the principal. Answer: No correction required Hinglish Explanation: Women teachers is already the correct plural phrase. English Explanation: Women teachers is already the correct plural phrase. Rule: Woman → women when it modifies a plural profession noun. Quick Rule: Woman → women when it modifies a plural profession noun."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=3", "t": "Meaning & Number Changes", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the correct noun for a chief/capital city. Mumbai is ___ . Answer: a metropolis Hinglish Explanation: Metropolis is a noun meaning a major/chief city. English Explanation: Metropolis is a noun meaning a major/chief city. Rule: Metropolis = noun; metropolitan = adjective or inhabitant noun. Quick Rule: Metropolis = noun; metropolitan = adjective or inhabitant noun.","Choose the correct plural noun. There are four ___ in India. Answer: metropolises Hinglish Explanation: The plural of metropolis is metropolises . English Explanation: The plural of metropolis is metropolises. Rule: metropolis → metropolises. Quick Rule: metropolis → metropolises.","Identify the use of “metropolitan”. metropolitan police Answer: It is an adjective meaning “relating to a metropolis/large city.” Hinglish Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. English Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective. Quick Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective.","Identify the use of “metropolitan”. metropolitan life Answer: It is an adjective meaning “relating to a metropolis/large city.” Hinglish Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. English Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective. Quick Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective.","Identify the use of “metropolitan”. metropolitan culture Answer: It is an adjective meaning “relating to a metropolis/large city.” Hinglish Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. English Explanation: In this phrase metropolitan modifies the following noun. Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective. Quick Rule: Metropolitan can function as an adjective.","Choose the natural noun sentence. He lives in ___ . Answer: a metropolis Hinglish Explanation: A place/city itself is a metropolis . English Explanation: A place/city itself is a metropolis. Rule: Metropolis = city; metropolitan (noun) = inhabitant. Quick Rule: Metropolis = city; metropolitan (noun) = inhabitant.","Choose the natural noun sentence. Delhi is ___ . Answer: a metropolis Hinglish Explanation: The noun for a large/chief city is metropolis . English Explanation: The noun for a large/chief city is metropolis. Rule: Use metropolis for the city itself. Quick Rule: Use metropolis for the city itself.","Choose the correct noun for an inhabitant. He is ___ . Answer: a metropolitan Hinglish Explanation: As a noun, metropolitan can mean an inhabitant of a metropolis. English Explanation: As a noun, metropolitan can mean an inhabitant of a metropolis. Rule: metropolitan (noun) = inhabitant of a metropolis. Quick Rule: metropolitan (noun) = inhabitant of a metropolis.","Choose the correct plural. They are ___ . Answer: metropolitans Hinglish Explanation: The plural inhabitant noun is metropolitans . English Explanation: The plural inhabitant noun is metropolitans. Rule: metropolitan → metropolitans when referring to people. Quick Rule: metropolitan → metropolitans when referring to people.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. alphabets Answer: languages Hinglish Explanation: In the source, alphabets has the plural-specific sense languages , while alphabet can carry the singular sense a set of letters . English Explanation: In the source, alphabets has the plural-specific sense languages, while alphabet can carry the singular sense a set of letters. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. goods Answer: objects/things Hinglish Explanation: In the source, goods has the plural-specific sense objects/things , while good can carry the singular sense benefit . English Explanation: In the source, goods has the plural-specific sense objects/things, while good can carry the singular sense benefit. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. customs Answer: tax/duty Hinglish Explanation: In the source, customs has the plural-specific sense tax/duty , while custom can carry the singular sense tradition . English Explanation: In the source, customs has the plural-specific sense tax/duty, while custom can carry the singular sense tradition. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. irons Answer: handcuffs Hinglish Explanation: In the source, irons has the plural-specific sense handcuffs , while iron can carry the singular sense the metal . English Explanation: In the source, irons has the plural-specific sense handcuffs, while iron can carry the singular sense the metal. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. woods Answer: a forest Hinglish Explanation: In the source, woods has the plural-specific sense a forest , while wood can carry the singular sense wood material/branches . English Explanation: In the source, woods has the plural-specific sense a forest, while wood can carry the singular sense wood material/branches. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. airs Answer: affected manners Hinglish Explanation: In the source, airs has the plural-specific sense affected manners , while air can carry the singular sense the atmosphere . English Explanation: In the source, airs has the plural-specific sense affected manners, while air can carry the singular sense the atmosphere. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. forces Answer: armed forces/army Hinglish Explanation: In the source, forces has the plural-specific sense armed forces/army , while force can carry the singular sense strength . English Explanation: In the source, forces has the plural-specific sense armed forces/army, while force can carry the singular sense strength. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. quarters Answer: residence/lodging Hinglish Explanation: In the source, quarters has the plural-specific sense residence/lodging , while quarter can carry the singular sense one fourth . English Explanation: In the source, quarters has the plural-specific sense residence/lodging, while quarter can carry the singular sense one fourth. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. words Answer: language/utterances Hinglish Explanation: In the source, words has the plural-specific sense language/utterances , while word can carry the singular sense a promise . English Explanation: In the source, words has the plural-specific sense language/utterances, while word can carry the singular sense a promise. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the meaning that matches the plural form. works Answer: literary or industrial works Hinglish Explanation: In the source, works has the plural-specific sense literary or industrial works , while work can carry the singular sense business/labour . English Explanation: In the source, works has the plural-specific sense literary or industrial works, while work can carry the singular sense business/labour. Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural. Quick Rule: Some nouns change meaning when singular becomes plural.","Choose the correct idiom. Our teacher takes great pain to help us realize our goals. Answer: pains Hinglish Explanation: The fixed expression is take great pains , meaning make a lot of effort. English Explanation: The fixed expression is take great pains, meaning make a lot of effort. Rule: Idiom: take pains / take great pains. Quick Rule: Idiom: take pains / take great pains.","Choose the correct noun in the idiom. She hates people who break their words . Answer: word Hinglish Explanation: The idiom is break one’s word , meaning fail to keep a promise. English Explanation: The idiom is break one’s word, meaning fail to keep a promise. Rule: Word (singular) can mean a promise: keep/break one’s word. Quick Rule: Word (singular) can mean a promise: keep/break one’s word.","Choose the correct plural for literary creations. Writers may connote differently in work of literature. Answer: works Hinglish Explanation: When referring to separate literary creations, use works . English Explanation: When referring to separate literary creations, use works. Rule: Works can mean literary/artistic creations. Quick Rule: Works can mean literary/artistic creations.","Choose the correct noun. One does not need to define all the word . Answer: words Hinglish Explanation: After all the in this context, multiple vocabulary items are meant, so words is required. English Explanation: After all the in this context, multiple vocabulary items are meant, so words is required. Rule: Countable word → plural words for multiple items. Quick Rule: Countable word → plural words for multiple items.","Choose the correct noun. I have learnt all the alphabets of the English language. Answer: alphabet Hinglish Explanation: English has one alphabet ; its individual units are letters. In the source, alphabets is reserved for multiple writing systems/languages. English Explanation: English has one alphabet; its individual units are letters. In the source, alphabets is reserved for multiple writing systems/languages. Rule: One language → one alphabet. Quick Rule: One language → one alphabet.","Choose the correct uncountable form. While I am doing the house works , I like to listen to music. Answer: housework Hinglish Explanation: Housework is normally an uncountable mass noun. English Explanation: Housework is normally an uncountable mass noun. Rule: Housework is uncountable; do not pluralize it as works. Quick Rule: Housework is uncountable; do not pluralize it as works."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=4", "t": "Uncountable Nouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the standard phrase. a sugar Answer: some sugar Hinglish Explanation: When sugar means the substance, it is uncountable and normally takes some , not a/an. English Explanation: When sugar means the substance, it is uncountable and normally takes some, not a/an. Rule: Mass nouns usually do not take a/an or regular plural -s. Quick Rule: Mass nouns usually do not take a/an or regular plural -s.","Choose the standard phrase. an information Answer: a piece of information Hinglish Explanation: Information is uncountable; use a counting unit such as a piece of information . English Explanation: Information is uncountable; use a counting unit such as a piece of information. Rule: Uncountable noun + piece/item/etc. for counting. Quick Rule: Uncountable noun + piece/item/etc. for counting.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of information . Answer: a piece of information Hinglish Explanation: information is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a piece of information . English Explanation: information is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a piece of information. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of furniture . Answer: an item of furniture Hinglish Explanation: furniture is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression an item of furniture . English Explanation: furniture is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression an item of furniture. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of anger . Answer: a fit of anger Hinglish Explanation: anger is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a fit of anger . English Explanation: anger is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a fit of anger. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of chocolate . Answer: a bar of chocolate Hinglish Explanation: chocolate is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a bar of chocolate . English Explanation: chocolate is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a bar of chocolate. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of rubbish . Answer: a piece of rubbish Hinglish Explanation: rubbish is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a piece of rubbish . English Explanation: rubbish is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a piece of rubbish. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the source-approved counting expression. Count one unit of rubbish . Answer: a pile of rubbish Hinglish Explanation: rubbish is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a pile of rubbish . English Explanation: rubbish is treated as uncountable here, so it is counted through the expression a pile of rubbish. Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun. Quick Rule: Use a partitive/unit expression with an uncountable noun.","Choose the correction. He has given me an information . Answer: a piece of information Hinglish Explanation: Information is uncountable; a piece of information gives it one countable unit. English Explanation: Information is uncountable; a piece of information gives it one countable unit. Rule: information: no a/an; use a piece of information. Quick Rule: information: no a/an; use a piece of information.","Choose the correction. He wants to buy some furnitures . Answer: furniture Hinglish Explanation: Furniture is an uncountable noun in standard English. English Explanation: Furniture is an uncountable noun in standard English. Rule: Furniture has no ordinary plural *furnitures*. Quick Rule: Furniture has no ordinary plural *furnitures*.","Choose the correction. All the evidences were against her, so she was punished. Answer: evidence was Hinglish Explanation: Evidence is normally uncountable, so use singular agreement: evidence was . English Explanation: Evidence is normally uncountable, so use singular agreement: evidence was. Rule: Evidence is uncountable and takes a singular verb. Quick Rule: Evidence is uncountable and takes a singular verb.","Choose the correct noun form. Rohan managed the luggages in three trips while Rashmi and Sohil handled the paperwork. Answer: luggage Hinglish Explanation: luggage is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form luggages is not used. English Explanation: luggage is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form luggages is not used. Rule: Luggage is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Luggage is treated as an uncountable noun here.","Choose the correct noun form. The paintings of natural sceneries are selling like hot cakes. Answer: scenery Hinglish Explanation: scenery is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form sceneries is not used. English Explanation: scenery is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form sceneries is not used. Rule: Scenery is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Scenery is treated as an uncountable noun here.","Choose the correct noun form. Lockdown-weary travelers eager for a change of sceneries can find room discounted as much as 50% at some properties. Answer: scenery Hinglish Explanation: scenery is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form sceneries is not used. English Explanation: scenery is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form sceneries is not used. Rule: Scenery is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Scenery is treated as an uncountable noun here.","Choose the correct noun form. The handsome boy with the long hairs sighed deeply without removing his hand from his hat. Answer: hair Hinglish Explanation: hair is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form hairs is not used. English Explanation: hair is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form hairs is not used. Rule: Hair is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Hair is treated as an uncountable noun here.","Choose the correct noun form. She put her cell phone on its charger and explored the house, admiring his taste in everything from furnitures to paintings. Answer: furniture Hinglish Explanation: furniture is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form furnitures is not used. English Explanation: furniture is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form furnitures is not used. Rule: Furniture is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Furniture is treated as an uncountable noun here.","Choose the correct noun form. If a student needs advices about careers, he or she should consult the career officer. Answer: advice Hinglish Explanation: advice is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form advices is not used. English Explanation: advice is uncountable in this meaning, so the plural form advices is not used. Rule: Advice is treated as an uncountable noun here. Quick Rule: Advice is treated as an uncountable noun here."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=5", "t": "Special Noun Agreement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the correct verb. Mathematics ___ my weakest subject. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: As the name of a school/academic subject, Mathematics is singular in function. English Explanation: As the name of a school/academic subject, Mathematics is singular in function. Rule: Names of subjects ending in -s usually take singular verbs. Quick Rule: Names of subjects ending in -s usually take singular verbs.","Choose the correct verb. Politics ___ an interesting subject. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: As the name of a field of study, Politics is treated as singular. English Explanation: As the name of a field of study, Politics is treated as singular. Rule: Academic subject name → singular verb. Quick Rule: Academic subject name → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb for “mathematical skills”. His mathematics ___ weak. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: With a possessive adjective, his mathematics can mean his mathematical abilities/calculations, so the source treats it as plural. English Explanation: With a possessive adjective, his mathematics can mean his mathematical abilities/calculations, so the source treats it as plural. Rule: Meaning can change agreement: subject name singular; a person’s mathematical skills plural in this source. Quick Rule: Meaning can change agreement: subject name singular; a person’s mathematical skills plural in this source.","Choose the correct verb. Diabetes mellitus, commonly known as diabetes, ___ a metabolic disease. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Diabetes is the name of a disease and takes singular agreement. English Explanation: Diabetes is the name of a disease and takes singular agreement. Rule: Disease names ending in -s can be singular in function. Quick Rule: Disease names ending in -s can be singular in function.","Choose the form intended by the source. Her physics is very good. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Here her physics refers to her knowledge/ability in physics rather than the subject title, so the source uses plural agreement. English Explanation: Here her physics refers to her knowledge/ability in physics rather than the subject title, so the source uses plural agreement. Rule: Possessive + subject-word can refer to skills/knowledge and take plural agreement in exam-style grammar. Quick Rule: Possessive + subject-word can refer to skills/knowledge and take plural agreement in exam-style grammar.","Choose the correct verb. The ethics of the majority of teenagers is in very petty conditions nowadays. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Here ethics means moral principles/standards (plural), not the academic subject title. English Explanation: Here ethics means moral principles/standards (plural), not the academic subject title. Rule: Ethics meaning moral principles → plural agreement. Quick Rule: Ethics meaning moral principles → plural agreement.","Choose the correct verb. The politics of India is very pathetic because politicians are corrupt and inefficient. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: The politics of India means political affairs/activities, so the source treats it as plural. English Explanation: The politics of India means political affairs/activities, so the source treats it as plural. Rule: Politics as affairs/activities → plural; Politics as a subject → singular. Quick Rule: Politics as affairs/activities → plural; Politics as a subject → singular.","Is this sentence correct? The Arabian Nights is a collection of very interesting episodes of adventure. Answer: No correction required Hinglish Explanation: A title of a book/work is treated as a single title, so is is correct. English Explanation: A title of a book/work is treated as a single title, so is is correct. Rule: Titles of books/works take singular agreement even when the title looks plural. Quick Rule: Titles of books/works take singular agreement even when the title looks plural.","Choose the correct verb. The measles cause fever and red rash. Answer: causes Hinglish Explanation: Measles is the name of a disease and is singular in function. English Explanation: Measles is the name of a disease and is singular in function. Rule: Measles → singular verb. Quick Rule: Measles → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb. Even though the cattle is alive when they are milked, vegans cite poor treatment. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Cattle is plural in function and takes a plural verb. English Explanation: Cattle is plural in function and takes a plural verb. Rule: Cattle has no ordinary singular *a cattle* and takes plural agreement. Quick Rule: Cattle has no ordinary singular *a cattle* and takes plural agreement.","Choose the correct verb. The gentry of Mumbai does not like Salman Khan at all. Answer: do Hinglish Explanation: Gentry is singular in form but plural in function in this use. English Explanation: Gentry is singular in form but plural in function in this use. Rule: Gentry → plural agreement when referring to people collectively. Quick Rule: Gentry → plural agreement when referring to people collectively.","Choose the noun form. In England a variety of causes hindered nobilities or gentries from obtaining the importance they obtained in France. Answer: nobility or gentry Hinglish Explanation: The source treats nobility and gentry as collective class nouns in singular form. English Explanation: The source treats nobility and gentry as collective class nouns in singular form. Rule: Certain collective class nouns are singular in form but plural in sense. Quick Rule: Certain collective class nouns are singular in form but plural in sense.","What does “peoples” mean here? Many different peoples live in Europe. Answer: different nations, races, or ethnic groups Hinglish Explanation: People is the ordinary plural for persons; peoples can mean distinct nations/races/ethnic groups. English Explanation: People is the ordinary plural for persons; peoples can mean distinct nations/races/ethnic groups. Rule: people = persons generally; peoples = distinct groups/nations. Quick Rule: people = persons generally; peoples = distinct groups/nations."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=6", "t": "Plural-only & Unchanged Nouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the standard counting expression. I want to buy a pants . Answer: a pair of pants Hinglish Explanation: Pants is plural-only in this use; one garment is a pair of pants . English Explanation: Pants is plural-only in this use; one garment is a pair of pants. Rule: Plural-only two-part nouns are counted with a pair of / pairs of. Quick Rule: Plural-only two-part nouns are counted with a pair of / pairs of.","Choose the correct phrase for more than one. Two pair of pants Answer: two pairs of pants Hinglish Explanation: Use pairs for the counting unit and keep pants plural. English Explanation: Use pairs for the counting unit and keep pants plural. Rule: two pairs of + plural-only noun. Quick Rule: two pairs of + plural-only noun.","Choose the best correction. He wants to buy a trousers . Answer: a pair of trousers Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. Your pyjama is torn. Answer: Your pajamas are torn. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. She has lost her binocular. Answer: She has lost her binoculars. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. Your spectacle is not good. Answer: Your spectacles are not good. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. He gifted her a jeans that she has been eyeing for so long. Answer: He gifted her a pair of jeans that she has been eyeing for so long. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. Spectacles is recommended for people suffering from myopia. Answer: Spectacles are recommended for people suffering from myopia. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Is the sentence correct? The jeans which you have bought are not cool. Answer: No correction required Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. I have bought a goggle for you. Answer: I have bought a pair of goggles for you. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the best correction. The scissors that you asked for is in the desk drawer. Answer: The scissors that you asked for are in the desk drawer. Hinglish Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. English Explanation: These nouns are plural in form and normally take plural agreement; when one physical item is counted, use a pair of where appropriate. Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item. Quick Rule: Two-part/plural-only nouns stay plural; use pair of for one item.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. The beautiful surrounding of the place enchanted me. Answer: surroundings Hinglish Explanation: The source treats surroundings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats surroundings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. Give some alm to the beggar. Answer: alms Hinglish Explanation: The source treats alms as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats alms as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. Somebody has stolen my belonging . Answer: belongings Hinglish Explanation: The source treats belongings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats belongings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. You can see the ruin of the fort. Answer: ruins Hinglish Explanation: The source treats ruins as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats ruins as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. My belonging had been stolen from the waiting room. Answer: belongings Hinglish Explanation: The source treats belongings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats belongings as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. You are in the habit of giving alm to all the beggars. Answer: alms Hinglish Explanation: The source treats alms as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats alms as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Choose the noun form used in this meaning. Keep your valuable safe in the locker as you live alone in the big house. Answer: valuables Hinglish Explanation: The source treats valuables as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. English Explanation: The source treats valuables as a fixed plural noun in this meaning. Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings. Quick Rule: Some nouns are normally used only in the plural for these meanings.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: corps Answer: corps Hinglish Explanation: The source lists corps among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists corps among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: barracks Answer: barracks Hinglish Explanation: The source lists barracks among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists barracks among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: headquarters Answer: headquarters Hinglish Explanation: The source lists headquarters among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists headquarters among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: crossroads Answer: crossroads Hinglish Explanation: The source lists crossroads among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists crossroads among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: means Answer: means Hinglish Explanation: The source lists means among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists means among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: works Answer: works Hinglish Explanation: The source lists works among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists works among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: innings Answer: innings Hinglish Explanation: The source lists innings among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists innings among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: sheep Answer: sheep Hinglish Explanation: The source lists sheep among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists sheep among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: deer Answer: deer Hinglish Explanation: The source lists deer among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists deer among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: swine Answer: swine Hinglish Explanation: The source lists swine among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists swine among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: data Answer: data Hinglish Explanation: The source lists data among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists data among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: series Answer: series Hinglish Explanation: The source lists series among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists series among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Which plural form is shown in the source? Singular/plural form: species Answer: species Hinglish Explanation: The source lists species among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). English Explanation: The source lists species among nouns whose written form is unchanged between singular and plural (or fixed in that form). Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list. Quick Rule: These nouns do not form an ordinary plural by simply adding -s/-es in the source list.","Choose the correct plural form. I have seen ten deers in the forest. Answer: deer Hinglish Explanation: Deer has the same singular and plural form. English Explanation: Deer has the same singular and plural form. Rule: one deer, ten deer. Quick Rule: one deer, ten deer.","Choose the correct noun form. Why do you want to sell all your sheeps ? Answer: sheep Hinglish Explanation: Sheep is unchanged in the plural. English Explanation: Sheep is unchanged in the plural. Rule: one sheep, many sheep. Quick Rule: one sheep, many sheep.","Choose the fixed noun. There is a dangerous crossroad before you turn right. Answer: crossroads Hinglish Explanation: The source treats crossroads as the fixed noun form for an intersection. English Explanation: The source treats crossroads as the fixed noun form for an intersection. Rule: crossroads is fixed in form. Quick Rule: crossroads is fixed in form.","Choose the correction. ... by watching his sheeps . Answer: sheep Hinglish Explanation: The plural of sheep is still sheep . English Explanation: The plural of sheep is still sheep. Rule: sheep is unchanged in number. Quick Rule: sheep is unchanged in number.","Choose the correction. ... playing so many wonderful inning in the Indian Premier League. Answer: innings Hinglish Explanation: In cricket, the noun is innings ; the source lists it as fixed in that form. English Explanation: In cricket, the noun is innings; the source lists it as fixed in that form. Rule: Cricket term: innings. Quick Rule: Cricket term: innings.","Choose the correction. PLA has commissioned a new, modern barrack for soldiers. Answer: barracks Hinglish Explanation: The source uses barracks for the building/accommodation noun. English Explanation: The source uses barracks for the building/accommodation noun. Rule: Barracks is fixed in form in this usage. Quick Rule: Barracks is fixed in form in this usage.","Is this noun form correct? There is a very dangerous crossroads near South Extension Part II. Answer: No correction required Hinglish Explanation: Crossroads is the fixed form used for the intersection. English Explanation: Crossroads is the fixed form used for the intersection. Rule: crossroads can be singular in meaning while ending in -s. Quick Rule: crossroads can be singular in meaning while ending in -s."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=7", "t": "Special Meanings: Means, Hair, Fruit & Time", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the correct verb. This means of transport ___ a very old one. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Here means = one method, so it is singular in meaning. English Explanation: Here means = one method, so it is singular in meaning. Rule: means can be singular or plural depending on meaning/context. Quick Rule: means can be singular or plural depending on meaning/context.","Choose the correct verb. Several means of transport ___ available to you. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Several makes the noun plural in meaning. English Explanation: Several makes the noun plural in meaning. Rule: means has the same written form in singular and plural. Quick Rule: means has the same written form in singular and plural.","Choose the correct verb. His means (monetary resources) ___ insufficient to keep him. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: When means means financial resources, it is plural here. English Explanation: When means means financial resources, it is plural here. Rule: means = financial resources → usually plural agreement. Quick Rule: means = financial resources → usually plural agreement.","Choose the correct noun. Your hairs is black. Answer: hair Hinglish Explanation: When referring to hair collectively on the head, hair is uncountable. English Explanation: When referring to hair collectively on the head, hair is uncountable. Rule: Hair as a mass = singular uncountable. Quick Rule: Hair as a mass = singular uncountable.","Choose the correct noun. She is brushing her hairs . Answer: hair Hinglish Explanation: Collective hair on the head is normally hair . English Explanation: Collective hair on the head is normally hair. Rule: Mass hair → no plural -s. Quick Rule: Mass hair → no plural -s.","Choose the correct countable form. There is ___ in the soup. Answer: a hair Hinglish Explanation: A single strand is countable: a hair . English Explanation: A single strand is countable: a hair. Rule: Hair can be countable when it means individual strands. Quick Rule: Hair can be countable when it means individual strands.","Choose the correct form. You have three grey hair . Answer: hairs Hinglish Explanation: Individual strands are countable, so the plural is hairs . English Explanation: Individual strands are countable, so the plural is hairs. Rule: Countable strand → hair/hairs. Quick Rule: Countable strand → hair/hairs.","Choose the correct form. Five of your hair are brown. Answer: hairs Hinglish Explanation: Five individual strands require hairs . English Explanation: Five individual strands require hairs. Rule: Individual strands are countable. Quick Rule: Individual strands are countable.","How is “fruit” being used? Fruit is expensive nowadays. Answer: as an uncountable mass noun Hinglish Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. English Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically. Quick Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically.","How is “fruit” being used? She does not like fruit . Answer: as an uncountable mass noun Hinglish Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. English Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically. Quick Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically.","How is “fruit” being used? He is going to buy some fruit . Answer: as an uncountable mass noun Hinglish Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. English Explanation: Here fruit refers to fruit generally, not separately counted kinds. Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically. Quick Rule: Fruit can be uncountable when used generically.","What does the countable use express? A banana is a fruit . Answer: one kind/item of fruit Hinglish Explanation: Here a fruit is countable because one type/item is identified. English Explanation: Here a fruit is countable because one type/item is identified. Rule: Fruit is countable when referring to kinds/items. Quick Rule: Fruit is countable when referring to kinds/items.","Choose the correct plural for two kinds. A mango and an apple are two ___ . Answer: fruits Hinglish Explanation: When counting different kinds/types, fruits can be used. English Explanation: When counting different kinds/types, fruits can be used. Rule: Different kinds of fruit → fruits. Quick Rule: Different kinds of fruit → fruits.","What does “fruits” mean here? Hard work bears fruits . Answer: results or rewards Hinglish Explanation: In this idiom, fruits means results/rewards. English Explanation: In this idiom, fruits means results/rewards. Rule: Fruits can have the figurative meaning ‘results’. Quick Rule: Fruits can have the figurative meaning ‘results’.","What does “fruits” mean here? The fruits of the industry are very sweet. Answer: results/outcomes Hinglish Explanation: The source uses fruits figuratively for outcomes/results. English Explanation: The source uses fruits figuratively for outcomes/results. Rule: Figurative fruits = results. Quick Rule: Figurative fruits = results.","How is “time” used? Time waits for no man. Answer: as an uncountable noun meaning time in general Hinglish Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. English Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. Rule: General time → uncountable. Quick Rule: General time → uncountable.","How is “time” used? Time is money. Answer: as an uncountable noun meaning time in general Hinglish Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. English Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. Rule: General time → uncountable. Quick Rule: General time → uncountable.","How is “time” used? We should not waste time . Answer: as an uncountable noun meaning time in general Hinglish Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. English Explanation: The sentence refers to time generally, so time is uncountable. Rule: General time → uncountable. Quick Rule: General time → uncountable.","Choose the correct countable form. I went there two time last week. Answer: times Hinglish Explanation: Separate occasions are countable: two times . English Explanation: Separate occasions are countable: two times. Rule: time = occasion → countable times. Quick Rule: time = occasion → countable times.","Choose the correct countable form. She came to see me several time last month. Answer: times Hinglish Explanation: Several occasions require the plural times . English Explanation: Several occasions require the plural times. Rule: Separate occasions → times. Quick Rule: Separate occasions → times.","Choose the meaning of the highlighted expression. at times Answer: occasionally Hinglish Explanation: at times is used with the sense occasionally . English Explanation: at times is used with the sense occasionally. Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses. Quick Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses.","Choose the meaning of the highlighted expression. at all times Answer: always Hinglish Explanation: at all times is used with the sense always . English Explanation: at all times is used with the sense always. Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses. Quick Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses.","Choose the meaning of the highlighted expression. Roman times Answer: a historical period Hinglish Explanation: Roman times is used with the sense a historical period . English Explanation: Roman times is used with the sense a historical period. Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses. Quick Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses.","Choose the meaning of the highlighted expression. Greek times Answer: a historical period Hinglish Explanation: Greek times is used with the sense a historical period . English Explanation: Greek times is used with the sense a historical period. Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses. Quick Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses.","Choose the meaning of the highlighted expression. ancient times Answer: historical periods Hinglish Explanation: ancient times is used with the sense historical periods . English Explanation: ancient times is used with the sense historical periods. Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses. Quick Rule: Times has idiomatic/historical uses."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=8", "t": "Numeral Nouns & Number Compounds", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Choose the standard exact-number form. two hundreds Answer: two hundred Hinglish Explanation: After an exact counting number, hundred stays singular: two hundred . English Explanation: After an exact counting number, hundred stays singular: two hundred. Rule: Exact number + hundred/thousand/million/dozen → no plural -s. Quick Rule: Exact number + hundred/thousand/million/dozen → no plural -s.","Choose the standard exact-number form. ten millions Answer: ten million Hinglish Explanation: With an exact count, million stays singular. English Explanation: With an exact count, million stays singular. Rule: Exact number + million → ten million. Quick Rule: Exact number + million → ten million.","Choose the correct phrase. one dozen of eggs Answer: one dozen eggs Hinglish Explanation: When dozen follows an exact number, the source omits of . English Explanation: When dozen follows an exact number, the source omits of. Rule: Exact number + dozen + noun: one dozen eggs. Quick Rule: Exact number + dozen + noun: one dozen eggs.","Choose the correct phrase. two hundreds of books Answer: two hundred books Hinglish Explanation: With an exact number, use two hundred books —no plural -s on hundred and no of . English Explanation: With an exact number, use two hundred books—no plural -s on hundred and no of. Rule: Exact number + hundred + noun. Quick Rule: Exact number + hundred + noun.","Is this non-exact quantity pattern correct? dozens of eggs Answer: Yes — plural numeral noun + of is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of . English Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of. Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun. Quick Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun.","Is this non-exact quantity pattern correct? hundreds of books Answer: Yes — plural numeral noun + of is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of . English Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of. Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun. Quick Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun.","Is this non-exact quantity pattern correct? thousands of villages Answer: Yes — plural numeral noun + of is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of . English Explanation: Without an exact number before it, the numeral noun can take plural -s and is followed by of. Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun. Quick Rule: Non-exact quantity: dozens/hundreds/thousands of + noun.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. According to some estimates, there are seven thousands types of plants . Answer: seven thousand types of plants Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Would you please lend me ten thousands of rupees ? Answer: ten thousand rupees Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Two million of people attended the meeting held in the Parade Ground. Answer: Two million people Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Manjeet has bought four dozen eggs and a few pieces of pencil for his nephew. Answer: four dozen eggs Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Thousand of people came to see the final test match between India and Australia yesterday. Answer: Thousands of people Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Two hundreds crore has been allocated in the budget. Answer: Two hundred crore Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. The Centre announced one-time assistance of ten thousands rupee each to migrant workers. Answer: ten thousand rupees Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the corrected numerical noun phrase. Million of devout Hindus plunged into the frigid waters of the Ganges. Answer: Millions of devout Hindus Hinglish Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. English Explanation: The correction follows the exact-number vs non-exact-number rule for dozen/hundred/thousand/million. Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of. Quick Rule: Exact count: numeral stays singular and no of; vague large quantity: plural numeral + of.","Choose the correct compound adjective. I have a ten rupees note . Answer: a ten-rupee note Hinglish Explanation: When a number+noun phrase works as an adjective before another noun, the unit noun is singular and hyphenated: ten-rupee . English Explanation: When a number+noun phrase works as an adjective before another noun, the unit noun is singular and hyphenated: ten-rupee. Rule: Number + singular unit noun as modifier → hyphenated compound adjective. Quick Rule: Number + singular unit noun as modifier → hyphenated compound adjective.","Choose the correct phrase. Five ten rupees notes Answer: five ten-rupee notes Hinglish Explanation: Each note is a ten-rupee note ; only the head noun notes becomes plural. English Explanation: Each note is a ten-rupee note; only the head noun notes becomes plural. Rule: Pluralize the head noun, not the unit inside the compound modifier. Quick Rule: Pluralize the head noun, not the unit inside the compound modifier.","Choose the correct compound adjective. a five years plan Answer: a five-year plan Hinglish Explanation: Before plan , the time unit is singular and hyphenated: five-year . English Explanation: Before plan, the time unit is singular and hyphenated: five-year. Rule: Number + singular unit as adjective: five-year plan. Quick Rule: Number + singular unit as adjective: five-year plan.","Choose the correct plural phrase. two five years plans Answer: two five-year plans Hinglish Explanation: The compound modifier stays five-year ; the head noun becomes plural: plans . English Explanation: The compound modifier stays five-year; the head noun becomes plural: plans. Rule: Compound modifier stays singular; head noun carries plural. Quick Rule: Compound modifier stays singular; head noun carries plural.","Is this ordinary duration phrase correct? I have spent five years in university. Answer: Yes — “five years” is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Here the phrase is not acting before another noun as an adjective, so normal plural years is required. English Explanation: Here the phrase is not acting before another noun as an adjective, so normal plural years is required. Rule: Use five-year only before a noun; use five years as a duration. Quick Rule: Use five-year only before a noun; use five years as a duration.","Choose the correct compound. a two men committee Answer: a two-man committee Hinglish Explanation: As a compound modifier, man stays singular: two-man committee . English Explanation: As a compound modifier, man stays singular: two-man committee. Rule: Number + singular noun modifier: two-man committee. Quick Rule: Number + singular noun modifier: two-man committee.","Choose the correct plural phrase. two two men committees Answer: two two-man committees Hinglish Explanation: The modifier remains two-man ; the head noun committees becomes plural. English Explanation: The modifier remains two-man; the head noun committees becomes plural. Rule: Compound adjective stays singular; pluralize the head noun. Quick Rule: Compound adjective stays singular; pluralize the head noun.","Is the non-modifier phrase correct? Our company lost ten men in the fighting that day. Answer: Yes — “ten men” is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Here ten men is the actual noun phrase, not an adjective before another noun. English Explanation: Here ten men is the actual noun phrase, not an adjective before another noun. Rule: Normal counted noun phrase uses the true plural: ten men. Quick Rule: Normal counted noun phrase uses the true plural: ten men.","Choose the correct compound. a two rooms flat Answer: a two-room flat Hinglish Explanation: Before flat , use singular room inside the hyphenated modifier. English Explanation: Before flat, use singular room inside the hyphenated modifier. Quick Rule: two-room flat.","Choose the correct plural phrase. two three rooms flat Answer: two three-room flats Hinglish Explanation: Each is a three-room flat ; plural marking goes on flats . English Explanation: Each is a three-room flat; plural marking goes on flats. Rule: Keep unit singular inside compound adjective. Quick Rule: Keep unit singular inside compound adjective.","Is this ordinary noun phrase correct? This apartment has three rooms . Answer: Yes — “three rooms” is correct. Hinglish Explanation: Here rooms is the counted noun itself, so the normal plural is used. English Explanation: Here rooms is the counted noun itself, so the normal plural is used. Rule: Compound-adjective rule applies only when the phrase modifies another noun. Quick Rule: Compound-adjective rule applies only when the phrase modifies another noun.","Choose the correct compound adjective. Why don’t you take a two kilometers walk in the morning? Answer: a two-kilometer walk Hinglish Explanation: The measurement unit remains singular inside the modifier: two-kilometer walk . English Explanation: The measurement unit remains singular inside the modifier: two-kilometer walk. Rule: Measurement compound before noun → singular unit + hyphen. Quick Rule: Measurement compound before noun → singular unit + hyphen.","Choose the correct compound adjective. Can you bring a ten inches ruler for me now? Answer: a ten-inch ruler Hinglish Explanation: Use ten-inch as the adjective before ruler . English Explanation: Use ten-inch as the adjective before ruler. Rule: Number + singular measurement unit before noun. Quick Rule: Number + singular measurement unit before noun.","Choose the correct compound noun phrase. Congress has constituted a five-members committee . Answer: five-member committee Hinglish Explanation: The unit noun in the compound modifier is singular: five-member committee . English Explanation: The unit noun in the compound modifier is singular: five-member committee. Rule: Number + singular noun modifier. Quick Rule: Number + singular noun modifier.","Choose the correct measurement compound. He ordered three six-gallons barrels of paint. Answer: six-gallon barrels Hinglish Explanation: Each barrel is a six-gallon barrel ; the head noun barrels is plural. English Explanation: Each barrel is a six-gallon barrel; the head noun barrels is plural. Rule: Measurement modifier uses singular unit: six-gallon barrels. Quick Rule: Measurement modifier uses singular unit: six-gallon barrels."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=04.+Nouns&section=9", "t": "Possessive Case & Apostrophes", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 04. Nouns", "x": ["Identify the possessive pattern. a boy&#x27;s shirt Answer: singular noun + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a boy's shirt illustrates singular noun + ’s . English Explanation: a boy's shirt illustrates singular noun + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a girl&#x27;s dress Answer: singular noun + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a girl's dress illustrates singular noun + ’s . English Explanation: a girl's dress illustrates singular noun + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a teacher&#x27;s life Answer: singular noun + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a teacher's life illustrates singular noun + ’s . English Explanation: a teacher's life illustrates singular noun + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. men&#x27;s work Answer: irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s Hinglish Explanation: men's work illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s . English Explanation: men's work illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a women&#x27;s college Answer: irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a women's college illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s . English Explanation: a women's college illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. children&#x27;s toys Answer: irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s Hinglish Explanation: children's toys illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s . English Explanation: children's toys illustrates irregular plural not ending in -s + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. Rahul&#x27;s car Answer: living person + ’s Hinglish Explanation: Rahul's car illustrates living person + ’s . English Explanation: Rahul's car illustrates living person + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. Tina&#x27;s bag Answer: living person + ’s Hinglish Explanation: Tina's bag illustrates living person + ’s . English Explanation: Tina's bag illustrates living person + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a dog&#x27;s tail Answer: animal + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a dog's tail illustrates animal + ’s . English Explanation: a dog's tail illustrates animal + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a bird&#x27;s nest Answer: bird + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a bird's nest illustrates bird + ’s . English Explanation: a bird's nest illustrates bird + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Identify the possessive pattern. a spider&#x27;s web Answer: animal + ’s Hinglish Explanation: a spider's web illustrates animal + ’s . English Explanation: a spider's web illustrates animal + ’s. Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s. Quick Rule: Singular nouns and irregular plurals normally form possessive with ’s.","Choose the correct possessive of the compound noun. mother-in-law&#x27;s behaviour Answer: mother-in-law's behaviour Hinglish Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound . English Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound. Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound. Quick Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound.","Choose the correct possessive of the compound noun. son-in-law&#x27;s car Answer: son-in-law's car Hinglish Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound . English Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound. Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound. Quick Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound.","Choose the correct possessive of the compound noun. maid-servant&#x27;s salary Answer: maid-servant's salary Hinglish Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound . English Explanation: For a compound noun, the possessive marker is added at the end of the whole compound. Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound. Quick Rule: Compound noun possessive → add ’s to the end of the compound.","Choose the corrected possessive. The mother’s-in-law attitude towards the bride was reprehensible. Answer: mother-in-law’s attitude Hinglish Explanation: The possessive belongs to the complete compound mother-in-law , so add ’s at the end. English Explanation: The possessive belongs to the complete compound mother-in-law, so add ’s at the end. Rule: mother-in-law → mother-in-law’s. Quick Rule: mother-in-law → mother-in-law’s.","Choose the corrected possessive. Every soldier follows his commander’s-in-chief orders diligently. Answer: commander-in-chief’s orders Hinglish Explanation: Add the possessive marker to the end of the whole compound: commander-in-chief’s . English Explanation: Add the possessive marker to the end of the whole compound: commander-in-chief’s. Rule: Compound possessive marker goes at the end. Quick Rule: Compound possessive marker goes at the end.","Which phrase shows a jointly owned shop? Ram and Sham’s shop Answer: Ram and Sham’s shop Hinglish Explanation: One possessive marker after the final name signals joint ownership in this pattern. English Explanation: One possessive marker after the final name signals joint ownership in this pattern. Rule: Joint possession: ’s on the last name only. Quick Rule: Joint possession: ’s on the last name only.","Which phrase follows the same joint-ownership pattern? Mr. Lola and Son’s shop Answer: Mr. Lola and Son’s shop Hinglish Explanation: The possessive marker appears at the end of the combined owner expression. English Explanation: The possessive marker appears at the end of the combined owner expression. Rule: Joint owner expression → possessive at the end. Quick Rule: Joint owner expression → possessive at the end.","Which phrase shows separate works by two authors? Bhagat’s and Sharma’s novels Answer: Bhagat’s and Sharma’s novels Hinglish Explanation: When possession is separate, each owner takes a possessive marker. English Explanation: When possession is separate, each owner takes a possessive marker. Rule: Separate possession → each owner gets ’s. Quick Rule: Separate possession → each owner gets ’s.","Choose the phrase for one jointly owned house. He went to ___ . Answer: Rahul and Sonali’s house Hinglish Explanation: One house shared by Rahul and Sonali is expressed with ’s after the last name. English Explanation: One house shared by Rahul and Sonali is expressed with ’s after the last name. Rule: Joint possession → last name only takes ’s. Quick Rule: Joint possession → last name only takes ’s.","Choose the phrase for two separately owned houses. He went to ___ . Answer: Rahul’s and Sonali’s houses Hinglish Explanation: Separate houses belonging to each person use possessive marking on both names and a plural head noun. English Explanation: Separate houses belonging to each person use possessive marking on both names and a plural head noun. Rule: Separate possession → each owner gets ’s. Quick Rule: Separate possession → each owner gets ’s.","Choose the correction for a jointly created mural. Rahul’s and Shail’s mural is vibrant and detailed. Answer: Rahul and Shail’s mural Hinglish Explanation: The mural is presented as a joint work, so only the last name takes ’s. English Explanation: The mural is presented as a joint work, so only the last name takes ’s. Rule: Joint possession/creation → ’s on final owner. Quick Rule: Joint possession/creation → ’s on final owner.","Choose the correct possessive. I have stolen ___ watch. Answer: somebody’s Hinglish Explanation: Indefinite pronouns such as somebody can take ’s . English Explanation: Indefinite pronouns such as somebody can take ’s. Rule: somebody/anybody/everybody/nobody + ’s. Quick Rule: somebody/anybody/everybody/nobody + ’s.","Choose the correct possessive. She needs to check ___ bag. Answer: everyone’s Hinglish Explanation: Everyone’s is the possessive form. English Explanation: Everyone’s is the possessive form. Rule: everyone + ’s. Quick Rule: everyone + ’s.","Choose the correct expression with “else”. Nobody’s else Answer: nobody else’s Hinglish Explanation: When else follows an indefinite pronoun, the possessive marker is attached to else : nobody else’s . English Explanation: When else follows an indefinite pronoun, the possessive marker is attached to else: nobody else’s. Rule: indefinite pronoun + else + ’s. Quick Rule: indefinite pronoun + else + ’s.","Choose the correction. I like nobody’s else advice but yours. Answer: nobody else’s advice Hinglish Explanation: The possessive marker belongs after else . English Explanation: The possessive marker belongs after else. Rule: nobody else’s, somebody else’s, everyone else’s. Quick Rule: nobody else’s, somebody else’s, everyone else’s.","Choose the correction. Your performance is better than everyone’s else . Answer: everyone else’s Hinglish Explanation: Place ’s after else : everyone else’s . English Explanation: Place ’s after else: everyone else’s. Rule: Possessive of pronoun + else → else’s. Quick Rule: Possessive of pronoun + else → else’s.","Choose the exam-style form used in the source. the wall’s painting Answer: the painting of the wall Hinglish Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. English Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions). Quick Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions).","Choose the exam-style form used in the source. the house’s roof Answer: the roof of the house Hinglish Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. English Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions). Quick Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions).","Choose the exam-style form used in the source. a table’s legs Answer: the legs of a table Hinglish Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. English Explanation: For ordinary non-living things, the source prefers an of-phrase rather than ’s. Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions). Quick Rule: Traditional exam rule: non-living thing → of-phrase (with listed exceptions).","Is this ’s construction accepted as an exception? India’s future Answer: Yes — the source accepts it. Hinglish Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. English Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution. Quick Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution.","Is this ’s construction accepted as an exception? China’s population Answer: Yes — the source accepts it. Hinglish Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. English Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution. Quick Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution.","Is this ’s construction accepted as an exception? America’s history Answer: Yes — the source accepts it. Hinglish Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. English Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution. Quick Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution.","Is this ’s construction accepted as an exception? Delhi’s pollution Answer: Yes — the source accepts it. Hinglish Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. English Explanation: The source explicitly allows ’s with countries and place names. Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution. Quick Rule: Place/country names can take ’s: India’s future, Delhi’s pollution.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the plan’s importance Answer: non-living thing linked to human activity Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the plan’s importance as an accepted possessive use: non-living thing linked to human activity . English Explanation: The source lists the plan’s importance as an accepted possessive use: non-living thing linked to human activity. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the report’s conclusion Answer: non-living thing linked to human activity Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the report’s conclusion as an accepted possessive use: non-living thing linked to human activity . English Explanation: The source lists the report’s conclusion as an accepted possessive use: non-living thing linked to human activity. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the university’s president Answer: institution linked to human activity Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the university’s president as an accepted possessive use: institution linked to human activity . English Explanation: The source lists the university’s president as an accepted possessive use: institution linked to human activity. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the book’s author Answer: work linked to human activity Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the book’s author as an accepted possessive use: work linked to human activity . English Explanation: The source lists the book’s author as an accepted possessive use: work linked to human activity. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the crowd’s reaction Answer: collective noun Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the crowd’s reaction as an accepted possessive use: collective noun . English Explanation: The source lists the crowd’s reaction as an accepted possessive use: collective noun. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the government’s decision Answer: collective noun Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the government’s decision as an accepted possessive use: collective noun . English Explanation: The source lists the government’s decision as an accepted possessive use: collective noun. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? the nation’s security Answer: collective noun Hinglish Explanation: The source lists the nation’s security as an accepted possessive use: collective noun . English Explanation: The source lists the nation’s security as an accepted possessive use: collective noun. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? a moment’s delay Answer: time expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists a moment’s delay as an accepted possessive use: time expression . English Explanation: The source lists a moment’s delay as an accepted possessive use: time expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? a week’s holiday Answer: time expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists a week’s holiday as an accepted possessive use: time expression . English Explanation: The source lists a week’s holiday as an accepted possessive use: time expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? a day’s journey Answer: time expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists a day’s journey as an accepted possessive use: time expression . English Explanation: The source lists a day’s journey as an accepted possessive use: time expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? tomorrow’s programme Answer: time expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists tomorrow’s programme as an accepted possessive use: time expression . English Explanation: The source lists tomorrow’s programme as an accepted possessive use: time expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? a rupee’s worth Answer: money/value expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists a rupee’s worth as an accepted possessive use: money/value expression . English Explanation: The source lists a rupee’s worth as an accepted possessive use: money/value expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Why is the possessive accepted here? a dollar’s worth Answer: money/value expression Hinglish Explanation: The source lists a dollar’s worth as an accepted possessive use: money/value expression . English Explanation: The source lists a dollar’s worth as an accepted possessive use: money/value expression. Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc. Quick Rule: ’s is accepted in several conventional non-human relationships: places, institutions, collectives, time, money, etc.","Choose the source-preferred exam form. The car’s front is in extremely bad shape. Answer: front of the car Hinglish Explanation: In this exercise, an ordinary non-living possession is rewritten with of : front of the car . (Everyday modern English can also use “the car’s front.”) English Explanation: In this exercise, an ordinary non-living possession is rewritten with of: front of the car. (Everyday modern English can also use “the car’s front.”) Rule: For this source’s exam rule, ordinary non-living possession prefers of. Quick Rule: For this source’s exam rule, ordinary non-living possession prefers of.","Choose the correct time possessive. These are several items on the agenda for tonight meeting . Answer: tonight’s meeting Hinglish Explanation: Time expressions can take ’s: tonight’s meeting . English Explanation: Time expressions can take ’s: tonight’s meeting. Rule: Time word + ’s is an accepted possessive construction. Quick Rule: Time word + ’s is an accepted possessive construction.","Choose the correct agreement. The sicks and the olds was helped. Answer: sick and the old were Hinglish Explanation: With the + adjective , the adjective does not take plural -s, and the group normally takes a plural verb: the sick ... the old were . English Explanation: With the + adjective, the adjective does not take plural -s, and the group normally takes a plural verb: the sick ... the old were. Rule: the + adjective denotes a class of people: no plural -s, plural agreement. Quick Rule: the + adjective denotes a class of people: no plural -s, plural agreement.","Choose the correction. The rich always looks down upon poor . Answer: look; the poor Hinglish Explanation: The rich is plural in meaning, so use look ; the class noun is the poor . English Explanation: The rich is plural in meaning, so use look; the class noun is the poor. Rule: the + adjective → plural class noun, plural verb. Quick Rule: the + adjective → plural class noun, plural verb.","Choose the corrected verb. Most of the time it is the bourgeois who suffers the indifference the most. Answer: suffer Hinglish Explanation: The source answer treats bourgeois here as plural in reference, so suffer is used. English Explanation: The source answer treats bourgeois here as plural in reference, so suffer is used. Rule: Group/class reference can require plural agreement. Quick Rule: Group/class reference can require plural agreement.","Choose the corrected noun. Teachers should be trained in sign languages since teaching the deafs is not that easy. Answer: deaf Hinglish Explanation: The class is expressed as the deaf , not *the deafs*. English Explanation: The class is expressed as the deaf, not *the deafs*. Rule: the + adjective: the deaf, the blind, the poor — no plural -s. Quick Rule: the + adjective: the deaf, the blind, the poor — no plural -s.","Choose the corrected class nouns. ... you help the blinds or the deafs because it is your moral duty. Answer: blind or the deaf Hinglish Explanation: Use the blind and the deaf ; adjectives themselves do not take plural -s in this construction. English Explanation: Use the blind and the deaf; adjectives themselves do not take plural -s in this construction. Rule: the + adjective denotes a group. Quick Rule: the + adjective denotes a group.","Choose the source-preferred possessive construction. the rich’s hobbies Answer: the hobbies of the rich Hinglish Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase . English Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase. Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class. Quick Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class.","Choose the source-preferred possessive construction. the poor’s honesty Answer: the honesty of the poor Hinglish Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase . English Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase. Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class. Quick Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class.","Choose the source-preferred possessive construction. the old’s plight Answer: the plight of the old Hinglish Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase . English Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase. Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class. Quick Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class.","Choose the source-preferred possessive construction. the blind’s inner eyes Answer: the inner eyes of the blind Hinglish Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase . English Explanation: The source does not attach ’s directly to a the + adjective class expression; it uses an of-phrase. Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class. Quick Rule: the + adjective possession → noun + of + the adjective-class.","Choose the source-preferred form. everything’s beauty Answer: the beauty of everything Hinglish Explanation: For these -thing indefinite pronouns, the source prefers an of construction. English Explanation: For these -thing indefinite pronouns, the source prefers an of construction. Rule: Source rule: something/anything/everything/nothing → prefer of-phrase for possession. Quick Rule: Source rule: something/anything/everything/nothing → prefer of-phrase for possession.","Choose the source-preferred form. nothing’s existence Answer: the existence of nothing Hinglish Explanation: For these -thing indefinite pronouns, the source prefers an of construction. English Explanation: For these -thing indefinite pronouns, the source prefers an of construction. Rule: Source rule: something/anything/everything/nothing → prefer of-phrase for possession. Quick Rule: Source rule: something/anything/everything/nothing → prefer of-phrase for possession.","Which form does the source prefer? Rahul’s car / the car of Rahul Answer: Rahul’s car Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which form does the source prefer? the cow’s milk / the milk of the cow Answer: the cow’s milk Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which form does the source prefer? a girl’s dress / the dress of a girl Answer: a girl’s dress Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which statement matches the source? Shakespeare’s plays / the plays of Shakespeare Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? Gandhi’s death / the death of Gandhi Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? Mr. Sinha’s son / the son of Mr. Sinha Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? a saint’s life / the life of a saint Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which form does the source prefer? Tina’s bag / the bag of Tina Answer: Tina’s bag Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which statement matches the source? your wife’s health / the health of your wife Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which form does the source prefer? your wife’s laptop / the laptop of your wife Answer: your wife’s laptop Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which form does the source prefer? my father’s house / the house of my father Answer: my father’s house Hinglish Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. English Explanation: For this concrete possession relation, the source prefers the ’s construction. Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source. Quick Rule: Concrete possession by a living being is normally expressed with ’s in this source.","Which statement matches the source? India’s future / the future of India Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? China’s population / the population of China Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? America’s history / the history of America Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? Delhi’s population / the population of Delhi Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the plan’s importance / the importance of the plan Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the report’s conclusion / the conclusion of the report Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the university’s president / the president of the university Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the book’s author / the author of the book Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the crowd’s reaction / the reaction of the crowd Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the government’s decision / the decision of the government Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Which statement matches the source? the nation’s security / the security of the nation Answer: Both expressions are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. English Explanation: The source marks both the ’s form and the corresponding of form as acceptable in this relationship. Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of. Quick Rule: Some semantic relationships allow both ’s and of.","Choose the possessive of plural “boys”. boys college Answer: boys’ college Hinglish Explanation: A regular plural ending in -s forms the possessive with an apostrophe only: boys’ . English Explanation: A regular plural ending in -s forms the possessive with an apostrophe only: boys’. Rule: Plural ending in -s → add apostrophe only. Quick Rule: Plural ending in -s → add apostrophe only.","Choose the possessive of plural “girls”. girls hostel Answer: girls’ hostel Hinglish Explanation: A regular plural ending in -s takes apostrophe only: girls’ . English Explanation: A regular plural ending in -s takes apostrophe only: girls’. Rule: Plural ending in -s → apostrophe only. Quick Rule: Plural ending in -s → apostrophe only.","Choose the possessive form shown in the source. Pythagoras’ Theorem Answer: Pythagoras’ Theorem Hinglish Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s. Quick Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s.","Choose the possessive form shown in the source. Socrates’ Idealism Answer: Socrates’ Idealism Hinglish Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s. Quick Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s.","Choose the possessive form shown in the source. Sophocles’ plays Answer: Sophocles’ plays Hinglish Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s. Quick Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s.","Choose the possessive form shown in the source. Jesus’ life Answer: Jesus’ life Hinglish Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s. Quick Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s.","Choose the possessive form shown in the source. Moses’ laws Answer: Moses’ laws Hinglish Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source uses an apostrophe after these classical/Biblical names ending in -s. Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s. Quick Rule: Traditional style may use apostrophe only with certain classical names ending in -s.","Which forms are accepted by the source? Gurudas’ car / Gurudas’s car Answer: Both forms are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style. Quick Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style.","Which forms are accepted by the source? Mr. Jones’ house / Mr. Jones’s house Answer: Both forms are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style. Quick Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style.","Which forms are accepted by the source? Kalidas’ poems / Kalidas’s poems Answer: Both forms are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style. Quick Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style.","Which forms are accepted by the source? Yeats’ poems / Yeats’s poems Answer: Both forms are accepted. Hinglish Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. English Explanation: The source accepts both apostrophe only and ’s for these names ending in -s. Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style. Quick Rule: Names ending in -s may allow either ’ or ’s depending on style.","Choose the source form before “sake”. for justice’ sake Answer: for justice’ sake Hinglish Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake . English Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake. Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake. Quick Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake.","Choose the source form before “sake”. for goodness’ sake Answer: for goodness’ sake Hinglish Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake . English Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake. Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake. Quick Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake.","Choose the source form before “sake”. for conscience’ sake Answer: for conscience’ sake Hinglish Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake . English Explanation: The source uses apostrophe only after these nouns ending in an s-sound before sake. Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake. Quick Rule: Traditional expressions: for justice’ sake, goodness’ sake, conscience’ sake.","Is this fixed expression correct? for God’s sake Answer: Yes — this is the fixed form. Hinglish Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s . English Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s. Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake. Quick Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake.","Is this fixed expression correct? for heaven’s sake Answer: Yes — this is the fixed form. Hinglish Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s . English Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s. Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake. Quick Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake.","Is this fixed expression correct? for mercy’s sake Answer: Yes — this is the fixed form. Hinglish Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s . English Explanation: This is one of the fixed expressions the source explicitly marks as correct with ’s. Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake. Quick Rule: Fixed exceptions: God’s sake, heaven’s sake, mercy’s sake."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=1", "t": "Pronoun Basics & Personal Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Replace the repeated noun with the suitable pronoun. Pooja lives with Pooja’s brother. Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: Pooja is a female singular noun; repeating the name sounds awkward, so her works as the possessive adjective before brother . English Explanation: Pooja is a female singular noun; repeating the name sounds awkward, so her works as the possessive adjective before brother. Rule: A pronoun is used to avoid unnecessary repetition of a noun. Quick Rule: Pronoun ka kaam noun ki unnecessary repetition ko avoid karna hai.","Replace the repeated noun with the suitable pronoun. Pooja loves Karan. Answer: She Hinglish Explanation: Pooja sentence ka subject hai, isliye subject-form she chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “She”. Subject position → I/we/you/he/she/it/they. Quick Rule: Subject position → I/we/you/he/she/it/they.","Choose the natural pronoun-based version. Pooja is a beautiful girl. Pooja lives with Pooja’s brother. Answer: She lives with her brother. Hinglish Explanation: Same person ko baar-baar name se repeat karne ke badle she (subject) aur her (possessive adjective) use hote hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “She lives with her brother.”. She = subject; her + noun = possessive adjective. Quick Rule: She = subject; her + noun = possessive adjective.","Choose the natural pronoun-based version. Pooja is a beautiful girl. Pooja loves Karan. Answer: She loves Karan. Hinglish Explanation: Verb loves se pehle subject chahiye, isliye she . English Explanation: The correct answer is “She loves Karan.”. When a pronoun is the doer or subject before the verb, use the subjective case. Quick Rule: Verb se pehle doer/subject ho to subjective case use hota hai."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=2", "t": "Subjective vs Objective Case", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the correct replacement for the highlighted word. Me am a teacher. Answer: I Hinglish Explanation: Am ka subject first-person singular hota hai, isliye I am . English Explanation: The correct answer is “I”. Subject + verb → subjective case. Quick Rule: Subject + verb → subjective case.","Choose the correct replacement for the highlighted word. Her loves me. Answer: She Hinglish Explanation: Loves ka doer female singular hai, so subject-form she . English Explanation: The correct answer is “She”. She = subject; her = object/possessive adjective. Quick Rule: She = subject; her = object/possessive adjective.","Correct the subject phrase. Him and me are going there. Answer: He and I Hinglish Explanation: Dono pronouns verb are going ke subjects hain, so he and I . English Explanation: The correct answer is “He and I”. Use subjective pronoun forms in a compound subject as well. Quick Rule: Compound subject me bhi subjective forms use hote hain.","Choose the exam-style correction. It was him who had brought you to the hospital. Answer: he Hinglish Explanation: Notes ka formal/exam rule linking verb was ke baad subject complement ke liye he prefer karta hai: It was he who… English Explanation: The correct answer is “he”. Competitive-exam convention: be + subjective case in formal constructions. Quick Rule: Competitive-exam convention: be + subjective case in formal constructions.","Choose the exam-style correction. It was them who were trying to bunk the class. Answer: they Hinglish Explanation: Relative clause ka subject who were trying plural hai; notes formal pattern It was they who… use karte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “they”. Be + subject complement: exam-style subjective case. Quick Rule: Be + subject complement: exam-style subjective case.","Choose the correct object pronoun. He took she to Disneyland. Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: Took whom? → her. Verb ke baad receiver/object hai, so objective case. English Explanation: The correct answer is “her”. Verb + object → objective case. Quick Rule: Verb + object → objective case.","Correct the object phrase. She scolded you and I . Answer: you and me Hinglish Explanation: Scolded whom? → you and me. I subject form hai; yahan object-form me chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “you and me”. As the object of a transitive verb, use objective forms such as me, him, her, us, and them. Quick Rule: Transitive verb ke object me me/him/her/us/them use hote hain.","Correct the phrase after the preposition. There is no problem between he and I . Answer: him and me Hinglish Explanation: Between preposition hai; preposition ke baad objective forms him, me aate hain. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “him and me”. Preposition + objective case. Quick Rule: Preposition + objective case.","Choose the correct pronoun after “except for”. Everyone attended the wedding except for she . Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: For preposition hai, so uske baad object-form her . English Explanation: The correct answer is “her”. Use an objective pronoun after a preposition. Quick Rule: Preposition ke baad objective pronoun.","Choose the correct pronoun after “let”. Let I go. Answer: me Hinglish Explanation: Let ke baad object pronoun aata hai: Let me go. English Explanation: The correct answer is “me”. Let + objective case + V1. Quick Rule: Let + objective case + V1.","Correct the pronoun phrase after “let”. Let he and I talk. Answer: him and me Hinglish Explanation: Let whom talk? → him and me . English Explanation: Let whom talk? → him and me. Rule: Use the objective case after “let”. Quick Rule: Let ke baad objective case.","Choose the pronoun form used as the gerund’s object in these notes. Talking she wasn’t fun at all. Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: Notes yahan her ko objective form ke roop me correct karte hain: Talking her wasn’t fun at all. English Explanation: The correct answer is “her”. Source rule: gerund/object context me objective form. Quick Rule: Source rule: gerund/object context me objective form.","Choose the pronoun form used as the gerund’s object in these notes. Visiting they is delightful. Answer: them Hinglish Explanation: Visiting whom? → them . English Explanation: The correct answer is “them”. Gerund functioning transitively can take an object pronoun. Quick Rule: Gerund functioning transitively can take an object pronoun.","Choose the correct pronoun after conjunction “but”. She is very talented but she / her lacks confidence. Answer: she Hinglish Explanation: Yahan but do clauses join kar raha hai. Second clause ka subject chahiye: she lacks . English Explanation: The correct answer is “she”. If a conjunction introduces a new clause, use the subject form of the pronoun when it is the subject of that clause. Quick Rule: Conjunction ke baad naya clause ho to us clause ka subject form use hota hai.","Choose the correct pronoun when “but” means “except”. Everybody but she / her was happy for them. Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: Yahan but = except , yani preposition jaisa kaam kar raha hai; therefore her . English Explanation: The correct answer is “her”. But = except → objective case. Quick Rule: But = except → objective case.","Choose the correct pronoun after conjunction “but”. He is very stubborn but he / him is compassionate towards elders. Answer: he Hinglish Explanation: Second side me complete clause he is compassionate hai, so subject-form he . English Explanation: The correct answer is “he”. But as conjunction joins clauses; clause needs a subject. Quick Rule: But as conjunction joins clauses; clause needs a subject.","Choose the correct pronoun when “but” means “except”. Everyone acknowledged her in the town but he / him . Answer: him Hinglish Explanation: Sentence ka sense ‘everyone except him’ hai; but prepositional sense me objective him leta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “him”. But = except → objective case. Quick Rule: But = except → objective case.","Correct the pronoun phrase. It took she and I twenty minutes to get dressed. Answer: her and me Hinglish Explanation: Took whom twenty minutes? → her and me . Dono object hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “her and me”. Verb object → objective case. Quick Rule: Verb object → objective case.","Correct the highlighted pronoun. She had seen he three years back. Answer: him Hinglish Explanation: Seen whom? → him . English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “him”. Verb + object pronoun. Quick Rule: Verb + object pronoun.","Correct the comparison phrase. The best sailors on this lake are you and me . Answer: you and I Hinglish Explanation: Notes formal subject-complement rule ke under you and I prefer karte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “you and I”. Exam-style formal rule after be: subjective case. Quick Rule: Exam-style formal rule after be: subjective case.","Correct the object of the preposition. The mural was drawn by Rakhi and I . Answer: Rakhi and me Hinglish Explanation: By preposition hai, isliye me objective form. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “Rakhi and me”. Preposition + objective case. Quick Rule: Preposition + objective case.","Correct the object after “give”. Just give we a chance before you reject the idea. Answer: us Hinglish Explanation: Give whom a chance? → us . English Explanation: Give whom a chance? → us. Rule: Indirect object after verb → objective pronoun. Quick Rule: Indirect object after verb → objective pronoun.","Choose the exam-style form after “It was”. It was he, not me , who put forth the remarkable proposition. Answer: he, not I Hinglish Explanation: Source answer formal balance maintain karta hai: It was he, not I . English Explanation: The correct answer is “he, not I”. Exam-style be-complement uses subjective forms. Quick Rule: Exam-style be-complement uses subjective forms.","Correct the phrase after “except for”. Except for you and I , everyone brought a present to the party. Answer: you and me Hinglish Explanation: For ke baad objective case: you and me . English Explanation: The correct answer is “you and me”. Preposition + objective case. Quick Rule: Preposition + objective case."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=3", "t": "Possessive Adjectives & Possessive Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Correct the possessive pronoun construction. Your house is here but mine house is there. Answer: mine Hinglish Explanation: Mine already means ‘my + noun’, so uske baad house repeat nahi hota. English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “mine”. Possessive pronoun stands alone: mine, ours, yours, hers, theirs. Quick Rule: Possessive pronoun stands alone: mine, ours, yours, hers, theirs.","Choose the correct possessive form before the noun. This is my bag and where is yours bag ? Answer: your bag Hinglish Explanation: Noun bag present hai, isliye possessive adjective your chahiye, not yours . English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “your bag”. Possessive adjective + noun; possessive pronoun stands alone. Quick Rule: Possessive adjective + noun; possessive pronoun stands alone.","Complete the reciprocal possession correctly. We gave them our telephone number, and they gave us their . Answer: theirs Hinglish Explanation: Second telephone number repeat nahi karna, so standalone possessive pronoun theirs . English Explanation: The correct answer is “theirs”. Their + noun; theirs = their + noun. Quick Rule: Their + noun; theirs = their + noun.","Choose the source-marked status. Ours is a humble teacher. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Source page is sentence ko accepted example ke roop me mark karta hai. Ours can stand as a pronoun when the omitted noun is understood from context, though without context this sounds unusual. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. Possessive pronoun can stand without a following noun. Quick Rule: Possessive pronoun can stand without a following noun.","Choose the source-marked status. Yours is an old phone. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Yours is a possessive pronoun and can stand before is with the possessed item understood. English Explanation: Yours is a possessive pronoun and can stand before is with the possessed item understood. Rule: Yours is standalone; your must modify a noun. Quick Rule: Yours is standalone; your must modify a noun.","Correct the possessive adjective. Jane has already eaten hers lunch. Answer: her Hinglish Explanation: Noun lunch follows, so adjective form her is needed. English Explanation: Noun lunch follows, so adjective form her is needed. Rule: Her + noun; hers stands alone. Quick Rule: Her + noun; hers stands alone."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=4", "t": "Pronouns after As / Than", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the exam-style comparison form. She is as smart as me . Answer: I Hinglish Explanation: Source uses the expanded idea as smart as I am , so exam-style answer I . English Explanation: Source uses the expanded idea as smart as I am, so exam-style answer I. Rule: Formal exam rule: as/than + implied clause → subjective case. Quick Rule: Formal exam rule: as/than + implied clause → subjective case.","Choose the exam-style comparison form. I eat faster than him . Answer: he Hinglish Explanation: Source expands it as than he does , therefore he . English Explanation: Source expands it as than he does, therefore he. Rule: Formal comparison with an implied verb may take subjective case. Quick Rule: Formal comparison with an implied verb may take subjective case.","Choose the source-approved form. She likes you as much as I . Answer: I Hinglish Explanation: As much as I can mean ‘as much as I like you’; source marks it grammatical. English Explanation: As much as I can mean ‘as much as I like you’; source marks it grammatical. Rule: Case can change meaning when an implied clause is present. Quick Rule: Case can change meaning when an implied clause is present.","Choose the source-approved form. She likes you as much as me . Answer: me Hinglish Explanation: As much as me can mean ‘as much as she likes me’; source marks this grammatical too. Context decides meaning. English Explanation: As much as me can mean ‘as much as she likes me’; source marks this grammatical too. Context decides meaning. Rule: Both I/me can be grammatical in different ellipses; meaning differs. Quick Rule: Both I/me can be grammatical in different ellipses; meaning differs.","Correct the comparison according to the source rule. No other employee in this office is as efficient as her . Answer: she Hinglish Explanation: Expanded form is as efficient as she is , so the notes choose she . English Explanation: Expanded form is as efficient as she is, so the notes choose she. Rule: Exam-style implied clause after as → subjective case. Quick Rule: Exam-style implied clause after as → subjective case.","Choose the source-marked status. I respect you more than he. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: This can mean I respect you more than he respects you ; source marks no error. English Explanation: This can mean I respect you more than he respects you; source marks no error. Rule: Pronoun case after than can signal the intended elliptical meaning. Quick Rule: Pronoun case after than can signal the intended elliptical meaning.","Correct the comparison according to the source. Though she appears taller, she is just an inch taller than me . Answer: I Hinglish Explanation: Source follows the implied-clause form than I am and therefore uses I . English Explanation: Source follows the implied-clause form than I am and therefore uses I. Rule: Exam-style: than + implied verb → subjective pronoun. Quick Rule: Exam-style: than + implied verb → subjective pronoun.","Correct the highlighted comparison phrase. Contrary to earlier reports, I found him brighter than I during our meeting. Answer: brighter than me Hinglish Explanation: Here him is the object being compared with the speaker as another object/reference point, so source corrects to than me . English Explanation: Here him is the object being compared with the speaker as another object/reference point, so source corrects to than me. Rule: Case after than depends on the understood grammatical role. Quick Rule: Case after than depends on the understood grammatical role."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=5", "t": "Order of Personal Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Arrange the pronouns in the general-order rule. I, she and you will come to the party. Answer: You, she and I Hinglish Explanation: General neutral order in these notes is 2nd + 3rd + 1st : You, she, and I. English Explanation: General neutral order in these notes is 2nd + 3rd + 1st: You, she, and I. Rule: General order: 2 + 3 + 1. Quick Rule: General order: 2 + 3 + 1.","Arrange pronouns while admitting a mistake. He, you, and I should admit our mistake. Answer: I, you, and he Hinglish Explanation: Mistake/confession context me notes 1st + 2nd + 3rd order use karte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I, you, and he”. Confession/blame accepted → 1 + 2 + 3. Quick Rule: Confession/blame accepted → 1 + 2 + 3.","Correct the two-pronoun general order. I and you should go to the party. Answer: You and I Hinglish Explanation: Neutral statement me second person ko first person se pehle rakha gaya: You and I . English Explanation: The correct answer is “You and I”. General courtesy order puts you before I. Quick Rule: General courtesy order puts you before I.","Arrange the pronouns for responsibility/blame. You and I are responsible for the loss. Answer: I and you Hinglish Explanation: Responsibility accept karte hue source first person ko pehle rakhta hai: I and you . English Explanation: The correct answer is “I and you”. Admission of fault/responsibility → first person first. Quick Rule: Admission of fault/responsibility → first person first.","Correct both case and order. Me and you will go to Shimla. Answer: You and I Hinglish Explanation: Dono sentence ke subjects hain, so I not me ; neutral order gives You and I . English Explanation: The correct answer is “You and I”. Subject case + general 2-before-1 order. Quick Rule: Subject case + general 2-before-1 order.","Correct the object-order phrase. She distributed the money between me and you . Answer: you and me Hinglish Explanation: Preposition between objective forms leta hai; source order you and me rakhta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “you and me”. Between + objective pronouns. Quick Rule: Between + objective pronouns."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=6", "t": "Mixed-Person Pronoun Agreement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the possessive adjective referring to “you and I”. You and I must obey your parents. Answer: our Hinglish Explanation: You + I includes the speaker, so combined reference first-person plural we/our banta hai. English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “our”. 1st + 2nd person together → we/us/our/ours/ourselves. Quick Rule: 1st + 2nd person together → we/us/our/ours/ourselves.","Choose the possessive adjective referring to “you and he”. You and he have done their homework. Answer: your Hinglish Explanation: When you is included without I/we, source treats combined person as second-person plural: your . English Explanation: When you is included without I/we, source treats combined person as second-person plural: your. Rule: 2nd + 3rd → you/your/yours/yourselves. Quick Rule: 2nd + 3rd → you/your/yours/yourselves.","Correct both possessive and reflexive reference. He and I should complete their task themselves . Answer: our task ourselves Hinglish Explanation: He + I includes first person, so plural reference becomes our … ourselves . English Explanation: He + I includes first person, so plural reference becomes our … ourselves. Rule: 1st + 3rd together → we/our/ourselves. Quick Rule: 1st + 3rd together → we/our/ourselves."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=7", "t": "Distributive Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the source-approved form. Each of the two students played ice hockey. Answer: Each of the two students Hinglish Explanation: Each can refer individually to members of a group of two or more. English Explanation: Each can refer individually to members of a group of two or more. Rule: Each = every member considered separately. Quick Rule: Each = every member considered separately.","Choose the correct verb. Each of the ten boys is / are going there. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Head pronoun each is singular, so verb is . English Explanation: Head pronoun each is singular, so verb is. Rule: Each takes a singular verb. Quick Rule: Each takes a singular verb.","Choose the distributive pronoun meaning “one out of two”. ___ of these two persons is guilty. Answer: Either Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two choices hain; either means one or the other of two. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Either”. Either is used for one of two. Quick Rule: Either is used for one of two.","Choose the source-approved pronoun. There are two dresses; ___ is red. Answer: either Hinglish Explanation: Two items ke beech one-or-the-other sense ke liye either . English Explanation: The correct answer is “either”. Either → one of two. Quick Rule: Either → one of two.","Correct the pronoun for a group of five. Either of the five students is absent. Answer: Any Hinglish Explanation: Either sirf two ke liye; five me unrestricted one member ke liye any . English Explanation: The correct answer is “Any”. More than two → any/any one, not either. Quick Rule: More than two → any/any one, not either.","Choose the correct form for more than two people. ___ of the five students is absent. Answer: Any Hinglish Explanation: Group five ka hai; source Any of the five… use karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Any”. Either/Neither only for two; any/none for larger sets. Quick Rule: Either/Neither only for two; any/none for larger sets.","Choose the pronoun meaning “not one nor the other” of two. ___ of the two boys is efficient for this job. Answer: Neither Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two boys hain aur dono me se koi nahi suitable: neither . English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Neither”. Neither = not either of two. Quick Rule: Neither = not either of two.","Complete the sentence for two shirts. There are two shirts; ___ is clean. Answer: neither Hinglish Explanation: Dono shirts clean nahi hain, so neither . English Explanation: The correct answer is “neither”. Neither is used with two. Quick Rule: Neither is used with two.","Correct the pronoun for four participants. Neither of the four participants came on time. Answer: None Hinglish Explanation: Four > two, so negative selection is none , not neither . English Explanation: Four > two, so negative selection is none, not neither. Rule: None for more than two; neither for two. Quick Rule: None for more than two; neither for two.","Correct the pronoun for exactly two candidates. I asked whether any of the two major presidential candidates could bring about a better future. Answer: either Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two candidates hain; source chooses either . English Explanation: The correct answer is “either”. One of two → either. Quick Rule: One of two → either.","Correct the negative pronoun for two machines. Still none of the two machines is shipped on time. Answer: neither Hinglish Explanation: There are exactly two machines, so ‘not one nor the other’ = neither . English Explanation: There are exactly two machines, so ‘not one nor the other’ = neither. Rule: Negative choice of two → neither. Quick Rule: Negative choice of two → neither.","Correct the negative pronoun for four candidates. Neither of the four candidates is suitable for the job. Answer: None Hinglish Explanation: Four candidates ke liye none . English Explanation: The correct answer is “None”. More than two → none, not neither. Quick Rule: More than two → none, not neither.","Correct the pronoun referring to the speaker’s two arms. None of my arms is strong enough to lift that. Answer: Neither Hinglish Explanation: A person normally has two arms; source therefore uses neither of my arms . English Explanation: A person normally has two arms; source therefore uses neither of my arms. Rule: Two-item negative selection → neither. Quick Rule: Two-item negative selection → neither.","Correct the choice word for two dresses. I have kept two dresses in my bed; you may take any one . Answer: either Hinglish Explanation: Only two dresses hain, so one of the two = either . English Explanation: The correct answer is “either”. Either = either one of two. Quick Rule: Either = either one of two.","Correct the choice word for two men. I believe that any of the two men can do it without putting in much effort. Answer: either Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two men: either of the two . English Explanation: Exactly two men: either of the two. Rule: Either is restricted to two alternatives. Quick Rule: Either is restricted to two alternatives."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=8", "t": "Reflexive & Emphatic Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Identify the object in the sentence. She admires your courage . Answer: your courage Hinglish Explanation: Admires what? → your courage ; object already present, so reflexive pronoun is not needed. English Explanation: Admires what? → your courage; object already present, so reflexive pronoun is not needed. Rule: Reflexive is needed only when the subject acts on itself or a required object is otherwise missing. Quick Rule: Reflexive is needed only when the subject acts on itself or a required object is otherwise missing.","Choose the reflexive object. She admires ___ . Answer: herself Hinglish Explanation: Subject and object same person hain: she admires herself . English Explanation: The correct answer is “herself”. Subject = object → reflexive pronoun. Quick Rule: Subject = object → reflexive pronoun.","Choose the ordinary object; no reflexive is needed. Radhika hurt the cat on the playground. Answer: the cat Hinglish Explanation: Object the cat already present hai; action Radhika par return nahi kar raha. English Explanation: The correct answer is “the cat”. Different object present → ordinary noun/object, not reflexive. Quick Rule: Different object present → ordinary noun/object, not reflexive.","Supply the missing reflexive pronoun. Radhika hurt ___ on the playground. Answer: herself Hinglish Explanation: When no other object is given, subject Radhika receives the action herself. English Explanation: When no other object is given, subject Radhika receives the action herself. Rule: Hurt oneself → reflexive object. Quick Rule: Hurt oneself → reflexive object.","Correct the reflexive construction. He prides on his honesty and impartiality. Answer: prides himself on his Hinglish Explanation: Pride in this pattern requires a reflexive object: pride oneself on . English Explanation: Pride in this pattern requires a reflexive object: pride oneself on. Rule: pride oneself on + noun/gerund. Quick Rule: pride oneself on + noun/gerund.","Correct the reflexive construction. You should avail of this opportunity. Answer: avail yourself of Hinglish Explanation: Standard exam pattern: avail yourself of an opportunity. English Explanation: Standard exam pattern: avail yourself of an opportunity. Rule: avail oneself of. Quick Rule: avail oneself of.","Correct the reflexive construction. He acquitted of a debt which had long lain heavy on him. Answer: acquitted himself of Hinglish Explanation: Source rule uses acquit oneself of when the person frees himself from something. English Explanation: Source rule uses acquit oneself of when the person frees himself from something. Rule: acquit oneself of. Quick Rule: acquit oneself of.","Correct the reflexive construction. He absented from the parliament for ten days. Answer: absented himself from Hinglish Explanation: Verb pattern is absent oneself from . English Explanation: Verb pattern is absent oneself from. Quick Rule: absent oneself from.","Correct the reflexive construction. She prides on her beauty. Answer: prides herself on Hinglish Explanation: Subject she needs reflexive object: prides herself on . English Explanation: Subject she needs reflexive object: prides herself on. Rule: pride oneself on. Quick Rule: pride oneself on.","Choose the correct object construction. I enjoyed the class last week. Answer: enjoyed the class Hinglish Explanation: Here the class is the direct object of enjoyed ; reflexive is unnecessary. English Explanation: Here the class is the direct object of enjoyed; reflexive is unnecessary. Rule: Enjoy + object is complete. Quick Rule: Enjoy + object is complete.","Correct the sentence when “enjoy” has no direct object. I enjoyed in the class last week. Answer: enjoyed myself in the class Hinglish Explanation: Without a direct object, standard construction is enjoy oneself . English Explanation: Without a direct object, standard construction is enjoy oneself. Rule: enjoy oneself when no other object follows. Quick Rule: enjoy oneself when no other object follows.","Choose the correct transitive construction. He introduced the issue to the C.M. Answer: introduced the issue Hinglish Explanation: Object the issue is present, so introduced the issue to… is complete. English Explanation: Object the issue is present, so introduced the issue to… is complete. Rule: Introduce + object + to + person. Quick Rule: Introduce + object + to + person.","Correct the sentence when a person introduces himself. He introduced to the C.M. . Answer: introduced himself to the C.M. Hinglish Explanation: No other object is present; the person is introducing himself, so himself . English Explanation: No other object is present; the person is introducing himself, so himself. Rule: introduce oneself to someone. Quick Rule: introduce oneself to someone.","Correct the reflexive pronoun used as subject. Myself is Tarun Grover. Answer: I Hinglish Explanation: Reflexive pronoun normally cannot be the grammatical subject by itself; use I am… . English Explanation: Reflexive pronoun normally cannot be the grammatical subject by itself; use I am…. Rule: Reflexive pronoun is not used as a standalone subject. Quick Rule: Reflexive pronoun is not used as a standalone subject.","Correct the compound subject. Myself and he are going there. Answer: He and I Hinglish Explanation: Subject position needs personal pronouns, not standalone reflexive: He and I . English Explanation: Subject position needs personal pronouns, not standalone reflexive: He and I. Rule: Reflexive cannot replace I/he as an ordinary subject. Quick Rule: Reflexive cannot replace I/he as an ordinary subject.","Identify the function of the highlighted pronoun. I myself saw him. Answer: Emphatic pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Myself sirf emphasis de raha hai—‘I personally saw him’; verb ka object him hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic pronoun”. Emphatic pronoun adds emphasis and can be removed without changing core grammar. Quick Rule: Emphatic pronoun adds emphasis and can be removed without changing core grammar.","Identify the function of the highlighted pronoun. They themselves are late. Answer: Emphatic pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Themselves ‘they themselves’ par emphasis deta hai; action wapas subject par nahi ja raha. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic pronoun”. Emphatic pronoun = emphasis, not object. Quick Rule: Emphatic pronoun = emphasis, not object."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=9", "t": "Indefinite Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the correct verb. Someone have stolen my purse. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Someone is grammatically singular, so has . English Explanation: Someone is grammatically singular, so has. Rule: Someone/anyone/everyone/no one → singular verb. Quick Rule: Someone/anyone/everyone/no one → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb. Everyone want to succeed in life. Answer: wants Hinglish Explanation: Everyone takes a singular verb: wants . English Explanation: Everyone takes a singular verb: wants. Rule: Everyone is grammatically singular. Quick Rule: Everyone is grammatically singular.","Choose the traditional exam-style possessive reference. Is everyone happy with their gift? Answer: his Hinglish Explanation: These notes follow the older competitive-exam convention everyone … his . Modern standard English widely accepts singular they/their as well. English Explanation: These notes follow the older competitive-exam convention everyone … his. Modern standard English widely accepts singular they/their as well. Rule: Source convention: indefinite -one/-body → singular reference. Quick Rule: Source convention: indefinite -one/-body → singular reference.","Choose the traditional exam-style continuation. Anyone can be happy if they have no expectations. Answer: he has Hinglish Explanation: Source treats anyone as singular and uses he has . Modern English can use singular they have . English Explanation: Source treats anyone as singular and uses he has. Modern English can use singular they have. Rule: Source/exam convention: anyone → singular verb/pronoun. Quick Rule: Source/exam convention: anyone → singular verb/pronoun.","Correct both agreement and pronoun under the source rule. If anybody try to interrupt me, I will argue with them . Answer: tries … him Hinglish Explanation: Anybody is singular, so tries ; source also uses singular him . Modern English may use singular them . English Explanation: Anybody is singular, so tries; source also uses singular him. Modern English may use singular them. Rule: Anybody → singular verb; source uses singular personal reference. Quick Rule: Anybody → singular verb; source uses singular personal reference.","Correct the traditional exam-style references. Everyone should keep their country before themselves . Answer: his country before himself Hinglish Explanation: Source treats everyone as singular: his … himself . Modern inclusive English often uses their … themselves . English Explanation: Source treats everyone as singular: his … himself. Modern inclusive English often uses their … themselves. Rule: Source convention: everyone → singular possessive/reflexive. Quick Rule: Source convention: everyone → singular possessive/reflexive.","Choose the correct verb. Nothing are permanent in this world. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Nothing is singular: Nothing is permanent. English Explanation: Nothing is singular: Nothing is permanent. Rule: -thing indefinite pronouns take singular verbs. Quick Rule: -thing indefinite pronouns take singular verbs.","Choose the correct verb. Something are wrong with the stew. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Something is singular, so is . English Explanation: Something is singular, so is. Rule: Something/anything/everything/nothing → singular verb. Quick Rule: Something/anything/everything/nothing → singular verb.","Correct the agreement and possessive reference. Everything have his own price, even relationships. Answer: has its Hinglish Explanation: Everything is singular and non-living in this rule set: has its . English Explanation: Everything is singular and non-living in this rule set: has its. Rule: Everything → singular verb + its. Quick Rule: Everything → singular verb + its.","Correct the agreement and possessive reference. It is necessary that everything remain in his order. Answer: remains in its Hinglish Explanation: Everything takes singular remains and non-living possessive its . English Explanation: Everything takes singular remains and non-living possessive its. Rule: Everything → singular agreement; non-living reference → it/its/itself. Quick Rule: Everything → singular agreement; non-living reference → it/its/itself."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=10", "t": "Pronoun “One”", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the matching possessive when rewriting with “one”. Everyone should keep his promise. = One should keep ___ promise. Answer: one’s Hinglish Explanation: Once sentence one se start hota hai, possessive reference one’s maintain karna chahiye. English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “one’s”. one → one / one’s / oneself. Quick Rule: one → one / one’s / oneself.","Correct the possessive reference. One should keep his promise. Answer: one’s Hinglish Explanation: Source consistency rule: one … one’s . English Explanation: Source consistency rule: one … one’s. Rule: Do not switch from one to he/his in the same reference chain. Quick Rule: Do not switch from one to he/his in the same reference chain.","Correct both agreement and pronoun consistency. If one work hard, he is sure to succeed in life. Answer: works hard, one Hinglish Explanation: One is singular → works ; repeated reference should remain one . English Explanation: One is singular → works; repeated reference should remain one. Rule: one + singular verb; keep one-reference consistent. Quick Rule: one + singular verb; keep one-reference consistent.","Correct the reflexive reference to “one”. One should always think of others before one thinks of himself . Answer: oneself Hinglish Explanation: Reflexive of one is oneself . English Explanation: Reflexive of one is oneself. Rule: one → oneself. Quick Rule: one → oneself.","Correct the reflexive reference to “one”. One should love himself . Answer: oneself Hinglish Explanation: The subject is one , so reflexive must be oneself . English Explanation: The subject is one, so reflexive must be oneself. Rule: Maintain one/one’s/oneself consistency. Quick Rule: Maintain one/one’s/oneself consistency.","Correct the source-style possessive reference. Everyone agreed to share their information . Answer: his information Hinglish Explanation: Source uses singular reference for everyone : his information . Modern English commonly accepts their information . English Explanation: Source uses singular reference for everyone: his information. Modern English commonly accepts their information. Rule: Source/exam convention for everyone: singular reference. Quick Rule: Source/exam convention for everyone: singular reference.","Correct the relative clause and later reference under the source rule. Anyone who have worked for the company … but they have to submit the forms. Answer: has worked … he has Hinglish Explanation: Anyone is singular: has worked ; source continues with singular he has . English Explanation: Anyone is singular: has worked; source continues with singular he has. Rule: Anyone → singular verb/reference in this source. Quick Rule: Anyone → singular verb/reference in this source.","Correct the pronoun referring to “everything”. Everything I found in books … I retained in my memory and adapted them . Answer: it Hinglish Explanation: Everything is singular, so later object pronoun is it . English Explanation: Everything is singular, so later object pronoun is it. Rule: Everything → it/its/itself. Quick Rule: Everything → it/its/itself.","Choose the fully consistent “one” version. One must work hard not only for himself but also for his family and friends. Answer: oneself … one’s Hinglish Explanation: Once subject is one , reflexive and possessive should stay oneself and one’s . English Explanation: Once subject is one, reflexive and possessive should stay oneself and one’s. Rule: One-reference chain: one → one’s → oneself. Quick Rule: One-reference chain: one → one’s → oneself.","Choose the form meaning “one selected member of us”. ___ of us can solve this question. Answer: Any one Hinglish Explanation: Any one of us means any single member selected from a defined group. English Explanation: Any one of us means any single member selected from a defined group. Rule: any one + of + group = selection from a set. Quick Rule: any one + of + group = selection from a set.","Choose the form meaning “any person generally”. ___ can solve this question. Answer: Anyone Hinglish Explanation: Without of + group , one-word anyone means any person in a wide sense. English Explanation: Without of + group, one-word anyone means any person in a wide sense. Rule: Anyone = any person; any one = one selected item/person. Quick Rule: Anyone = any person; any one = one selected item/person."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=11", "t": "Relative Pronouns: Who / Whom / Whose / Which", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Combine the two statements with a relative pronoun. I met Radhika. She had been the topper of the class. Answer: I met Radhika who had been the topper of the class. Hinglish Explanation: Radhika relative clause me verb had been ki subject hai, so who . English Explanation: The correct answer is “I met Radhika who had been the topper of the class.”. Human + subject of relative clause → who. Quick Rule: Human + subject of relative clause → who.","Choose the relative pronoun acting as subject. This is the man ___ broke the window. Answer: who Hinglish Explanation: Relative clause ___ broke ko subject chahiye → who . English Explanation: The correct answer is “who”. Who = subject for people. Quick Rule: Who = subject for people.","Choose the relative pronoun acting as subject. I like the students ___ are hardworking. Answer: who Hinglish Explanation: ___ are hardworking me pronoun subject hai → who . English Explanation: The correct answer is “who”. Who + verb when antecedent is a person. Quick Rule: Who + verb when antecedent is a person.","Choose the relative pronoun acting as object. This is the boy ___ she will marry. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: She will marry whom? Relative pronoun object hai, so formal whom . English Explanation: The correct answer is “whom”. Whom = object for people. Quick Rule: Whom = object for people.","Choose the possessive relative pronoun. I have a friend ___ cat is annoying. Answer: whose Hinglish Explanation: Cat friend ka hai; possession dikhane ke liye whose . English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “whose”. Whose + noun = possessive relative form. Quick Rule: Whose + noun = possessive relative form.","Choose the possessive relative pronoun. She is the girl ___ dog we have found. Answer: whose Hinglish Explanation: Dog belongs to the girl, so whose dog . English Explanation: Dog belongs to the girl, so whose dog. Rule: Whose shows possession. Quick Rule: Whose shows possession.","Correct the relative pronoun. I don’t like people whom interrupt me. Answer: who Hinglish Explanation: ___ interrupt me me relative pronoun khud subject hai → who . English Explanation: The correct answer is “who”. Who = subject; whom = object. Quick Rule: Who = subject; whom = object.","Correct the relative pronoun. The singer who we have injured proved to be safe. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: We injured whom? Pronoun is object of injured , so formal whom . English Explanation: We injured whom? Pronoun is object of injured, so formal whom. Rule: Object of verb → whom. Quick Rule: Object of verb → whom.","Correct the relative pronoun. The people whom just boarded the plane are in a rock band. Answer: who Hinglish Explanation: ___ just boarded needs a subject → who . English Explanation: ___ just boarded needs a subject → who. Rule: Who performs the action in the relative clause. Quick Rule: Who performs the action in the relative clause.","Correct the relative pronoun. The children, who we love dearly, need better education. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: We love whom? Pronoun is object, so formal whom . English Explanation: We love whom? Pronoun is object, so formal whom. Rule: Object relative for people → whom. Quick Rule: Object relative for people → whom.","Correct the relative pronoun. The response of those managers who I have consulted has been very positive. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: I have consulted whom? → managers; object form whom . English Explanation: I have consulted whom? → managers; object form whom. Rule: Whom = object of consulted. Quick Rule: Whom = object of consulted.","Correct the relative pronoun. She was a celebrated actress who he had known and loved. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: He had known/loved whom? → actress, so formal object whom . English Explanation: He had known/loved whom? → actress, so formal object whom. Rule: Object relative pronoun for a person → whom. Quick Rule: Object relative pronoun for a person → whom.","Choose the source-marked status. She smiled as she remembered the quiet scholar with whom she had shared a love of books. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: After preposition with , formal object form whom is correct. English Explanation: After preposition with, formal object form whom is correct. Rule: Preposition + whom for a person. Quick Rule: Preposition + whom for a person.","Choose the relative pronoun for a non-human object as object. This is the dog ___ we rescued. Answer: which Hinglish Explanation: Dog is non-human/animal in this source rule and pronoun is object → which . English Explanation: Dog is non-human/animal in this source rule and pronoun is object → which. Rule: Which is used for things/animals in this chapter’s rule set. Quick Rule: Which is used for things/animals in this chapter’s rule set.","Choose the relative pronoun for an animal as subject. This is the bird ___ can fly. Answer: which Hinglish Explanation: Relative pronoun is subject of can fly ; source uses which for bird. English Explanation: Relative pronoun is subject of can fly; source uses which for bird. Rule: Which can be subject/object for non-human antecedents. Quick Rule: Which can be subject/object for non-human antecedents.","Choose the relative pronoun. This is the house ___ belongs to my great-grandfather. Answer: which Hinglish Explanation: House is non-living and ___ belongs needs subject → which . English Explanation: House is non-living and ___ belongs needs subject → which. Rule: Non-living antecedent → which/that depending context. Quick Rule: Non-living antecedent → which/that depending context.","Choose the possessive construction used in the notes. This is the showroom ___ inauguration has been delayed. Answer: of which Hinglish Explanation: For non-living possession, source uses of which . English Explanation: For non-living possession, source uses of which. Rule: Possession with which → of which; modern English may also use whose. Quick Rule: Possession with which → of which; modern English may also use whose.","Choose the possessive construction for the chairs. The chairs ___ handles are broken are yours. Answer: of which Hinglish Explanation: Handles belong to chairs; source pattern is of which . English Explanation: Handles belong to chairs; source pattern is of which. Rule: of which expresses possession for things. Quick Rule: of which expresses possession for things.","Choose the source-approved possessive relative form. This is the machine ___ properties I described. Answer: whose Hinglish Explanation: Source explicitly notes that modern English can use whose for things too; of which is also possible. English Explanation: Source explicitly notes that modern English can use whose for things too; of which is also possible. Rule: Whose can refer to people and, in modern English, things. Quick Rule: Whose can refer to people and, in modern English, things.","Correct the relative pronoun. This is the boy which works hard. Answer: who Hinglish Explanation: Person + relative clause subject → who . English Explanation: Person + relative clause subject → who. Rule: Who for people as subject. Quick Rule: Who for people as subject.","Correct the relative pronoun. This is the girl which I shall marry. Answer: whom Hinglish Explanation: I shall marry whom? → girl. Formal object form whom . English Explanation: I shall marry whom? → girl. Formal object form whom. Rule: Whom for a person as object. Quick Rule: Whom for a person as object."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=12", "t": "Relative Pronoun “That”", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the equivalent relative pronoun. This is the boy who works hard. = This is the boy ___ works hard. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: In a defining clause, that can replace who here. English Explanation: In a defining clause, that can replace who here. Rule: That can refer to people or things in defining relative clauses. Quick Rule: That can refer to people or things in defining relative clauses.","Choose the equivalent relative pronoun. This is the house which my father built. = This is the house ___ my father built. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: That can replace which in this defining clause. English Explanation: That can replace which in this defining clause. Rule: That can replace who/which in many defining clauses. Quick Rule: That can replace who/which in many defining clauses.","Use the source-preferred relative pronoun after a superlative. This is the best article which I have read in recent times. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: After superlative best , source prefers that . English Explanation: After superlative best, source prefers that. Rule: Source rule: superlative degree → that. Quick Rule: Source rule: superlative degree → that.","Use the source-preferred relative pronoun after “the same”. He is the same guy who helped me during debates. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: Notes list the same among triggers preferring that . English Explanation: Notes list the same among triggers preferring that. Rule: Source rule: the same/the few/the little/only/none → that. Quick Rule: Source rule: the same/the few/the little/only/none → that.","Use the source-preferred relative pronoun after “the few”. I received the few gifts which I ordered last week. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: The few is listed as a context where source prefers that . English Explanation: The few is listed as a context where source prefers that. Rule: Source preference after the few → that. Quick Rule: Source preference after the few → that.","Use the source-preferred relative pronoun after an indefinite -thing word. I do nothing which hurts your sentiments. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: After nothing , source rule uses that . English Explanation: After nothing, source rule uses that. Rule: Something/anything/everything/nothing → that in this source rule. Quick Rule: Something/anything/everything/nothing → that in this source rule.","Use the source-preferred relative pronoun after “all”. All which glitters is not gold. Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: Source equates all with ‘everything’ and prefers that . English Explanation: Source equates all with ‘everything’ and prefers that. Rule: After all/everything, source prefers that. Quick Rule: After all/everything, source prefers that."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=13", "t": "Relative Pronoun “What”", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the correct version when a noun antecedent is present. You can wear the dress what you want. Answer: the dress which Hinglish Explanation: What already means ‘the thing which’ and normally does not take a separate antecedent noun. With the dress , use which . English Explanation: What already means ‘the thing which’ and normally does not take a separate antecedent noun. With the dress, use which. Rule: What has no separate antecedent; noun antecedent + which/that. Quick Rule: What has no separate antecedent; noun antecedent + which/that.","Choose the source-approved sentence. You can wear the dress which you want. Answer: the dress which Hinglish Explanation: Because antecedent the dress is explicitly present, which is suitable. English Explanation: Because antecedent the dress is explicitly present, which is suitable. Rule: Antecedent present → which/that, not what. Quick Rule: Antecedent present → which/that, not what.","Choose the source-approved no-antecedent form. You can wear ___ you want. Answer: what Hinglish Explanation: No noun antecedent precedes the blank; what itself means ‘the thing which’. English Explanation: No noun antecedent precedes the blank; what itself means ‘the thing which’. Rule: What = the thing which. Quick Rule: What = the thing which.","Correct the sentence with an explicit noun antecedent. You can take the book what you like. Answer: the book which Hinglish Explanation: The book is antecedent; use which (or that), not what . English Explanation: The book is antecedent; use which (or that), not what. Rule: What does not follow a separate antecedent noun. Quick Rule: What does not follow a separate antecedent noun.","Choose the source-approved sentence. You can take the book which you like. Answer: the book which Hinglish Explanation: Which links back to the explicit noun book . English Explanation: Which links back to the explicit noun book. Rule: Explicit thing antecedent → which/that. Quick Rule: Explicit thing antecedent → which/that.","Choose the source-approved no-antecedent form. You can take ___ you like. Answer: what Hinglish Explanation: What you like = ‘the thing that you like’. English Explanation: What you like = ‘the thing that you like’. Rule: What contains its own antecedent meaning. Quick Rule: What contains its own antecedent meaning.","Correct the relative construction. …because the lies what he says are going to blow your mind. Answer: the lies which Hinglish Explanation: Noun antecedent lies is present, so use which (or that), not what. English Explanation: Noun antecedent lies is present, so use which (or that), not what. Rule: Antecedent noun + relative pronoun which/that. Quick Rule: Antecedent noun + relative pronoun which/that.","Choose the source-approved version with an antecedent. …because the lies which he says are going to blow your mind. Answer: the lies which Hinglish Explanation: Lies is the antecedent and which refers back to it. English Explanation: Lies is the antecedent and which refers back to it. Rule: Which can refer to things with a stated antecedent. Quick Rule: Which can refer to things with a stated antecedent.","Choose the source-approved no-antecedent version. …because ___ he says is going to blow your mind. Answer: what Hinglish Explanation: No separate antecedent is stated; what he says acts as a noun clause meaning ‘the thing he says’. English Explanation: No separate antecedent is stated; what he says acts as a noun clause meaning ‘the thing he says’. Rule: What = the thing which; no antecedent needed. Quick Rule: What = the thing which; no antecedent needed."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=14", "t": "Reciprocal Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the source-preferred reciprocal for more than two students. The students must cooperate with each other on this project. Answer: one another Hinglish Explanation: Source distinction: one another for more than two; each other for two. English Explanation: Source distinction: one another for more than two; each other for two. Rule: Exam-style source rule: each other = two; one another = more than two. Quick Rule: Exam-style source rule: each other = two; one another = more than two.","Choose the source-preferred reciprocal for two people. Rahul and Sahil are meeting one another at the cafe. Answer: each other Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two people hain, so source prefers each other . English Explanation: The correct answer is “each other”. Each other for two in this source convention. Quick Rule: Each other for two in this source convention."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=15", "t": "Demonstrative Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the demonstrative for one nearby thing. ___ is my dog. Answer: This Hinglish Explanation: Singular + near = this . English Explanation: Singular + near = this. Rule: This: singular near. Quick Rule: This: singular near.","Choose the demonstrative for one distant thing. ___ is your dog. Answer: That Hinglish Explanation: Singular + far = that . English Explanation: Singular + far = that. Rule: That: singular far. Quick Rule: That: singular far.","Choose the demonstrative for nearby plural things. ___ are my shoes. Answer: These Hinglish Explanation: Plural + near = these . English Explanation: Plural + near = these. Rule: These: plural near. Quick Rule: These: plural near.","Choose the demonstrative for distant plural things. ___ are my shoes. Answer: Those Hinglish Explanation: Plural + far = those . English Explanation: Plural + far = those. Rule: Those: plural far. Quick Rule: Those: plural far."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=16", "t": "Interrogative Pronouns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Choose the interrogative pronoun used as subject. ___ is crying here? Answer: Who Hinglish Explanation: The unknown person is the doer of is crying , so who . English Explanation: The unknown person is the doer of is crying, so who. Rule: Who asks about a person as subject. Quick Rule: Who asks about a person as subject.","Choose the formal interrogative object pronoun. ___ have you invited? Answer: Whom Hinglish Explanation: You have invited whom? → object of invited . English Explanation: You have invited whom? → object of invited. Rule: Whom asks about a person as object. Quick Rule: Whom asks about a person as object.","Choose the interrogative possessive pronoun. ___ are these books? Answer: Whose Hinglish Explanation: Question ownership ka hai: Whose are these books? English Explanation: The correct possessive form is “Whose”. Whose asks possession. Quick Rule: Whose asks possession.","Choose the interrogative pronoun for selection. ___ is your house? Answer: Which Hinglish Explanation: From identifiable alternatives, which asks for selection. English Explanation: From identifiable alternatives, which asks for selection. Rule: Which asks choice among known/limited possibilities. Quick Rule: Which asks choice among known/limited possibilities.","Choose the interrogative pronoun for information about a thing/name. ___ is your name? Answer: What Hinglish Explanation: The question asks for the name itself: What is your name? English Explanation: The question asks for the name itself: What is your name? Rule: What asks identity/information about a thing or fact. Quick Rule: What asks identity/information about a thing or fact."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=05.+Pronouns&section=17", "t": "Exclamatory Pronoun & Types of “What”", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 05. Pronouns", "x": ["Identify the highlighted “what”. What! You haven’t attended the class. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What! expresses sudden surprise/emotion, so source labels it exclamatory. English Explanation: What! expresses sudden surprise/emotion, so source labels it exclamatory. Rule: What + exclamation/surprise → exclamatory pronoun in this source. Quick Rule: What + exclamation/surprise → exclamatory pronoun in this source.","Identify the first “what”. What! I don’t believe what she says. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Initial What! is an exclamation; the later what she says has a different relative/noun-clause function. English Explanation: Initial What! is an exclamation; the later what she says has a different relative/noun-clause function. Rule: Position and function decide the type of what. Quick Rule: Position and function decide the type of what.","Identify the highlighted “what”. What! You have already informed her. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: It conveys surprise rather than asking a question. English Explanation: It conveys surprise rather than asking a question. Rule: Exclamatory what expresses sudden emotion. Quick Rule: Exclamatory what expresses sudden emotion.","Identify the highlighted “what”. What! When did she ask me? Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: The standalone What! is surprise; When did…? is the actual question. English Explanation: The standalone What! is surprise; When did…? is the actual question. Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory. Quick Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory.","Identify the type of “what”. What he says is not true. Answer: Relative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What he says means ‘the thing which he says’ and forms a noun/relative clause. English Explanation: What he says means ‘the thing which he says’ and forms a noun/relative clause. Rule: What without antecedent = the thing which → relative function. Quick Rule: What without antecedent = the thing which → relative function.","Identify the type of “what”. What are your problems? Answer: Interrogative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: It directly asks a question seeking information, so interrogative. English Explanation: It directly asks a question seeking information, so interrogative. Rule: Question-form what → interrogative pronoun. Quick Rule: Question-form what → interrogative pronoun.","Identify the type of “what”. What! Rahul has gone mad. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What! shows surprise/shock. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Emotional What! → exclamatory. Quick Rule: Emotional What! → exclamatory.","Identify the type of “what”. I have told you what I know. Answer: Relative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What I know means ‘the thing(s) that I know’; it introduces a noun/relative clause. English Explanation: What I know means ‘the thing(s) that I know’; it introduces a noun/relative clause. Rule: No separate antecedent; what carries ‘the thing which’ meaning. Quick Rule: No separate antecedent; what carries ‘the thing which’ meaning.","Identify the type of “what”. What! You don’t know Narendra Modi. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: The speaker is reacting with surprise, not asking what. English Explanation: The speaker is reacting with surprise, not asking what. Rule: Exclamatory what expresses sudden emotion. Quick Rule: Exclamatory what expresses sudden emotion.","Identify the type of “what”. What has he bought today? Answer: Interrogative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: It asks for the object he bought, so what is interrogative. English Explanation: It asks for the object he bought, so what is interrogative. Rule: What in a direct question → interrogative pronoun. Quick Rule: What in a direct question → interrogative pronoun."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=1", "t": "Adjective Basics & Position", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Identify the adjective use. A beautiful girl. Answer: Attributive use Hinglish Explanation: “beautiful” noun “girl” ke directly before hai, isliye adjective attributive position me use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Attributive use”. Adjective + noun → attributive use. Quick Rule: Adjective + noun → attributive use.","Identify the adjective use. A new building. Answer: Attributive use Hinglish Explanation: “new” directly noun “building” ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Attributive use”. An adjective placed before a noun is used attributively. Quick Rule: Noun ke before adjective → attributive.","Identify the adjective use. She is beautiful . Answer: Predicative use Hinglish Explanation: “beautiful” linking verb “is” ke baad subject “she” ki quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Predicative use”. Linking verb + adjective → predicative use. Quick Rule: Linking verb + adjective → predicative use.","Choose the correct description of the highlighted word. She looks happy . Answer: Predicative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “looks” yahan linking verb hai; “happy” subject ki state batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Predicative adjective”. An adjective can follow a linking verb as a subject complement. Quick Rule: Linking verb ke baad adjective aata hai.","Choose the correct description of the highlighted word. He is smart . Answer: Predicative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “smart” “he” ki quality batata hai aur “is” ke baad hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Predicative adjective”. Be + adjective → predicative complement. Quick Rule: Be + adjective → predicative complement."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=2", "t": "Kinds of Adjectives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Identify the type of adjective. An American student. Answer: Proper adjective Hinglish Explanation: “American” proper noun/name se derived nationality adjective hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Proper adjective”. An adjective derived from a proper noun is called a proper adjective. Quick Rule: Proper noun se bana adjective → proper adjective.","Identify the type of adjective. An Indian student. Answer: Proper adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Indian” proper noun/name se derived nationality adjective hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Proper adjective”. An adjective derived from a proper noun is called a proper adjective. Quick Rule: Proper noun se bana adjective → proper adjective.","Identify the type of adjective. An Chinese student. Answer: Proper adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Chinese” proper noun/name se derived nationality adjective hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Proper adjective”. An adjective derived from a proper noun is called a proper adjective. Quick Rule: Proper noun se bana adjective → proper adjective.","Identify the type of adjective. An Japanese student. Answer: Proper adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Japanese” proper noun/name se derived nationality adjective hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Proper adjective”. An adjective derived from a proper noun is called a proper adjective. Quick Rule: Proper noun se bana adjective → proper adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. my car Answer: Possessive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “my” noun ke before ownership dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Possessive adjective”. my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective. Quick Rule: my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. your bag Answer: Possessive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “your” noun ke before ownership dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Possessive adjective”. my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective. Quick Rule: my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. his wife Answer: Possessive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “his” noun ke before ownership dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Possessive adjective”. my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective. Quick Rule: my/our/your/his/her/its/their + noun → possessive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Each boy is going to the party. Answer: Distributive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Each” members ko individually/distributively refer karta hai aur noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Distributive adjective”. Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective. Quick Rule: Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Every student of this class is intelligent. Answer: Distributive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Every” members ko individually/distributively refer karta hai aur noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Distributive adjective”. Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective. Quick Rule: Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. You can buy either shirt. Answer: Distributive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “either” members ko individually/distributively refer karta hai aur noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Distributive adjective”. Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective. Quick Rule: Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Neither car is new. Answer: Distributive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Neither” members ko individually/distributively refer karta hai aur noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Distributive adjective”. Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective. Quick Rule: Each/every/either/neither + noun → distributive adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. This car Answer: Demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “This” noun ke saath used hai, isliye adjective hai; noun absent hota to pronoun hota. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative adjective”. this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. That car Answer: Demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “That” noun ke saath used hai, isliye adjective hai; noun absent hota to pronoun hota. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative adjective”. this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. These cars Answer: Demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “These” noun ke saath used hai, isliye adjective hai; noun absent hota to pronoun hota. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative adjective”. this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. Those cars Answer: Demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Those” noun ke saath used hai, isliye adjective hai; noun absent hota to pronoun hota. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative adjective”. this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: this/that/these/those + noun → demonstrative adjective.","In this sentence, what is the highlighted word? This is my dog. Answer: Demonstrative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “This” ke baad koi noun nahi hai; ye khud noun-phrase ki jagah khada hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative pronoun”. Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun. Quick Rule: Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun.","In this sentence, what is the highlighted word? That is your dog. Answer: Demonstrative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “That” ke baad koi noun nahi hai; ye khud noun-phrase ki jagah khada hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative pronoun”. Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun. Quick Rule: Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun.","In this sentence, what is the highlighted word? These are my shoes. Answer: Demonstrative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “These” ke baad koi noun nahi hai; ye khud noun-phrase ki jagah khada hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative pronoun”. Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun. Quick Rule: Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun.","In this sentence, what is the highlighted word? Those are my shoes. Answer: Demonstrative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “Those” ke baad koi noun nahi hai; ye khud noun-phrase ki jagah khada hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Demonstrative pronoun”. Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun. Quick Rule: Demonstrative word without following noun → pronoun.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Some old man is knocking at the door. Answer: Indefinite demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Some” noun ko non-specific/indefinite sense me point out karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite demonstrative adjective”. some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Any graduate can apply for this post. Answer: Indefinite demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Any” noun ko non-specific/indefinite sense me point out karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite demonstrative adjective”. some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. I met a certain person yesterday. Answer: Indefinite demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “certain” noun ko non-specific/indefinite sense me point out karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite demonstrative adjective”. some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. The other boys live in another flat. Answer: Indefinite demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “other” noun ko non-specific/indefinite sense me point out karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite demonstrative adjective”. some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: some/any/certain/other/another + noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. The other boys live in another flat. Answer: Indefinite demonstrative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “another” ek non-specific additional flat ko indicate karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite demonstrative adjective”. another + singular countable noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective. Quick Rule: another + singular countable noun → indefinite demonstrative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Many persons were killed. Answer: Indefinite numeral adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Many” countable persons/books/people ki indefinite number batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite numeral adjective”. An indefinite number or quantity referring to countable nouns functions as an indefinite numeral adjective. Quick Rule: Countable nouns ki indefinite number → numeral adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. She has few books. Answer: Indefinite numeral adjective Hinglish Explanation: “few” countable persons/books/people ki indefinite number batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite numeral adjective”. An indefinite number or quantity referring to countable nouns functions as an indefinite numeral adjective. Quick Rule: Countable nouns ki indefinite number → numeral adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Most people will like this film. Answer: Indefinite numeral adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Most” countable persons/books/people ki indefinite number batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite numeral adjective”. An indefinite number or quantity referring to countable nouns functions as an indefinite numeral adjective. Quick Rule: Countable nouns ki indefinite number → numeral adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Some books are missing. Answer: Indefinite numeral adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Some” countable persons/books/people ki indefinite number batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Indefinite numeral adjective”. An indefinite number or quantity referring to countable nouns functions as an indefinite numeral adjective. Quick Rule: Countable nouns ki indefinite number → numeral adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. Much money is needed. Answer: Quantitative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Much” uncountable noun ki quantity batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Quantitative adjective”. Uncountable quantity → quantitative adjective. Quick Rule: Uncountable quantity → quantitative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. She has paid little attention to my advice. Answer: Quantitative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “little” uncountable noun ki quantity batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Quantitative adjective”. Uncountable quantity → quantitative adjective. Quick Rule: Uncountable quantity → quantitative adjective.","Identify the highlighted adjective. I saw her with my own eyes. Answer: Emphatic adjective Hinglish Explanation: “own” noun phrase me emphasis add karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic adjective”. own/very before a noun can work as emphatic adjectives. Quick Rule: own/very before a noun can work as emphatic adjectives.","Identify the highlighted adjective. He killed his son before my very eyes. Answer: Emphatic adjective Hinglish Explanation: “very” noun phrase me emphasis add karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic adjective”. own/very before a noun can work as emphatic adjectives. Quick Rule: own/very before a noun can work as emphatic adjectives.","Identify the highlighted word. I myself saw him. Answer: Emphatic pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “myself” noun ke before adjective nahi hai; pronoun ko emphatically repeat karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic pronoun”. -self/-selves used for emphasis → emphatic pronoun. Quick Rule: -self/-selves used for emphasis → emphatic pronoun.","Identify the highlighted word. They themselves are late. Answer: Emphatic pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “themselves” noun ke before adjective nahi hai; pronoun ko emphatically repeat karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Emphatic pronoun”. -self/-selves used for emphasis → emphatic pronoun. Quick Rule: -self/-selves used for emphasis → emphatic pronoun.","Identify the highlighted word. What type of shirt do you want to buy? Answer: Interrogative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” question me following noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adjective”. what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective. Quick Rule: what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. Which movie do you like most? Answer: Interrogative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Which” question me following noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adjective”. what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective. Quick Rule: what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. Whose pen is this? Answer: Interrogative adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Whose” question me following noun ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adjective”. what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective. Quick Rule: what/which/whose + noun in a question → interrogative adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. What a beautiful girl! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” yahan noun phrase ke saath strong emotion express karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + a/an + adjective + noun! → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + a/an + adjective + noun! → exclamatory adjective.","Identify the highlighted word. What a cute baby! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” yahan noun phrase ke saath strong emotion express karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + a/an + adjective + noun! → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + a/an + adjective + noun! → exclamatory adjective."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=3", "t": "Exclamatory Adjective vs Pronoun & Uses of What", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Classify the initial “What!”. What! Have you met our Principal? Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Initial “What!” akela sudden emotion/reaction express karta hai; iske turant baad noun nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source. Quick Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source.","Classify the initial “What!”. What! You haven’t attended the class. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Initial “What!” akela sudden emotion/reaction express karta hai; iske turant baad noun nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source. Quick Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source.","Classify the initial “What!”. What! I don’t believe what she says. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Initial “What!” akela sudden emotion/reaction express karta hai; iske turant baad noun nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source. Quick Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source.","Classify the initial “What!”. What! You have already informed her. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Initial “What!” akela sudden emotion/reaction express karta hai; iske turant baad noun nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source. Quick Rule: Standalone emotional What! → exclamatory pronoun in the source.","Classify “What” in this exclamation. What a strange incident! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” noun phrase ke saath strong emotion intensify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective.","Classify “What” in this exclamation. What a cute baby! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” noun phrase ke saath strong emotion intensify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective.","Classify “What” in this exclamation. What a band of great musicians! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” noun phrase ke saath strong emotion intensify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective.","Classify “What” in this exclamation. What a board of eminent directors! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: “What” noun phrase ke saath strong emotion intensify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory adjective”. What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective. Quick Rule: What + noun phrase in exclamation → exclamatory adjective.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What book do you want to read? Answer: Interrogative adjective Hinglish Explanation: What modifies “book” in a direct question. English Explanation: What modifies “book” in a direct question. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What a big building it is! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: What modifies the noun phrase “a big building” in an exclamation. English Explanation: What modifies the noun phrase “a big building” in an exclamation. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What ! I don’t believe that. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Standalone What! expresses emotion. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What she said made me cry. Answer: Relative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “What she said” means the thing that she said and forms a noun clause. English Explanation: “What she said” means the thing that she said and forms a noun clause. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What is your friend’s name? Answer: Interrogative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What itself asks for information; no noun follows it. English Explanation: What itself asks for information; no noun follows it. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What food have you never eaten but would like to try? Answer: Interrogative adjective Hinglish Explanation: What modifies “food”. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adjective”. Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What a big fool! Answer: Exclamatory adjective Hinglish Explanation: What modifies the noun phrase “a big fool”. English Explanation: What modifies the noun phrase “a big fool”. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. We can’t give you what you need. Answer: Relative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: “what you need” = the thing that you need. English Explanation: “what you need” = the thing that you need. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What do you want for dinner? Answer: Interrogative pronoun Hinglish Explanation: What asks directly for the object wanted. English Explanation: What asks directly for the object wanted. Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion.","Identify the type/function of the highlighted “What”. What ! She has gone there without my permission. Answer: Exclamatory pronoun Hinglish Explanation: Standalone What! shows surprise/disapproval. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory pronoun”. Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion. Quick Rule: Function depends on whether What modifies a noun, asks by itself, introduces “the thing that…”, or expresses emotion."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=4", "t": "Qualitative Adjectives & Degrees of Comparison", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Identify the adjective type. She is a beautiful girl. Answer: Qualitative / descriptive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “beautiful” noun/person/thing ki quality describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Qualitative / descriptive adjective”. Adjective of quality describes what kind. Quick Rule: Adjective of quality describes what kind.","Identify the adjective type. I have a red car. Answer: Qualitative / descriptive adjective Hinglish Explanation: “red” noun/person/thing ki quality describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Qualitative / descriptive adjective”. Adjective of quality describes what kind. Quick Rule: Adjective of quality describes what kind.","Identify the degree of the highlighted adjective. Meerut is a big city. Answer: Positive degree Hinglish Explanation: Koi comparison nahi ho raha; simple quality stated hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Positive degree”. No comparison → positive degree. Quick Rule: No comparison → positive degree.","Identify the degree of the highlighted adjective. Meerut is bigger than Ghaziabad. Answer: Comparative degree Hinglish Explanation: Do cities compare ho rahi hain aur “than” use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Comparative degree”. Two items → comparative degree. Quick Rule: Two items → comparative degree.","Identify the degree of the highlighted adjective. Meerut is the biggest city in West Uttar Pradesh. Answer: Superlative degree Hinglish Explanation: Ek city ko group me highest degree par place kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Superlative degree”. More than two/group comparison → superlative. Quick Rule: More than two/group comparison → superlative."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=5", "t": "Degree of Comparison — Corrections", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. Everyone knows that of the two boys in the college, Dean is the bravest . Answer: braver Hinglish Explanation: Of two → comparative, so “braver”, not superlative “bravest”. English Explanation: Of two → comparative, so “braver”, not superlative “bravest”. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. The path is decorated with more beautiful flowers and ponds. Answer: very beautiful Hinglish Explanation: The sentence is not comparing the flowers with another set; “very beautiful” fits the descriptive meaning. English Explanation: The sentence is not comparing the flowers with another set; “very beautiful” fits the descriptive meaning. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. Literature and poetry are the two most sweetest passions known to man. Answer: sweetest Hinglish Explanation: Double superlative is wrong; use “sweetest”. English Explanation: Double superlative is wrong; use “sweetest”. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. It is very challenging to prepare for this presentation when we have so less time. Answer: little Hinglish Explanation: “time” is uncountable and the intended meaning is small quantity, so “little time”. English Explanation: “time” is uncountable and the intended meaning is small quantity, so “little time”. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. Hermione Granger is the most diligent and wisest witch of her age. Answer: wisest and most diligent Hinglish Explanation: When two adjectives share the same superlative frame, each should carry the correct superlative form without awkward double marking. English Explanation: When two adjectives share the same superlative frame, each should carry the correct superlative form without awkward double marking. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. The Indian Army is good and stronger than the Pakistan Army. Answer: better and stronger than Hinglish Explanation: Parallel comparison needs comparative forms: better and stronger. English Explanation: Parallel comparison needs comparative forms: better and stronger. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison.","Choose the best correction for the highlighted idea. Atal Bihari Vajpayee is the best and the honest prime minister of our time. Answer: the best and most honest Hinglish Explanation: Within the superlative idea, “honest” needs “most honest”. English Explanation: Within the superlative idea, “honest” needs “most honest”. Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison. Quick Rule: Keep the degree consistent with the number of things compared and avoid double comparison."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=6", "t": "Adjective vs Adverb & Linking Verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["What part of speech is the highlighted word? She sings beautifully . Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: “beautifully” verb ki manner/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. An action verb is normally modified by an adverb. Quick Rule: Action verb ko qualify karne ke liye normally adverb.","What part of speech is the highlighted word? They fought bravely . Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: “bravely” verb ki manner/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. An action verb is normally modified by an adverb. Quick Rule: Action verb ko qualify karne ke liye normally adverb.","What part of speech is the highlighted word? She cried loudly . Answer: Adverb Hinglish Explanation: “loudly” verb ki manner/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb”. An action verb is normally modified by an adverb. Quick Rule: Action verb ko qualify karne ke liye normally adverb.","Choose the correct form/function of the highlighted word. She grew impatient . Answer: Adjective after a linking/copular verb Hinglish Explanation: “impatient” action ko modify nahi karta; subject/object ki resulting state/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Adjective after a linking/copular verb”. Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements. Quick Rule: Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements.","Choose the correct form/function of the highlighted word. She made me happy . Answer: Adjective after a linking/copular verb Hinglish Explanation: “happy” action ko modify nahi karta; subject/object ki resulting state/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Adjective after a linking/copular verb”. Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements. Quick Rule: Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements.","Choose the correct form/function of the highlighted word. She looks beautiful . Answer: Adjective after a linking/copular verb Hinglish Explanation: “beautiful” action ko modify nahi karta; subject/object ki resulting state/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Adjective after a linking/copular verb”. Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements. Quick Rule: Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements.","Choose the correct form/function of the highlighted word. He seems lucky . Answer: Adjective after a linking/copular verb Hinglish Explanation: “lucky” action ko modify nahi karta; subject/object ki resulting state/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Adjective after a linking/copular verb”. Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements. Quick Rule: Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements.","Choose the correct form/function of the highlighted word. Sugar tastes sweet . Answer: Adjective after a linking/copular verb Hinglish Explanation: “sweet” action ko modify nahi karta; subject/object ki resulting state/quality batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Adjective after a linking/copular verb”. Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements. Quick Rule: Linking/result verbs can take adjective complements.","Why is an adverb correct here? She looked angrily at her husband. Answer: The verb describes a deliberate action/manner Hinglish Explanation: Yahan verb deliberate action hai; “angrily” us action ka manner batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The verb describes a deliberate action/manner”. look/taste/smell etc. action sense me ho to adverb possible. Quick Rule: look/taste/smell etc. action sense me ho to adverb possible.","Why is an adverb correct here? I tasted the soup suspiciously . Answer: The verb describes a deliberate action/manner Hinglish Explanation: Yahan verb deliberate action hai; “suspiciously” us action ka manner batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The verb describes a deliberate action/manner”. look/taste/smell etc. action sense me ho to adverb possible. Quick Rule: look/taste/smell etc. action sense me ho to adverb possible."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=7", "t": "Latin Comparative & Absolute Adjectives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Correct the sentence. Tanu is two years more senior than me in college. Answer: Tanu is two years senior to me in college. Hinglish Explanation: “senior” already comparative in meaning hai; iske saath “more” nahi aur “than” ki jagah “to” use hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Tanu is two years senior to me in college.”. senior/junior/inferior/superior/prior/preferable + to. Quick Rule: senior/junior/inferior/superior/prior/preferable + to.","Correct the sentence. Coffee is more preferable than tea. Answer: Coffee is preferable to tea. Hinglish Explanation: “preferable” ko more/than ke saath double comparison nahi karte. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Coffee is preferable to tea.”. preferable + to. Quick Rule: preferable + to.","Correct the highlighted comparison. As a playwright William Shakespeare is superior than any other sixteenth-century playwright. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: “superior” Latin comparative adjective hai, isliye “to” leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to”. superior to, not superior than. Quick Rule: superior to, not superior than.","Identify the highlighted word’s use in this sentence. He is a senior player. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: Context me “senior” adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. Words such as senior, junior, and inferior can function as adjectives; senior and junior can also function as nouns. Quick Rule: senior/junior/inferior etc. adjective bhi ho sakte hain; senior/junior nouns bhi ho sakte hain.","Identify the highlighted word’s use in this sentence. He sells goods of inferior quality. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: Context me “inferior” adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. Words such as senior, junior, and inferior can function as adjectives; senior and junior can also function as nouns. Quick Rule: senior/junior/inferior etc. adjective bhi ho sakte hain; senior/junior nouns bhi ho sakte hain.","Identify the highlighted word’s use in this sentence. Rahul is my senior . Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: Context me “senior” noun ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. Words such as senior, junior, and inferior can function as adjectives; senior and junior can also function as nouns. Quick Rule: senior/junior/inferior etc. adjective bhi ho sakte hain; senior/junior nouns bhi ho sakte hain.","Identify the highlighted word’s use in this sentence. They are my juniors . Answer: Noun Hinglish Explanation: Context me “juniors” noun ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Noun”. Words such as senior, junior, and inferior can function as adjectives; senior and junior can also function as nouns. Quick Rule: senior/junior/inferior etc. adjective bhi ho sakte hain; senior/junior nouns bhi ho sakte hain.","Choose the standard correction. This is the most unique animal that I have ever seen. Answer: unique Hinglish Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. English Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar. Quick Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar.","Choose the standard correction. Romanoff was the chiefest of all spies. Answer: chief Hinglish Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. English Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar. Quick Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar.","Choose the standard correction. The Constitution of India is rightly said to be the most supreme power of the country. Answer: supreme Hinglish Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. English Explanation: The highlighted adjective is treated as absolute/non-gradable in this exam rule, so comparative/superlative intensification is removed. Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar. Quick Rule: Absolute adjectives normally do not take more/most/-er/-est in traditional exam grammar."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=8", "t": "As…as / So…as & “Too / Enough”", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the correct form. Dean is as good as Jess. Answer: Correct as written Hinglish Explanation: Affirmative equality uses as + positive adjective + as. English Explanation: Affirmative equality uses as + positive adjective + as. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. Dean is not as better as Jess. Answer: not as good as Hinglish Explanation: After as/so use positive degree, not comparative “better”. English Explanation: After as/so use positive degree, not comparative “better”. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. Dean is not so good as Jess. Answer: Correct as written Hinglish Explanation: Negative comparison can use so + positive + as. English Explanation: Negative comparison can use so + positive + as. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. That last offer was so high as we could unreasonably go. Answer: as high as Hinglish Explanation: Affirmative equality/extent uses as…as. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “as high as”. as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. These days, job opportunities are not as better as they used to be. Answer: as good as Hinglish Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “good”. English Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “good”. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. Then we went back to the woods again as faster as he could. Answer: as fast as Hinglish Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “fast”. English Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “fast”. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. It was not as tastier as the one cooked by my mother. Answer: as tasty as Hinglish Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “tasty”. English Explanation: After as…as, use positive degree “tasty”. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Choose the correct form. These days relationships are not as more patriarchal as they used to be. Answer: as patriarchal as Hinglish Explanation: Do not use “more” inside as…as construction. English Explanation: Do not use “more” inside as…as construction. Rule: as/so + positive degree + as. Quick Rule: as/so + positive degree + as.","Which degree should follow “too” here? He is too weak to walk. Answer: Positive degree Hinglish Explanation: “too” ke baad basic adjective form aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Positive degree”. too + positive adjective + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: too + positive adjective + to-infinitive.","Which degree should follow “too” here? He is too lazy to do this type of work. Answer: Positive degree Hinglish Explanation: “too” ke baad basic adjective form aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Positive degree”. too + positive adjective + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: too + positive adjective + to-infinitive.","Correct the highlighted word. She is too busiest to go with you. Answer: busy Hinglish Explanation: “too” ke baad positive degree chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “busy”. too + positive degree. Quick Rule: too + positive degree.","Correct the highlighted word. He is too cleverest to be cheated. Answer: clever Hinglish Explanation: “too” ke baad positive degree chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “clever”. too + positive degree. Quick Rule: too + positive degree.","Identify the correct placement pattern of “enough”. I can’t hear the music. It’s not loud enough . Answer: adjective + enough Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern here is adjective + enough . English Explanation: Source pattern here is adjective + enough. Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns. Quick Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns.","Identify the correct placement pattern of “enough”. The room is large enough for all the students. Answer: adjective + enough Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern here is adjective + enough . English Explanation: Source pattern here is adjective + enough. Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns. Quick Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns.","Identify the correct placement pattern of “enough”. He has enough money to buy a new car. Answer: enough + noun Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern here is enough + noun . English Explanation: Source pattern here is enough + noun. Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns. Quick Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns.","Identify the correct placement pattern of “enough”. They forgot to buy enough food for the camping trip. Answer: enough + noun Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern here is enough + noun . English Explanation: Source pattern here is enough + noun. Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns. Quick Rule: Enough comes after adjectives but before nouns.","Correct the highlighted phrase. Are you older enough to vote? Answer: old enough Hinglish Explanation: Enough ke pehle positive adjective “old” aata hai, comparative “older” nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “old enough”. positive adjective + enough. Quick Rule: positive adjective + enough.","Correct the highlighted phrase. The exercise was easier enough for them to complete. Answer: easy enough Hinglish Explanation: Enough ke saath positive adjective “easy” use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “easy enough”. positive adjective + enough. Quick Rule: positive adjective + enough.","Choose the source-rule correction. The weather is relatively better today. Answer: relatively good Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam rule here uses relatively/comparatively + positive degree. English Explanation: Traditional exam rule here uses relatively/comparatively + positive degree. Rule: comparatively/relatively + positive degree (source rule). Quick Rule: comparatively/relatively + positive degree (source rule).","Choose the source-rule correction. The technology is still comparatively newer . Answer: comparatively new Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam rule here uses relatively/comparatively + positive degree. English Explanation: Traditional exam rule here uses relatively/comparatively + positive degree. Rule: comparatively/relatively + positive degree (source rule). Quick Rule: comparatively/relatively + positive degree (source rule).","Choose the better correction. The weather of Delhi is comparatively better than that of Chennai. Answer: better than Hinglish Explanation: Because an explicit “than” comparison is already present, simple comparative “better than” is sufficient. English Explanation: Because an explicit “than” comparison is already present, simple comparative “better than” is sufficient. Rule: Avoid redundant comparative markers. Quick Rule: Avoid redundant comparative markers."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=9", "t": "“Other” in Comparison & Parallel Comparison", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the correct expression. Rahul is as smart as any boy in the class. Answer: any other boy Hinglish Explanation: Rahul khud class ke boys me included hai, so self-comparison avoid karne ke liye “other”. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “any other boy”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. Rahul is smarter than any boy in the class. Answer: any other boy Hinglish Explanation: Same class/group me comparison ho raha hai; “any other boy” chahiye. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “any other boy”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. Rahul is as smart as any other girl in the class. Answer: any girl Hinglish Explanation: Rahul girl-group ka member nahi hai, so “other” unnecessary. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “any girl”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. Nile is longer than any river in the world. Answer: any other river Hinglish Explanation: Nile world ke rivers me included hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “any other river”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. Nile is longer than any other river in India. Answer: any river Hinglish Explanation: Nile India ka river nahi hai; outside-group comparison me “other” nahi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “any river”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. No president in India is as famous as Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam. Answer: No other president Hinglish Explanation: Kalam khud Indian presidents ke group me included hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “No other president”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Choose the correct expression. No other president in the USA is as famous as Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam. Answer: No president Hinglish Explanation: Kalam USA presidents ke group ka member nahi hai; “other” remove. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “No president”. Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group. Quick Rule: Use “other” only when the compared person/thing belongs to the same group.","Correct the parallel-comparison structure. The higher we go, cooler we feel. Answer: the cooler Hinglish Explanation: Parallel increase/decrease takes “the + comparative …, the + comparative …” and normal clause order. English Explanation: Parallel increase/decrease takes “the + comparative …, the + comparative …” and normal clause order. Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative…. Quick Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative….","Correct the parallel-comparison structure. The kinder I get, the braver am I . Answer: the braver I am Hinglish Explanation: Parallel increase/decrease takes “the + comparative …, the + comparative …” and normal clause order. English Explanation: Parallel increase/decrease takes “the + comparative …, the + comparative …” and normal clause order. Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative…. Quick Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative….","Correct the highlighted phrase. The more books you read, most ideas you have. Answer: the more ideas Hinglish Explanation: Parallel pattern requires “the more …, the more …”. English Explanation: Parallel pattern requires “the more …, the more …”. Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative…. Quick Rule: The + comparative…, the + comparative….","Correct the highlighted clause. The more leisure time she has, the happier is she . Answer: she is Hinglish Explanation: After the comparative phrase, normal declarative order subject + verb is used. English Explanation: After the comparative phrase, normal declarative order subject + verb is used. Rule: The happier she is, not inversion. Quick Rule: The happier she is, not inversion.","Correct the highlighted ending. The sooner you do, better is it . Answer: the better it is Hinglish Explanation: Second half also needs “the + comparative” and normal word order. English Explanation: Second half also needs “the + comparative” and normal word order. Rule: The sooner…, the better…. Quick Rule: The sooner…, the better…."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=10", "t": "Superlatives, Faulty Comparison & Repetition", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the correct sentence. Gautam is the laziest of all his other brothers . Answer: brothers Hinglish Explanation: Superlative “of all” already includes the full group; “other” is unnecessary. English Explanation: Superlative “of all” already includes the full group; “other” is unnecessary. Rule: Superlative + of all + plural noun; avoid redundant “other”. Quick Rule: Superlative + of all + plural noun; avoid redundant “other”.","Choose the correct comparison wording. No medicine is as effective as this medicine to cure your illness. Answer: No other medicine Hinglish Explanation: The compared item belongs to the same group, so “other” is needed. English Explanation: The compared item belongs to the same group, so “other” is needed. Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it. Quick Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it.","Choose the correct comparison wording. No students in this school is as intelligent and brave as Ginny. Answer: No other student Hinglish Explanation: The compared item belongs to the same group, so “other” is needed. English Explanation: The compared item belongs to the same group, so “other” is needed. Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it. Quick Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it.","Choose the correct comparison wording. Of all the other people I hate, he is the rudest and most irritating. Answer: people Hinglish Explanation: “Of all” already defines the group; “other” is redundant. English Explanation: “Of all” already defines the group; “other” is redundant. Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it. Quick Rule: Same-group logic decides “other”; superlative “of all” normally does not need it.","Choose the correct comparative form. Gold is more precious than any metal . Answer: more precious than any other metal Hinglish Explanation: Gold itself is a metal, so compare it with “any other metal”; comparative “more precious than”. English Explanation: Gold itself is a metal, so compare it with “any other metal”; comparative “more precious than”. Rule: Comparative + than + any other + singular noun. Quick Rule: Comparative + than + any other + singular noun.","Choose the correct superlative wording. Humans are the most intelligent of all other species in the world. Answer: the most intelligent of all species Hinglish Explanation: With superlative “of all”, “other” is redundant. English Explanation: With superlative “of all”, “other” is redundant. Rule: the most + adjective + of all + plural noun. Quick Rule: the most + adjective + of all + plural noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. The railway station of Jaipur is cleaner than Indore . Answer: that of Indore Hinglish Explanation: Station ko city se compare nahi kar sakte; “that” repeats singular noun “station”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “that of Indore”. Singular repeated noun → that of; plural repeated noun → those of. Quick Rule: Singular repeated noun → that of; plural repeated noun → those of.","Correct the faulty comparison. The students of Mukherjee Nagar are smarter than Rajendra Nagar . Answer: those of Rajendra Nagar Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. The weather of Bengaluru is better than Delhi . Answer: that of Delhi Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. The roads of Chandigarh are cleaner than Jaipur . Answer: those of Jaipur Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. She wished that her writing could be as effective as J.K. Rowling . Answer: that of J.K. Rowling Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. The mass of its tiny body is far lower than a human . Answer: that of a human Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is singular, so “that of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Correct the faulty comparison. The opinions of politicians are no more relevant than that of citizens . Answer: those of citizens Hinglish Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. English Explanation: The repeated noun is plural, so “those of” is required. Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun. Quick Rule: Compare like with like; use that/those to stand for the repeated noun.","Choose the grammatically matching comparison. Despite the injury, her reflexes are as fast as a normal participant . Answer: are as fast as those of a normal participant Hinglish Explanation: Plural “reflexes” must be compared with plural reflexes of another participant → “those of”. English Explanation: Plural “reflexes” must be compared with plural reflexes of another participant → “those of”. Rule: Plural repeated noun → those of. Quick Rule: Plural repeated noun → those of.","Correct the highlighted comparison. The defense forces of India are better trained than that of Pakistan . Answer: are better trained than those of Pakistan Hinglish Explanation: “forces” plural hai, so repeated plural noun ko “those” se replace karte hain. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “are better trained than those of Pakistan”. Plural repeated noun → those of. Quick Rule: Plural repeated noun → those of."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=11", "t": "Comparing Two Qualities & Special Comparative Structures", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["When comparing two qualities of the same person, choose the source-rule form. Dean is smarter than brave . Answer: more smart than brave Hinglish Explanation: Do qualities ek hi person ki compare ho rahi hain, so source uses “more + positive + than + positive”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “more smart than brave”. Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective. Quick Rule: Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective.","When comparing two qualities of the same person, choose the source-rule form. Tanu is wiser than beautiful . Answer: more wise than beautiful Hinglish Explanation: Do qualities ek hi person ki compare ho rahi hain, so source uses “more + positive + than + positive”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “more wise than beautiful”. Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective. Quick Rule: Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective.","When comparing two qualities of the same person, choose the source-rule form. Rahul is taller than handsome . Answer: more tall than handsome Hinglish Explanation: Do qualities ek hi person ki compare ho rahi hain, so source uses “more + positive + than + positive”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “more tall than handsome”. Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective. Quick Rule: Two qualities of same person → more + positive adjective + than + positive adjective.","Identify the correct comparative structure. This material is better than that material. Answer: better than Hinglish Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. English Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two. Quick Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two.","Identify the correct comparative structure. This material is the better of the two materials. Answer: the better of the two Hinglish Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. English Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two. Quick Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two.","Identify the correct comparative structure. This player is better than that player. Answer: better than Hinglish Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. English Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two. Quick Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two.","Identify the correct comparative structure. This player is the better of the two players. Answer: the better of the two Hinglish Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. English Explanation: The sentence uses the correct comparative pattern for exactly two items. Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two. Quick Rule: Compare two → comparative; selection of one from two → the + comparative + of the two.","Correct the question. Of Delhi and Mumbai, which is coolest ? Answer: the cooler Hinglish Explanation: Only two cities are involved, so use “the + comparative”. English Explanation: Only two cities are involved, so use “the + comparative”. Rule: Selection from two → the + comparative. Quick Rule: Selection from two → the + comparative.","Correct the highlighted word. Of the two films in which he starred, I think the former one is the best . Answer: better Hinglish Explanation: Only two films are compared, so comparative “better”. English Explanation: Only two films are compared, so comparative “better”. Rule: Two items → comparative, not superlative. Quick Rule: Two items → comparative, not superlative.","Correct the highlighted phrase. Of the two boys in the college, Dean is braver . Answer: the braver Hinglish Explanation: Selecting/comparing within two requires “the braver”. English Explanation: Selecting/comparing within two requires “the braver”. Rule: Of the two + the comparative. Quick Rule: Of the two + the comparative.","Choose the correction that completes both comparison frames. She is as beautiful, if not more beautiful than you. Answer: as beautiful as, if not Hinglish Explanation: Both halves need their own required comparison marker. English Explanation: Both halves need their own required comparison marker. Rule: Keep each comparison frame complete: as…as; more…than. Quick Rule: Keep each comparison frame complete: as…as; more…than.","Choose the correction that completes both comparison frames. Rahul is more honest and as hard working as you. Answer: more honest than and Hinglish Explanation: Both halves need their own required comparison marker. English Explanation: Both halves need their own required comparison marker. Rule: Keep each comparison frame complete: as…as; more…than. Quick Rule: Keep each comparison frame complete: as…as; more…than.","What does the highlighted structure express? She is getting fatter and fatter . Answer: Gradual increase/change Hinglish Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. English Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change. Quick Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change.","What does the highlighted structure express? He is becoming richer and richer . Answer: Gradual increase/change Hinglish Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. English Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change. Quick Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change.","What does the highlighted structure express? He is becoming more and more ambitious . Answer: Gradual increase/change Hinglish Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. English Explanation: Repeated comparative form shows progressive/gradual development. Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change. Quick Rule: Comparative + and + comparative → gradual change."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=12", "t": "Equal Comparison & Superlative Prepositions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the correct equality expression. The world’s biggest bull is equally as big as a small elephant. Answer: as big as Hinglish Explanation: Source rule: “equally” aur “as…as” ko ek saath stack nahi karna; ya “as…as” use karo ya “equally + adjective”. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “as big as”. Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as. Quick Rule: Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as.","Choose the correct equality expression. You are equally as good as your brother. Answer: as good as Hinglish Explanation: Source rule: “equally” aur “as…as” ko ek saath stack nahi karna; ya “as…as” use karo ya “equally + adjective”. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “as good as”. Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as. Quick Rule: Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as.","Choose the correct equality expression. You and your brother are equally good . Answer: equally good Hinglish Explanation: Source rule: “equally” aur “as…as” ko ek saath stack nahi karna; ya “as…as” use karo ya “equally + adjective”. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “equally good”. Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as. Quick Rule: Use either as…as or equally + adjective, not equally as…as.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. He is the shortest of all the brothers . Answer: of + plural group Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. Iron is the most useful of all metals . Answer: of + plural group Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. He is the shortest person in his family . Answer: in + group/place Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. She is the fastest player in the team . Answer: in + group/place Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. Burj Khalifa is the tallest building in the world . Answer: in + group/place Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place.","Identify the superlative preposition pattern. Mariana Trench is the deepest point in the ocean . Answer: in + group/place Hinglish Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. English Explanation: The preposition matches the superlative pattern shown in the source. Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place. Quick Rule: Superlative + of + plural noun; superlative + in + group/place."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=13", "t": "Predicative-only Adjectives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Which sentence is standard? She had been asleep for over three hours. Answer: She had been asleep for over three hours. Hinglish Explanation: “asleep” normally appears predicatively after a linking verb. English Explanation: “asleep” normally appears predicatively after a linking verb. Rule: asleep/afraid/alive/alike etc. are usually predicative, not before a noun. Quick Rule: asleep/afraid/alive/alike etc. are usually predicative, not before a noun.","Choose the correct noun phrase. Do not disturb an asleep baby . Answer: a sleeping baby Hinglish Explanation: “asleep” is not normally attributive; use “sleeping” before a noun. English Explanation: “asleep” is not normally attributive; use “sleeping” before a noun. Rule: Predicative-only adjective → use an attributive alternative before noun. Quick Rule: Predicative-only adjective → use an attributive alternative before noun.","Choose the correct use of “afloat”. The ship is still afloat. Answer: The ship is still afloat. Hinglish Explanation: “afloat” is used predicatively after “is”. English Explanation: “afloat” is used predicatively after “is”. Rule: afloat is normally predicative. Quick Rule: afloat is normally predicative.","Choose the natural attributive alternative. An afloat leaf Answer: a floating leaf Hinglish Explanation: Before a noun, use “floating”, not predicative-only “afloat”. English Explanation: Before a noun, use “floating”, not predicative-only “afloat”. Rule: Use a normal attributive participle/adjective before noun. Quick Rule: Use a normal attributive participle/adjective before noun.","Choose the correct use of “afraid”. He was afraid. Answer: He was afraid. Hinglish Explanation: “afraid” naturally follows a linking verb. English Explanation: “afraid” naturally follows a linking verb. Rule: afraid is normally predicative. Quick Rule: afraid is normally predicative.","Choose the natural noun phrase. John is an afraid man . Answer: a frightened man Hinglish Explanation: Use “frightened” before the noun “man”. English Explanation: Use “frightened” before the noun “man”. Rule: Predicative afraid; attributive frightened/scared. Quick Rule: Predicative afraid; attributive frightened/scared."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=14", "t": "Good / Well; Bad / Ill", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["What is the highlighted word? She is a good player. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “good” noun ki quality batata hai, so adjective. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. good = adjective. Quick Rule: good = adjective.","What is the highlighted word? She speaks good English. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “good” noun ki quality batata hai, so adjective. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. good = adjective. Quick Rule: good = adjective.","Choose the source-preferred response about health. How are you? — I am good . Answer: I am well. Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam usage health/condition me “well” prefer karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I am well.”. well can be adjective meaning healthy. Quick Rule: well can be adjective meaning healthy.","Correct the sentence. She speaks English good . Answer: well Hinglish Explanation: Verb “speaks” ki manner batani hai, so adverb “well”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “well”. well = adverb meaning in a good manner. Quick Rule: well = adverb meaning in a good manner.","Choose the correct word. The quality of apples is good / well . Answer: good Hinglish Explanation: “quality” ko adjective “good” describe karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “good”. Quality of thing → good (adjective). Quick Rule: Quality of thing → good (adjective).","Choose the source-preferred word for physical condition. I am not feeling good / well today. Answer: well Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam convention physical health me “well”. English Explanation: Traditional exam convention physical health me “well”. Rule: well can describe health condition. Quick Rule: well can describe health condition.","Correct the highlighted word. The Indian U-19 team played pretty good in the World Cup. Answer: well Hinglish Explanation: “played” verb ko modify karna hai, so adverb “well”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “well”. Action verb + well (adverb). Quick Rule: Action verb + well (adverb).","Correct the highlighted phrase. Rosy is a well student even if she didn’t get the call back from Harvard. Answer: good student Hinglish Explanation: Noun “student” se pehle quality adjective “good” chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “good student”. good + noun; well normally adverb or health adjective. Quick Rule: good + noun; well normally adverb or health adjective.","Identify the highlighted use. He is a bad man. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “bad” context me source ke according adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. It is bad to steal. Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “bad” context me source ke according adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. The quality of this product is bad . Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “bad” context me source ke according adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. His pronunciation is bad . Answer: Adjective Hinglish Explanation: “bad” context me source ke according adjective ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. My friend is ill . Answer: Adjective (condition) Hinglish Explanation: “ill” context me source ke according adjective (condition) ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective (condition)”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. She is ill with fever. Answer: Adjective (condition) Hinglish Explanation: “ill” context me source ke according adjective (condition) ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective (condition)”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. an ill -informed person Answer: Adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly/insufficiently Hinglish Explanation: “ill” context me source ke according adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly/insufficiently ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly/insufficiently”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds.","Identify the highlighted use. an ill -treated community Answer: Adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly Hinglish Explanation: “ill” context me source ke according adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly ki tarah use hua hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverbial prefix/modifier meaning badly”. bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds. Quick Rule: bad is an adjective; ill can mean sick as adjective or badly/insufficiently in compounds."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=15", "t": "First/Foremost, Former/Latter, Elder/Older, Farther/Further", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Choose the intended correction. Indira Gandhi was the first prime minister of India. Answer: foremost Hinglish Explanation: Historically she was not India’s first PM; source changes the sense to “foremost” = prominent/notable. English Explanation: Historically she was not India’s first PM; source changes the sense to “foremost” = prominent/notable. Rule: first = order; foremost = most prominent. Quick Rule: first = order; foremost = most prominent.","Choose the intended correction. Gandhiji was the first leader of his time. Answer: foremost Hinglish Explanation: The intended meaning is prominent/notable, not ordinal order. English Explanation: The intended meaning is prominent/notable, not ordinal order. Rule: foremost = prominent/notable. Quick Rule: foremost = prominent/notable.","Interpret the highlighted word. Rahul and Sahil are intelligent but the former is more intelligent than the latter. Answer: the first of two Hinglish Explanation: “former” ka reference sentence ke number of items par depend karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “the first of two”. former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two. Quick Rule: former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two.","Interpret the highlighted word. Rahul and Sahil are intelligent but the former is more intelligent than the latter . Answer: the second of two Hinglish Explanation: “latter” ka reference sentence ke number of items par depend karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “the second of two”. former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two. Quick Rule: former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two.","Interpret the highlighted word. Rahul, Sahil and Karan are intelligent but the first is more intelligent than the last. Answer: first among more than two Hinglish Explanation: “first” ka reference sentence ke number of items par depend karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “first among more than two”. former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two. Quick Rule: former/latter → only two; first/last can be used for more than two.","Correct the highlighted word. The meals arrived latter than we expected. Answer: later Hinglish Explanation: Time comparison me “later”; “latter” means second of two. English Explanation: Time comparison me “later”; “latter” means second of two. Rule: later = after in time; latter = second of two. Quick Rule: later = after in time; latter = second of two.","Correct the highlighted word. We will discuss it latter when we reach home. Answer: later Hinglish Explanation: Future time reference me “later”. English Explanation: Future time reference me “later”. Rule: later = afterwards. Quick Rule: later = afterwards.","Choose the correct comparison for two books. I like the latter most . Answer: latter more Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two books hain, so comparative “more”, and “latter” = second of two. English Explanation: The correct answer is “latter more”. Two items → former/latter and comparative. Quick Rule: Two items → former/latter and comparative.","Correct the highlighted expression. The Hindu and The Indian Express are two well-written newspapers, but I prefer the last one . Answer: latter one Hinglish Explanation: Exactly two newspapers are named, so “latter” = second of two. English Explanation: Exactly two newspapers are named, so “latter” = second of two. Rule: former/latter for two items. Quick Rule: former/latter for two items.","Choose the correct sentence for two movies. Of the two Marvel movies, I think the first one is the best . Answer: the former one is the better Hinglish Explanation: For two items use former/latter and comparative “better”, not superlative “best”. English Explanation: For two items use former/latter and comparative “better”, not superlative “best”. Rule: Two items → former/latter + comparative. Quick Rule: Two items → former/latter + comparative.","Choose the correct usage description. He is my elder brother. Answer: blood relation / family seniority Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. She is your elder sister. Answer: blood relation / family seniority Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. My eldest son lives in London. Answer: oldest among family members Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. Rahul is older than Sahil. Answer: general age comparison Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. Mr. Sharma is the oldest man in this village. Answer: general superlative age Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. This school is older than that. Answer: age of things/institutions Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","Choose the correct usage description. This is the oldest temple in this town. Answer: age of things/institutions Hinglish Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. English Explanation: The sentence illustrates the source distinction between elder/eldest and older/oldest. Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than. Quick Rule: elder/eldest mainly family relations; older/oldest for general persons/things and with than.","What meaning does the highlighted word express? Let us walk a little farther . Answer: physical distance Hinglish Explanation: “farther” yahan physical distance ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “physical distance”. farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction). Quick Rule: farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction).","What meaning does the highlighted word express? The fog is so thick, I can’t see farther than about ten metres. Answer: physical distance Hinglish Explanation: “farther” yahan physical distance ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “physical distance”. farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction). Quick Rule: farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction).","What meaning does the highlighted word express? She should not be given further punishment. Answer: additional / extra Hinglish Explanation: “further” yahan additional / extra ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “additional / extra”. farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction). Quick Rule: farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction).","What meaning does the highlighted word express? If you have any further problems, let me know. Answer: additional / extra Hinglish Explanation: “further” yahan additional / extra ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “additional / extra”. farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction). Quick Rule: farther → distance; further → additional/extra (traditional distinction)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=06.+Adjectives&section=16", "t": "Numeral Adjective Order", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 06. Adjectives", "x": ["Correct the highlighted phrase. The two first chapters of this book have a slow pace. Answer: first two chapters Hinglish Explanation: Ordinal adjective normally cardinal se pehle aata hai: first two. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “first two chapters”. Ordinal + cardinal + noun. Quick Rule: Ordinal + cardinal + noun.","Correct the highlighted phrase. The only way to win the race is to run faster than anybody else because last to cross the red line will be out of the race. Answer: the last to cross Hinglish Explanation: Superlative/ordinal-like “last” before a specific role takes “the”. English Explanation: Superlative/ordinal-like “last” before a specific role takes “the”. Rule: the + last + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: the + last + to-infinitive.","Correct the highlighted phrase. The two last parts of the Harry Potter series are emotionally exhausting. Answer: last two parts Hinglish Explanation: Order adjective “last” comes before cardinal “two”. English Explanation: Order adjective “last” comes before cardinal “two”. Rule: Ordinal/order word + cardinal + noun. Quick Rule: Ordinal/order word + cardinal + noun.","Choose the correct full clause. The last two parts of the Harry Potter series is emotionally exhausting. Answer: last two parts of the Harry Potter series are Hinglish Explanation: Plural subject “parts” needs “are”, and order is “last two”. English Explanation: Plural subject “parts” needs “are”, and order is “last two”. Rule: Ordinal/order word before cardinal; plural subject → plural verb. Quick Rule: Ordinal/order word before cardinal; plural subject → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted phrase. Securing first position did not give her that much pleasure as seeing that proud face of her parents did. Answer: the first position Hinglish Explanation: A specific ordinal position normally takes the definite article. English Explanation: A specific ordinal position normally takes the definite article. Rule: the + ordinal number + noun. Quick Rule: the + ordinal number + noun."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=1", "t": "Core Agreement & Compound Subjects", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. A dog is barking. Answer: Singular subject Hinglish Explanation: “dog” ek singular noun hai aur subject ke roop me use hua hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Singular subject”. A singular subject generally takes a singular verb. Quick Rule: Singular subject generally singular verb leta hai.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. We shall go to the party. Answer: Plural personal-pronoun subject Hinglish Explanation: “We” first-person plural subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Plural personal-pronoun subject”. I is treated separately; we/you/they take plural-type verb forms. Quick Rule: I is treated separately; we/you/they take plural-type verb forms.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. I ___ a student. Answer: am Hinglish Explanation: First-person singular “I” present tense me “am” leta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “am”. I + am. Quick Rule: I + am.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. I ___ there yesterday. Answer: was Hinglish Explanation: Past tense me “I” ke saath “was” aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was”. I + was in standard singular past agreement. Quick Rule: I + was in standard singular past agreement.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. I ___ completed the work. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “I” ke saath present perfect auxiliary “have” hota hai, “has” nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. I/you/we/they + have; he/she/it + has. Quick Rule: I/you/we/they + have; he/she/it + has.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. I ___ my work daily. Answer: do Hinglish Explanation: Simple present auxiliary/verb me “I do” hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “do”. I + base verb/do. Quick Rule: I + base verb/do.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. I ___ letters every week. Answer: write Hinglish Explanation: Simple present me “I” base form “write” leta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “write”. I + V1, not V1+s/es. Quick Rule: I + V1, not V1+s/es.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Pooja ___ a strong woman. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Pooja singular proper noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Singular noun + singular verb. Quick Rule: Singular noun + singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. He ___ to the market daily. Answer: goes Hinglish Explanation: Third-person singular subject “he” ke saath simple present me -s/-es lagta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “goes”. He/she/it + V1+s/es. Quick Rule: He/she/it + V1+s/es.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Aman and Karan ___ done well in exams. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Do alag persons “Aman and Karan” plural subject banate hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. A + and + B (different persons) → plural verb. Quick Rule: A + and + B (different persons) → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Sonali and Neha ___ sisters. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do alag singular nouns “and” se jude hain, isliye plural verb. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Two distinct subjects joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two distinct subjects joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. You and he ___ won the match. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “You and he” compound plural subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. Two different persons joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two different persons joined by and → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. The politician and his son wants to talk to you about elections. Answer: want Hinglish Explanation: “The politician and his son” do persons hain, isliye plural verb “want”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “want”. Distinct nouns joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Distinct nouns joined by and → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. He and she was allowed to sit there. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: “He and she” plural subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “were”. Compound subject with and → were/plural verb. Quick Rule: Compound subject with and → were/plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Rice and curry ___ my favourite dish. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “Rice and curry” yahan ek combined dish/unit ke sense me hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Two nouns denoting one idea/dish may take a singular verb. Quick Rule: Two nouns denoting one idea/dish may take a singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Bread and butter ___ a healthy breakfast. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Bread and butter ek combined meal idea hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. One combined idea/pair → singular verb. Quick Rule: One combined idea/pair → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Bread and butter ___ sold here. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Yahan bread aur butter alag-alag items ke roop me sold hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. If the coordinated nouns denote separate items, use plural agreement. Quick Rule: If the coordinated nouns denote separate items, use plural agreement.","Correct the highlighted part. Bread and butter is made from wheat and milk respectively. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: “respectively” dikhata hai ki bread aur butter alag cheezen hain. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “are”. Separate referents + respectively → plural verb. Quick Rule: Separate referents + respectively → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Rice and curry is ordered from Taj and Radisson respectively for this party. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Different sources with “respectively” make the nouns separate referents. English Explanation: Different sources with “respectively” make the nouns separate referents. Rule: Separate items joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Separate items joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The principal and director ___ donated some money for the charity. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Ek hi article “the” common hai; source isse same person/title-pair treat karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. One article before two titles of the same person → singular verb. Quick Rule: One article before two titles of the same person → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The principal and the director ___ donated some money for the charity. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Repeat hua article do different persons dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. Repeated article before coordinated titles → plural subject. Quick Rule: Repeated article before coordinated titles → plural subject.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. My friend and brother ___ very kind. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Ek hi possessive adjective common hai; same person ka sense ban sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. One common possessive before two roles of same person → singular verb. Quick Rule: One common possessive before two roles of same person → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. My friend and my brother ___ very kind. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Possessive adjective repeat hua hai, so two different persons. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Repeated possessive before coordinated nouns → plural verb. Quick Rule: Repeated possessive before coordinated nouns → plural verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=2", "t": "Many / Much / A Good Many / Amount & Number", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Many students ___ qualified the exam. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “Many + plural countable noun” plural subject hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. many + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: many + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A good many students ___ qualified the exam. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “A good many” ke baad plural countable noun aur plural verb aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. a good many + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: a good many + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A great many students ___ qualified the exam. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “A great many students” plural expression hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. a great many + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: a great many + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Many men ___ tried to climb Mount Everest but very few succeeded. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “Many men” plural subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. many + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: many + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Many a man ___ tried to climb Mount Everest but very few succeeded. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: “Many a + singular noun” meaning plural ho sakta hai, lekin grammar singular hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. many a + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: many a + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Many a man along with a few friends of mine are going to temple. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “many a man” singular hai; “along with…” verb number change nahi karta. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. many a + singular noun → singular verb; along with is parenthetical. Quick Rule: many a + singular noun → singular verb; along with is parenthetical.","Correct the highlighted part. Many an employee of this company are supposed to be transferred. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “Many an employee” singular grammar follow karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. many an + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: many an + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Many boy have not done their homework properly. Answer: Many boys Hinglish Explanation: “Many” directly plural countable noun ke saath aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Many boys”. many + plural countable noun. Quick Rule: many + plural countable noun.","Correct the highlighted part. A good deal of banks are closed today. Answer: A number of banks Hinglish Explanation: Banks countable plural hain, isliye “a number of” fit hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “A number of banks”. a number of + plural countable noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: a number of + plural countable noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A great deal of milk ___ used every year in Shiv temples. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Milk uncountable singular mass noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. a great deal of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: a great deal of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. A good deal of schools have started new extracurricular activities. Answer: A number of schools Hinglish Explanation: Schools countable plural hain. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “A number of schools”. a number of + plural countable noun. Quick Rule: a number of + plural countable noun.","Correct the highlighted part. A large number of money is required to build a startup. Answer: The amount of money Hinglish Explanation: Money uncountable hai; quantity ke liye “amount” use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “The amount of money”. amount of + uncountable noun; number of + countable plural noun. Quick Rule: amount of + uncountable noun; number of + countable plural noun.","Correct the highlighted part. A number of crimes in New York has increased this year. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “A number of + plural noun” plural verb leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have”. a number of + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: a number of + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The number of crimes in New York ___ increased this year. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Subject “the number” singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. the number of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: the number of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. The number of visitors are increasing. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Head noun “number” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. the number of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: the number of + plural noun → singular verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=3", "t": "Fractions, Percentages, No + Noun & Parenthetical Phrases", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. One third of the sugar ___ yet to be sold. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Fraction ka agreement “of” ke baad noun se hota hai; sugar uncountable singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. fraction + of + uncountable/singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: fraction + of + uncountable/singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Two thirds of the sugar ___ yet to be sold. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Numerator plural hone se verb decide nahi hota; “sugar” mass noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Agreement follows the noun after of. Quick Rule: Agreement follows the noun after of.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Three fourths of the sugar ___ yet to be sold. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “sugar” uncountable hai, hence singular agreement. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. fraction + of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: fraction + of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. One third of the students ___ passed. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Students plural countable noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. fraction + of + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: fraction + of + plural noun → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Most of us understand their duty towards the nation. Answer: our Hinglish Explanation: “us” first-person plural hai, pronoun reference “our” hona chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “our”. Pronoun reference should agree with antecedent/person. Quick Rule: Pronoun reference should agree with antecedent/person.","Correct the highlighted part. Only one third of the work have been finished. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Work uncountable singular noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. fraction + of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: fraction + of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Most of the people in our country is undernourished. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: People plural noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “are”. most of + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: most of + plural noun → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Three fourths of the Eiffel Tower are made of wrought iron. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Eiffel Tower singular whole hai; fraction usi singular whole ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. fraction + of + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: fraction + of + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Most of the money allocated for beautification have not been used properly. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Money uncountable singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. most of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: most of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Twenty percent of the milk have been used for making curd and cheese. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Milk uncountable mass noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. percentage + of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: percentage + of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Fifty percent of the information are false. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Information uncountable singular noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. percentage + of + uncountable noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: percentage + of + uncountable noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. No work ___ been done today. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Work uncountable singular noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. no + uncountable/singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: no + uncountable/singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. No problem ___ big for us. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Problem singular noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. no + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: no + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. No problems ___ big for us. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Problems plural noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. no + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: no + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The moon, as well as Venus, ___ visible in the night sky. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “the moon” singular hai; “as well as Venus” extra phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. as well as/along with/together with do not change number of main subject. Quick Rule: as well as/along with/together with do not change number of main subject.","Correct the highlighted part. Raj as well as Ranveer want to express their views. Answer: wants Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “Raj” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “wants”. Verb agrees with first/main subject before as well as. Quick Rule: Verb agrees with first/main subject before as well as.","Correct the highlighted part. Raj as well as Ranveer wants to express their views. Answer: his Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam rule me main subject Raj ke according possessive “his” use kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “his”. Parenthetical phrase does not change person/number of main subject in traditional exam grammar. Quick Rule: Parenthetical phrase does not change person/number of main subject in traditional exam grammar.","Correct the highlighted part. The band leader along with other members are practising in the auditorium. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “band leader” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. along with → verb with main subject. Quick Rule: along with → verb with main subject.","Correct the highlighted part. All the students including clueless Emma was shocked. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “all the students” plural hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “were”. including phrase is parenthetical; plural head controls verb. Quick Rule: including phrase is parenthetical; plural head controls verb.","Correct the highlighted part. He together with his brother was enjoying themselves at the party. Answer: himself Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “he” hai, so reflexive “himself”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “himself”. Reflexive pronoun agrees with main subject. Quick Rule: Reflexive pronoun agrees with main subject.","Correct the highlighted part. My mother with two younger brothers of mine are going to Mumbai. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “my mother” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. with + noun phrase does not change subject number. Quick Rule: with + noun phrase does not change subject number.","Correct the highlighted part. All the people living in the house including the servant was invited there. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: “All the people” plural subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “were”. including phrase does not change plural head. Quick Rule: including phrase does not change plural head."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=4", "t": "More Than One, Than & One-of Structures", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More than one boy ___ absent in the class. Answer: was Hinglish Explanation: “More than one + singular noun” traditionally singular verb leta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was”. more than one + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: more than one + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More than one house ___ vacant in this building. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “house” singular after “more than one”. English Explanation: “house” singular after “more than one”. Rule: more than one + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: more than one + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More than two boys ___ absent in the class. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: More than two + plural noun → plural verb. English Explanation: More than two + plural noun → plural verb. Rule: more than two/three... + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: more than two/three... + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More than three houses ___ vacant in this building. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Houses plural noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. more than two/three... + plural noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: more than two/three... + plural noun → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More boys than one ___ absent. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: Structure me head noun “boys” plural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “were”. more + plural noun + than one → plural verb. Quick Rule: more + plural noun + than one → plural verb.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. They talked on the phone for more than an hour. Answer: Preposition-like use before a noun phrase Hinglish Explanation: “than” ke baad “an hour” noun phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Preposition-like use before a noun phrase”. Than may introduce a noun phrase or clause depending on structure. Quick Rule: Than may introduce a noun phrase or clause depending on structure.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. We shouldn’t spend more than we earn. Answer: Conjunction introducing a clause Hinglish Explanation: “than we earn” me subject + verb hai, so clause. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Conjunction introducing a clause”. Than + clause functions as conjunction/comparative marker. Quick Rule: Than + clause functions as conjunction/comparative marker.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. He wished he could have helped her more than he did. Answer: Conjunction introducing a clause Hinglish Explanation: “he did” complete clause hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Conjunction introducing a clause”. Than can link comparative clauses. Quick Rule: Than can link comparative clauses.","Choose the grammatically complete comparison. You are older than ___ . Answer: I am Hinglish Explanation: Elliptical formal comparison me “I am” complete clause hota hai; colloquial “me” bhi common hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I am”. For full-clause comparison: than + subject + verb. Quick Rule: For full-clause comparison: than + subject + verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More boys than one ___ absent in the class. Answer: were Hinglish Explanation: Head noun boys plural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “were”. more + plural noun + than one → plural verb. Quick Rule: more + plural noun + than one → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. More houses than one ___ vacant in this building. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Head noun houses plural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. more + plural noun + than one → plural verb. Quick Rule: more + plural noun + than one → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. More than one successful candidate of his institute have taken the interview. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: “More than one + singular candidate” singular agreement leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. more than one + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: more than one + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the best analysis. More books than one have been written by him. Answer: No error in the plural agreement Hinglish Explanation: “books” plural head noun hai; source is structure ko plural agreement ke saath accept karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “No error in the plural agreement”. more + plural noun + than one → plural verb. Quick Rule: more + plural noun + than one → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. More than one book on lifestyle and motivation by this writer have been published so far. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: “More than one book” singular grammar leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. more than one + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: more than one + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. More paintings than one is created by the greatest post-impressionist painter Vincent van Gogh. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: “Paintings” plural head noun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “are”. more + plural noun + than one → plural verb. Quick Rule: more + plural noun + than one → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. One of the dogs ___ missing from the daycare. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Head “one” singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. One of the dog is missing from the daycare. Answer: dogs Hinglish Explanation: “one of” ke baad plural noun aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “dogs”. one of + plural noun. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun.","Correct the highlighted part. He is one of the best representative for the MUN meeting. Answer: representatives Hinglish Explanation: “one of the best” ke baad plural noun chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “representatives”. one of + the + superlative + plural noun. Quick Rule: one of + the + superlative + plural noun.","Choose the best analysis. Raj is one of our best chefs . Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: “one of” ke baad plural noun “chefs” sahi hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Karan is one of the student who is going to London. Answer: students who are Hinglish Explanation: “one of the students” plural antecedent banata hai; relative clause me “who are”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “students who are”. one of + plural noun + who/that + plural verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun + who/that + plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. This is one of the building which is damaged in fire. Answer: buildings which are Hinglish Explanation: Relative pronoun plural noun “buildings” ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “buildings which are”. one of + plural noun + relative pronoun → plural verb in relative clause. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun + relative pronoun → plural verb in relative clause.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Karan is only one of the students who ___ going to London. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “only one” ko antecedent banane par relative verb singular hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. the only one of + plural noun + who → singular relative verb. Quick Rule: the only one of + plural noun + who → singular relative verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. This is only one of the buildings which ___ damaged in fire. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “only one” singular antecedent hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. the only one of + plural noun + which → singular relative verb. Quick Rule: the only one of + plural noun + which → singular relative verb.","Correct the highlighted part. One of the greatest cricket players are playing in the IPL this year. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “one” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Detachment is one of those virtues that makes you strong and weak at the same time. Answer: make Hinglish Explanation: Relative pronoun “that” antecedent “virtues” plural ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “make”. one of those + plural noun + that → plural verb in relative clause. Quick Rule: one of those + plural noun + that → plural verb in relative clause.","Correct the highlighted part. One of the representatives are knowledgeable and good at handling people. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “one” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. One of the greatest cricket players in India are on the verge of announcing his retirement. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject one singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. One of the peace-loving nations like Iceland, New Zealand and Portugal ___ that peaceful nations enjoy lower interest rates. Answer: proves Hinglish Explanation: Head “one” singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “proves”. one of + plural noun → singular main verb. Quick Rule: one of + plural noun → singular main verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=5", "t": "Infinitive, Gerund, Clauses, Measures, Groups & But", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. To walk ___ a good exercise. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Single infinitive phrase as subject singular maana jata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single to-infinitive subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single to-infinitive subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. To save money ___ difficult. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “To save money” ek infinitive phrase subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single infinitive phrase → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single infinitive phrase → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. To sing and to weep ___ two contrary activities. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do separate infinitive activities “and” se joined hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Two distinct infinitive subjects joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two distinct infinitive subjects joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. To walk and to swim ___ good exercises. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do distinct activities plural subject banate hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Two infinitive phrases joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two infinitive phrases joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Walking ___ a good exercise. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Single gerund phrase subject singular hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single gerund subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single gerund subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Dancing ___ an art. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Single gerund “dancing” singular subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single gerund subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single gerund subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Walking and swimming ___ good exercises. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do gerund activities joined by and plural subject banate hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Two separate gerund subjects joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two separate gerund subjects joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Reading and writing ___ two different skills. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do distinct gerund subjects. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Gerund + and + gerund → plural verb when distinct. Quick Rule: Gerund + and + gerund → plural verb when distinct.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. That he is a liar ___ well known. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Pura noun clause ek singular subject unit hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single noun clause as subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single noun clause as subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. What he says ___ not true. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Single “what” clause ek unit hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single clause as subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single clause as subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Where he lives ___ still a mystery. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “Where he lives” single clause subject hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Single clause subject → singular verb. Quick Rule: Single clause subject → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. What he says and what he does ___ not in agreement. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do separate clauses “and” se jude hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Clause + and + clause → plural verb when two distinct facts/ideas. Quick Rule: Clause + and + clause → plural verb when two distinct facts/ideas.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Where he comes and where he goes ___ not known to us. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Do parallel clauses combined plural sense dete hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. Two distinct clauses joined by and → plural verb. Quick Rule: Two distinct clauses joined by and → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Fifty thousand rupees ___ too much for this work. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Amount ek total sum ke roop me treated hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Fixed amount/weight/time/distance as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Fixed amount/weight/time/distance as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Five years ___ a long period. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Five years ek single period/unit ke sense me hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Fixed period as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Fixed period as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Ten kilometres ___ a long distance. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Distance ko ek unit treat kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Fixed distance as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Fixed distance as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The director and producer ___ arrived in his studio. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Single article aur singular possessive “his” same person ka signal hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. Two titles for same person → singular verb. Quick Rule: Two titles for same person → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The director and the producer ___ arrived in their studio. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: Repeated article aur “their” do persons dikhate hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. Repeated article → two persons → plural verb. Quick Rule: Repeated article → two persons → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. My uncle and my guardian ___ very generous and kind. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Repeated possessive indicates two roles/persons in source example. English Explanation: Repeated possessive indicates two roles/persons in source example. Rule: Repeated possessive before nouns → plural verb. Quick Rule: Repeated possessive before nouns → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Nobody but a boy ___ there in the room. Answer: was Hinglish Explanation: “but a boy” prepositional exception phrase hai; subject “nobody” singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was”. With but = except, verb agrees with subject before but. Quick Rule: With but = except, verb agrees with subject before but.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Nobody but three boys ___ there in the room. Answer: was Hinglish Explanation: Source exam rule me subject “nobody” singular hi controller hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “was”. Nobody but + object → verb with nobody. Quick Rule: Nobody but + object → verb with nobody.","Correct the highlighted part. Nothing but the marvellous and supernatural buildings have the charm in that city. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Head subject “nothing” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. nothing/everything/no one/everyone + but + object → singular verb. Quick Rule: nothing/everything/no one/everyone + but + object → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Everyone but my grandparents support her in all her decisions and struggles. Answer: supports Hinglish Explanation: Subject “everyone” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “supports”. everyone + but + object → singular verb. Quick Rule: everyone + but + object → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Nobody but my friends have planned for the homecoming party. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Subject “nobody” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “has”. nobody + but + object → singular verb. Quick Rule: nobody + but + object → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Ten thousand rupees ___ to be distributed among them. Answer: are Hinglish Explanation: Yahan amount ko individual distributable units ke sense me treat kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “are”. When amount/time/distance clearly refers to separate units, plural agreement can be used. Quick Rule: When amount/time/distance clearly refers to separate units, plural agreement can be used.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Five years ___ passed since I left the university. Answer: have Hinglish Explanation: “Years have passed” elapsed individual years ka plural event hai, not a single measurement complement. English Explanation: The correct answer is “have”. Plural time units as countable elapsed units → plural verb. Quick Rule: Plural time units as countable elapsed units → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A crowd of people ___ waiting for you. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Source collective noun “crowd” ko singular unit treat karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Collective noun as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A team of players ___ arrived. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Head noun “team” singular collective noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “has”. Collective noun as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A fleet of ships ___ sailing. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Head “fleet” singular collective noun hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Collective noun as one unit → singular verb. Quick Rule: Collective noun as one unit → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. A galaxy of stars ___ seen in the sky. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Head noun “galaxy” singular hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. Head noun controls agreement. Quick Rule: Head noun controls agreement."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=6", "t": "Each, Every, Either, Neither & Correlative Subjects", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Each student ___ come. Answer: has Hinglish Explanation: Each + singular noun takes singular verb. English Explanation: Each + singular noun takes singular verb. Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Every review ___ . Answer: matters Hinglish Explanation: Every + singular noun takes third-person singular verb. English Explanation: Every + singular noun takes third-person singular verb. Rule: every + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Either dress ___ fine. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Either + singular noun takes singular verb. English Explanation: Either + singular noun takes singular verb. Rule: either + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: either + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Neither dish ___ perfect. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Neither + singular noun takes singular verb. English Explanation: Neither + singular noun takes singular verb. Rule: neither + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: neither + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Each of the ten students want his share. Answer: wants Hinglish Explanation: Head “each” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “wants”. each of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Either of the two students are missing. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “Either” singular distributive pronoun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. either of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: either of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Neither of the two flowers are fresh. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: “Neither” singular distributive pronoun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. neither of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: neither of + plural noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Each student ___ his share. Answer: wants Hinglish Explanation: Each modifies singular noun student. English Explanation: Each modifies singular noun student. Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. The students each ___ their share. Answer: want Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “students” plural hai; “each” appositional/distributive adverbial role me hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “want”. Plural subject + each → plural verb. Quick Rule: Plural subject + each → plural verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Either he or I ___ a fool. Answer: am Hinglish Explanation: Correlative structure me verb nearest subject “I” ke according. English Explanation: The correct answer is “am”. either...or / neither...nor → verb with nearest subject in exam grammar. Quick Rule: either...or / neither...nor → verb with nearest subject in exam grammar.","Correct the highlighted part. During mitosis, each of the parent cells split into two genetically identical daughter cells. Answer: splits Hinglish Explanation: Head “each” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “splits”. each of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Each of our students pay their tuition fee at the beginning of the month. Answer: pays Hinglish Explanation: Head “each” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “pays”. each of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Either of the two officers need to visit the house of the victim. Answer: needs Hinglish Explanation: Either singular distributive pronoun hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “needs”. either of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: either of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Not only the boy but also his parents regretted his mistakes. Answer: their Hinglish Explanation: Nearest/real plural referent “parents” ke possessive reference ke liye “their”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “their”. Pronoun reference should match the intended plural antecedent. Quick Rule: Pronoun reference should match the intended plural antecedent.","Correct the highlighted part. Each of the candidates who have been chosen to work as a probationary officer in different parts of the country are very intelligent. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Main subject “each” singular hai, even though relative clause has plural “have”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. Each of + plural noun → singular main verb; relative clause may agree with plural noun. Quick Rule: Each of + plural noun → singular main verb; relative clause may agree with plural noun.","Correct the highlighted part. Neither leaders nor the prime minister are responsible for the poverty and corruption rampant in our country. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Nearest subject “the prime minister” singular hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is”. neither...nor → verb agrees with nearest subject (exam rule). Quick Rule: neither...nor → verb agrees with nearest subject (exam rule).","Correct the highlighted part. Neither of the two children said their prayer before going to bed. Answer: his Hinglish Explanation: Source traditional exam rule uses singular reference after “neither”. English Explanation: Source traditional exam rule uses singular reference after “neither”. Rule: Traditional exam grammar often uses singular pronoun after neither/either/each. Quick Rule: Traditional exam grammar often uses singular pronoun after neither/either/each.","Correct the highlighted part. Either the counsellor or the teacher of that institute always tries to misguide students. Answer: tries Hinglish Explanation: Nearest subject “teacher” singular hai; “tries” correct hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “tries”. either...or → nearest subject controls verb. Quick Rule: either...or → nearest subject controls verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Either your sister or your brother has to present themselves before the magistrate. Answer: himself Hinglish Explanation: Nearest subject “your brother” singular masculine in source example. English Explanation: Nearest subject “your brother” singular masculine in source example. Rule: Reflexive pronoun should agree with controlling subject. Quick Rule: Reflexive pronoun should agree with controlling subject.","Correct the highlighted part. She had paint on every leg. Answer: each Hinglish Explanation: When referring individually to two legs, “each leg” is preferred in the source distinction. English Explanation: When referring individually to two legs, “each leg” is preferred in the source distinction. Rule: each focuses on individual members; every views members of a group collectively/distributively. Quick Rule: each focuses on individual members; every views members of a group collectively/distributively.","Correct the highlighted part. My parents are arriving tomorrow, and every of them will be on a different plane. Answer: each of them Hinglish Explanation: “Every” directly “of” ke saath pronoun nahi banta; “each of them” correct. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “each of them”. each of + pronoun; every one of + pronoun. Quick Rule: each of + pronoun; every one of + pronoun.","Choose the acceptable form in the source rule. He has a ring on ___ finger . Answer: each / every Hinglish Explanation: Direct singular noun ke saath both each/every possible, depending intended focus. English Explanation: The correct answer is “each / every”. each/every + singular noun. Quick Rule: each/every + singular noun.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Pronoun/adjective use Answer: Each of us has a bicycle. Hinglish Explanation: “Each” pronoun ke roop me “each of us” ban sakta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Each of us has a bicycle.”. each of + pronoun + singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + pronoun + singular verb.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Every with of-phrase Answer: Every one of us has a bicycle. Hinglish Explanation: “Every one of us” correct structure hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Every one of us has a bicycle.”. every one of + plural/pronoun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every one of + plural/pronoun → singular verb.","Choose the correct sentence. Each / Every one Answer: Each of the children received a special gift. Hinglish Explanation: “Each of + plural noun” grammatical hai and takes singular verb. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Each of the children received a special gift.”. each of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct sentence. Every one Answer: Every one of the children received a special gift. Hinglish Explanation: Every one of + plural noun takes singular verb. English Explanation: Every one of + plural noun takes singular verb. Rule: every one of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every one of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. We have three windows; every has a great view. Answer: each Hinglish Explanation: Pronoun use me “each” standalone ho sakta hai; “every” normally noun ke saath. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “each”. each can be pronoun; every is normally adjective/determiner. Quick Rule: each can be pronoun; every is normally adjective/determiner.","Correct the highlighted part. The dogs are unique. Every has different marking. Answer: Each Hinglish Explanation: Standalone distributive pronoun ke roop me “each” use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Each”. each can stand alone; every needs a noun or “one”. Quick Rule: each can stand alone; every needs a noun or “one”.","Correct the highlighted part. Every of the children received a present. Answer: Each of Hinglish Explanation: “Every of” incorrect; “each of” correct. English Explanation: “Every of” incorrect; “each of” correct. Rule: each of + plural noun; every one of + plural noun. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun; every one of + plural noun.","Choose the best analysis. Every one of the windows was broken. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Head expression “every one” singular hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. every one of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every one of + plural noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. I’ve seen three films. Every was very interesting. Answer: Each Hinglish Explanation: Standalone pronoun ke liye “each” better. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Each”. each can function as pronoun; every cannot normally stand alone. Quick Rule: each can function as pronoun; every cannot normally stand alone.","Correct the highlighted part. Rahul has two sons and every one of them is brilliant in studies. Answer: each Hinglish Explanation: Source distinction me exactly two individuals par “each” preferred. English Explanation: Source distinction me exactly two individuals par “each” preferred. Rule: each is preferred for individual members, especially two. Quick Rule: each is preferred for individual members, especially two.","Choose the best analysis. Each of my ten pens writes smoothly. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: “Each of + plural noun” singular verb leta hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. each of + plural noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each of + plural noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Each child ___ performing a different task. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Each child singular hai and individual task focus karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. each + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Every child ___ performing the same task. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Every child singular agreement leta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. every + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every + singular noun → singular verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Karan is using different pencils every time. Answer: each Hinglish Explanation: Different pencil on individual occasions ke sense me source “each time” choose karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “each”. each emphasizes individual occasions; every can denote all occasions generally. Quick Rule: each emphasizes individual occasions; every can denote all occasions generally.","Correct the highlighted part. Sahil is using the same pencils each time. Answer: every Hinglish Explanation: Repeated same pattern across all occasions ke liye source “every time” choose karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “every”. every emphasizes the whole recurring set of occasions. Quick Rule: every emphasizes the whole recurring set of occasions.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Every artist ___ sensitive. Answer: is Hinglish Explanation: Every + singular noun. English Explanation: The correct answer is “is”. every + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: every + singular noun → singular verb.","Choose the correct verb/form for the highlighted part. Each artist ___ things differently. Answer: sees Hinglish Explanation: Each + singular noun → third-person singular verb. English Explanation: Each + singular noun → third-person singular verb. Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb. Quick Rule: each + singular noun → singular verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=07.+Subject%E2%80%93Verb+Agreement&section=7", "t": "Few / A Few / The Few / Little / A Little / The Little", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 07. Subject–Verb Agreement", "x": ["Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. I read few books. Answer: Hardly any; negative sense Hinglish Explanation: “few” plural countable noun ke saath almost none ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Hardly any; negative sense”. few + plural countable noun → hardly any (negative sense). Quick Rule: few + plural countable noun → hardly any (negative sense).","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. I read a few books. Answer: Some; positive sense Hinglish Explanation: “a few” means some, though not many. English Explanation: “a few” means some, though not many. Rule: a few + plural countable noun → some (positive). Quick Rule: a few + plural countable noun → some (positive).","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. I read the few books that I bought. Answer: Not many, but all the available ones Hinglish Explanation: “the few” refers to all of a small definite set. English Explanation: “the few” refers to all of a small definite set. Rule: the few = not many, but all those available/mentioned. Quick Rule: the few = not many, but all those available/mentioned.","Correct the highlighted part. Because of extreme heat, a few girls have come to attend the class. Answer: few Hinglish Explanation: Context “because of extreme heat” implies hardly any attended, negative sense. English Explanation: Context “because of extreme heat” implies hardly any attended, negative sense. Rule: few = hardly any; a few = some. Quick Rule: few = hardly any; a few = some.","Correct the highlighted part. I cannot go there; I have a little money. Answer: little Hinglish Explanation: Cannot go because money is insufficient → negative “little”. English Explanation: Cannot go because money is insufficient → negative “little”. Rule: little + uncountable noun = hardly any. Quick Rule: little + uncountable noun = hardly any.","Correct the highlighted part. Despite extreme heat, few girls have come to attend the class. Answer: a few Hinglish Explanation: “Despite” suggests some girls did come despite heat → positive sense. English Explanation: “Despite” suggests some girls did come despite heat → positive sense. Rule: a few = some, positive sense. Quick Rule: a few = some, positive sense.","Correct the highlighted part. Give me little food. I’m hungry. Answer: a little Hinglish Explanation: Request me some food ka positive meaning hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “a little”. a little + uncountable noun = some. Quick Rule: a little + uncountable noun = some.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. She gave the few books she had to the needy person. Answer: All of a small definite number Hinglish Explanation: Definite small set ke sab books diye gaye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “All of a small definite number”. the few = all of the small number available. Quick Rule: the few = all of the small number available.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. The little water that I had was spilt on the table. Answer: All of the small amount available Hinglish Explanation: Definite small quantity ke entire amount ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “All of the small amount available”. the little = all of the small amount available. Quick Rule: the little = all of the small amount available.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. I have 100 rupees less than what is required. Answer: Difference in amount/number Hinglish Explanation: “less than” yahan numerical shortfall express karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Difference in amount/number”. less can be used with amounts, numbers and distance in common exam usage. Quick Rule: less can be used with amounts, numbers and distance in common exam usage.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. In less than 7 days our results will be out. Answer: Numerical/time comparison Hinglish Explanation: Time/number measurement ke saath “less than” common hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Numerical/time comparison”. less than + number/time/distance. Quick Rule: less than + number/time/distance.","Correct the highlighted part. Will you lend me a few rupees in this hour of need? Answer: a few Hinglish Explanation: Rupees countable plural hain aur request me “some” positive sense hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “a few”. a few + plural countable noun = some. Quick Rule: a few + plural countable noun = some.","Choose the best analysis. I gave him the little money that I could spare then. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Money uncountable hai; definite small amount ke liye “the little money” sahi hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. the little + uncountable noun = all of a small definite amount. Quick Rule: the little + uncountable noun = all of a small definite amount.","Choose the best expression. ___ fifty students came for the hunger strike. Answer: No less than Hinglish Explanation: With a numeric minimum emphasis, “no less than fifty” is acceptable exam usage. English Explanation: With a numeric minimum emphasis, “no less than fifty” is acceptable exam usage. Rule: no less than + number = at least that number. Quick Rule: no less than + number = at least that number.","Correct the highlighted part. The doctor advised him to take rest for few days . Answer: a few days Hinglish Explanation: Positive “some days” meaning chahiye. English Explanation: Positive “some days” meaning chahiye. Rule: a few + plural countable noun = some. Quick Rule: a few + plural countable noun = some.","Choose the correct grammatical analysis of the highlighted part. She spent the little amount of money she had. Answer: All of the small definite amount Hinglish Explanation: Definite small amount ka whole. English Explanation: The correct answer is “All of the small definite amount”. the little + uncountable amount = all of it. Quick Rule: the little + uncountable amount = all of it.","Correct the highlighted part. If you look up tea in a cookery book you may find a little instructions . Answer: a few instructions Hinglish Explanation: Instructions countable plural hain. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “a few instructions”. a few + plural countable noun; a little + uncountable noun. Quick Rule: a few + plural countable noun; a little + uncountable noun.","Choose the best analysis. There were no less than forty boys in the class when this happened. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Source marks this numeric expression acceptable. English Explanation: Source marks this numeric expression acceptable. Rule: no less than + number = at least. Quick Rule: no less than + number = at least.","Correct the highlighted part. There are only a few company that can handle a project of this magnitude. Answer: companies Hinglish Explanation: A few requires plural countable noun. English Explanation: A few requires plural countable noun. Rule: a few + plural noun. Quick Rule: a few + plural noun.","Choose the best analysis. The few clothes they had were all tattered and torn. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: “the few clothes” is a definite small plural set. English Explanation: “the few clothes” is a definite small plural set. Rule: the few + plural countable noun → plural verb. Quick Rule: the few + plural countable noun → plural verb.","Correct the highlighted part. I am very thirsty, so give me a little water to drink. Answer: a little water Hinglish Explanation: Water uncountable hai and some positive quantity requested hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “a little water”. a little + uncountable noun = some. Quick Rule: a little + uncountable noun = some.","Correct the highlighted part. Few books I had kept for you have been stolen. Answer: The few books Hinglish Explanation: Definite small set “I had kept for you” ko refer karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “The few books”. the few = all of a small definite set. Quick Rule: the few = all of a small definite set.","Choose the best analysis. The captain declared that his ship had carried no less than three hundred passengers on the last voyage. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Source accepts “no less than + number” for emphatic minimum. English Explanation: Source accepts “no less than + number” for emphatic minimum. Rule: no less than + number. Quick Rule: no less than + number."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=08.+Prepositions&section=1", "t": "No-preposition verbs & fixed structures", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 08. Prepositions", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. She entered into my room quietly. Answer: entered Hinglish Explanation: “Enter” jab directly place-object leta hai, uske baad preposition nahi lagti. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “entered”. enter + place (no preposition); come/go + into for motion into a place. Quick Rule: enter + place (no preposition); come/go + into for motion into a place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She came ___ my room quietly. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: “Came into” movement from outside to inside dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “into”. come into + place = enter a place. Quick Rule: come into + place = enter a place.","Correct the highlighted part. My beloved friend returned back from U.S.A. Answer: returned Hinglish Explanation: “Return” me “back” ka meaning already included hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “returned”. return = come back; avoid return back in standard exam English. Quick Rule: return = come back; avoid return back in standard exam English.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My beloved friend came ___ from U.S.A. Answer: back Hinglish Explanation: “Come back” return ka natural phrasal expression hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “back”. come back from + place. Quick Rule: come back from + place.","Correct the highlighted part. The detective investigated into the murder case. Answer: investigated Hinglish Explanation: “Investigate” transitive verb hai; case ko direct object banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “investigated”. investigate + object; enquire/inquire into + matter. Quick Rule: investigate + object; enquire/inquire into + matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The detective enquired ___ the murder case. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: “Enquire into” = kisi matter ki formal investigation karna. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “into”. enquire/inquire into + matter. Quick Rule: enquire/inquire into + matter.","Correct the highlighted part. Despite of being well-off, she is greedy. Answer: Despite Hinglish Explanation: “Despite” khud preposition hai; iske saath “of” nahi aata. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Despite”. despite + noun/gerund; in spite of + noun/gerund. Quick Rule: despite + noun/gerund; in spite of + noun/gerund.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. ___ being well-off, she is greedy. Answer: In spite of Hinglish Explanation: “In spite of” fixed phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “In spite of”. in spite of + noun/gerund. Quick Rule: in spite of + noun/gerund.","Correct the highlighted part. This class comprises of 50 students. Answer: comprises Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam usage me “comprise” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “comprises”. comprise + parts; consist of + parts. Quick Rule: comprise + parts; consist of + parts.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. This class consists ___ 50 students. Answer: of Hinglish Explanation: “Consist of” fixed combination hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “of”. consist of + constituents. Quick Rule: consist of + constituents.","Correct the highlighted part. The poet described about the beauty of the landscape. Answer: described Hinglish Explanation: “Describe” direct object leta hai; “about” extra hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “described”. describe + something; describe something to/for someone. Quick Rule: describe + something; describe something to/for someone.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Can you describe it ___ me? Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Person recipient ke liye “describe something to someone” natural hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. describe something to someone. Quick Rule: describe something to someone.","Correct the highlighted part. The students discussed about the problem with their teacher. Answer: discussed Hinglish Explanation: “Discuss” ke baad matter direct object hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “discussed”. discuss + something; discuss something with someone. Quick Rule: discuss + something; discuss something with someone.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I discussed the question ___ my friend. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Jis person ke saath discussion ho, uske liye “with”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. discuss something with someone. Quick Rule: discuss something with someone.","Correct the highlighted part. The teacher explained about the question. Answer: explained Hinglish Explanation: “Explain” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “explained”. explain + something; explain something to someone. Quick Rule: explain + something; explain something to someone.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She explained her problems ___ me. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Recipient ke liye “to”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. explain something to someone. Quick Rule: explain something to someone.","Correct the highlighted part. The tiger attacked on me. Answer: attacked Hinglish Explanation: Verb “attack” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “attacked”. attack + person/thing; attack someone with a weapon. Quick Rule: attack + person/thing; attack someone with a weapon.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She attacked him ___ a dagger. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Instrument/weapon ke liye “with”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. attack someone with + weapon. Quick Rule: attack someone with + weapon.","Correct the highlighted part. Our mathematics teacher emphasizes on the need for a lot of practice. Answer: emphasizes Hinglish Explanation: Verb “emphasize” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “emphasizes”. emphasize + object; lay emphasis on + object. Quick Rule: emphasize + object; lay emphasis on + object.","Correct the highlighted part. Will you be able to cope up with the new job? Answer: cope with Hinglish Explanation: Standard expression “cope with” hai; “up” extra hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “cope with”. cope with + situation/problem. Quick Rule: cope with + situation/problem.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I can cope ___ this situation very easily. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: “Cope with” = manage/deal successfully. English Explanation: “Cope with” = manage/deal successfully. Rule: cope with + noun. Quick Rule: cope with + noun.","Correct the highlighted part. He ordered for a cup of tea. Answer: ordered Hinglish Explanation: Food/drink order karte waqt verb “order” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “ordered”. order + thing; ask for + thing. Quick Rule: order + thing; ask for + thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He asked ___ a cup of tea. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: “Ask for” = request something. English Explanation: “Ask for” = request something. Rule: ask for + thing. Quick Rule: ask for + thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I ordered my servant ___ clean the car. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Order + person + to-infinitive structure hota hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. order someone to do something. Quick Rule: order someone to do something.","Correct the highlighted part. She resembles with her mother. Answer: resembles Hinglish Explanation: “Resemble” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “resembles”. resemble + person/thing (no preposition). Quick Rule: resemble + person/thing (no preposition).","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Ridicule Answer: Some girls ridiculed him. Hinglish Explanation: “Ridicule” transitive verb hai; person direct object hota hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Some girls ridiculed him.”. ridicule + person/thing. Quick Rule: ridicule + person/thing.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Ridicule Answer: He ridiculed his wife. Hinglish Explanation: “Ridicule” ke baad preposition nahi. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He ridiculed his wife.”. ridicule + object. Quick Rule: ridicule + object.","Correct the highlighted part. The train is approaching to Delhi. Answer: approaching Hinglish Explanation: Verb “approach” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “approaching”. approach + place/person; noun “approach to” may take to in other contexts. Quick Rule: approach + place/person; noun “approach to” may take to in other contexts.","Correct the highlighted part. I advised to him to give up smoking. Answer: advised him Hinglish Explanation: Advise + person + to-infinitive. English Explanation: Advise + person + to-infinitive. Rule: advise/ask/beg/encourage/invite/tell + someone + to do something. Quick Rule: advise/ask/beg/encourage/invite/tell + someone + to do something.","Correct the highlighted part. I told to you to do it. Answer: told you Hinglish Explanation: “Tell” direct person-object leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “told you”. tell someone to do something. Quick Rule: tell someone to do something.","Correct the highlighted part. He proposed us that we should go to the cinema. Answer: proposed to us Hinglish Explanation: Jab “propose” ke baad person recipient ho, “to” use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “proposed to us”. propose/report/say/suggest something to someone. Quick Rule: propose/report/say/suggest something to someone.","Correct the highlighted part. She reported me that two files were missing. Answer: reported to me Hinglish Explanation: “Report something to someone” standard structure hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “reported to me”. report + to + person. Quick Rule: report + to + person."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=08.+Prepositions&section=2", "t": "Different prepositions with changing meaning", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 08. Prepositions", "x": ["Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Rahul married ___ Pooja. Answer: No preposition Hinglish Explanation: Active verb “marry” direct object leta hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “No preposition”. marry + person; be married to + person. Quick Rule: marry + person; be married to + person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Pooja was married ___ Rahul. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Passive/adjectival “be married” ke saath “to”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. be married to + person. Quick Rule: be married to + person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Don't argue ___ your seniors over this simple matter. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Person ke saath argument = argue with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. argue with + person; argue over/about + matter. Quick Rule: argue with + person; argue over/about + matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Don't argue with your seniors ___ this simple matter. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Matter/topic ke liye “over/about”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “over”. argue over/about + matter. Quick Rule: argue over/about + matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. You must attend ___ your old parents. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Source ke fixed-phrase distinction me “attend on” = look after/serve. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “on”. attend on = serve/take care of; attend to = give attention to. Quick Rule: attend on = serve/take care of; attend to = give attention to.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Could you please attend ___ my bike for a minute? Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Thing/problem par attention dene ke liye “attend to”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. attend to + thing/task. Quick Rule: attend to + thing/task.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The actor must apologize ___ his fans for his rude behaviour. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Person recipient ke liye “to”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. apologize to + person; apologize for + act. Quick Rule: apologize to + person; apologize for + act.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The actor must apologize to his fans ___ his rude behaviour. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Reason/offence ke liye “for”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. apologize for + action/behaviour. Quick Rule: apologize for + action/behaviour.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am not accountable ___ you for my coming late. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Jis authority/person ko answerable ho = accountable to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. accountable to + person; accountable for + action/result. Quick Rule: accountable to + person; accountable for + action/result.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am not accountable to you ___ my coming late. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Jis cheez ke liye responsibility ho = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. accountable for + thing/action. Quick Rule: accountable for + thing/action.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My mom is not angry ___ me but at my mobile. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Person ke saath angry = with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. angry with + person; angry at + thing/situation. Quick Rule: angry with + person; angry at + thing/situation.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My mom is not angry with me but ___ my mobile. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Thing/situation par anger = at. English Explanation: Thing/situation par anger = at. Rule: angry at + thing. Quick Rule: angry at + thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My father is anxious ___ me about the result of my exam. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Person ki safety/welfare ke liye anxiety = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. anxious for + person; anxious about + result/matter. Quick Rule: anxious for + person; anxious about + result/matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My father is anxious for me ___ the result of my exam. Answer: about Hinglish Explanation: Matter/result ke liye “about”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “about”. anxious about + matter/result. Quick Rule: anxious about + matter/result.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She burst ___ laughter at my joke. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: Sudden laughter/tears = burst into. English Explanation: Sudden laughter/tears = burst into. Rule: burst into + laughter/tears. Quick Rule: burst into + laughter/tears.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The American air forces burst ___ the cities of Syria. Answer: upon Hinglish Explanation: Attack sense me source “burst upon” use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “upon”. burst upon + place/person = attack suddenly (formal/literary). Quick Rule: burst upon + place/person = attack suddenly (formal/literary).","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She is blind ___ one eye. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Ek eye me blindness = blind in one eye. English Explanation: Ek eye me blindness = blind in one eye. Rule: blind in + one/left/right eye. Quick Rule: blind in + one/left/right eye.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He is blind ___ both eyes. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Source exam distinction: both eyes ke saath “with”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. blind with both eyes (traditional exam usage); blind in one eye. Quick Rule: blind with both eyes (traditional exam usage); blind in one eye.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am not blind ___ your faults. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Figurative “blind to” = unable/unwilling to notice. English Explanation: Figurative “blind to” = unable/unwilling to notice. Rule: blind to + faults/facts. Quick Rule: blind to + faults/facts.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. You must care ___ your old parents. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Person ki care = care for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. care for + person; take care of + thing/person. Quick Rule: care for + person; take care of + thing/person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Will you take care ___ my bag for a minute? Answer: of Hinglish Explanation: Fixed phrase “take care of”. English Explanation: Fixed phrase “take care of”. Rule: take care of + noun. Quick Rule: take care of + noun.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She complained ___ the principal against the peon about his carelessness. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Jis authority ko complaint ki jaaye = to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. complain to + person; against + person complained about; about/of + matter. Quick Rule: complain to + person; against + person complained about; about/of + matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She complained to the principal ___ the peon about his carelessness. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Jiske khilaaf complaint = against. English Explanation: Jiske khilaaf complaint = against. Rule: complain against + person. Quick Rule: complain against + person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She complained to the principal against the peon ___ his carelessness. Answer: about Hinglish Explanation: Matter/reason = about/of. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “about”. complain about/of + matter. Quick Rule: complain about/of + matter.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She concurred ___ me in my opinion. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Person ke saath agreement = with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. concur with + person; concur in + opinion/decision. Quick Rule: concur with + person; concur in + opinion/decision.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She concurred with me ___ my opinion. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Opinion/decision ke relation me = in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. concur in + opinion. Quick Rule: concur in + opinion.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I congratulated him ___ his success. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Success/achievement par congratulations = on. English Explanation: Success/achievement par congratulations = on. Rule: congratulate + person + on + achievement. Quick Rule: congratulate + person + on + achievement.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I call ___ the doctor at the hospital every month. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Person ko visit = call on. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “on”. call on + person; call at + place. Quick Rule: call on + person; call at + place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I call on the doctor ___ the hospital every month. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Place par visit = at. English Explanation: Place par visit = at. Rule: call at + place. Quick Rule: call at + place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The truck dashed ___ a running dog and later dashed against the wall. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Kisi object/person ke upar chadh jana/cross-hit = dash/run over. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “over”. dash/run over + person/thing; dash against + obstacle. Quick Rule: dash/run over + person/thing; dash against + obstacle.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The truck dashed over a running dog and later dashed ___ the wall. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Wall/obstacle se takrana = against. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “against”. dash against + wall/obstacle. Quick Rule: dash against + wall/obstacle.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I don't know how to deal ___ this problem. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Problem/person ko handle karna = deal with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. deal with + problem/person; deal in + goods/business. Quick Rule: deal with + problem/person; deal in + goods/business.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My father deals ___ drugs. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Business/commodity = deal in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. deal in + commodity. Quick Rule: deal in + commodity.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She died ___ cancer. Answer: of Hinglish Explanation: Disease ka direct cause = die of. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “of”. die of + disease/illness. Quick Rule: die of + disease/illness.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Her friend died ___ overeating. Answer: from Hinglish Explanation: External/behavioural cause = die from. English Explanation: External/behavioural cause = die from. Rule: die from + external cause/overexertion/poisoning. Quick Rule: die from + external cause/overexertion/poisoning.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The soldier died ___ the battlefield for his country. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Battle/accident context me “die in”. English Explanation: Battle/accident context me “die in”. Rule: die in + battle/accident; die for + cause/country. Quick Rule: die in + battle/accident; die for + cause/country.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The soldier died in the battlefield ___ his country. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Cause/country ke liye sacrifice = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. die for + cause/country. Quick Rule: die for + cause/country.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He differs ___ you in this opinion. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Person se disagreement = differ with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. differ with + person; differ from + thing/person in characteristics. Quick Rule: differ with + person; differ from + thing/person in characteristics.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. This book differs ___ that book. Answer: from Hinglish Explanation: Comparison/difference between things = from. English Explanation: Comparison/difference between things = from. Rule: differ from + thing/person. Quick Rule: differ from + thing/person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She entered ___ room. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: “Enter” direct place-object leta hai; blank me article “the” hi chahiye. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “the”. enter + place; enter into + agreement/business/relationship. Quick Rule: enter + place; enter into + agreement/business/relationship.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My younger brother has entered ___ a new business this year. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: Business/agreement/relationship me enter into use hota hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “into”. enter into + business/agreement/relationship; enter + place. Quick Rule: enter into + business/agreement/relationship; enter + place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Lately, I have been engaged ___ this new project. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Work/activity me involved = engaged in. English Explanation: Work/activity me involved = engaged in. Rule: engaged in + work/activity; engaged to + person (betrothed). Quick Rule: engaged in + work/activity; engaged to + person (betrothed).","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I think they got engaged ___ each other pretty fast. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Marriage engagement = engaged to. English Explanation: Marriage engagement = engaged to. Rule: engaged to + person. Quick Rule: engaged to + person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am familiar ___ Madhuri Dixit. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Aap kisi person/fact ko jaante ho = familiar with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. be familiar with + person/fact/language; something may be familiar to someone. Quick Rule: be familiar with + person/fact/language; something may be familiar to someone.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am familiar ___ Urdu language. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Standard English me language ko jaanna = familiar with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. familiar with + language; “Urdu is familiar to me” is the converse pattern. Quick Rule: familiar with + language; “Urdu is familiar to me” is the converse pattern.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Razia fell ___ robbers on the way back home. Answer: among Hinglish Explanation: Group ke beech aa jana = among. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “among”. fall among + people/group; fall in love with + person. Quick Rule: fall among + people/group; fall in love with + person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Tarun Sir has fallen ___ love with his students. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Fixed phrase “fall in love with”. English Explanation: Fixed phrase “fall in love with”. Rule: fall in love with + person/thing. Quick Rule: fall in love with + person/thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I have shown gratitude ___ him for his kind work. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Person recipient = to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. gratitude to + person; gratitude for + act/thing. Quick Rule: gratitude to + person; gratitude for + act/thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I have shown gratitude to him ___ his kind work. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Reason/act = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. gratitude for + thing/action. Quick Rule: gratitude for + thing/action.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She jumped ___ this lucrative offer. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Offer/opportunity ko eagerly accept = jump at. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. jump at + offer/chance; jump to + conclusion. Quick Rule: jump at + offer/chance; jump to + conclusion.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Don't jump ___ any conclusion without observing the facts. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Fixed phrase “jump to a conclusion”. English Explanation: Fixed phrase “jump to a conclusion”. Rule: jump to + conclusion. Quick Rule: jump to + conclusion.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. This path will lead ___ success. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Destination/result = lead to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. lead to + place/result; lead someone into + state/situation. Quick Rule: lead to + place/result; lead someone into + state/situation.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. These habits will lead us ___ a good personality. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: Someone ko state/condition me le jana = lead into. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “into”. lead someone into + state/situation. Quick Rule: lead someone into + state/situation.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Don't lean ___ the wall. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Physical support against a surface = against. English Explanation: Physical support against a surface = against. Rule: lean against + wall; lean on + person; lean to + opinion. Quick Rule: lean against + wall; lean on + person; lean to + opinion.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. We are not leaning ___ you for this big project. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Depend/rely on a person = lean on. English Explanation: Depend/rely on a person = lean on. Rule: lean on + person = depend on. Quick Rule: lean on + person = depend on.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. We should all lean ___ this opinion that we should not hurt others. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Preference/opinion ki taraf incline = lean to/towards. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. lean to/towards + opinion. Quick Rule: lean to/towards + opinion.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. We should live ___ our country and die for our country. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Cause/purpose = live for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. live for + cause; live by + means; live on + food; live within + means. Quick Rule: live for + cause; live by + means; live on + food; live within + means.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The worker always lives ___ his sincere labour. Answer: by Hinglish Explanation: Means/source of livelihood = by. English Explanation: Means/source of livelihood = by. Rule: live by + work/means. Quick Rule: live by + work/means.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. In Navratras, people live ___ fruit and vegetables. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Food subsistence = live on. English Explanation: Food subsistence = live on. Rule: live on + food/income. Quick Rule: live on + food/income.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. My father told me to live ___ my means. Answer: within Hinglish Explanation: Spend within available income = within. English Explanation: Spend within available income = within. Rule: live within one’s means. Quick Rule: live within one’s means.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He met ___ an accident on his way back home. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Fixed exam phrase “meet with an accident”. English Explanation: Fixed exam phrase “meet with an accident”. Rule: meet with + accident; meet + person (no preposition). Quick Rule: meet with + accident; meet + person (no preposition).","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am going to meet ___ my best friend today. Answer: No preposition Hinglish Explanation: Verb “meet” person ko direct object bana sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “No preposition”. meet + person; meet with may mean have a meeting or encounter an event. Quick Rule: meet + person; meet with may mean have a meeting or encounter an event.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I can part ___ my girlfriend but not with my phone. Answer: from Hinglish Explanation: Person se separation = part from. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “from”. part from + person; part with + possession. Quick Rule: part from + person; part with + possession.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I can part from my girlfriend but not ___ my phone. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Possession chhodna = part with. English Explanation: Possession chhodna = part with. Rule: part with + thing/possession. Quick Rule: part with + thing/possession.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. If I don't get the job in govt sector, I will proceed ___ my family business. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Work/business continue = proceed with. English Explanation: Work/business continue = proceed with. Rule: proceed with + work/business; proceed on + principle/basis. Quick Rule: proceed with + work/business; proceed on + principle/basis.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. We all should proceed ___ this principle that we should not lie. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Principle/basis = on. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “on”. proceed on + principle/basis. Quick Rule: proceed on + principle/basis.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I have reconciled ___ my opponent. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Person ke saath relationship restore = with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. reconcile with + person; reconcile oneself to + situation/loss. Quick Rule: reconcile with + person; reconcile oneself to + situation/loss.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He reconciled ___ his loss-making investments. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Unpleasant situation ko accept = reconcile oneself to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. reconcile to + situation/loss. Quick Rule: reconcile to + situation/loss.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The teacher is talking ___ my mother about my future. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Person addressed = to/with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. talk to/with + person; talk about/of + topic. Quick Rule: talk to/with + person; talk about/of + topic.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The teacher is talking to my mother ___ my future. Answer: about Hinglish Explanation: Topic = about/of. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “about”. talk about/of + topic. Quick Rule: talk about/of + topic.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The state is thankful ___ the government for new reforms. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Person/institution recipient = to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. thankful to + person/institution; thankful for + thing. Quick Rule: thankful to + person/institution; thankful for + thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The state is thankful to the government ___ new reforms. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Reason/thing = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. thankful for + thing. Quick Rule: thankful for + thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I will not trade ___ you in gold anymore. Answer: with Hinglish Explanation: Trading partner = with. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “with”. trade with + person; trade in + commodity. Quick Rule: trade with + person; trade in + commodity.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I will not trade with you ___ gold anymore. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Commodity = in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. trade in + commodity. Quick Rule: trade in + commodity.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She is waiting ___ the platform for the train. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Place point = at. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. wait at + place; wait for + person/thing. Quick Rule: wait at + place; wait for + person/thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She is waiting at the platform ___ the train. Answer: for Hinglish Explanation: Person/thing awaited = for. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “for”. wait for + person/thing. Quick Rule: wait for + person/thing."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=08.+Prepositions&section=3", "t": "To / Towards / For / Home", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 08. Prepositions", "x": ["Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. They are going ___ the station. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Destination fixed hai = to. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. to = destination; towards = direction without necessarily reaching. Quick Rule: to = destination; towards = direction without necessarily reaching.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. They are going ___ the station. Answer: towards Hinglish Explanation: Sirf direction emphasize karna ho = towards. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “towards”. towards = in the direction of. Quick Rule: towards = in the direction of.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am going ___ the post office to send this parcel. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Post office destination hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. go to + place. Quick Rule: go to + place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She is going ___ Paris tomorrow. Answer: to Hinglish Explanation: Paris destination hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “to”. go to + place. Quick Rule: go to + place.","Correct the highlighted part. Can you please return this book in the library ? Answer: to the library Hinglish Explanation: Return/send/go type motion destination ke liye “to”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to the library”. return/send/go/come + to + place. Quick Rule: return/send/go/come + to + place.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Invitation Answer: I was not invited to the inaugural party. Hinglish Explanation: Invite + person + to + event. English Explanation: Invite + person + to + event. Rule: invite someone to + event/place. Quick Rule: invite someone to + event/place.","Correct the highlighted part. Guru will come at the station to receive you. Answer: to the station Hinglish Explanation: Motion destination = to. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to the station”. come to + place. Quick Rule: come to + place.","Correct the highlighted part. I am sending the remaining amount at your office . Answer: to your office Hinglish Explanation: Send ka destination = to. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to your office”. send something to + place/person. Quick Rule: send something to + place/person.","Correct the highlighted part. He has gone in the town to run some errands for me. Answer: to the town Hinglish Explanation: Go ka destination = to. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to the town”. go to + place. Quick Rule: go to + place.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Purpose with “for” Answer: Send the pen to the bank for submitting the documents. Hinglish Explanation: Destination “to the bank”; purpose “for submitting”. English Explanation: Destination “to the bank”; purpose “for submitting”. Rule: to + destination; for + purpose/noun/gerund. Quick Rule: to + destination; for + purpose/noun/gerund.","Correct the highlighted part. We shall start on the journey tomorrow. Answer: for the journey Hinglish Explanation: Source exam usage treats “start for + place/journey” as setting out. English Explanation: Source exam usage treats “start for + place/journey” as setting out. Rule: start/leave for + destination/journey. Quick Rule: start/leave for + destination/journey.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Leave for work Answer: I will leave for work tomorrow morning. Hinglish Explanation: Destination/purpose noun “work” ke saath leave for natural hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I will leave for work tomorrow morning.”. leave for + place/work. Quick Rule: leave for + place/work.","Correct the highlighted part. I went to home after the class. Answer: home Hinglish Explanation: “Home” adverb of direction ho to “to” nahi lagta. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “home”. go/come/return home (no to). Quick Rule: go/come/return home (no to).","Correct the highlighted part. The kids should go to home after lunch. Answer: home Hinglish Explanation: “Go home” fixed natural structure hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “home”. go home (no preposition before adverbial home). Quick Rule: go home (no preposition before adverbial home).","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Possessive + home Answer: The teacher came to my home yesterday. Hinglish Explanation: Possessive adjective “my” ke saath home noun phrase ban jaata hai, isliye to possible hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The teacher came to my home yesterday.”. to + possessive adjective + home is grammatical. Quick Rule: to + possessive adjective + home is grammatical.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Possessive + home Answer: It's getting late; you should go to your home. Hinglish Explanation: “Your home” noun phrase hai; “to” acceptable hai, though “go home” more idiomatic. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “It's getting late; you should go to your home.”. go home; go to your/my/his home. Quick Rule: go home; go to your/my/his home.","Correct the highlighted part. I think everyone had come their home for the preparatory rituals. Answer: come to their home Hinglish Explanation: Possessive adjective se pehle destination “to” chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “come to their home”. come to + possessive + home. Quick Rule: come to + possessive + home.","Correct the highlighted part. The office party was so boring that I went to home to spend time with my cat. Answer: went home Hinglish Explanation: Bare “home” direction-adverb hai, isliye “to” nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “went home”. go/went home (no to). Quick Rule: go/went home (no to)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=08.+Prepositions&section=4", "t": "Direction & relationship prepositions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 08. Prepositions", "x": ["Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a park ___ the road. Answer: along Hinglish Explanation: Same line/route par = along. English Explanation: Same line/route par = along. Rule: along = in the same line/direction as. Quick Rule: along = in the same line/direction as.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Go ___ this path and you will reach the place. Answer: along Hinglish Explanation: Path ke line ko follow karna = along. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “along”. go/walk along + path/road. Quick Rule: go/walk along + path/road.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is an old fort ___ the river. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Opposite side = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. across = on the opposite side of. Quick Rule: across = on the opposite side of.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She lives one block away ___ the street. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Opposite side of street = across. English Explanation: Opposite side of street = across. Rule: across the street = on the opposite side. Quick Rule: across the street = on the opposite side.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a bridge ___ the river. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Bridge ek side se doosri side tak span karta hai = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. across = from one side to the other. Quick Rule: across = from one side to the other.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Let us swim ___ the river. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: One bank se other bank tak = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. swim across + river. Quick Rule: swim across + river.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He ran ___ the road when he saw a dog. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Road ko side-to-side cross = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. run across + road. Quick Rule: run across + road.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She walked ___ the garden to reach the temple. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Garden ke ek side se doosri taraf movement = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. walk across + open area. Quick Rule: walk across + open area.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The rebels protested ___ the new act promulgated by the government. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Opposition = against. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “against”. protest/go against + rule/act/idea. Quick Rule: protest/go against + rule/act/idea.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. One should not go ___ one's ethics and morals. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Oppose/violate = against. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “against”. go against + principles/ethics. Quick Rule: go against + principles/ethics.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He challenged her to swim ___ the current. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Opposite direction to flow = against. English Explanation: Opposite direction to flow = against. Rule: against the current/wind = opposite direction. Quick Rule: against the current/wind = opposite direction.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The airplane flew ___ the wind and crashed. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Wind ki opposite direction = against. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “against”. fly against + wind. Quick Rule: fly against + wind.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. You can lean ___ the wall if you don't feel like standing. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Physical support/contact against surface = against. English Explanation: Physical support/contact against surface = against. Rule: lean against + wall. Quick Rule: lean against + wall.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Do not lean ___ the antique table. Answer: against Hinglish Explanation: Source example surface pressure/contact ke sense me “against”. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “against”. lean against + surface; lean on can also mean rest weight on, but source contrasts against a wall/table. Quick Rule: lean against + surface; lean on can also mean rest weight on, but source contrasts against a wall/table.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There are no similarities ___ you and me. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Two entities = between. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “between”. between = usually two; among = more than two/group. Quick Rule: between = usually two; among = more than two/group.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I cannot choose ___ books and travel. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Two alternatives = between. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “between”. choose between A and B. Quick Rule: choose between A and B.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a multilateral agreement ___ the three countries. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Distinct reciprocal parties ke relation me “between” more than two ke saath bhi possible hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “between”. between can be used for distinct reciprocal relationships among more than two entities. Quick Rule: between can be used for distinct reciprocal relationships among more than two entities.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a cordial relationship ___ all the villages in the district. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Reciprocal relationship between distinct entities = between. English Explanation: Reciprocal relationship between distinct entities = between. Rule: between for reciprocal one-to-one relationships. Quick Rule: between for reciprocal one-to-one relationships.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am available ___ 12 pm and 4 pm for consultations. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Paired limits “between ... and ...”. English Explanation: Paired limits “between ... and ...”. Rule: between A and B. Quick Rule: between A and B.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The weight of the baby is ___ 10 kg and 15 kg. Answer: between Hinglish Explanation: Range ke do limits = between. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “between”. between + lower and upper limit. Quick Rule: between + lower and upper limit.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The property was divided ___ the three sisters. Answer: among Hinglish Explanation: Group of more than two = among. English Explanation: Group of more than two = among. Rule: among + group/more than two. Quick Rule: among + group/more than two.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Distribute the sweets ___ the school children. Answer: among Hinglish Explanation: Group distribution = among. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “among”. distribute among + group. Quick Rule: distribute among + group.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. ___ the college girls, there was great excitement. Answer: Among Hinglish Explanation: Group of people = among. English Explanation: Group of people = among. Rule: among/amongst + plural group. Quick Rule: among/amongst + plural group.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. ___ us, no one knew the full story. Answer: Amongst Hinglish Explanation: Source notes “amongst” before vowel-sound wording is stylistic; both among/amongst are standard. English Explanation: Source notes “amongst” before vowel-sound wording is stylistic; both among/amongst are standard. Rule: among/amongst generally interchangeable; amongst is a stylistic variant. Quick Rule: among/amongst generally interchangeable; amongst is a stylistic variant.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I am looking for an important document ___ the trash. Answer: amid Hinglish Explanation: Mass/uncountable surroundings = amid. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “amid”. amid + mass/uncountable surroundings. Quick Rule: amid + mass/uncountable surroundings.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He looked for his friend ___ the crowd. Answer: amid Hinglish Explanation: Crowd as surrounding mass = amid; “among the crowd” is also common, but source teaches amid. English Explanation: Crowd as surrounding mass = amid; “among the crowd” is also common, but source teaches amid. Rule: amid = in the middle of/surrounded by. Quick Rule: amid = in the middle of/surrounded by.","Correct the highlighted part. The plan was hastily devised after the collapse of an agreement among the White House and the pharmaceutical industry . Answer: between the White House and the pharmaceutical industry Hinglish Explanation: Do parties hain, isliye between. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “between the White House and the pharmaceutical industry”. between + two parties. Quick Rule: between + two parties.","Correct the highlighted part. There was already a sizable gap amongst her wagon and the one in front of her . Answer: between her wagon and the one in front of her Hinglish Explanation: Do objects ke gap ke liye between. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “between her wagon and the one in front of her”. between + two objects. Quick Rule: between + two objects.","Correct the highlighted part. The cost should be shared equally between the three of you . Answer: among the three of you Hinglish Explanation: Simple group-sharing among three people = among. English Explanation: Simple group-sharing among three people = among. Rule: among + group when no distinct reciprocal pairwise relation is emphasized. Quick Rule: among + group when no distinct reciprocal pairwise relation is emphasized.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Between more than two Answer: An agreement was reached between the five companies. Hinglish Explanation: Distinct negotiating parties ke reciprocal relation me between acceptable hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “An agreement was reached between the five companies.”. between may be used with more than two distinct parties in reciprocal relationships. Quick Rule: between may be used with more than two distinct parties in reciprocal relationships.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is another plate ___ the kitchen. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Position = in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. in = position inside; into = motion from outside to inside. Quick Rule: in = position inside; into = motion from outside to inside.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I ran ___ the drawing room when I heard the baby cry. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: Movement into an interior = into. English Explanation: Movement into an interior = into. Rule: into = motion to the inside. Quick Rule: into = motion to the inside.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I do not have a penny ___ my pocket. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Static position = in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. in + container/place for position. Quick Rule: in + container/place for position.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The frustrated lover jumped ___ the river. Answer: into Hinglish Explanation: Movement entering water = into. English Explanation: Movement entering water = into. Rule: jump into + water/place. Quick Rule: jump into + water/place.","Correct the highlighted part. My mother drove the car in the garage . Answer: into the garage Hinglish Explanation: Driving from outside to inside = into. English Explanation: Driving from outside to inside = into. Rule: into for motion; in for position. Quick Rule: into for motion; in for position.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. In/into with “came” Answer: The neighbour came into the house to see what's going on. Hinglish Explanation: Motion into house = into. English Explanation: Motion into house = into. Rule: come into + enclosed place. Quick Rule: come into + enclosed place.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a first-aid kit ___ top of the shelf. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Static contact position = on. English Explanation: Static contact position = on. Rule: on = position/contact; onto = motion to a surface. Quick Rule: on = position/contact; onto = motion to a surface.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I have kept a book ___ the shelf. Answer: onto Hinglish Explanation: Source exercise treats placing/motion to the surface as “onto”. English Explanation: Source exercise treats placing/motion to the surface as “onto”. Rule: onto = motion to a surface; on = resulting position. Quick Rule: onto = motion to a surface; on = resulting position.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The dog is sitting ___ the sofa. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Static position/contact = on. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “on”. on + surface position. Quick Rule: on + surface position.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The cat climbed ___ the pillow. Answer: onto Hinglish Explanation: Movement up to surface = onto. English Explanation: Movement up to surface = onto. Rule: climb onto + surface. Quick Rule: climb onto + surface.","Correct the highlighted part. The book is onto the table. Answer: on Hinglish Explanation: Static position hai, motion nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “on”. on = position; onto = motion. Quick Rule: on = position; onto = motion.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Stage position Answer: The host walked on the stage and welcomed us. Hinglish Explanation: Source sentence activity “on the stage” location dikhata hai. “Walked onto the stage” bhi motion-focus me possible hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The host walked on the stage and welcomed us.”. on = position/location; onto = motion to a surface. Quick Rule: on = position/location; onto = motion to a surface."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=08.+Prepositions&section=5", "t": "Academic prepositions: time, place & level", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 08. Prepositions", "x": ["Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I love taking a walk ___ dawn. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Dawn ek point/small time expression hai. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. at + exact/small time points: dawn, dusk, midnight, sunrise, sunset. Quick Rule: at + exact/small time points: dawn, dusk, midnight, sunrise, sunset.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The meeting starts at 7 ___ the morning. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Part of day = in the morning. English Explanation: Part of day = in the morning. Rule: in the morning/afternoon/evening. Quick Rule: in the morning/afternoon/evening.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I often work late ___ night. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: General nighttime expression = at night. English Explanation: General nighttime expression = at night. Rule: at night = generally during nights. Quick Rule: at night = generally during nights.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I love eating ice cream ___ night. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: General habit at night. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. at night = general time. Quick Rule: at night = general time.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I had to get up to receive my parents ___ the night. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Particular night/event during the night = in the night. English Explanation: Particular night/event during the night = in the night. Rule: in the night = during a particular night. Quick Rule: in the night = during a particular night.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He could not sleep ___ the night because of pressure of the match. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Specific night context = in the night. English Explanation: Specific night context = in the night. Rule: in the night for a specific night period. Quick Rule: in the night for a specific night period.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I live ___ Rohini in Delhi. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Specific locality/smaller place = at in source rule. English Explanation: Specific locality/smaller place = at in source rule. Rule: at for a point/small locality; in for larger area/city/state/country. Quick Rule: at for a point/small locality; in for larger area/city/state/country.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She lives ___ Bapu Nagar in Jaipur. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Specific colony/locality = at. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. at + locality/colony; in + city. Quick Rule: at + locality/colony; in + city.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The museum is ___ the city. Answer: in Hinglish Explanation: Large area/city = in. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in”. in + city/state/country/continent. Quick Rule: in + city/state/country/continent.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. They live ___ 10 Park Road. Answer: at Hinglish Explanation: Specific address = at. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “at”. at + specific address. Quick Rule: at + specific address.","Correct the highlighted part. They left the camp early at the morning . Answer: in the morning Hinglish Explanation: Part of day = in. English Explanation: Part of day = in. Rule: in the morning/afternoon/evening. Quick Rule: in the morning/afternoon/evening.","Correct the highlighted part. They attacked the enemy positions in dawn . Answer: at dawn Hinglish Explanation: Dawn point of time = at. English Explanation: Dawn point of time = at. Quick Rule: at dawn/dusk/midnight.","Correct the highlighted part. If you want to cook pasta tonight, we have to shop in the supermarket on the way home. Answer: at the supermarket Hinglish Explanation: Shopping destination/point treated as “at the supermarket” in source exercise. English Explanation: Shopping destination/point treated as “at the supermarket” in source exercise. Rule: at + specific point/place of activity; in can describe being physically inside, but source asks activity-point usage. Quick Rule: at + specific point/place of activity; in can describe being physically inside, but source asks activity-point usage.","Correct the highlighted part. I have to take an afternoon nap today because I have to receive some relatives at the night . Answer: in the night Hinglish Explanation: Specific night = in the night. English Explanation: Specific night = in the night. Rule: in the night for a particular night; at night generally. Quick Rule: in the night for a particular night; at night generally.","Correct the highlighted part. Holidaying at France is easy if you speak French. Answer: in France Hinglish Explanation: Country = in. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “in France”. in + country/state/city. Quick Rule: in + country/state/city.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He went ___ the stairs to see his new room. Answer: up Hinglish Explanation: Higher level ki direction = up. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “up”. up/down = movement to higher/lower level. Quick Rule: up/down = movement to higher/lower level.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The kids ran ___ the footpath along the park. Answer: down Hinglish Explanation: Lower/along-direction movement = down. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “down”. up/down can mark movement along a route or slope. Quick Rule: up/down can mark movement along a route or slope.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He climbed ___ the steps. Answer: up Hinglish Explanation: Higher level movement = up. English Explanation: Higher level movement = up. Rule: climb up + steps/stairs. Quick Rule: climb up + steps/stairs.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She was walking ___ the street. Answer: down Hinglish Explanation: Along a street in one direction = down. English Explanation: Along a street in one direction = down. Rule: walk down + street/road. Quick Rule: walk down + street/road.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. His monthly income is ___ 10k. Answer: above Hinglish Explanation: Higher than a stated level = above. English Explanation: Higher than a stated level = above. Rule: above/below = higher/lower than a level or standard. Quick Rule: above/below = higher/lower than a level or standard.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. His monthly income is ___ 10k. Answer: below Hinglish Explanation: Lower than a stated level = below. English Explanation: Lower than a stated level = below. Rule: below + level/standard. Quick Rule: below + level/standard.","Correct the highlighted part. The expenses are far above than expected . Answer: above what was expected Hinglish Explanation: “Above” comparative sense already deta hai; “than” direct is redundant here. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “above what was expected”. above/below + level/standard, not “above than”. Quick Rule: above/below + level/standard, not “above than”.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. This work is ___ my dignity. Answer: below Hinglish Explanation: Figuratively lower than one’s dignity/status = below. English Explanation: Figuratively lower than one’s dignity/status = below. Rule: below + standard/dignity/average. Quick Rule: below + standard/dignity/average.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The temperature has been ___ normal for the past couple of weeks. Answer: above Hinglish Explanation: Standard “normal” se higher = above. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “above”. above normal; below average. Quick Rule: above normal; below average.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Her grades are ___ average. Answer: below Hinglish Explanation: Average se lower = below. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “below”. below average. Quick Rule: below average.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Please put the blanket ___ the baby. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Covering/touching surface = over. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “over”. over = covering/touching or higher vertical relation; under = beneath. Quick Rule: over = covering/touching or higher vertical relation; under = beneath.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She is sitting ___ the tree. Answer: under Hinglish Explanation: Directly beneath/covered by tree canopy = under. English Explanation: Directly beneath/covered by tree canopy = under. Rule: under + object, often with vertical/cover relation. Quick Rule: under + object, often with vertical/cover relation.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Please cover the wound by placing cotton ___ it. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Covering contact = over. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “over”. cover/place something over + object. Quick Rule: cover/place something over + object.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a mouse ___ the bed. Answer: under Hinglish Explanation: Physical beneath relation = under. English Explanation: Physical beneath relation = under. Rule: under + physical object. Quick Rule: under + physical object.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. You are ___ CCTV surveillance. Answer: under Hinglish Explanation: Control/supervision = under. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “under”. under supervision/control/surveillance. Quick Rule: under supervision/control/surveillance.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The plane flew ___ the clouds. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Movement across a higher position = over. English Explanation: Movement across a higher position = over. Rule: over can indicate movement above something. Quick Rule: over can indicate movement above something.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The babies are ___ constant supervision. Answer: under Hinglish Explanation: Supervision relation = under. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “under”. under + supervision/control. Quick Rule: under + supervision/control.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She drove her car ___ the hill. Answer: over Hinglish Explanation: Movement over and across a hill = over. English Explanation: Movement over and across a hill = over. Rule: over + obstacle/hill. Quick Rule: over + obstacle/hill.","Correct the highlighted part. The college will not accept candidates with test scores over 60 . Answer: below 60 Hinglish Explanation: Meaning is rejection of low scores, so “below 60” fits context. English Explanation: Meaning is rejection of low scores, so “below 60” fits context. Rule: below = lower than a threshold; over/above = higher than. Quick Rule: below = lower than a threshold; over/above = higher than.","Correct the highlighted part. The Simpsons live on the first floor above the shop . Answer: over the shop Hinglish Explanation: Source exercise chooses “over” for a directly vertical location. In normal English “above” can also be valid; here we follow the intended direct-over relation. English Explanation: Source exercise chooses “over” for a directly vertical location. In normal English “above” can also be valid; here we follow the intended direct-over relation. Rule: over often suggests directly above; above can be more general higher position. Quick Rule: over often suggests directly above; above can be more general higher position.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I was standing ___ him in the queue. Answer: before Hinglish Explanation: Queue/order context me “before” = earlier/ahead of a person. English Explanation: Queue/order context me “before” = earlier/ahead of a person. Rule: before can mark order; in front of marks spatial position before a thing/person. Quick Rule: before can mark order; in front of marks spatial position before a thing/person.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. She appeared ___ the magistrate. Answer: before Hinglish Explanation: Formal appearance in the presence of authority = before. English Explanation: Formal appearance in the presence of authority = before. Rule: appear before + court/magistrate. Quick Rule: appear before + court/magistrate.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Now a young artist is appearing ___ us. Answer: before Hinglish Explanation: Formal/presentation sense = before us. English Explanation: Formal/presentation sense = before us. Rule: appear before + audience/persons. Quick Rule: appear before + audience/persons.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. All this happened ___ my eyes. Answer: before Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression “before my eyes”. English Explanation: Fixed expression “before my eyes”. Rule: before one’s eyes = visibly in one’s presence. Quick Rule: before one’s eyes = visibly in one’s presence.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There is a park ___ my office. Answer: in front of Hinglish Explanation: Thing/place ke spatial front = in front of. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “in front of”. in front of + thing/place; before often used for person/order/formal presence. Quick Rule: in front of + thing/place; before often used for person/order/formal presence.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. I have parked my car ___ the house. Answer: in front of Hinglish Explanation: Spatial location before a building = in front of. English Explanation: Spatial location before a building = in front of. Rule: in front of + building/thing. Quick Rule: in front of + building/thing.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Rahul was behind Kiran but ___ Raj in the queue. Answer: in front of Hinglish Explanation: Queue me spatial relation explicitly opposite of behind = in front of (before is also possible for order). English Explanation: Queue me spatial relation explicitly opposite of behind = in front of (before is also possible for order). Rule: in front of = physically ahead; before = earlier/ahead in order. Quick Rule: in front of = physically ahead; before = earlier/ahead in order.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. There are ___ fifty girls in the class. Answer: around / about Hinglish Explanation: Approximate number = around/about. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “around / about”. around/about = approximately. Quick Rule: around/about = approximately.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Please come here ___ four o'clock. Answer: around / about Hinglish Explanation: Approximate time = around/about. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “around / about”. around/about + time/number = approximately. Quick Rule: around/about + time/number = approximately.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Nobody has swum ___ this river. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: One side to the other on open surface = across. English Explanation: One side to the other on open surface = across. Rule: across = from one side to the other; through = through an interior/space. Quick Rule: across = from one side to the other; through = through an interior/space.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He went ___ the road to talk to his friend. Answer: across Hinglish Explanation: Road ko cross = across. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “across”. go across + road. Quick Rule: go across + road.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The train passes ___ a dense forest. Answer: through Hinglish Explanation: Interior/within space se pass = through. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “through”. through = movement within/from one end of an enclosed or dense space to another. Quick Rule: through = movement within/from one end of an enclosed or dense space to another.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The thief came into the room ___ the window. Answer: through Hinglish Explanation: Opening ke through movement = through. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “through”. through + opening/window. Quick Rule: through + opening/window.","Correct the highlighted part. She came and sat besides me. Answer: beside Hinglish Explanation: Side-by-side position = beside; besides = in addition to. English Explanation: Side-by-side position = beside; besides = in addition to. Rule: beside = at the side of; besides = in addition to. Quick Rule: beside = at the side of; besides = in addition to.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. ___ the Gita, I have read the Bible. Answer: Besides Hinglish Explanation: “In addition to” = besides. English Explanation: “In addition to” = besides. Rule: besides = in addition to. Quick Rule: besides = in addition to.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. The Earth moves ___ the sun. Answer: round Hinglish Explanation: Circular movement = round/around. English Explanation: The correct preposition/form here is “round”. round/around = encircling movement. Quick Rule: round/around = encircling movement.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. They are sitting ___ the table. Answer: round Hinglish Explanation: People positioned encircling table = round/around. English Explanation: People positioned encircling table = round/around. Rule: sit round/around + table. Quick Rule: sit round/around + table.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. He walked ___ the bus and looked at the damaged parts. Answer: round Hinglish Explanation: Different sides of an object inspect karna = round/around. English Explanation: Different sides of an object inspect karna = round/around. Rule: walk round/around + object. Quick Rule: walk round/around + object.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Why are those people running ___ ? Answer: around / about Hinglish Explanation: Here and there without fixed direction = around/about. English Explanation: Here and there without fixed direction = around/about. Rule: around/about = here and there, in different places/directions. Quick Rule: around/about = here and there, in different places/directions.","Choose the correct word/preposition for the blank. Stop going ___ and start some work. Answer: around / about Hinglish Explanation: Aimless here-and-there activity = around/about. English Explanation: Aimless here-and-there activity = around/about. Rule: go around/about = move here and there. Quick Rule: go around/about = move here and there."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=1", "t": "Verb forms · Main vs Helping Verb", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She goes to college daily. Answer: Main verb · V5 (s/es form) Hinglish Explanation: “goes” sentence ka action/main verb hai aur singular subject “she” ke saath V5 form me hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V5 (s/es form)”. Simple present: singular he/she/it + V1+s/es. Quick Rule: Simple present: singular he/she/it + V1+s/es.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He played cricket. Answer: Main verb · V2 (past form) Hinglish Explanation: “played” completed past action dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V2 (past form)”. Simple past normally uses V2. Quick Rule: Simple past normally uses V2.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I am writing a letter. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “am” tense/aspect banane me “writing” ko help kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. Present continuous: am/is/are + V-ing. Quick Rule: Present continuous: am/is/are + V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I am writing a letter. Answer: Main verb · V4 (-ing form) Hinglish Explanation: “writing” lexical action batata hai; “am” uska helper hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V4 (-ing form)”. Continuous tense: helping verb + main verb in V-ing form. Quick Rule: Continuous tense: helping verb + main verb in V-ing form.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He is writing a letter. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “is” continuous construction ka auxiliary hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. is/am/are can be auxiliaries before V-ing. Quick Rule: is/am/are can be auxiliaries before V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They have written a letter. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “have” present perfect banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. Present perfect: have/has + V3. Quick Rule: Present perfect: have/has + V3.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They have written a letter. Answer: Main verb · V3 (past participle) Hinglish Explanation: “written” main verb “write” ka V3 hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V3 (past participle)”. Perfect tense uses have/has + V3. Quick Rule: Perfect tense uses have/has + V3.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I am a teacher. Answer: Main/linking verb Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “am” ke baad koi main verb nahi; “am” khud subject ko complement “teacher” se link karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main/linking verb”. Forms of be can act as main/linking verbs when no lexical verb follows. Quick Rule: Forms of be can act as main/linking verbs when no lexical verb follows.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She was a lawyer. Answer: Main/linking verb Hinglish Explanation: “was” khud predicate ka main/linking verb hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main/linking verb”. Be can be main verb: subject + be + complement. Quick Rule: Be can be main verb: subject + be + complement.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She has a new car. Answer: Main verb (possession) Hinglish Explanation: “has” yahan possession dikhata hai, isliye main verb hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb (possession)”. Have/has/had can be main verbs when they mean possess/eat/experience etc. Quick Rule: Have/has/had can be main verbs when they mean possess/eat/experience etc.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I have a lot of homework to complete before tomorrow. Answer: Main verb (possession) Hinglish Explanation: “have” ka meaning yahan “possess” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb (possession)”. Have as main verb can express possession. Quick Rule: Have as main verb can express possession.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I have visited that museum multiple times. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “have” + “visited” present perfect hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. Present perfect: have/has + V3. Quick Rule: Present perfect: have/has + V3.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I have visited that museum multiple times. Answer: Main verb · V3 Hinglish Explanation: “visited” lexical action ka past participle hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V3”. In perfect constructions, the lexical verb appears as V3. Quick Rule: In perfect constructions, the lexical verb appears as V3.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She did her best to complete the project on time. Answer: Main verb · V2 Hinglish Explanation: “did” yahan “do” ka main verb hai; uske baad koi bare verb nahi aata. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V2”. Do/did can be main verbs when they carry lexical meaning. Quick Rule: Do/did can be main verbs when they carry lexical meaning.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I did not see him at the party last night. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: Negative simple past me “did” auxiliary hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. did + not + V1. Quick Rule: did + not + V1.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I did not see him at the party last night. Answer: Main verb · V1 Hinglish Explanation: “did” ke baad main verb base form me aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V1”. After did/did not, use V1. Quick Rule: After did/did not, use V1.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He was happy to see his friends. Answer: Main/linking verb Hinglish Explanation: “was” adjective “happy” ko subject se link karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main/linking verb”. Be + adjective/noun complement = linking/main verb. Quick Rule: Be + adjective/noun complement = linking/main verb.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I was studying when the phone rang. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “was” past continuous ka auxiliary hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. Past continuous: was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: Past continuous: was/were + V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I was studying when the phone rang. Answer: Main verb · V-ing Hinglish Explanation: “studying” actual action batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · V-ing”. Continuous tense uses auxiliary be + lexical V-ing. Quick Rule: Continuous tense uses auxiliary be + lexical V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He has eaten lunch. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “has” perfect aspect banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. has + V3 = present perfect. Quick Rule: has + V3 = present perfect.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They have eaten lunch. Answer: Helping verb Hinglish Explanation: “have” “eaten” ko perfect tense me support karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Helping verb”. have + V3 = present perfect. Quick Rule: have + V3 = present perfect."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=2", "t": "Have as a main verb · perfect forms", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I have a brand new phone. Answer: Main verb · possession Hinglish Explanation: “have” ka direct meaning possess karna hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · possession”. Have as main verb can mean possession. Quick Rule: Have as main verb can mean possession.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She has a lovely husband. Answer: Main verb · relationship Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “has” relationship/possession dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · relationship”. Have/has may be lexical/main verbs. Quick Rule: Have/has may be lexical/main verbs.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I had dinner. Answer: Main verb · ate Hinglish Explanation: “had dinner” me “had” ka meaning “ate” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · ate”. Have can mean eat/drink/experience. Quick Rule: Have can mean eat/drink/experience.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. We had a great time last week. Answer: Main verb · experienced/spent time Hinglish Explanation: “had a great time” lexical expression hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Main verb · experienced/spent time”. Have can mean experience/spend time. Quick Rule: Have can mean experience/spend time.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Simple present alternatives Answer: I usually eat Idli Dosa for breakfast. Hinglish Explanation: Habit ke liye simple present V1 use hota hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I usually eat Idli Dosa for breakfast.”. Simple present with I/we/you/they uses V1. Quick Rule: Simple present with I/we/you/they uses V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Have = eat Answer: I usually have Idli Dosa for breakfast. Hinglish Explanation: “have breakfast” idiomatic lexical use hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I usually have Idli Dosa for breakfast.”. Have can mean eat/take a meal. Quick Rule: Have can mean eat/take a meal.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Drink in simple present Answer: She drinks black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: She singular hai, isliye simple present me “drinks”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She drinks black coffee in the morning.”. He/she/it + V1+s/es. Quick Rule: He/she/it + V1+s/es.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Have = drink/take Answer: She has black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: “has black coffee” ka meaning “drinks/takes black coffee” ho sakta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has black coffee in the morning.”. Have/has may be a lexical verb meaning eat/drink. Quick Rule: Have/has may be a lexical verb meaning eat/drink.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Simple past Answer: I ate dinner. Hinglish Explanation: Past completed action me V2 “ate”. English Explanation: Past completed action me V2 “ate”. Rule: Simple past uses V2. Quick Rule: Simple past uses V2.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Have in simple past Answer: I had dinner. Hinglish Explanation: “had” yahan V2 main verb hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I had dinner.”. Past of have = had. Quick Rule: Past of have = had.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Drink in past Answer: She drank black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: Past of drink = drank. English Explanation: Past of drink = drank. Quick Rule: drink–drank–drunk.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Have in past Answer: She had black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: Past lexical “have” = had. English Explanation: Past lexical “have” = had. Quick Rule: have–had–had.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Present perfect with eat Answer: I have eaten dinner. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect me have + V3 “eaten”. English Explanation: Present perfect me have + V3 “eaten”. Rule: eat–ate–eaten; present perfect = have/has + V3. Quick Rule: eat–ate–eaten; present perfect = have/has + V3.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Present perfect with have Answer: I have had dinner. Hinglish Explanation: Main verb “have” ka V3 bhi “had” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I have had dinner.”. have–had–had; have had = auxiliary have + main-verb V3 had. Quick Rule: have–had–had; have had = auxiliary have + main-verb V3 had.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Present perfect singular Answer: She has eaten dinner. Hinglish Explanation: Singular subject ke saath “has + eaten”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has eaten dinner.”. She/he/it + has + V3. Quick Rule: She/he/it + has + V3.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Present perfect: has had Answer: She has had dinner. Hinglish Explanation: “has” auxiliary aur “had” main verb ka V3 hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has had dinner.”. has had = present perfect of have. Quick Rule: has had = present perfect of have.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Drink in present perfect Answer: I have drunk black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: Drink ka V3 “drunk” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I have drunk black coffee in the morning.”. drink–drank–drunk. Quick Rule: drink–drank–drunk.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Have as drink in present perfect Answer: I have had black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: Perfect of lexical have = have had. English Explanation: Perfect of lexical have = have had. Quick Rule: have–had–had.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Singular present perfect: drink Answer: She has drunk black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: “has + drunk” correct form hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has drunk black coffee in the morning.”. has + V3; drink V3 = drunk. Quick Rule: has + V3; drink V3 = drunk.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Singular present perfect: have Answer: She has had black coffee in the morning. Hinglish Explanation: “has had” = present perfect of have. English Explanation: “has had” = present perfect of have. Rule: has + V3(had). Quick Rule: has + V3(had).","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect with eat Answer: She had eaten dinner before I reached home. Hinglish Explanation: Earlier past action ke liye had + V3. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She had eaten dinner before I reached home.”. Past perfect = had + V3. Quick Rule: Past perfect = had + V3.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect with have Answer: She had had dinner before I reached home. Hinglish Explanation: Past perfect of have = had had. English Explanation: Past perfect of have = had had. Rule: First “had” auxiliary; second “had” main verb V3. Quick Rule: First “had” auxiliary; second “had” main verb V3.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect with drink Answer: She had drunk black coffee before she left for the office. Hinglish Explanation: Drink V3 “drunk” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She had drunk black coffee before she left for the office.”. Past perfect = had + V3. Quick Rule: Past perfect = had + V3.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect with have (coffee) Answer: She had had black coffee before she left for the office. Hinglish Explanation: “had had” past perfect of lexical “have” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She had had black coffee before she left for the office.”. had + V3; have V3 = had. Quick Rule: had + V3; have V3 = had.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect sequence Answer: I had had breakfast before I left for work. Hinglish Explanation: Source question earlier completed action ko past perfect se dikhata hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I had had breakfast before I left for work.”. Past perfect action happened before another past action. Quick Rule: Past perfect action happened before another past action.","Correct the highlighted part. Last weekend I just wanted to relax because I had have a busy week. Answer: had had Hinglish Explanation: Busy week pehle complete hui thi; “have” ka V3 “had” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “had had”. Past perfect of have = had had. Quick Rule: Past perfect of have = had had.","Choose the correct form for the blank. By the time they reached the theater, the movie ___ already. Answer: had started Hinglish Explanation: Movie unke pahunchne se pehle start ho chuki thi. English Explanation: The blank should be filled with “had started”. By the time + past often pairs with past perfect for the earlier action. Quick Rule: By the time + past often pairs with past perfect for the earlier action.","Correct the highlighted part. He did not had any money to pay the college fees. Answer: did not have Hinglish Explanation: Did ke baad base form aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “did not have”. did + not + V1. Quick Rule: did + not + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. I was perfectly healthy until now . Answer: have been perfectly healthy until now Hinglish Explanation: “Until now” present tak span dikhata hai, isliye present perfect natural hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “have been perfectly healthy until now”. Present perfect connects a past state/action with the present. Quick Rule: Present perfect connects a past state/action with the present.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Correct use of “had had” Answer: He had already had dinner before we arrived. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan first “had” auxiliary aur second “had” lexical have ka V3 hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He had already had dinner before we arrived.”. Use had had only when lexical verb itself is “have”. Quick Rule: Use had had only when lexical verb itself is “have”.","Correct the highlighted part. Before I met him personally, I admit that I had have a poor opinion about him. Answer: had had Hinglish Explanation: Past-perfect form of lexical “have” = had had. English Explanation: Past-perfect form of lexical “have” = had had. Rule: had + V3; have V3 = had. Quick Rule: had + V3; have V3 = had.","Correct the highlighted part. She must had completed her work by now because she is very punctual. Answer: must have completed Hinglish Explanation: Modal “must” ke baad perfect infinitive “have + V3” aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “must have completed”. must have + V3 = strong deduction about a completed action. Quick Rule: must have + V3 = strong deduction about a completed action."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=3", "t": "Confusing verbs · Lie/Lay, Rise/Raise, Fall/Fell etc.", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Lie (recline) Answer: To lie on a beach. Hinglish Explanation: Recline ke meaning me intransitive verb “lie” hota hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “To lie on a beach.”. lie–lay–lain; lie = recline, no object. Quick Rule: lie–lay–lain; lie = recline, no object.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past of lie Answer: A pen lay on the desk. Hinglish Explanation: “Lay” yahan “lie” ka past form hai, object nahi hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “A pen lay on the desk.”. lie–lay–lain. Quick Rule: lie–lay–lain.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Lay takes an object Answer: She laid the baby on the bed. Hinglish Explanation: “Lay” = put/place; object “the baby” present hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She laid the baby on the bed.”. lay–laid–laid; lay requires an object. Quick Rule: lay–laid–laid; lay requires an object.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Lay takes an object Answer: He laid the tray on the table. Hinglish Explanation: Tray direct object hai, isliye past of lay = laid. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He laid the tray on the table.”. lay–laid–laid. Quick Rule: lay–laid–laid.","Correct the highlighted part. She laid on the couch and read a book. Answer: lay Hinglish Explanation: Object nahi hai aur meaning recline hai; past of lie = lay. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “lay”. lie–lay–lain; lay–laid–laid. Quick Rule: lie–lay–lain; lay–laid–laid.","Correct the highlighted part. He was laying comfortably on the bed. Answer: lying Hinglish Explanation: Continuous form of lie is “lying”. English Explanation: Continuous form of lie is “lying”. Rule: lie → lying; lay → laying. Quick Rule: lie → lying; lay → laying.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Lie vs lay Answer: The cat likes to lie in the sun. Hinglish Explanation: No object; cat khud recline karta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The cat likes to lie in the sun.”. Use lie for reclining without an object. Quick Rule: Use lie for reclining without an object.","Correct the highlighted part. I'm going to lay down in the room. Answer: lie down Hinglish Explanation: Subject khud recline karega; no object. English Explanation: Subject khud recline karega; no object. Rule: lie down = recline; lay something down = place an object. Quick Rule: lie down = recline; lay something down = place an object.","Correct the highlighted part. He lay the blanket out and sat. Answer: laid the blanket Hinglish Explanation: Blanket object hai; past of lay = laid. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “laid the blanket”. lay–laid–laid takes an object. Quick Rule: lay–laid–laid takes an object.","Correct the highlighted part. We are tired, let's lay in the shade for a while. Answer: lie in the shade Hinglish Explanation: People khud recline karenge, object nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “lie in the shade”. lie = recline. Quick Rule: lie = recline.","Correct the highlighted part. The plan is to lie the foundations for the new apartments in October. Answer: lay the foundations Hinglish Explanation: “Foundations” object hai; idiom “lay the foundations”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “lay the foundations”. lay + object = put/place. Quick Rule: lay + object = put/place.","Correct the highlighted part. She lay her head against my shoulder and fell asleep. Answer: laid her head Hinglish Explanation: Head object hai aur action past me hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “laid her head”. Past of lay = laid. Quick Rule: Past of lay = laid.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Rise in simple present Answer: The sun rises in the east. Hinglish Explanation: Sun khud upar aata hai; rise intransitive hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The sun rises in the east.”. rise–rose–risen; no object. Quick Rule: rise–rose–risen; no object.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past of rise Answer: The aircraft rose into the air. Hinglish Explanation: Past of rise = rose. English Explanation: Past of rise = rose. Quick Rule: rise–rose–risen.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Raise takes object Answer: He raised an important issue. Hinglish Explanation: “Issue” object hai; raise is transitive. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He raised an important issue.”. raise–raised–raised + object. Quick Rule: raise–raised–raised + object.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Raise takes object Answer: They will not raise taxes. Hinglish Explanation: Taxes object hain; modal ke baad V1 “raise”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “They will not raise taxes.”. will + V1; raise takes an object. Quick Rule: will + V1; raise takes an object.","Correct the highlighted part. The price of the stock raised significantly yesterday. Answer: rose Hinglish Explanation: Price khud badha; no object, so rise. English Explanation: Price khud badha; no object, so rise. Rule: Past of rise = rose. Quick Rule: Past of rise = rose.","Correct the highlighted part. It's a wonder we didn't rise our arms in surrender. Answer: raise our arms Hinglish Explanation: Arms object hain, so raise. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “raise our arms”. raise + object; rise has no object. Quick Rule: raise + object; rise has no object.","Correct the highlighted part. The balloon raised gracefully into the sky. Answer: rose Hinglish Explanation: Balloon khud upar gaya, object nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “rose”. Past of rise = rose. Quick Rule: Past of rise = rose.","Correct the highlighted part. Because of the incessant rain, the flood water is raising . Answer: is rising Hinglish Explanation: Water level khud upar aa raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “is rising”. Present continuous of rise = is rising. Quick Rule: Present continuous of rise = is rising.","Correct the highlighted part. Against all odds, he raises above his challenges and achieved great success. Answer: rose above Hinglish Explanation: Sentence past “achieved” ke saath past form “rose” fit hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “rose above”. Past of rise = rose; rise above = overcome. Quick Rule: Past of rise = rose; rise above = overcome.","Correct the highlighted part. The teacher rose her voice to get the attention of the noisy students. Answer: raised her voice Hinglish Explanation: Voice object hai, so raise. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “raised her voice”. raise + object; rise has no object. Quick Rule: raise + object; rise has no object.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Raise concerns Answer: They raised their concerns at the meeting. Hinglish Explanation: Concerns object hain. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “They raised their concerns at the meeting.”. raise + issue/question/concern. Quick Rule: raise + issue/question/concern.","Correct the highlighted part. Considering the high demand for flights to gulf countries, airlines may rise prices . Answer: raise prices Hinglish Explanation: Prices object hain, so raise. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “raise prices”. may + V1; raise takes an object. Quick Rule: may + V1; raise takes an object.","Correct the highlighted part. We have to let more women raise to the top . Answer: rise to the top Hinglish Explanation: Women khud upward position me jayengi; no object. English Explanation: Women khud upward position me jayengi; no object. Rule: rise to the top; let + object + V1. Quick Rule: rise to the top; let + object + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Fall Answer: The tree fell down. Hinglish Explanation: Tree khud gira; past of fall = fell. English Explanation: Tree khud gira; past of fall = fell. Quick Rule: fall–fell–fallen.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Fall Answer: He fell into the river. Hinglish Explanation: Past of intransitive fall = fell. English Explanation: Past of intransitive fall = fell. Quick Rule: fall–fell–fallen.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Fell = cut down Answer: He felled the tree. Hinglish Explanation: Transitive “fell” = cut down; its past is felled. English Explanation: Transitive “fell” = cut down; its past is felled. Rule: fell–felled–felled = cut down. Quick Rule: fell–felled–felled = cut down.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Present habitual fall Answer: The leaves fall from the trees in autumn. Hinglish Explanation: General fact/habit me present “fall”. English Explanation: General fact/habit me present “fall”. Rule: Simple present for general truths/habits. Quick Rule: Simple present for general truths/habits.","Correct the highlighted part. Silence felled between them and then he finally spoke. Answer: fell Hinglish Explanation: Silence metaphorically “fell”; no object. English Explanation: Silence metaphorically “fell”; no object. Rule: Past of fall = fell. Quick Rule: Past of fall = fell.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past perfect of fall Answer: She had fallen asleep before the movie started. Hinglish Explanation: Past perfect requires had + V3 “fallen”. English Explanation: Past perfect requires had + V3 “fallen”. Rule: fall–fell–fallen; had + V3. Quick Rule: fall–fell–fallen; had + V3.","Correct the highlighted part. The powerful earthquake fell buildings , bridges, and other structures. Answer: felled buildings Hinglish Explanation: Buildings direct object hain; transitive “fell” means cut/bring down, past “felled”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “felled buildings”. fell–felled–felled is transitive. Quick Rule: fell–felled–felled is transitive.","Correct the highlighted part. You tried to dance and fall upon the floor . Answer: fell upon the floor Hinglish Explanation: Past context “tried” ke parallel past “fell”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “fell upon the floor”. Past of fall = fell. Quick Rule: Past of fall = fell.","Correct the highlighted part. The strong wind gusts fell several power lines causing a widespread blackout. Answer: felled several power lines Hinglish Explanation: Power lines object hain; wind caused them to fall. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “felled several power lines”. Transitive fell = bring/cut down; past = felled. Quick Rule: Transitive fell = bring/cut down; past = felled.","Correct the highlighted part. No one is allowed to fall the trees in the forest. Answer: fell the trees Hinglish Explanation: Trees ko cut down karna = fell. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “fell the trees”. Base transitive verb: fell; fell–felled–felled. Quick Rule: Base transitive verb: fell; fell–felled–felled.","Correct the highlighted part. The bathtub has overflown , causing water to spill onto the floor. Answer: overflowed Hinglish Explanation: Verb “overflow” regular hai: overflowed. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “overflowed”. overflow–overflowed–overflowed. Quick Rule: overflow–overflowed–overflowed.","Correct the highlighted part. I am going to have a Christmas tree in the office so people can hung all the gifts upon it. Answer: hang Hinglish Explanation: Modal “can” ke baad V1. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hang”. can + V1; hang–hung–hung for objects. Quick Rule: can + V1; hang–hung–hung for objects.","Correct the highlighted part. Four men convicted of the gang rape and murder have been hung . Answer: hanged Hinglish Explanation: Execution ke meaning me traditional form “hanged”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hanged”. People are hanged as punishment; objects are hung. Quick Rule: People are hanged as punishment; objects are hung.","Correct the highlighted part. The coat was drenched in rain water. I hanged it on the hook. Answer: hung Hinglish Explanation: Coat/object ko latkana = hung. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hung”. hang–hung–hung for objects. Quick Rule: hang–hung–hung for objects.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Object hanging Answer: Hang your clothes on the line to dry. Hinglish Explanation: Imperative me base form “hang”. English Explanation: Imperative me base form “hang”. Rule: Imperative uses V1. Quick Rule: Imperative uses V1.","Correct the highlighted part. The servant hanged the lamp on the wall. Answer: hung Hinglish Explanation: Lamp object hai, execution nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hung”. Objects are hung; people are hanged when executed. Quick Rule: Objects are hung; people are hanged when executed.","Correct the highlighted part. For his crime, the accused will be hung till death . Answer: hanged till death Hinglish Explanation: Execution sense me “hanged”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hanged till death”. Person executed = hanged. Quick Rule: Person executed = hanged.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Find = discover Answer: The explorers found the hidden treasure. Hinglish Explanation: Past of find = found. English Explanation: Past of find = found. Quick Rule: find–found–found.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Found = establish Answer: The committee founded the new organization. Hinglish Explanation: “Found” as verb means establish; past “founded”. English Explanation: “Found” as verb means establish; past “founded”. Rule: found–founded–founded = establish. Quick Rule: found–founded–founded = establish.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Passive find Answer: Such inequalities can be found in the constitutions of other countries also. Hinglish Explanation: Meaning locate/discover hai; V3 of find = found. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Such inequalities can be found in the constitutions of other countries also.”. can be + V3; find–found–found. Quick Rule: can be + V3; find–found–found.","Correct the highlighted part. In a group you may found one or two people who tend to be extremely vocal. Answer: may find Hinglish Explanation: Modal “may” ke baad V1 “find”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “may find”. may + V1. Quick Rule: may + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. Our organization was found twenty years ago. Answer: was founded Hinglish Explanation: Organization ko establish karna = found → founded. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “was founded”. Passive of found(establish): was founded. Quick Rule: Passive of found(establish): was founded.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Find a solution Answer: She has found a solution to the problem. Hinglish Explanation: Solution discover kiya, organization establish nahi ki. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has found a solution to the problem.”. find–found–found. Quick Rule: find–found–found.","Correct the highlighted part. The eminent speaker's speech was broadcasted over all the major stations. Answer: broadcast Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam form broadcast–broadcast–broadcast. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “broadcast”. Broadcast is commonly unchanged in V1/V2/V3; “broadcasted” also exists but exam keys often prefer “broadcast”. Quick Rule: Broadcast is commonly unchanged in V1/V2/V3; “broadcasted” also exists but exam keys often prefer “broadcast”.","Correct the highlighted part. It cannot be forecasted how society will emerge a generation hence. Answer: forecast Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam rule forecast–forecast–forecast. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “forecast”. Forecast may be unchanged in V1/V2/V3; “forecasted” is also acceptable in modern usage. Quick Rule: Forecast may be unchanged in V1/V2/V3; “forecasted” is also acceptable in modern usage.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past of slay Answer: The knight slew the dragon with his sword. Hinglish Explanation: Traditional past of slay = slew. English Explanation: Traditional past of slay = slew. Quick Rule: slay–slew–slain.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Past participle of slay Answer: The monster was slain by the brave warrior. Hinglish Explanation: Passive requires V3 “slain”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The monster was slain by the brave warrior.”. slay–slew–slain. Quick Rule: slay–slew–slain.","Correct the highlighted part. He has slayed his rival. Answer: slain Hinglish Explanation: Exam-style past participle “slain” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “slain”. has + V3; slay V3 = slain (traditional). Quick Rule: has + V3; slay V3 = slain (traditional)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=4", "t": "Finite vs Non-finite · Infinitives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I went to watch a movie yesterday. Answer: Finite verb Hinglish Explanation: “went” tense (past) carry karta hai aur subject se agree karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Finite verb”. Finite verbs are marked for tense/person/number. Quick Rule: Finite verbs are marked for tense/person/number.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I went to watch a movie yesterday. Answer: Non-finite infinitive Hinglish Explanation: “to watch” tense/person ke according change nahi hota. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Non-finite infinitive”. to + V1 = to-infinitive. Quick Rule: to + V1 = to-infinitive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He has gone to watch a movie. Answer: Finite verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “has gone” subject/tense se bound finite phrase hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Finite verb phrase”. Finite verb phrase carries tense/agreement. Quick Rule: Finite verb phrase carries tense/agreement.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He has gone to watch a movie. Answer: Non-finite infinitive Hinglish Explanation: “to watch” unchanged non-finite form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Non-finite infinitive”. Infinitives are non-finite. Quick Rule: Infinitives are non-finite.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They have gone to watch a movie. Answer: Non-finite infinitive Hinglish Explanation: Subject badalne par bhi “to watch” same rehta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Non-finite infinitive”. Non-finite verb is not bound by subject/tense. Quick Rule: Non-finite verb is not bound by subject/tense.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They will go to watch a movie tomorrow. Answer: Finite verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “will go” future tense carry karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Finite verb phrase”. Finite verb phrase marks tense/modal. Quick Rule: Finite verb phrase marks tense/modal.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. To walk is a good exercise. Answer: Infinitive used as subject Hinglish Explanation: Poora infinitive phrase sentence ka subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive used as subject”. To-infinitive can function as a noun-like subject. Quick Rule: To-infinitive can function as a noun-like subject.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. To swim is good for health. Answer: Infinitive used as subject Hinglish Explanation: “To swim” subject position me hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive used as subject”. Infinitive can function as subject. Quick Rule: Infinitive can function as subject.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I want to go . Answer: Infinitive used as object/complement Hinglish Explanation: “want” ke baad infinitive action complement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive used as object/complement”. want + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: want + to-infinitive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I want to speak . Answer: Infinitive used as object/complement Hinglish Explanation: “to speak” non-finite complement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive used as object/complement”. want + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: want + to-infinitive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She loves to play . Answer: Infinitive complement Hinglish Explanation: “to play” love ka complement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive complement”. love may take a to-infinitive. Quick Rule: love may take a to-infinitive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I am about to go . Answer: Infinitive after “about” expression Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression “be about to + V1” imminent action batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Infinitive after “about” expression”. be about to + V1 = be on the point of doing. Quick Rule: be about to + V1 = be on the point of doing.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Nothing but Answer: They want nothing but to earn money. Hinglish Explanation: Source example me “to earn” infinitive phrase “nothing but” ke baad purpose/object idea deta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “They want nothing but to earn money.”. Infinitive can follow noun phrases like “nothing but …” depending on structure. Quick Rule: Infinitive can follow noun phrases like “nothing but …” depending on structure.","Correct the highlighted part. I would rather to wait for her. Answer: wait Hinglish Explanation: “Would rather” ke baad bare infinitive aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “wait”. would rather + V1. Quick Rule: would rather + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. I would rather go than to waste my time. Answer: waste Hinglish Explanation: Parallel bare infinitive: would rather go than waste. English Explanation: Parallel bare infinitive: would rather go than waste. Rule: would rather + V1 + than + V1. Quick Rule: would rather + V1 + than + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. I would sooner spend my vacation at the beach than to go camping in the mountains. Answer: go Hinglish Explanation: Would sooner ke comparison me bare infinitive use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “go”. would sooner + V1 + than + V1. Quick Rule: would sooner + V1 + than + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. You had better left the country and come to see me as soon as possible. Answer: leave Hinglish Explanation: Had better ke baad base form aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “leave”. had better + V1. Quick Rule: had better + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. You had better gone abroad for higher studies. Answer: go Hinglish Explanation: Had better + base infinitive. English Explanation: Had better + base infinitive. Rule: had better + V1. Quick Rule: had better + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. If I had the chance, I would sooner quit my job and pursues my passion for painting. Answer: pursue Hinglish Explanation: “Quit” aur “pursue” parallel V1 forms honge. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “pursue”. would sooner + parallel bare infinitives. Quick Rule: would sooner + parallel bare infinitives.","Correct the highlighted part. If you see something you like, you had better bought it. Answer: buy Hinglish Explanation: Had better + V1. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “buy”. had better + V1. Quick Rule: had better + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. I informed her that she had better got here soon or she'll miss the opening ceremony. Answer: get Hinglish Explanation: Had better ke baad V1 “get”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “get”. had better + V1. Quick Rule: had better + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. We would rather save our money for a trip than to spend it on expensive gadgets. Answer: spend Hinglish Explanation: Parallel bare infinitives. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “spend”. would rather + V1 + than + V1. Quick Rule: would rather + V1 + than + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. If you're going to do a startup, you had better been serious about it. Answer: be serious Hinglish Explanation: Had better + bare infinitive “be”. English Explanation: Had better + bare infinitive “be”. Rule: had better + V1. Quick Rule: had better + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. Why to play alone when we have a number of friends who can take part in this game? Answer: Why play Hinglish Explanation: Direct “why” question ke baad bare infinitive natural hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Why play”. Why/why not + V1 in elliptical questions. Quick Rule: Why/why not + V1 in elliptical questions.","Correct the highlighted part. Why to talk to him if he is not ready to act on the advice you have given to him? Answer: Why talk Hinglish Explanation: Why + bare infinitive. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Why talk”. Why + V1. Quick Rule: Why + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. Why not to go to a dentist if all your teeth are decaying rapidly? Answer: Why not go Hinglish Explanation: Why not + bare infinitive. English Explanation: Why not + bare infinitive. Rule: Why not + V1. Quick Rule: Why not + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. Why to pay more at this shop when it is available for 500 rupees only at Vishal Mega Mart? Answer: Why pay Hinglish Explanation: Why + V1. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Why pay”. Why + bare infinitive. Quick Rule: Why + bare infinitive.","Correct the highlighted part. He knows to befool customers. Answer: knows how to befool Hinglish Explanation: “Know how to” skill/method batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “knows how to befool”. know/learn/teach + how to + V1 when the meaning is method/ability. Quick Rule: know/learn/teach + how to + V1 when the meaning is method/ability.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Teaching a method Answer: She taught me how to speak English. Hinglish Explanation: “How to + V1” explicitly method/skill batata hai. “She taught me to speak English” bhi standard English me grammatical ho sakta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She taught me how to speak English.”. teach someone how to + V1 = teach the method/skill. Quick Rule: teach someone how to + V1 = teach the method/skill.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Teaching a method Answer: My teacher has taught me how to speak English and French in a very short span of time. Hinglish Explanation: Source ka intended structure “teach + how to + V1” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “My teacher has taught me how to speak English and French in a very short span of time.”. how to + V1 after teach/learn/know when emphasizing method. Quick Rule: how to + V1 after teach/learn/know when emphasizing method.","Correct the highlighted part. Rahul does not know to swim , so he is afraid of water. Answer: know how to swim Hinglish Explanation: Ability/skill ke liye “know how to”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “know how to swim”. know how to + V1. Quick Rule: know how to + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. Do you know to prepare a balance sheet? Answer: know how to prepare Hinglish Explanation: Method/skill ke sense me “know how to prepare”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “know how to prepare”. know how to + V1. Quick Rule: know how to + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Learning a skill Answer: When learning how to swim, one of the most important things is to relax. Hinglish Explanation: Source skill-learning pattern “learn how to + V1” par focus karta hai. “Learn to swim” bhi standard hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “When learning how to swim, one of the most important things is to relax.”. learn how to + V1 / learn to + V1 are both possible; how emphasizes method. Quick Rule: learn how to + V1 / learn to + V1 are both possible; how emphasizes method."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=5", "t": "Perception & causative verbs · Bare infinitive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. I heard him to say so. Answer: say Hinglish Explanation: Active voice me hear + object + bare infinitive complete action ke liye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “say”. hear/see/watch/notice/feel + object + V1 for a complete action. Quick Rule: hear/see/watch/notice/feel + object + V1 for a complete action.","Correct the highlighted part. I watched him to proceed to that location. Answer: proceed Hinglish Explanation: Watch + object + bare infinitive. English Explanation: Watch + object + bare infinitive. Rule: watch + object + V1. Quick Rule: watch + object + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. I saw her to talk to her friend. Answer: talk Hinglish Explanation: See + object + V1. English Explanation: See + object + V1. Rule: see + object + bare infinitive. Quick Rule: see + object + bare infinitive.","Correct the highlighted part. She made me to laugh . Answer: laugh Hinglish Explanation: Active causative “make” ke baad object + bare infinitive. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “laugh”. make + object + V1 in active voice. Quick Rule: make + object + V1 in active voice.","Correct the highlighted part. I noticed him to make his way there. Answer: make Hinglish Explanation: Notice + object + bare infinitive. English Explanation: Notice + object + bare infinitive. Rule: notice + object + V1. Quick Rule: notice + object + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Passive of make Answer: I was made to laugh. Hinglish Explanation: Passive voice me “make” ke baad to-infinitive wapas aata hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “I was made to laugh.”. Active: make + object + V1; passive: be made + to + V1. Quick Rule: Active: make + object + V1; passive: be made + to + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Passive perception Answer: She was seen to enter the office. Hinglish Explanation: Passive reporting/perception structure me to-infinitive. English Explanation: Passive reporting/perception structure me to-infinitive. Rule: Passive of see/hear + object + V1 often becomes be seen/heard + to + V1. Quick Rule: Passive of see/hear + object + V1 often becomes be seen/heard + to + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Passive perception Answer: They were heard to say that they loved the movie. Hinglish Explanation: Passive me “to say”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “They were heard to say that they loved the movie.”. be heard + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: be heard + to-infinitive.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Help + bare infinitive Answer: He helped me buy a house. Hinglish Explanation: Help ke baad bare infinitive acceptable hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He helped me buy a house.”. help + object + V1 / to + V1 both standard. Quick Rule: help + object + V1 / to + V1 both standard.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Help + to-infinitive Answer: He helped me to buy a house. Hinglish Explanation: Help ke saath to-infinitive bhi standard hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He helped me to buy a house.”. help + object + (to) + V1. Quick Rule: help + object + (to) + V1."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=6", "t": "Gerunds · noun functions & possessive forms", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Singing is an art. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: “Singing” noun-like subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. V-ing used as a noun is a gerund. Quick Rule: V-ing used as a noun is a gerund.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Sleeping is necessary to life. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: “Sleeping” subject ki position me noun function karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. Gerund can function as subject. Quick Rule: Gerund can function as subject.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She likes dancing . Answer: Gerund · object/complement Hinglish Explanation: “dancing” verb “likes” ka noun-like complement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · object/complement”. Gerund can function as object of a verb. Quick Rule: Gerund can function as object of a verb.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I enjoyed drinking . Answer: Gerund · object/complement Hinglish Explanation: Enjoy generally gerund leta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · object/complement”. enjoy + V-ing. Quick Rule: enjoy + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. Rahul is fond of eat . Answer: eating Hinglish Explanation: Preposition “of” ke baad gerund V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “eating”. preposition + V-ing. Quick Rule: preposition + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. He assisted me in do his work. Answer: doing Hinglish Explanation: Preposition “in” ke baad V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “doing”. preposition + gerund. Quick Rule: preposition + gerund.","Correct the highlighted part. He is ambitious of become the P.M. of India. Answer: becoming Hinglish Explanation: Preposition “of” ke baad gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “becoming”. preposition + V-ing. Quick Rule: preposition + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. What did you do on see your result? Answer: seeing Hinglish Explanation: “On” preposition ke baad V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “seeing”. on + V-ing. Quick Rule: on + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. I am interested in learn more. Answer: learning Hinglish Explanation: Interested in + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “learning”. be interested in + V-ing. Quick Rule: be interested in + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. You should not mind hers coming late. Answer: her coming Hinglish Explanation: Traditional formal rule me gerund se pehle possessive adjective “her”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “her coming”. Before a gerund, formal exam grammar often prefers a possessive adjective/genitive. Quick Rule: Before a gerund, formal exam grammar often prefers a possessive adjective/genitive.","Correct the highlighted part. My teacher did not object to me using the calculator. Answer: my using Hinglish Explanation: Traditional formal exam style “my using”. Modern English me “me using” bhi common hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “my using”. Formal style often uses possessive + gerund after prepositions/verbs. Quick Rule: Formal style often uses possessive + gerund after prepositions/verbs.","Correct the highlighted part. Owing to him being a newcomer, he was unable to get the job. Answer: his being Hinglish Explanation: Formal exam rule possessive “his” before gerund “being”. English Explanation: Formal exam rule possessive “his” before gerund “being”. Rule: possessive adjective + gerund in formal grammar. Quick Rule: possessive adjective + gerund in formal grammar.","Correct the highlighted part. Being a successful high-profile lawyer, he thought that he can easily defeat me. But I am pretty confident of me winning the case. Answer: my winning Hinglish Explanation: “Of” ke baad gerund; traditional formal form “my winning”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “my winning”. preposition + possessive adjective + gerund (formal exam style). Quick Rule: preposition + possessive adjective + gerund (formal exam style).","Correct the highlighted part. She had been him wasting his time the whole month. He had not even attended classes. That is why she was not sure of him passing the examination. Answer: his passing Hinglish Explanation: “Of” ke baad gerund; formal possessive “his”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “his passing”. be sure of + possessive + V-ing (formal). Quick Rule: be sure of + possessive + V-ing (formal).","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Possessive + gerund Answer: Owing to my being a girl, I was given fewer rights and facilities in this world since the day I was born. Hinglish Explanation: Traditional formal structure “my being”. English Explanation: Traditional formal structure “my being”. Rule: owing to + possessive adjective + gerund (formal exam usage). Quick Rule: owing to + possessive adjective + gerund (formal exam usage)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=7", "t": "Gerund after fixed expressions / prepositions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. He comes to me with a view to get some help. Answer: to getting Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “to” preposition hai, infinitive marker nahi; isliye V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to getting”. with a view to + V-ing. Quick Rule: with a view to + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. Rahul is used to work at night. Answer: to working Hinglish Explanation: “Be used to” me “to” preposition hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to working”. be used/accustomed to + V-ing/noun. Quick Rule: be used/accustomed to + V-ing/noun.","Correct the highlighted part. He is addicted to gamble . Answer: to gambling Hinglish Explanation: Addicted to + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to gambling”. be addicted to + V-ing/noun. Quick Rule: be addicted to + V-ing/noun.","Correct the highlighted part. He looks forward to meet his friends. Answer: to meeting Hinglish Explanation: Look forward to me “to” preposition hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to meeting”. look forward to + V-ing. Quick Rule: look forward to + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. We are committed to withdraw our troops by the end of the year. Answer: to withdrawing Hinglish Explanation: Committed to + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to withdrawing”. be committed to + V-ing/noun. Quick Rule: be committed to + V-ing/noun.","Correct the highlighted part. I am looking forward to meet you. Answer: to meeting Hinglish Explanation: Look forward to + V-ing. English Explanation: Look forward to + V-ing. Rule: look forward to + gerund. Quick Rule: look forward to + gerund.","Correct the highlighted part. He has called a meeting of all parties tomorrow, with a view to form a national reconciliation government. Answer: to forming Hinglish Explanation: “With a view to” ke baad gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to forming”. with a view to + V-ing. Quick Rule: with a view to + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. Sandy is addicted to collect stamps. Answer: to collecting Hinglish Explanation: Addicted to + V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “to collecting”. be addicted to + gerund. Quick Rule: be addicted to + gerund.","Correct the highlighted part. It is no use cry over spilt milk. Answer: crying Hinglish Explanation: No use + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “crying”. It is no use + V-ing. Quick Rule: It is no use + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. Is it any use support a corrupt leader? Answer: supporting Hinglish Explanation: Any use + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “supporting”. Is it any use + V-ing? Quick Rule: Is it any use + V-ing?","Correct the highlighted part. It is no good help the terrorist. Answer: helping Hinglish Explanation: No good + gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “helping”. It is no good + V-ing. Quick Rule: It is no good + V-ing.","Correct the highlighted part. They are adults now. It is no good hide their mistakes. Answer: hiding Hinglish Explanation: No good + V-ing. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hiding”. It is no good + gerund. Quick Rule: It is no good + gerund."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=8", "t": "Participles · Gerund vs Participle", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. A dog is barking . Answer: Present participle in a verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “is barking” progressive verb phrase hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle in a verb phrase”. be + V-ing forms continuous tense; the V-ing form is a present participle. Quick Rule: be + V-ing forms continuous tense; the V-ing form is a present participle.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The barking dog alerted the neighbours. Answer: Participle adjective Hinglish Explanation: “barking” noun “dog” ko describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective”. V-ing modifying a noun = present participle/adjective. Quick Rule: V-ing modifying a noun = present participle/adjective.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The dog jumped up, barking excitedly at the arrival of its owner. Answer: Participle adverbial phrase Hinglish Explanation: “barking excitedly” main action “jumped” ke saath circumstance batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adverbial phrase”. V-ing phrase can act adverbially as a participial clause. Quick Rule: V-ing phrase can act adverbially as a participial clause.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Barking annoys the neighbours. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: “Barking” noun-like subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. V-ing as a noun = gerund. Quick Rule: V-ing as a noun = gerund.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She has finished her work. Answer: Past participle in perfect verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “has + finished” perfect construction hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Past participle in perfect verb phrase”. Perfect: have/has + V3. Quick Rule: Perfect: have/has + V3.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The students submitted their finished assignments. Answer: Past participle adjective Hinglish Explanation: “finished” assignments ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Past participle adjective”. V3 modifying a noun = past participle adjective. Quick Rule: V3 modifying a noun = past participle adjective.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Studying is important for academic success. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: Noun-like subject. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. Gerund functions as a noun. Quick Rule: Gerund functions as a noun.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The studying environment should be free from distractions. Answer: Participle adjective Hinglish Explanation: “studying” noun “environment” ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective”. V-ing before a noun may function attributively as a participle/adjective. Quick Rule: V-ing before a noun may function attributively as a participle/adjective.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The professor is passionate about studying human behavior. Answer: Gerund after preposition Hinglish Explanation: “about” preposition ke baad V-ing noun-like form hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund after preposition”. preposition + gerund. Quick Rule: preposition + gerund.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They are studying for the upcoming exam. Answer: Present participle in continuous verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “are studying” finite progressive predicate hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle in continuous verb phrase”. be + V-ing = continuous tense. Quick Rule: be + V-ing = continuous tense.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I spent the whole day studying math. Answer: Participle/gerund-participial complement (adverbial in source) Hinglish Explanation: Source “studying” ko circumstance/action complement ke roop me classify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle/gerund-participial complement (adverbial in source)”. V-ing can form a supplementary/adverbial clause after a main verb. Quick Rule: V-ing can form a supplementary/adverbial clause after a main verb.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. He joined a singing competition and impressed the judges with his talent. Answer: Participle/adjectival modifier Hinglish Explanation: “singing” competition ka type batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle/adjectival modifier”. V-ing modifying a noun functions adjectivally. Quick Rule: V-ing modifying a noun functions adjectivally.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She enjoys singing in the shower. Answer: Gerund · object Hinglish Explanation: Enjoy ke baad gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · object”. enjoy + V-ing. Quick Rule: enjoy + V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The children were singing their favourite songs at the concert. Answer: Present participle in continuous verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “were singing” past continuous hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle in continuous verb phrase”. was/were + V-ing. Quick Rule: was/were + V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Singing brings joy to my heart. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: Noun-like subject. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. Gerund functions as a noun. Quick Rule: Gerund functions as a noun.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. She came down the street, singing happily. Answer: Participle adverbial phrase Hinglish Explanation: “singing happily” describes accompanying action. English Explanation: “singing happily” describes accompanying action. Rule: Participial phrase can modify the whole clause/adverbially. Quick Rule: Participial phrase can modify the whole clause/adverbially.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I only know that the motorcyclist was fatally injured in the accident and is now fighting for his life. Answer: Present participle in a verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “is fighting” progressive verb phrase hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle in a verb phrase”. be + V-ing = continuous/progressive. Quick Rule: be + V-ing = continuous/progressive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Many of his paintings show the setting sun. Answer: Participle adjective Hinglish Explanation: “setting” sun ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective”. V-ing modifying a noun = participle adjective. Quick Rule: V-ing modifying a noun = participle adjective.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The girl dancing on the stage is my sister. Answer: Participle adjective / reduced relative clause Hinglish Explanation: “dancing on the stage” girl ko describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective / reduced relative clause”. Present participle can form a reduced relative clause. Quick Rule: Present participle can form a reduced relative clause.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Asking questions is a whole lot easier than answering them. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: “Asking questions” noun phrase subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. Gerund phrase can function as subject. Quick Rule: Gerund phrase can function as subject.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. We found some money lying on the ground. Answer: Participle adjective / object complement Hinglish Explanation: “lying on the ground” money ki state batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective / object complement”. Present participle can modify/object-complement a noun. Quick Rule: Present participle can modify/object-complement a noun.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Jolly decided that flying above the clouds was the most incredible experience she'd ever had. Answer: Gerund · subject of subordinate clause Hinglish Explanation: “flying above the clouds” noun-like subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject of subordinate clause”. Gerund phrase functions as noun. Quick Rule: Gerund phrase functions as noun.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The decision of the meeting looks fruitful. Answer: Gerund/noun form in source Hinglish Explanation: Source is V-ing form ko noun-like “meeting” ke roop me classify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund/noun form in source”. A V-ing form functioning as a noun is treated as a gerund in traditional grammar. Quick Rule: A V-ing form functioning as a noun is treated as a gerund in traditional grammar.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The magazine's cover, appealing to younger readers, will undoubtedly boost sales this month. Answer: Participle adjective/phrase Hinglish Explanation: “appealing to younger readers” cover ko describe karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective/phrase”. Participial phrase modifies a noun. Quick Rule: Participial phrase modifies a noun.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. I am looking forward to getting the statistics. Answer: Present participle in verb phrase Hinglish Explanation: “am looking” progressive verb phrase ka part hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle in verb phrase”. be + V-ing = progressive. Quick Rule: be + V-ing = progressive.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Tom lost his keys walking through the park. Answer: Participle adverbial phrase Hinglish Explanation: “walking through the park” circumstance/time batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adverbial phrase”. Supplementary V-ing clause can be adverbial. Quick Rule: Supplementary V-ing clause can be adverbial.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Do you mind my borrowing these supplies? Answer: Gerund · object Hinglish Explanation: “borrowing” noun-like complement hai; “my” possessive determiner. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · object”. mind + possessive + gerund (formal). Quick Rule: mind + possessive + gerund (formal).","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. They enjoyed taking long walks on the trail. Answer: Gerund · object Hinglish Explanation: Enjoy ke baad gerund. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · object”. enjoy + V-ing. Quick Rule: enjoy + V-ing.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. If I catch you stealing my apples again, there'll be trouble. Answer: Present participle / object complement Hinglish Explanation: “stealing” object “you” ki ongoing action batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Present participle / object complement”. catch/see/hear + object + V-ing for an action in progress. Quick Rule: catch/see/hear + object + V-ing for an action in progress.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. Reading a good novel always gives me pleasure. Answer: Gerund · subject Hinglish Explanation: “Reading a good novel” subject hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Gerund · subject”. Gerund phrase as subject. Quick Rule: Gerund phrase as subject.","Identify the grammatical role/form of the highlighted word. The man driving the car was not injured. Answer: Participle adjective / reduced relative clause Hinglish Explanation: “driving the car” man ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Participle adjective / reduced relative clause”. Present participle can reduce “the man who was driving…”. Quick Rule: Present participle can reduce “the man who was driving…”."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=9", "t": "Perception verbs · complete action vs V-ing", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Complete action after hear Answer: I heard him say so. Hinglish Explanation: Bare infinitive often presents the perceived action as a whole/complete event. English Explanation: Bare infinitive often presents the perceived action as a whole/complete event. Rule: hear/see/watch + object + V1 = complete event; + V-ing = action in progress. Quick Rule: hear/see/watch + object + V1 = complete event; + V-ing = action in progress.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Ongoing/perceived action after hear Answer: I heard him saying so. Hinglish Explanation: V-ing focuses on an action in progress; depending on context it may not imply completion. English Explanation: V-ing focuses on an action in progress; depending on context it may not imply completion. Rule: hear + object + V-ing = action perceived in progress. Quick Rule: hear + object + V-ing = action perceived in progress.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Complete action after see Answer: I saw her cross the road. Hinglish Explanation: Bare V1 presents crossing as a whole event. English Explanation: Bare V1 presents crossing as a whole event. Rule: see + object + V1 = complete action. Quick Rule: see + object + V1 = complete action.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Ongoing action after see Answer: I saw her crossing the road. Hinglish Explanation: V-ing focuses on the action while it was happening. English Explanation: V-ing focuses on the action while it was happening. Rule: see + object + V-ing = action in progress. Quick Rule: see + object + V-ing = action in progress."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=10", "t": "Present vs Perfect Participle", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. Having stood on the stage , the actor delivered a powerful monologue. Answer: Standing on the stage Hinglish Explanation: Simultaneous/background position ke liye present participle “Standing”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Standing on the stage”. Present participle for simultaneous/background action; perfect participle for prior completed action. Quick Rule: Present participle for simultaneous/background action; perfect participle for prior completed action.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Earlier completed action Answer: Having taken breakfast, he went to the office. Hinglish Explanation: Breakfast office jane se pehle complete hua. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Having taken breakfast, he went to the office.”. having + V3 = perfect participle for prior completed action. Quick Rule: having + V3 = perfect participle for prior completed action.","Correct the highlighted part. Having driven through the countryside , they admired the scenic views. Answer: Driving through the countryside Hinglish Explanation: Source correction simultaneous action ko “Driving…” se dikhata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Driving through the countryside”. Use present participle for simultaneous accompanying action. Quick Rule: Use present participle for simultaneous accompanying action.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Earlier completed action Answer: Having won the championship, the team celebrated their victory. Hinglish Explanation: Winning pehle complete hua, celebration baad me. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Having won the championship, the team celebrated their victory.”. having + V3 = prior completed action. Quick Rule: having + V3 = prior completed action.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Simultaneous/background action Answer: Wandering through the museum, they admired the artwork on display. Hinglish Explanation: Museum me ghoomte hue hi artwork admire kiya. English Explanation: Museum me ghoomte hue hi artwork admire kiya. Rule: Present participle for simultaneous action. Quick Rule: Present participle for simultaneous action.","Correct the highlighted part. Saving enough money , they planned a trip to their dream destination. Answer: Having saved enough money Hinglish Explanation: Money save karna planning se pehle complete hua. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Having saved enough money”. Perfect participle = having + V3 for prior completed action. Quick Rule: Perfect participle = having + V3 for prior completed action."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=09.+Verbs&section=11", "t": "Dangling participles · logical subject", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 09. Verbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. Walking in the park , a tree fell down. Answer: While he was walking in the park Hinglish Explanation: Original me “walking” ka logical subject “a tree” ban raha tha, jo nonsense hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While he was walking in the park”. The understood subject of a participial phrase should logically match the subject of the main clause. Quick Rule: Participial phrase ka understood subject main clause ke subject se logically match hona chahiye.","Correct the highlighted part. Waiting for a bus , a brick fell on my head. Answer: While I was waiting for a bus Hinglish Explanation: Brick wait nahi kar raha tha; subject “I” clarify karna hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While I was waiting for a bus”. Avoid dangling participles by making the actual doer the subject of the main clause. Quick Rule: Avoid dangling participles by naming the actual doer.","Correct the highlighted part. Being Sunday , I decided not to go out. Answer: It being Sunday Hinglish Explanation: “Sunday” ka logical subject “I” nahi hai; absolute construction “It being Sunday”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “It being Sunday”. Use an absolute phrase when the participial phrase has its own subject. Quick Rule: Use an absolute phrase when the participial phrase has its own subject.","Correct the highlighted part. Being a rainy day , we did not go out. Answer: It being a rainy day Hinglish Explanation: “We” rainy day nahi hain; “It” subject supply karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “It being a rainy day”. It being + noun/adjective forms an absolute construction. Quick Rule: It being + noun/adjective forms an absolute construction.","Correct the highlighted part. Watching TV , somebody came to meet me. Answer: While I was watching TV Hinglish Explanation: Original me somebody ko TV-watcher bana deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While I was watching TV”. The modifier must attach to the intended subject. Quick Rule: The modifier must attach to the intended subject.","Correct the highlighted part. Sitting on the chair , a ball hit him. Answer: While he was sitting on the chair Hinglish Explanation: Ball chair par nahi baitha tha; “he” action ka doer hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While he was sitting on the chair”. Avoid dangling modifiers. Quick Rule: Avoid dangling modifiers.","Correct the highlighted part. Sleeping in my room , a nurse came to check the temperature. Answer: While I was sleeping in my room Hinglish Explanation: Nurse sleeping nahi thi; intended subject “I” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While I was sleeping in my room”. Make the logical subject explicit. Quick Rule: Make the logical subject explicit.","Correct the highlighted part. Standing near the gate , a scooter hit her. Answer: While she was standing near the gate Hinglish Explanation: Scooter standing nahi tha; “she” ko subject banana hoga. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “While she was standing near the gate”. Participial modifier should logically modify the subject of the main clause. Quick Rule: Participial modifier should logically modify the subject of the main clause."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=1", "t": "May vs Might · possibility & permission", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The weather may be better tomorrow. Answer: Higher/ordinary possibility Hinglish Explanation: “May” yahan future possibility dikhata hai; speaker ko event possible lag raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Higher/ordinary possibility”. may + V1 = possibility; traditionally may is often treated as a little more likely than might. Quick Rule: may + V1 = possibility; traditionally may is often treated as a little more likely than might.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The weather might be better tomorrow. Answer: Weaker/tentative possibility Hinglish Explanation: “Might” yahan more tentative possibility dikhata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Weaker/tentative possibility”. might + V1 = weaker/tentative possibility. Quick Rule: might + V1 = weaker/tentative possibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We may go to the cinema tonight. Answer: Possibility Hinglish Explanation: Cinema jaana possible plan hai, fixed certainty nahi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Possibility”. may + V1 can express possible present/future action. Quick Rule: may + V1 can express possible present/future action.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We might go to the cinema tonight. Answer: Tentative possibility Hinglish Explanation: “Might” plan ko aur tentative banata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Tentative possibility”. might + V1 = tentative possibility. Quick Rule: might + V1 = tentative possibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? May I come in, sir? Answer: Formal permission request Hinglish Explanation: Speaker permission maang raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal permission request”. May I + V1...? is a formal/polite way to ask permission. Quick Rule: May I + V1...? is a formal/polite way to ask permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Yes, you may . Answer: Permission granted Hinglish Explanation: “You may” yahan permission dene ke liye hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission granted”. may can grant permission. Quick Rule: may can grant permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? May I go home now? Answer: Permission request Hinglish Explanation: Question permission ke liye hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission request”. May I + V1...? = ask permission. Quick Rule: May I + V1...? = ask permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? No, you may not . Answer: Permission refused / prohibition Hinglish Explanation: Context permission ka hai; “may not” means permission denied. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission refused / prohibition”. may not can mean “not permitted” when context is permission. Quick Rule: may not can mean “not permitted” when context is permission.","Correct the highlighted part. He asked if he may borrow the car. Answer: might borrow Hinglish Explanation: Traditional sequence-of-tense exam rule me past reporting verb “asked” ke baad may → might hota hai. Modern English me “may” kabhi-kabhi retained bhi hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “might borrow”. Traditional reported-speech shift: may → might after a past reporting verb. Quick Rule: Traditional reported-speech shift: may → might after a past reporting verb.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He asked if he might borrow the car. Answer: Reported/past-shifted permission or possibility Hinglish Explanation: “Asked” past hai; “might” source ke according may ka shifted form hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Reported/past-shifted permission or possibility”. In reported speech, may often shifts to might. Quick Rule: In reported speech, may often shifts to might.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I think I may go to the game. Answer: Present/future possibility Hinglish Explanation: Current thought me possible future action hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Present/future possibility”. may + V1 = present/future possibility. Quick Rule: may + V1 = present/future possibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I thought I might go to the game. Answer: Past viewpoint / tentative possibility Hinglish Explanation: Past viewpoint “thought” ke saath “might” natural hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past viewpoint / tentative possibility”. might commonly expresses possibility viewed from the past or extra tentativeness. Quick Rule: might commonly expresses possibility viewed from the past or extra tentativeness."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=2", "t": "Can & Could · ability, permission, truth, offers, possibility", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I can answer all the questions. Answer: Present ability Hinglish Explanation: Speaker apni current ability batata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Present ability”. can + V1 = present ability/capacity. Quick Rule: can + V1 = present ability/capacity.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She can speak English very well. Answer: Present ability Hinglish Explanation: English bolne ki current skill hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Present ability”. can + V1 = ability. Quick Rule: can + V1 = ability.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? This room can accommodate more than fifty students. Answer: Capacity Hinglish Explanation: Room ki capacity batayi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Capacity”. can can express capacity/ability of things. Quick Rule: can can express capacity/ability of things.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I could swim when I was 5 years old. Answer: Past general ability Hinglish Explanation: Past me general skill/ability thi. English Explanation: Past me general skill/ability thi. Rule: could + V1 = past general ability. Quick Rule: could + V1 = past general ability.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? When I was young, I could easily touch my toes. Answer: Past general ability Hinglish Explanation: Young age ki ability hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past general ability”. could often expresses past ability. Quick Rule: could often expresses past ability.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I couldn&#x27;t play chess two years ago, but I can now. Answer: Lack of past ability Hinglish Explanation: Past me ability nahi thi; “can now” se contrast clear hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Lack of past ability”. could not/couldn’t + V1 = past inability. Quick Rule: could not/couldn’t + V1 = past inability.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You can play with your friends after doing homework. Answer: Permission Hinglish Explanation: Context me “can” permission de raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission”. can can give informal permission. Quick Rule: can can give informal permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You can go on the trip. Answer: Permission granted Hinglish Explanation: Trip ke liye permission di gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission granted”. can = informal permission. Quick Rule: can = informal permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Can I have your number? Answer: Informal permission/request Hinglish Explanation: Speaker politely asks if it is allowed/acceptable. English Explanation: Speaker politely asks if it is allowed/acceptable. Rule: Can I...? is common informal permission/request language. Quick Rule: Can I...? is common informal permission/request language.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Can I ask a question, please? Answer: Permission request Hinglish Explanation: Question puchhne ki permission maangi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Permission request”. Can I + V1...? = informal permission/request. Quick Rule: Can I + V1...? = informal permission/request.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Water can extinguish fire. Answer: General possibility/capacity Hinglish Explanation: Water ki general capacity/typical fact batayi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “General possibility/capacity”. can can express general truth or typical capability. Quick Rule: can can express general truth or typical capability.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Fireworks can frighten pets. Answer: General possibility Hinglish Explanation: Fireworks ke common effect ki possibility batayi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “General possibility”. can can describe what is generally possible. Quick Rule: can can describe what is generally possible.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Can I help you lift that? Answer: Offer Hinglish Explanation: Speaker help offer kar raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Offer”. Can I + V1...? may be used to offer help. Quick Rule: Can I + V1...? may be used to offer help.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Can we do anything for you? Answer: Offer of help Hinglish Explanation: Question assistance offer karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Offer of help”. Can we...? can make an offer. Quick Rule: Can we...? can make an offer.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Standard negative spelling with can Answer: The children cannot be left unsupervised at any time. Hinglish Explanation: Default negative of “can” is written as one word: “cannot”. “Can not” exists only in special contrastive constructions. English Explanation: Default negative of “can” is written as one word: “cannot”. “Can not” exists only in special contrastive constructions. Rule: Default spelling: cannot / can’t. Quick Rule: Default spelling: cannot / can’t.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Could I ask you a personal question? Answer: Formal/polite permission request Hinglish Explanation: “Could I...?” “Can I...?” se zyada polite/formal lagta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal/polite permission request”. Could I + V1...? = polite permission request. Quick Rule: Could I + V1...? = polite permission request.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Could I use your phone? Answer: Polite permission request Hinglish Explanation: Phone use karne ki polite permission request hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Polite permission request”. Could I...? = polite request for permission. Quick Rule: Could I...? = polite request for permission.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? It could be yellow. Answer: Uncertain present possibility Hinglish Explanation: Speaker certain nahi hai, sirf possible guess hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Uncertain present possibility”. could + V1 can express uncertain present/future possibility. Quick Rule: could + V1 can express uncertain present/future possibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The storm could get worse. Answer: Future possibility Hinglish Explanation: Storm worsen ho sakta hai, certainty nahi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Future possibility”. could + V1 = possible future result. Quick Rule: could + V1 = possible future result."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=3", "t": "Will & Shall · future, determination, commands, promises", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She will graduate from college next spring. Answer: Future activity/prediction Hinglish Explanation: Future event bataya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Future activity/prediction”. will + V1 commonly marks future prediction/activity. Quick Rule: will + V1 commonly marks future prediction/activity.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? They will travel to Europe for their honeymoon. Answer: Future activity/intention Hinglish Explanation: Future travel bataya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Future activity/intention”. will + V1 = future. Quick Rule: will + V1 = future.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I shall call you later to discuss the details. Answer: Formal future with I/we Hinglish Explanation: Traditional/formal English me I/we ke saath “shall” future ke liye use ho sakta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal future with I/we”. Traditional BrE/formal: I/we shall + V1. Modern English often uses will. Quick Rule: Traditional BrE/formal: I/we shall + V1. Modern English often uses will.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We shall not tolerate any form of discrimination. Answer: Formal determination / strong future stance Hinglish Explanation: Yahan strong formal commitment/determination hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal determination / strong future stance”. shall with I/we can express determination in formal style. Quick Rule: shall with I/we can express determination in formal style.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Despite the difficulties, we will achieve our goals. Answer: Determination Hinglish Explanation: “Will” yahan resolve/determination ko stress karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Determination”. will with I/we can show determination/willingness. Quick Rule: will with I/we can show determination/willingness.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Rest assured, I will do my best to resolve the issue. Answer: Promise Hinglish Explanation: Speaker future action ka promise de raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Promise”. will can express promise. Quick Rule: will can express promise.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I will fire you if you don't work properly. Answer: Warning Hinglish Explanation: Conditional future consequence warning hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Warning”. will can be used in warnings/threats. Quick Rule: will can be used in warnings/threats.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We will kill you. Answer: Threat Hinglish Explanation: Sentence threat express karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Threat”. will can express threat depending on context. Quick Rule: will can express threat depending on context.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I will definitely attend the meeting tomorrow. Answer: Certainty / firm intention Hinglish Explanation: “Definitely” strong certainty/firm intention dikhata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Certainty / firm intention”. will can show certainty or firm intention. Quick Rule: will can show certainty or firm intention.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You shall clean your room before going out to play. Answer: Formal command Hinglish Explanation: “Shall” yahan command/order force deta hai; everyday English me “must”/imperative more common hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal command”. shall can express commands in formal/old-fashioned/legal style. Quick Rule: shall can express commands in formal/old-fashioned/legal style.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You shall receive the support you need to achieve your dreams. Answer: Formal promise/assurance Hinglish Explanation: Speaker assurance/promise de raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal promise/assurance”. shall can express promise/assurance in formal style. Quick Rule: shall can express promise/assurance in formal style.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Obey the rules or they shall face consequences. Answer: Formal warning Hinglish Explanation: Rule break hone par consequence ki warning hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal warning”. shall can express formal warning/inevitable consequence. Quick Rule: shall can express formal warning/inevitable consequence.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He shall be fired. Answer: Formal threat/strong future decision Hinglish Explanation: “Shall” yahan strong imposed future consequence hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal threat/strong future decision”. shall may express threat or authoritative determination. Quick Rule: shall may express threat or authoritative determination.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I shall be grateful to you for this act of kindness. Answer: Fixed/formal phrase Hinglish Explanation: “I shall be grateful...” formal fixed expression hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Fixed/formal phrase”. Some formal phrases retain shall, especially with I/we. Quick Rule: Some formal phrases retain shall, especially with I/we.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? They shall be obliged to comply with the new regulations. Answer: Formal obligation/fixed legal style Hinglish Explanation: “Shall be obliged” formal obligation dikhata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal obligation/fixed legal style”. shall is common in formal/legal obligation language, though modern drafting may prefer must. Quick Rule: shall is common in formal/legal obligation language, though modern drafting may prefer must.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He shall be at a loss when faced with such a complex problem. Answer: Formal/fixed future expression Hinglish Explanation: Source fixed/formal use dikhata hai; everyday English me “will be at a loss” more natural hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Formal/fixed future expression”. Some older/formal phrases use shall; modern usage often uses will. Quick Rule: Some older/formal phrases use shall; modern usage often uses will.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The sun will rise again tomorrow. Answer: Inevitable/certain future Hinglish Explanation: Natural event ko certain future ke roop me bataya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Inevitable/certain future”. will can express inevitable/certain result. Quick Rule: will can express inevitable/certain result.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Meditation will help you keep mentally fit. Answer: Expected/certain result Hinglish Explanation: Speaker expected result confidently states. English Explanation: Speaker expected result confidently states. Rule: will can state expected/certain consequence. Quick Rule: will can state expected/certain consequence.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Men will be men. Answer: Characteristic/inevitable behaviour Hinglish Explanation: Idiomatic generalization me “will” characteristic tendency show karta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Characteristic/inevitable behaviour”. will can express characteristic or expected behaviour. Quick Rule: will can express characteristic or expected behaviour."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=4", "t": "Should & Would · duty, advice, requests, habit, preference", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I said that I should take a break from work. Answer: Reported/past form of shall in traditional grammar Hinglish Explanation: Traditional reported speech me “shall” → “should” ho sakta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Reported/past form of shall in traditional grammar”. Traditional backshift: shall → should in reported speech. Quick Rule: Traditional backshift: shall → should in reported speech.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We said that we should follow the safety guidelines. Answer: Reported/formal obligation Hinglish Explanation: Reported context me “should” expected action/obligation batata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Reported/formal obligation”. should can be used in reported speech and for obligation. Quick Rule: should can be used in reported speech and for obligation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? As citizens, we should vote in the upcoming elections. Answer: Duty Hinglish Explanation: Civic duty/expected action hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Duty”. should + V1 = duty or mild obligation. Quick Rule: should + V1 = duty or mild obligation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Students should complete their assignments before the deadline. Answer: Obligation / expected duty Hinglish Explanation: Students ke expected duty ko batata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Obligation / expected duty”. should = obligation/expectation. Quick Rule: should = obligation/expectation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? If you want to improve your health, you should exercise regularly. Answer: Advice Hinglish Explanation: Health improve karne ka advice diya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Advice”. should + V1 = advice/recommendation. Quick Rule: should + V1 = advice/recommendation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Participants should follow the guidelines provided for the competition. Answer: Instruction / expected conduct Hinglish Explanation: Competition rules follow karne ki instruction hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Instruction / expected conduct”. should can express instruction/expected behaviour. Quick Rule: should can express instruction/expected behaviour.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He said that he would not sign the agreement. Answer: Past form of will in reported speech Hinglish Explanation: Reported speech me “will” → “would”. English Explanation: Reported speech me “will” → “would”. Rule: will often backshifts to would after a past reporting verb. Quick Rule: will often backshifts to would after a past reporting verb.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She asked me if they would go to the party that night. Answer: Reported future from a past viewpoint Hinglish Explanation: Past reporting context me future-in-the-past hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Reported future from a past viewpoint”. would = future viewed from the past / backshifted will. Quick Rule: would = future viewed from the past / backshifted will.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Would it be possible for you to pick me up from the airport? Answer: Polite request Hinglish Explanation: “Would it be possible...?” very polite request hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Polite request”. would is commonly used for polite requests. Quick Rule: would is commonly used for polite requests.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Would you mind helping me carry these boxes upstairs? Answer: Polite request Hinglish Explanation: “Would you mind...?” polite request structure hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Polite request”. Would you mind + V-ing...? = polite request. Quick Rule: Would you mind + V-ing...? = polite request.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? As a child, she would always play in the park with her friends. Answer: Past habitual action Hinglish Explanation: Repeated past habit bataya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past habitual action”. would + V1 can describe repeated past habits (not usually past states). Quick Rule: would + V1 can describe repeated past habits (not usually past states).","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Back in the day, we would often go camping during the holidays. Answer: Past habit Hinglish Explanation: Repeated past routine hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past habit”. would + V1 = repeated past habit. Quick Rule: would + V1 = repeated past habit.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She would prefer to travel by train rather than by bus. Answer: Preference / choice Hinglish Explanation: “Would prefer” present/future preference batata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Preference / choice”. would prefer + to-infinitive = preference. Quick Rule: would prefer + to-infinitive = preference.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He would prefer to wear comfortable clothes rather than dress up. Answer: Preference Hinglish Explanation: Clothing choice/preference hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Preference”. would prefer + to V1 rather than V1. Quick Rule: would prefer + to V1 rather than V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He would prefer to have a quiet evening at home with a good movie. Answer: Preference / wish Hinglish Explanation: Desired choice bataya gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Preference / wish”. would prefer + to-infinitive expresses preference. Quick Rule: would prefer + to-infinitive expresses preference."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=5", "t": "Must · obligation, orders, recommendation, deduction", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? One must obey one's teacher. Answer: Strong duty / obligation Hinglish Explanation: Strong duty batayi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong duty / obligation”. must + V1 = strong obligation/duty. Quick Rule: must + V1 = strong obligation/duty.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Employees must comply with the company's policies and procedures. Answer: Strong obligation Hinglish Explanation: Company rules follow karna mandatory hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong obligation”. must = obligation/necessity. Quick Rule: must = obligation/necessity.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? To excel in your career, you must constantly strive for improvement. Answer: Necessity / strong advice Hinglish Explanation: Goal achieve karne ke liye necessary action bataya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Necessity / strong advice”. must can express strong necessity/recommendation. Quick Rule: must can express strong necessity/recommendation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? There must be something wrong with the engine. Answer: Strong present deduction Hinglish Explanation: Evidence/context se strong conclusion nikala gaya hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong present deduction”. must + V1 can express strong logical deduction about the present. Quick Rule: must + V1 can express strong logical deduction about the present.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? All team members must submit their progress reports by Friday. Answer: Order / requirement Hinglish Explanation: Deadline ke saath mandatory order hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Order / requirement”. must = rule/order/requirement. Quick Rule: must = rule/order/requirement.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Students must follow the school's dress code at all times. Answer: Rule / order Hinglish Explanation: Dress code mandatory hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Rule / order”. must expresses rules and orders. Quick Rule: must expresses rules and orders.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Everything must be in its place before I come back. Answer: Order / requirement Hinglish Explanation: Speaker requirement impose kar raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Order / requirement”. must = strong order/necessity. Quick Rule: must = strong order/necessity.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You must try the seafood pasta at that restaurant. It's amazing! Answer: Strong recommendation Hinglish Explanation: Yahan legal obligation nahi; enthusiastic recommendation hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong recommendation”. must can be used for strong recommendation. Quick Rule: must can be used for strong recommendation.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? If you're interested in history, you must visit the ancient ruins in Rome. Answer: Strong recommendation Hinglish Explanation: Very strong travel recommendation hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong recommendation”. must can express emphatic recommendation. Quick Rule: must can express emphatic recommendation."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=6", "t": "Ought to & Used to · moral duty and habits", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We ought to respect our elders. Answer: Moral duty / advice Hinglish Explanation: Respect karna moral duty/expected behaviour hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Moral duty / advice”. ought to + V1 = moral obligation/advice. Quick Rule: ought to + V1 = moral obligation/advice.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Drivers ought to follow traffic rules. Answer: Duty / expected behaviour Hinglish Explanation: Traffic rules follow karna duty hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Duty / expected behaviour”. ought to + V1 = duty/advice. Quick Rule: ought to + V1 = duty/advice.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? People ought to be responsible for their actions. Answer: Moral obligation Hinglish Explanation: Responsibility moral expectation hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Moral obligation”. ought to + V1 expresses moral duty/advice. Quick Rule: ought to + V1 expresses moral duty/advice.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I used to play guitar in college. Answer: Past habit/state that is no longer current Hinglish Explanation: College time ka past habit hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past habit/state that is no longer current”. used to + V1 = past habit/state, typically no longer true now. Quick Rule: used to + V1 = past habit/state, typically no longer true now.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I used to go to school by bicycle. Answer: Past habit Hinglish Explanation: Repeated past routine batayi gayi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past habit”. used to + V1 = past habit. Quick Rule: used to + V1 = past habit.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He is used to smoking. Answer: Present familiarity / accustomed to Hinglish Explanation: “Be used to” ka meaning accustomed hai; yahan “to” preposition hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Present familiarity / accustomed to”. be used to + noun/V-ing = be accustomed to. Quick Rule: be used to + noun/V-ing = be accustomed to.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She is used to eating Idli Dosa. Answer: Present familiarity / accustomed to Hinglish Explanation: She is accustomed to this food habit. English Explanation: She is accustomed to this food habit. Rule: be used to + V-ing = be accustomed to. Quick Rule: be used to + V-ing = be accustomed to."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=7", "t": "Need · main verb vs modal verb", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He needs my help. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: “Needs” s/es le raha hai, isliye lexical/main verb hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb”. As a main verb, need conjugates normally: need/needs/needed and uses do-support for negatives/questions. Quick Rule: As a main verb, need conjugates normally: need/needs/needed and uses do-support for negatives/questions.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We need two more players for the team. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “need” ordinary lexical verb hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb”. Main-verb need can take a noun object. Quick Rule: Main-verb need can take a noun object.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He needed some more time to decide the question. Answer: Main verb · past form Hinglish Explanation: “Needed” past tense form hai, jo modal use me nahi hota. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb · past form”. Main-verb need has normal past “needed”. Quick Rule: Main-verb need has normal past “needed”.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He doesn't need my help. Answer: Main verb after do-support Hinglish Explanation: “doesn’t” helper hai; main verb base “need” hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb after do-support”. Main-verb need forms negatives with do/does/did + not + need. Quick Rule: Main-verb need forms negatives with do/does/did + not + need.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We don't need two more players for the team. Answer: Main verb after do-support Hinglish Explanation: “do not need” ordinary lexical construction hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb after do-support”. Main-verb need uses do-support in negatives. Quick Rule: Main-verb need uses do-support in negatives.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He didn't need any more time to decide the question. Answer: Main verb after did Hinglish Explanation: Past negative me “didn’t + need”, not “didn’t needed”. English Explanation: Past negative me “didn’t + need”, not “didn’t needed”. Rule: After did not, use V1: need. Quick Rule: After did not, use V1: need.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He need not ask my permission. Answer: Modal need · no necessity Hinglish Explanation: No do-support aur “need” unchanged hai; modal use hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal need · no necessity”. Modal need: need not + V1 = lack of necessity. Quick Rule: Modal need: need not + V1 = lack of necessity.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? They need not make such a fuss over it. Answer: Modal need · no necessity Hinglish Explanation: “need not + V1” means it is unnecessary. English Explanation: “need not + V1” means it is unnecessary. Rule: Modal need not + bare infinitive. Quick Rule: Modal need not + bare infinitive.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You need not mention this to anyone else. Answer: Modal need · no necessity Hinglish Explanation: Mention karna necessary nahi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal need · no necessity”. need not + V1 = do not need to. Quick Rule: need not + V1 = do not need to.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Need we write this down? Answer: Modal need in a question Hinglish Explanation: Question directly “Need we...?” se start hota hai, no do-support. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal need in a question”. Modal need can invert directly: Need + subject + V1...? Quick Rule: Modal need can invert directly: Need + subject + V1...?","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He needs new shoes. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: “needs” inflected form hai, clear main-verb use. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb”. Main need takes -s with third-person singular. Quick Rule: Main need takes -s with third-person singular.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Need I come again? Answer: Modal verb Hinglish Explanation: Direct inversion, no “do”; modal use. English Explanation: Direct inversion, no “do”; modal use. Rule: Modal need: Need I + V1...? Quick Rule: Modal need: Need I + V1...?","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You needn&#x27;t drive me to the station. I can take the bus. Answer: Modal need · lack of necessity Hinglish Explanation: Driving necessary nahi hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal need · lack of necessity”. needn’t + V1 = no need to do something. Quick Rule: needn’t + V1 = no need to do something.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Do you need any money? Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: “Do” helper shows “need” is main verb here. English Explanation: “Do” helper shows “need” is main verb here. Rule: Main-verb need uses do-support in questions. Quick Rule: Main-verb need uses do-support in questions.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I need to go there. Answer: Main verb + to-infinitive Hinglish Explanation: Main verb “need” ke baad to-infinitive aa raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb + to-infinitive”. Main need + to + V1. Quick Rule: Main need + to + V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I needn&#x27;t go there. Answer: Modal need Hinglish Explanation: No do-support; modal negative. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal need”. Modal need + not + V1. Quick Rule: Modal need + not + V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I need hardly say that I am very grateful. Answer: Modal/semi-modal in formal expression Hinglish Explanation: Formal negative-like “hardly” ke saath bare “say” aata hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal/semi-modal in formal expression”. In formal English, need can behave modally with negative/limiting adverbs such as hardly. Quick Rule: In formal English, need can behave modally with negative/limiting adverbs such as hardly."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=8", "t": "Dare · main verb vs modal verb", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Take it, I dare you. Answer: Main verb meaning challenge Hinglish Explanation: “Dare” ka lexical meaning challenge karna hai aur object “you” leta hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb meaning challenge”. Main-verb dare = challenge someone. Quick Rule: Main-verb dare = challenge someone.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? No one dared him to do that prank. Answer: Main verb · past form Hinglish Explanation: Past tense “dared” + object + to-infinitive shows main-verb use. English Explanation: Past tense “dared” + object + to-infinitive shows main-verb use. Rule: Main dare: dare/dared + object + to + V1. Quick Rule: Main dare: dare/dared + object + to + V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I didn't dare to say that to her face. Answer: Main verb after do-support Hinglish Explanation: “didn’t” helper hai aur “dare to say” main-verb pattern. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb after do-support”. Main dare often uses do-support and can take to-infinitive. Quick Rule: Main dare often uses do-support and can take to-infinitive.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I don't dare to mention it to him. Answer: Main verb after do-support Hinglish Explanation: “don’t dare to...” ordinary main-verb pattern hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb after do-support”. Main dare: do/does/did + dare + to V1. Quick Rule: Main dare: do/does/did + dare + to V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She didn't dare to go home late. Answer: Main verb after do-support Hinglish Explanation: Past negative with did shows main-verb use. English Explanation: Past negative with did shows main-verb use. Rule: Main-verb dare uses do-support. Quick Rule: Main-verb dare uses do-support.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Does anyone dare to go there? Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: “Does” helper + “dare to” = lexical/main verb. English Explanation: “Does” helper + “dare to” = lexical/main verb. Rule: Main-verb dare can form questions with do-support. Quick Rule: Main-verb dare can form questions with do-support.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? No one dare go there. Answer: Modal dare Hinglish Explanation: No -s and bare infinitive “go”; modal pattern. English Explanation: No -s and bare infinitive “go”; modal pattern. Rule: Modal dare is uninflected and takes bare infinitive. Quick Rule: Modal dare is uninflected and takes bare infinitive.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Dare anyone go there? Answer: Modal dare in inversion Hinglish Explanation: Question direct modal inversion se bana hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal dare in inversion”. Modal dare: Dare + subject + V1...? Quick Rule: Modal dare: Dare + subject + V1...?","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Dare I ask how the project's going? Answer: Modal dare · polite/bold question Hinglish Explanation: “Dare I ask...?” fixed/formal modal-like expression hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Modal dare · polite/bold question”. Dare I + V1...? is a common modal-like expression. Quick Rule: Dare I + V1...? is a common modal-like expression.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I daren&#x27;t tell him the truth; he'll go crazy. Answer: Modal dare · negative Hinglish Explanation: No do-support aur bare “tell”; modal form. English Explanation: No do-support aur bare “tell”; modal form. Rule: Modal dare: daren’t + V1. Quick Rule: Modal dare: daren’t + V1.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He doesn't dare to go there. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: “doesn’t + dare + to V1” main-verb construction hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Main verb”. Main dare uses do-support and often to-infinitive. Quick Rule: Main dare uses do-support and often to-infinitive.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? No one dares to go there. Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: Third-person -s and to-infinitive prove main-verb use. English Explanation: Third-person -s and to-infinitive prove main-verb use. Rule: Main dare inflects: dare/dares/dared. Quick Rule: Main dare inflects: dare/dares/dared.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? No one dare go there. Answer: Modal verb Hinglish Explanation: Uninflected dare + bare infinitive = modal-like use. English Explanation: Uninflected dare + bare infinitive = modal-like use. Rule: Modal dare takes bare infinitive and no -s. Quick Rule: Modal dare takes bare infinitive and no -s.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Dare anyone go there? Answer: Modal verb Hinglish Explanation: Direct inversion = modal-like dare. English Explanation: Direct inversion = modal-like dare. Rule: Modal dare inverts without do. Quick Rule: Modal dare inverts without do.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Doesn't he dare to go there? Answer: Main verb Hinglish Explanation: Doesn’t + dare to = lexical/main pattern. English Explanation: Doesn’t + dare to = lexical/main pattern. Rule: Main-verb dare uses do-support. Quick Rule: Main-verb dare uses do-support.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Daren&#x27;t he go there? Answer: Modal dare Hinglish Explanation: Direct negative inversion, no do-support. English Explanation: Direct negative inversion, no do-support. Rule: Modal dare can form daren’t + subject + V1...? Quick Rule: Modal dare can form daren’t + subject + V1...?","Choose the correct form for the blank. I didn't ___ the truth. Answer: dare to tell Hinglish Explanation: Source exercise main-verb pattern prefers “didn’t dare to tell”. English Explanation: Source exercise main-verb pattern prefers “didn’t dare to tell”. Rule: After did not, use dare + to-infinitive in the main-verb pattern. Quick Rule: After did not, use dare + to-infinitive in the main-verb pattern.","Choose the correct form for the blank. Do you ___ what you think? Answer: dare say Hinglish Explanation: Source accepts bare “dare say” in this expression; “dare to say” can also be standard in main-verb use. English Explanation: Source accepts bare “dare say” in this expression; “dare to say” can also be standard in main-verb use. Rule: “Dare say” is a common fixed expression; main-verb “dare to say” is also possible in other contexts. Quick Rule: “Dare say” is a common fixed expression; main-verb “dare to say” is also possible in other contexts.","Choose the correct form for the blank. I daren't ___ what I think. Answer: say Hinglish Explanation: Modal “daren’t” ke baad bare infinitive V1. English Explanation: The blank should be filled with “say”. Modal dare + V1, without “to”. Quick Rule: Modal dare + V1, without “to”.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Dare out at night Answer: He dare not go out at night. Hinglish Explanation: Modal dare ke baad bare infinitive “go”. “He does not dare to go...” bhi main-verb alternative hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He dare not go out at night.”. Modal dare + not + V1; main-verb alternative: does not dare to + V1. Quick Rule: Modal dare + not + V1; main-verb alternative: does not dare to + V1.","Choose the correct form for the blank. She ___ the truth. Answer: doesn't dare to speak Hinglish Explanation: Source main-verb pattern “doesn’t dare to speak” prefer karta hai. English Explanation: The blank should be filled with “doesn't dare to speak”. does not + dare + to + V1 in main-verb use. Quick Rule: does not + dare + to + V1 in main-verb use."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=9", "t": "Could have + V3 · past possibility, missed opportunity, guesses", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I could have stayed up late, but I decided to go to bed early. Answer: Past possibility/ability not used Hinglish Explanation: Staying up late possible tha, but speaker ne nahi kiya. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past possibility/ability not used”. could have + V3 = something was possible/available in the past but did not happen. Quick Rule: could have + V3 = something was possible/available in the past but did not happen.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? They could have won the race, but they didn't try hard enough. Answer: Missed past possibility/opportunity Hinglish Explanation: Winning possible tha but effort insufficient tha. English Explanation: Winning possible tha but effort insufficient tha. Rule: could have + V3 can describe an unrealized past possibility. Quick Rule: could have + V3 can describe an unrealized past possibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The boy could have run away from the bullies, but he decided to confront them. Answer: Past option not taken Hinglish Explanation: Run away karna option tha, choose nahi kiya. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past option not taken”. could have + V3 = past option/ability not used. Quick Rule: could have + V3 = past option/ability not used.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I couldn&#x27;t have arrived any earlier. There was a terrible traffic jam. Answer: Past impossibility Hinglish Explanation: Earlier arrival impossible thi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past impossibility”. couldn’t have + V3 = something was impossible in the past. Quick Rule: couldn’t have + V3 = something was impossible in the past.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? He couldn&#x27;t have passed the exam, even if he had studied harder. It's a really difficult exam. Answer: Past impossibility Hinglish Explanation: Speaker says passing was impossible even under better conditions. English Explanation: Speaker says passing was impossible even under better conditions. Rule: couldn’t have + V3 can express past impossibility. Quick Rule: couldn’t have + V3 can express past impossibility.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You could have crashed into that wall! Answer: Past possibility / near-miss guess Hinglish Explanation: Crash ho sakta tha; speaker potential past outcome describe kar raha hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past possibility / near-miss guess”. could have + V3 can refer to a possible past result that fortunately did not happen. Quick Rule: could have + V3 can refer to a possible past result that fortunately did not happen.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? We could have got into an accident. Answer: Possible past outcome Hinglish Explanation: Accident possible tha but actually nahi hua. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Possible past outcome”. could have + V3 = possible past outcome. Quick Rule: could have + V3 = possible past outcome."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=10", "t": "Should have + V3 · regret, criticism, past advice", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I should have studied harder! Answer: Past advice/regret about something not done Hinglish Explanation: Speaker regret karta hai ki usne enough study nahi ki. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past advice/regret about something not done”. should have + V3 = it would have been better/right to do it, but it did not happen. Quick Rule: should have + V3 = it would have been better/right to do it, but it did not happen.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I shouldn&#x27;t have eaten so much cake! Answer: Regret/criticism about a past action that happened Hinglish Explanation: Cake zyada khaya aur ab regret hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Regret/criticism about a past action that happened”. should not have + V3 = action happened but was a bad idea. Quick Rule: should not have + V3 = action happened but was a bad idea.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? You should have asked my permission before you used my computer. Answer: Past criticism / expected action not done Hinglish Explanation: Permission pehle leni chahiye thi but nahi li. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Past criticism / expected action not done”. should have + V3 = criticism/advice about a past action not taken. Quick Rule: should have + V3 = criticism/advice about a past action not taken."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=11", "t": "Would have + V3 · unreal past result / intended action", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. If I had enough money , I would have bought a car. Answer: had had enough money Hinglish Explanation: Past unreal result “would have bought” ke saath standard third conditional me if-clause “had had enough money” hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “had had enough money”. Third conditional: if + had + V3, would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third conditional: if + had + V3, would have + V3.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? If we had arrived earlier, we would have caught our flight. Answer: Unreal past result Hinglish Explanation: Arrival earlier nahi hui, isliye flight catch nahi hui. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Unreal past result”. Third conditional: if + had + V3 → would have + V3. Quick Rule: Third conditional: if + had + V3 → would have + V3.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? If I had brought my umbrella, I wouldn&#x27;t have got wet in the rain. Answer: Unreal past result Hinglish Explanation: Umbrella nahi laya; result me wet hua. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Unreal past result”. would have + V3 expresses an unreal/counterfactual past result. Quick Rule: would have + V3 expresses an unreal/counterfactual past result.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I would have helped you. I didn't know you needed help. Answer: Intended/willing past action that did not happen Hinglish Explanation: Help karna chahta tha, but information missing thi. English Explanation: Help karna chahta tha, but information missing thi. Rule: would have + V3 can describe what someone would have done under different past circumstances. Quick Rule: would have + V3 can describe what someone would have done under different past circumstances.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? I would have called you, but I didn't know your number. Answer: Intended past action not carried out Hinglish Explanation: Call karne ka intention tha, condition/information missing thi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Intended past action not carried out”. would have + V3 can express unrealized past intention. Quick Rule: would have + V3 can express unrealized past intention."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=10.+Modals&section=12", "t": "May/Might/Must have + V3 · past possibility & deduction", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 10. Modals", "x": ["What does the highlighted modal/form express here? Tanu hasn't arrived yet. She may have missed the train. Answer: Possible past explanation Hinglish Explanation: Train miss hona ek possible explanation hai, certainty nahi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Possible past explanation”. may have + V3 = possible past event. Quick Rule: may have + V3 = possible past event.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? What was that noise? It might have been an airplane. Answer: Tentative past possibility Hinglish Explanation: Speaker guess kar raha hai, uncertain hai. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Tentative past possibility”. might have + V3 = tentative possibility about the past. Quick Rule: might have + V3 = tentative possibility about the past.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? The roads are wet, so it must have rained earlier. Answer: Strong past deduction Hinglish Explanation: Wet roads evidence hai; speaker strong conclusion nikalta hai ki rain hui hogi. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong past deduction”. must have + V3 = strong logical deduction about a past event. Quick Rule: must have + V3 = strong logical deduction about a past event.","What does the highlighted modal/form express here? She must have forgotten about the meeting. Answer: Strong past deduction Hinglish Explanation: Current situation se strong conclusion hai ki she forgot. English Explanation: Here, the highlighted word or form expresses “Strong past deduction”. must have + V3 = strong inference about the past. Quick Rule: must have + V3 = strong inference about the past."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=1", "t": "Adverb basics · what an adverb modifies", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She is walking slowly . Answer: Adverb modifying a verb Hinglish Explanation: “Slowly” batata hai walking kis tarah ho rahi hai; isliye ye verb “walking” ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying a verb”. An adverb can modify a verb and tell how an action happens. Quick Rule: An adverb can modify a verb and tell how an action happens.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She is very beautiful. Answer: Adverb modifying an adjective Hinglish Explanation: “Very” adjective “beautiful” ki degree/intensity badha raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying an adjective”. An adverb can modify an adjective. Quick Rule: An adverb can modify an adjective.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She is walking very slowly. Answer: Adverb modifying another adverb Hinglish Explanation: “Very” adverb “slowly” ko intensify kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying another adverb”. An adverb can modify another adverb. Quick Rule: An adverb can modify another adverb.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. The bird flew exactly over his head. Answer: Adverb modifying a prepositional phrase Hinglish Explanation: “Exactly” phrase “over his head” ko precise banata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying a prepositional phrase”. Adverbs can qualify prepositional phrases. Quick Rule: Adverbs can qualify prepositional phrases.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I like her simply because she loves singing. Answer: Adverb modifying the reason-clause relation Hinglish Explanation: “Simply” poore because-clause ke reason ko qualify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying the reason-clause relation”. Adverbs can modify a clause/conjunctional relation, not only single verbs/adjectives. Quick Rule: Adverbs can modify a clause/conjunctional relation, not only single verbs/adjectives."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=2", "t": "Simple adverbs · time, place, frequency, degree, reason, manner", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She came yesterday . Answer: Adverb of time Hinglish Explanation: “Yesterday” action ka time batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of time”. Words answering “when?” are adverbs of time. Quick Rule: Words answering “when?” are adverbs of time.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. He went there . Answer: Adverb of place Hinglish Explanation: “There” batata hai he went where. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of place”. Words answering “where?” are adverbs of place. Quick Rule: Words answering “where?” are adverbs of place.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I always go to college. Answer: Adverb of frequency Hinglish Explanation: “Always” action kitni baar hota hai batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of frequency”. Words answering “how often?” are adverbs of frequency. Quick Rule: Words answering “how often?” are adverbs of frequency.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She is very beautiful. Answer: Adverb of degree Hinglish Explanation: “Very” adjective ki intensity batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of degree”. Degree adverbs show intensity, e.g. very, too, quite, much. Quick Rule: Degree adverbs show intensity, e.g. very, too, quite, much.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I therefore left college. Answer: Adverb of reason/result Hinglish Explanation: “Therefore” pehle reason se result connect karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of reason/result”. Therefore/consequently/hence commonly function as result/reason adverbs. Quick Rule: Therefore/consequently/hence commonly function as result/reason adverbs.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She sings beautifully . Answer: Adverb of manner Hinglish Explanation: “Beautifully” batata hai she sings how. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of manner”. Manner adverbs answer “how?” Quick Rule: Manner adverbs answer “how?”","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. They fought bravely . Answer: Adverb of manner Hinglish Explanation: “Bravely” fighting ka manner batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of manner”. Many manner adverbs are adjective + -ly. Quick Rule: Many manner adverbs are adjective + -ly."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=3", "t": "Interrogative adverbs · question words", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. When did she come? Answer: Interrogative adverb of time Hinglish Explanation: “When” directly time puchh raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of time”. Interrogative adverbs introduce questions and ask time/place/frequency/degree/reason/manner. Quick Rule: Interrogative adverbs introduce questions and ask time/place/frequency/degree/reason/manner.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Where do they live? Answer: Interrogative adverb of place Hinglish Explanation: “Where” place puchhta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of place”. Where = interrogative adverb of place in a direct question. Quick Rule: Where = interrogative adverb of place in a direct question.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. How often do you go there? Answer: Interrogative adverb of frequency Hinglish Explanation: “How often” frequency puchhta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of frequency”. How often / how many times ask frequency. Quick Rule: How often / how many times ask frequency.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. How much did you pay for this phone? Answer: Interrogative adverb of degree/amount Hinglish Explanation: Yahan amount/degree puchhi ja rahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of degree/amount”. How much can function interrogatively to ask degree/amount. Quick Rule: How much can function interrogatively to ask degree/amount.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Why did you do this? Answer: Interrogative adverb of reason Hinglish Explanation: “Why” reason puchhta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of reason”. Why asks reason. Quick Rule: Why asks reason.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. How did she behave with you? Answer: Interrogative adverb of manner Hinglish Explanation: “How” behaviour ka manner puchhta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative adverb of manner”. How asks manner/in what way. Quick Rule: How asks manner/in what way."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=4", "t": "Relative adverbs · connecting clauses", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. This is the time when she comes. Answer: Relative adverb of time Hinglish Explanation: “When” “time” ko relative clause se connect kar raha hai; direct question nahi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Relative adverb of time”. Relative adverbs connect a noun/idea to a clause: when (time), where (place), why (reason). Quick Rule: Relative adverbs connect a noun/idea to a clause: when (time), where (place), why (reason).","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Do you know the place where she lives? Answer: Relative adverb of place Hinglish Explanation: “Where” “place” ko clause “she lives” se join karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Relative adverb of place”. Relative where refers back to a place. Quick Rule: Relative where refers back to a place.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I do not know when he will come. Answer: Embedded/relative-type adverb of time Hinglish Explanation: Ye direct question nahi hai; “when” embedded clause introduce kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Embedded/relative-type adverb of time”. Question words can introduce embedded clauses without question-word inversion. Quick Rule: Question words can introduce embedded clauses without question-word inversion.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I know where Rahul lives. Answer: Embedded adverb of place Hinglish Explanation: “Where Rahul lives” ek embedded clause hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Embedded adverb of place”. In embedded questions, use statement word order: where + subject + verb. Quick Rule: In embedded questions, use statement word order: where + subject + verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=5", "t": "Manner adverbs · adjective vs adverb corrections", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. She answered the police officer's questions truthful because there is nothing to hide. Answer: truthfully Hinglish Explanation: “Answered” action ko qualify karna hai, isliye adjective “truthful” nahi, adverb “truthfully”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “truthfully”. A verb is generally modified by an adverb. Quick Rule: A verb is generally modified by an adverb.","Correct the highlighted part. Adam expert maneuvered the components of the machine into their proper positions. Answer: expertly Hinglish Explanation: “Maneuvered” verb ko manner chahiye: expertly. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “expertly”. Use an adverb to describe how an action is performed. Quick Rule: Use an adverb to describe how an action is performed.","Correct the highlighted part. Jenn is reading so quick that it is difficult to understand even a single word. Answer: quickly Hinglish Explanation: Reading ka manner hai, so “quickly”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “quickly”. Verb + manner → adverb, not adjective. Quick Rule: Verb + manner → adverb, not adjective.","Correct the highlighted part. I am full of energy today because I slept sound last night. Answer: soundly Hinglish Explanation: “Slept” ka manner “soundly” hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “soundly”. Sleep soundly = sleep deeply/well. Quick Rule: Sleep soundly = sleep deeply/well.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Her outfit showcased her delightfully quirky personality. Answer: Adverb modifying the adjective “quirky” Hinglish Explanation: “Delightfully” “quirky” ki quality/intensity ko modify karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb modifying the adjective “quirky””. An adverb can modify an adjective. Quick Rule: An adverb can modify an adjective.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Carefully sentence Answer: All of them work very carefully right from the beginning till they finish. Hinglish Explanation: “Work” ko “carefully” modify karta hai; source marks no error. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “All of them work very carefully right from the beginning till they finish.”. Manner after an action verb is normally adverbial. Quick Rule: Manner after an action verb is normally adverbial.","Correct the highlighted part. More than half the food products targeted at babies and toddlers have a high sugar content and are excessive sweet . Answer: excessively sweet Hinglish Explanation: “Sweet” adjective ko degree word chahiye; “excessively” adverb hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “excessively sweet”. Adverb + adjective: excessively sweet. Quick Rule: Adverb + adjective: excessively sweet.","Correct the highlighted part. She was so emotional stable that she was not moved by their decision to suspend her. Answer: emotionally stable Hinglish Explanation: “Stable” adjective ko “emotionally” modify karega. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “emotionally stable”. Use an adverb before an adjective when describing degree/manner of the quality. Quick Rule: Use an adverb before an adjective when describing degree/manner of the quality."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=6", "t": "Too, very & so...that · degree patterns", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. The old man is too weak to walk. Answer: Excessive degree causing inability Hinglish Explanation: “Too weak to walk” = itna weak ki walk nahi kar sakta. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Excessive degree causing inability”. too + adjective + to + V1 often means “more than desirable/possible”. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to + V1 often means “more than desirable/possible”.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. He is too dull to learn. Answer: Excessive degree preventing the action Hinglish Explanation: “Too dull” learning ko practically impossible/very difficult batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Excessive degree preventing the action”. too + adjective + to-infinitive expresses excess. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to-infinitive expresses excess.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. He is too lazy to clean his room. Answer: Excessive degree preventing the action Hinglish Explanation: Laziness itni hai ki room clean nahi karta/sakta. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Excessive degree preventing the action”. too + adjective + to + V1. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to + V1.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. This news is too good to be true . Answer: Idiomatic “so good that it seems unbelievable” Hinglish Explanation: “Too good to be true” standard idiom hai; yahan good quality ke saath “too” valid hai because phrase means beyond believable. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Idiomatic “so good that it seems unbelievable””. Too is not restricted to “bad” adjectives; it marks excess relative to a limit/context. Quick Rule: Too is not restricted to “bad” adjectives; it marks excess relative to a limit/context.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She is too beautiful to be rejected by any young man. Answer: Excessive degree in a result pattern Hinglish Explanation: Structure source ka example hai: too + adjective + to be + V3. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Excessive degree in a result pattern”. too + adjective + to be + past participle can express a result judged unlikely/impossible. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to be + past participle can express a result judged unlikely/impossible.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Too → so...that transformation Answer: She is so beautiful that she cannot be rejected by any young man. Hinglish Explanation: “Too beautiful to be rejected” ko “so...that + cannot” se paraphrase kiya ja sakta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She is so beautiful that she cannot be rejected by any young man.”. too + adjective + to-infinitive ≈ so + adjective + that + negative result, when meaning is inability/impossibility. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to-infinitive ≈ so + adjective + that + negative result, when meaning is inability/impossibility.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Too weak → so...that transformation Answer: The old man is so weak that he cannot walk. Hinglish Explanation: “Too weak to walk” ka clear result-paraphrase “so weak that he cannot walk” hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “The old man is so weak that he cannot walk.”. so + adjective + that + clause expresses result. Quick Rule: so + adjective + that + clause expresses result."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=7", "t": "Very vs much · positive, comparative & superlative degree", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. I am too glad . Answer: very glad Hinglish Explanation: Standalone positive adjective “glad” ko normal intensifier “very” chahiye; “too” excess/undesirable limit ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “very glad”. very + positive-degree adjective; too = excessive degree relative to a limit. Quick Rule: very + positive-degree adjective; too = excessive degree relative to a limit.","Correct the highlighted part. This news is too good . Answer: very good Hinglish Explanation: Without a limiting/result context, intended simple praise ke liye “very good” natural hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “very good”. Use very with a positive-degree adjective for ordinary intensification. Quick Rule: Use very with a positive-degree adjective for ordinary intensification.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Too + result sentence Answer: She is too beautiful to be rejected by any young man. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “too...to” result structure complete hai, so source marks it correct. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She is too beautiful to be rejected by any young man.”. too + adjective + to-infinitive is a complete excess/result construction. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to-infinitive is a complete excess/result construction.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Comparative intensifier Answer: She is much more honest than I am. Hinglish Explanation: Comparative “more honest” ko “much” intensify karta hai, “very” nahi. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She is much more honest than I am.”. much/far/a lot + comparative; very + positive degree. Quick Rule: much/far/a lot + comparative; very + positive degree.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Comparative with much Answer: She is much more honest than I am. Hinglish Explanation: “Much more honest” standard comparative intensification hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She is much more honest than I am.”. much + comparative form. Quick Rule: much + comparative form.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Superlative intensifier Answer: She is by far the most diligent girl in the class. Hinglish Explanation: Modern standard English me superlative ko “by far” strongly intensify karta hai. Source “much the most” bhi dikhata hai, jo formal/less common usage hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She is by far the most diligent girl in the class.”. Superlatives can be intensified by by far, easily, much (often “much the best/most…” in formal usage). Quick Rule: Superlatives can be intensified by by far, easily, much (often “much the best/most…” in formal usage).","Correct the highlighted part. Mehini won this beauty pageant because she is too smart and compassionate. Answer: very smart Hinglish Explanation: Sentence me excess/negative result nahi hai; intended praise ke liye “very smart” better hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “very smart”. Use very for ordinary positive intensification; too implies excess relative to a limit. Quick Rule: Use very for ordinary positive intensification; too implies excess relative to a limit."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=8", "t": "Degree adverbs in comparison · much, so, too", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. Time is very important than money. Answer: much more important Hinglish Explanation: “Than” comparative mangta hai: “more important”; comparative ko “much” intensify kar sakta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “much more important”. much + comparative + than. Quick Rule: much + comparative + than.","Correct the highlighted part. The famine was very severe that several people perished. Answer: so severe Hinglish Explanation: Result clause “that…” ke saath “so + adjective + that” chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “so severe”. so + adjective + that + result clause. Quick Rule: so + adjective + that + result clause.","Correct the highlighted part. Climbing plants had grown over the walls, giving the building an appearance very more ancient than it was before. Answer: much more ancient Hinglish Explanation: Comparative “more ancient” ke saath “much” lagta hai, “very” nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “much more ancient”. much/far/a lot + comparative. Quick Rule: much/far/a lot + comparative.","Correct the highlighted part. It was much difficult to come out because the stadium was full of viewers. Answer: too difficult Hinglish Explanation: Meaning hai difficulty limit se zyada thi; source “too difficult” correct karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “too difficult”. too + adjective + to-infinitive/result context expresses excessive degree. Quick Rule: too + adjective + to-infinitive/result context expresses excessive degree.","Correct the highlighted part. The policies framed by the Kerala state government nowadays are much good as compared to the previous policies. Answer: much better Hinglish Explanation: Comparison hai, so “good → better”; “much better” natural intensification hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “much better”. Irregular comparison: good → better; much can intensify better. Quick Rule: Irregular comparison: good → better; much can intensify better.","Correct the highlighted part. This is the much lowest price I can offer for quality like this. Answer: by far the lowest Hinglish Explanation: Modern standard idiom me “by far the lowest” sabse natural hai. Source “much the lowest” pattern dikhata hai, but “the much lowest” nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “by far the lowest”. Use by far + superlative, or in formal usage much + the + superlative. Quick Rule: Use by far + superlative, or in formal usage much + the + superlative."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=9", "t": "Fast, late & lately · similar-looking forms", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. The night was too cold that we had to wear two coats. Answer: so cold that Hinglish Explanation: Result-clause “that…” ke saath “so cold that” correct hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “so cold that”. so + adjective + that + result clause. Quick Rule: so + adjective + that + result clause.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. A fast train arrived. Answer: Adjective modifying “train” Hinglish Explanation: “Fast” noun “train” ko modify kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective modifying “train””. Fast can be an adjective; “fastly” is not standard. Quick Rule: Fast can be an adjective; “fastly” is not standard.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Bolt runs fast . Answer: Adverb of manner Hinglish Explanation: “Fast” verb “runs” ka manner batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of manner”. Fast is both adjective and adverb; do not use “fastly”. Quick Rule: Fast is both adjective and adverb; do not use “fastly”.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. The train is running fast . Answer: Adverb of manner Hinglish Explanation: Running ka manner “fast” hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb of manner”. The adverb form is fast, not fastly. Quick Rule: The adverb form is fast, not fastly.","Correct the highlighted part. He ran so fastly that he reached the destination in just two minutes. Answer: fast Hinglish Explanation: “Fastly” standard adverb nahi; “fast” hi adjective aur adverb dono hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “fast”. Use fast, not fastly. Quick Rule: Use fast, not fastly.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. My late grandfather was a teacher. Answer: Adjective meaning “deceased/former” in this context Hinglish Explanation: Noun “grandfather” ko modify kar raha hai; yahan “late” deceased ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adjective meaning “deceased/former” in this context”. Late can be an adjective; lately means recently. Quick Rule: Late can be an adjective; lately means recently.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. This train will come late . Answer: Adverb meaning “after the expected time” Hinglish Explanation: “Late” arrival ka time/manner batata hai: expected time se der se. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb meaning “after the expected time””. Late can be an adverb; lately means recently. Quick Rule: Late can be an adverb; lately means recently."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=10", "t": "Lately, hard & hardly · meaning changes", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. Have you seen Rahul lately ? Answer: Adverb meaning “recently” Hinglish Explanation: “Lately” = recently/in recent times. English Explanation: “Lately” = recently/in recent times. Rule: Lately means recently, not “late”. Quick Rule: Lately means recently, not “late”.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I have not seen him lately . Answer: Adverb meaning “recently” Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect ke saath “lately” recent period batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb meaning “recently””. Lately often occurs with present perfect to mean recently. Quick Rule: Lately often occurs with present perfect to mean recently.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She works hard . Answer: Adverb meaning “with great effort” Hinglish Explanation: “Hard” yahan effort batata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb meaning “with great effort””. Hard = with effort; hardly = scarcely/barely. Quick Rule: Hard = with effort; hardly = scarcely/barely.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. I hardly talk to them. Answer: Adverb meaning “scarcely/almost not” Hinglish Explanation: “Hardly” = almost not. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Adverb meaning “scarcely/almost not””. Hardly has a negative-like meaning: scarcely/barely. Quick Rule: Hardly has a negative-like meaning: scarcely/barely.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. She hardly goes there. Answer: Adverb meaning “rarely/almost never” Hinglish Explanation: “Hardly goes” means she almost never goes. English Explanation: “Hardly goes” means she almost never goes. Rule: Hardly can express very low frequency/degree. Quick Rule: Hardly can express very low frequency/degree.","Correct the highlighted part. She cannot succeed because she works hard . Answer: hardly works Hinglish Explanation: Source ka intended meaning “she barely works, so she cannot succeed” hai; isliye “hardly works”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hardly works”. Hardly = scarcely/almost not; position is normally before the main verb. Quick Rule: Hardly = scarcely/almost not; position is normally before the main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. He worked hardly and broke all the previous records. Answer: worked hard Hinglish Explanation: Record break karne ke context me “with great effort” chahiye = hard. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “worked hard”. Hard = with effort; hardly = scarcely. Quick Rule: Hard = with effort; hardly = scarcely.","Correct the highlighted part. She works hard , so she is sure to fail. Answer: hardly works Hinglish Explanation: Intended logic failure due to almost no work hai, so “hardly works”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hardly works”. Hardly normally goes before the main verb: she hardly works. Quick Rule: Hardly normally goes before the main verb: she hardly works."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=11", "t": "Adverbs of time · last/next and prepositions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. My father went to London on last Monday . Answer: last Monday Hinglish Explanation: With last/next + day used deictically, preposition is normally omitted: last Monday. English Explanation: With last/next + day used deictically, preposition is normally omitted: last Monday. Rule: Say last Monday / next Friday, not on last Monday / on next Friday. Quick Rule: Say last Monday / next Friday, not on last Monday / on next Friday.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Alternative order shown in source Answer: My father went to London on Monday last. Hinglish Explanation: “Monday last” is a formal/older alternative meaning last Monday, and “on” can precede it. English Explanation: “Monday last” is a formal/older alternative meaning last Monday, and “on” can precede it. Rule: on + Monday last / Friday next is possible, though ordinary modern English prefers last Monday / next Friday. Quick Rule: on + Monday last / Friday next is possible, though ordinary modern English prefers last Monday / next Friday.","Correct the highlighted part. He will go to Shimla on next Friday . Answer: next Friday Hinglish Explanation: Ordinary modern English omits preposition before “next Friday”. English Explanation: Ordinary modern English omits preposition before “next Friday”. Rule: No preposition before next/last + day when used as a time adverbial. Quick Rule: No preposition before next/last + day when used as a time adverbial.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Alternative order shown in source Answer: He will go to Shimla on Friday next. Hinglish Explanation: “Friday next” is a formal alternative; source uses this pattern. English Explanation: “Friday next” is a formal alternative; source uses this pattern. Rule: on + Friday next is possible in formal/older usage. Quick Rule: on + Friday next is possible in formal/older usage.","Correct the highlighted part. She completed her graduation in last year . Answer: last year Hinglish Explanation: “Last year” direct time adverbial hai, preposition nahi chahiye. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “last year”. Say last year, next month, last week without in/on/at in ordinary time-adverbial use. Quick Rule: Say last year, next month, last week without in/on/at in ordinary time-adverbial use.","Correct the highlighted part. My grandfather died in last month . Answer: last month Hinglish Explanation: “Last month” ke pehle “in” unnecessary hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “last month”. No preposition before last + month/week/year in ordinary adverbial use. Quick Rule: No preposition before last + month/week/year in ordinary adverbial use.","Correct the highlighted part. Donald Trump insisted on going to the US Supreme Court in last week . Answer: last week Hinglish Explanation: Source-target phrase “last week” hai; ordinary usage me “in” remove hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “last week”. Use last week directly as a time adverbial. Quick Rule: Use last week directly as a time adverbial.","Correct the highlighted part. They had scheduled one more meeting with us on next Tuesday . Answer: next Tuesday Hinglish Explanation: “Next Tuesday” ordinary modern English me without preposition use hota hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “next Tuesday”. No preposition before next + day when the meaning is the coming day. Quick Rule: No preposition before next + day when the meaning is the coming day."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=12", "t": "Negative-like adverbs/words · avoiding unintended double negatives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Intended meaning: he said he did not call her Answer: He denied that he had called her. Hinglish Explanation: Agar intended meaning “he rejected the claim that he called her” hai, “denied that he had called her” fit karta hai. Note: “denied that he had not called her” bhi grammatical hai, but uska meaning ulta hai—he rejected the claim that he did not call. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “He denied that he had called her.”. Avoid accidental double negatives, but judge meaning first; a negative inside a deny-clause can be grammatical if that is the intended proposition. Quick Rule: Avoid accidental double negatives, but judge meaning first; a negative inside a deny-clause can be grammatical if that is the intended proposition.","Correct the highlighted part. We are prohibited from not entering the staff room. Answer: from entering Hinglish Explanation: “Prohibited” already negative/restrictive meaning deta hai; extra “not” intended meaning ko reverse kar deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “from entering”. Prohibit someone from + -ing already means the action is not allowed. Quick Rule: Prohibit someone from + -ing already means the action is not allowed.","Correct the highlighted part. My father forbade me not to see Mohit again. Answer: to see Hinglish Explanation: “Forbade me to see” already means he told me not to see Mohit; extra not reverses the sense. English Explanation: “Forbade me to see” already means he told me not to see Mohit; extra not reverses the sense. Rule: Forbid + object + to-infinitive expresses prohibition; do not add not unless a special opposite meaning is intended. Quick Rule: Forbid + object + to-infinitive expresses prohibition; do not add not unless a special opposite meaning is intended.","Correct the highlighted part. Unless you do not study diligently, you'll never understand trigonometry. Answer: study Hinglish Explanation: “Unless” itself means “if not”; extra not creates an unintended double negative. English Explanation: “Unless” itself means “if not”; extra not creates an unintended double negative. Rule: unless = if...not; normally use an affirmative clause after unless. Quick Rule: unless = if...not; normally use an affirmative clause after unless.","Correct the highlighted part. Until she does not accept my apology, I will not leave. Answer: accepts Hinglish Explanation: “Until” marks the endpoint; ordinary intended meaning is “until she accepts”. English Explanation: “Until” marks the endpoint; ordinary intended meaning is “until she accepts”. Rule: In standard future-time clauses after until, use present simple, not an extra negative/future auxiliary. Quick Rule: In standard future-time clauses after until, use present simple, not an extra negative/future auxiliary.","Correct the highlighted part. Both of the girls have not brought the flowers. Answer: Neither of the girls has Hinglish Explanation: If meaning is “not one of the two girls”, “neither...has” is clear and avoids scope ambiguity. English Explanation: If meaning is “not one of the two girls”, “neither...has” is clear and avoids scope ambiguity. Rule: For exactly two people, neither of + plural noun + singular verb is standard formal agreement. Quick Rule: For exactly two people, neither of + plural noun + singular verb is standard formal agreement.","Correct the highlighted part. If your hands are dirty, don't touch your new dress, lest you would not spoil it . Answer: lest you should spoil it Hinglish Explanation: “Lest” already means “for fear that”; traditional exam pattern uses “lest + should + V1”, not an extra not. English Explanation: “Lest” already means “for fear that”; traditional exam pattern uses “lest + should + V1”, not an extra not. Rule: lest + subject + should + V1 (formal/traditional pattern). Quick Rule: lest + subject + should + V1 (formal/traditional pattern).","Correct the highlighted part. He noted that long-distance firms are still prohibited from not accessing certain local phone services. Answer: prohibited from accessing Hinglish Explanation: For the intended restriction, remove “not”: prohibited from accessing. English Explanation: For the intended restriction, remove “not”: prohibited from accessing. Rule: prohibit + from + -ing already carries the negative/restrictive sense. Quick Rule: prohibit + from + -ing already carries the negative/restrictive sense.","Correct the highlighted part. Our director would not have signed the contract unless she had not had a lawyer present. Answer: unless she had had Hinglish Explanation: “Unless” = if not; intended meaning: she signed only because a lawyer was present, so clause should be affirmative “had had”. English Explanation: “Unless” = if not; intended meaning: she signed only because a lawyer was present, so clause should be affirmative “had had”. Rule: Third conditional-style past condition: unless + past perfect, without an extra not when unless already supplies negation. Quick Rule: Third conditional-style past condition: unless + past perfect, without an extra not when unless already supplies negation.","Correct the highlighted part. You won't be able to get a ticket for the match unless you&#x27;re not prepared to pay a lot of money for it. Answer: unless you're prepared Hinglish Explanation: Meaning: ticket tabhi milega if you are prepared to pay; “unless” ke saath extra not intended meaning ko उलट deta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “unless you're prepared”. unless = if not; avoid redundant not. Quick Rule: unless = if not; avoid redundant not.","Correct the highlighted part. The woodman stirred the fire until the flames did not leap high and the sparks flew out of the roof hole. Answer: leaped Hinglish Explanation: Endpoint positive event hai: he stirred until the flames leaped high. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “leaped”. After until, use the positive event if that event marks the stopping point. Quick Rule: After until, use the positive event if that event marks the stopping point.","Correct the highlighted part. That was the end of the conversation and both of them had not brought the subject up again that night. Answer: neither of them had Hinglish Explanation: Intended meaning “neither person did it” ke liye “neither...had” clear hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “neither of them had”. Neither already carries negative meaning; do not add not. Quick Rule: Neither already carries negative meaning; do not add not.","Correct the highlighted part. I make it a rule to read the newspaper every day lest I may not fall behind the times. Answer: lest I should Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam English me “lest” ke baad “should + V1” use hota hai, extra not nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “lest I should”. lest + should + V1 (formal/traditional pattern). Quick Rule: lest + should + V1 (formal/traditional pattern)."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=13", "t": "Position of adverbs · before main verb / after auxiliary", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Correct the highlighted part. I will miss you certainly . Answer: certainly miss you Hinglish Explanation: Frequency/certainty-type sentence adverb “certainly” normally auxiliary “will” aur main verb “miss” ke beech aata hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “certainly miss you”. With an auxiliary, many mid-position adverbs go after the auxiliary and before the main verb. Quick Rule: With an auxiliary, many mid-position adverbs go after the auxiliary and before the main verb.","Correct the highlighted part. She goes to college hardly . Answer: hardly goes to college Hinglish Explanation: “Hardly” negative-frequency adverb hai; usual mid-position main verb se pehle. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hardly goes to college”. Frequency/negative adverbs usually precede the main verb. Quick Rule: Frequency/negative adverbs usually precede the main verb.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Seldom position Answer: She has seldom eaten chicken. Hinglish Explanation: Perfect tense me “seldom” auxiliary “has” aur main participle “eaten” ke beech natural hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “She has seldom eaten chicken.”. With auxiliaries, mid-position adverbs commonly follow the first auxiliary. Quick Rule: With auxiliaries, mid-position adverbs commonly follow the first auxiliary."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=11.+Adverbs&section=14", "t": "Inversion after negative/restrictive adverbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 11. Adverbs", "x": ["Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Never inversion Answer: Never have I gone there. Hinglish Explanation: Sentence-initial “never” negative/restrictive adverb hai, so auxiliary-subject inversion hoti hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Never have I gone there.”. Initial negative/restrictive adverb → auxiliary + subject + main verb. Quick Rule: Initial negative/restrictive adverb → auxiliary + subject + main verb.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Seldom inversion Answer: Seldom has she eaten chicken. Hinglish Explanation: Initial “seldom” inversion trigger karta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Seldom has she eaten chicken.”. Seldom/rarely/hardly/scarcely/never at the front usually trigger inversion. Quick Rule: Seldom/rarely/hardly/scarcely/never at the front usually trigger inversion.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Hardly inversion (present) Answer: Hardly does she go to college. Hinglish Explanation: Simple present me inversion ke liye “does + subject + V1”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Hardly does she go to college.”. Initial hardly + does/do + subject + V1. Quick Rule: Initial hardly + does/do + subject + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Rarely inversion Answer: Rarely do they talk to each other. Hinglish Explanation: Simple present plural subject ke saath “do they talk”. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Rarely do they talk to each other.”. Initial rarely + do/does + subject + V1. Quick Rule: Initial rarely + do/does + subject + V1.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Hardly inversion (past) Answer: Hardly did he go to college. Hinglish Explanation: Simple past inversion me did + subject + base verb. English Explanation: Simple past inversion me did + subject + base verb. Rule: Initial hardly + did + subject + V1. Quick Rule: Initial hardly + did + subject + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. He had hardly opened the door than the two strangers entered the house. Answer: hardly opened the door when Hinglish Explanation: Pair “hardly/scarcely...when” use hota hai, “than” nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “hardly opened the door when”. hardly/scarcely + past perfect ... when + simple past. Quick Rule: hardly/scarcely + past perfect ... when + simple past.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Full hardly inversion Answer: Hardly had he opened the door when the two strangers entered the house. Hinglish Explanation: Fronted “hardly” ke saath auxiliary “had” subject se pehle aata hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Hardly had he opened the door when the two strangers entered the house.”. Hardly had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past. Quick Rule: Hardly had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past.","Correct the highlighted part. We had scarcely reached home than it started raining. Answer: when it started Hinglish Explanation: “Scarcely...when” fixed pair hai. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “when it started”. scarcely/hardly ... when; no sooner ... than. Quick Rule: scarcely/hardly ... when; no sooner ... than.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Scarcely inversion Answer: Scarcely had we reached home when it started raining. Hinglish Explanation: Initial scarcely negative inversion leta hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Scarcely had we reached home when it started raining.”. Scarcely had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past. Quick Rule: Scarcely had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past.","Correct the highlighted part. No sooner the bell rang then the students came out of the class. Answer: No sooner did the bell ring than Hinglish Explanation: Simple past clause me fronted no sooner → did + subject + V1; pair “than”. English Explanation: Simple past clause me fronted no sooner → did + subject + V1; pair “than”. Rule: No sooner + auxiliary + subject + verb ... than. Quick Rule: No sooner + auxiliary + subject + verb ... than.","Correct the highlighted part. No sooner did she finished cooking than some guest arrived. Answer: did she finish Hinglish Explanation: “Did” ke baad base form V1 “finish”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “did she finish”. After do/does/did, use base verb. Quick Rule: After do/does/did, use base verb.","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. So + adverb inversion Answer: So rudely did she behave that everyone got offended. Hinglish Explanation: Fronted “so + adverb” emphatic structure me inversion hoti hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “So rudely did she behave that everyone got offended.”. So + adverb + auxiliary + subject + V1 + that... Quick Rule: So + adverb + auxiliary + subject + V1 + that...","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. So well inversion Answer: So well did she perform that everybody got impressed. Hinglish Explanation: “Perform” ko adverb “well” modify karta hai; fronted so well ke baad inversion. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “So well did she perform that everybody got impressed.”. So + adverb + did + subject + V1 + that... Quick Rule: So + adverb + did + subject + V1 + that...","Choose the grammatically correct sentence. Not only inversion Answer: Not only did she read, but she also wrote. Hinglish Explanation: “Not only” clause-initial ho to first clause me inversion hoti hai. English Explanation: The grammatically correct sentence/form is “Not only did she read, but she also wrote.”. Not only + auxiliary + subject + verb, but subject also + verb. Quick Rule: Not only + auxiliary + subject + verb, but subject also + verb.","Correct the highlighted part. Not only did she forgot my birthday, but she also didn't even apologise for forgetting it. Answer: did she forget Hinglish Explanation: “Did” ke baad base form “forget”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “did she forget”. After did, use V1. Quick Rule: After did, use V1.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. He went there. So did I . Answer: Affirmative agreement with inversion Hinglish Explanation: “So did I” means “I also went there.” English Explanation: “So did I” means “I also went there.” Rule: So + auxiliary + subject expresses agreement with an affirmative statement. Quick Rule: So + auxiliary + subject expresses agreement with an affirmative statement.","Identify the function/type of the highlighted word or phrase. He did not go there. Neither did I . Answer: Negative agreement with inversion Hinglish Explanation: “Neither did I” means “I also did not go.” English Explanation: “Neither did I” means “I also did not go.” Rule: Neither/Nor + auxiliary + subject expresses agreement with a negative statement. Quick Rule: Neither/Nor + auxiliary + subject expresses agreement with a negative statement.","Correct the highlighted part. Scarcely she had drunk the magic liquid then she began to see everything in strange and vivid colours. Answer: Scarcely had she drunk the magic liquid when Hinglish Explanation: Scarcely initial → inversion; pair “when”. English Explanation: Scarcely initial → inversion; pair “when”. Rule: Scarcely had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past. Quick Rule: Scarcely had + subject + V3 ... when + simple past.","Correct the highlighted part. No sooner the train had arrived at the station then the passengers rushed towards it. Answer: No sooner had the train arrived at the station than Hinglish Explanation: Past perfect inversion with “no sooner...than”. English Explanation: Past perfect inversion with “no sooner...than”. Rule: No sooner had + subject + V3 ... than + simple past. Quick Rule: No sooner had + subject + V3 ... than + simple past.","Correct the highlighted part. So nicely they organised the event that it was hard to tell that they are going through a financial crisis. Answer: So nicely did they organise Hinglish Explanation: Fronted “so nicely” ke baad did + subject + V1. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “So nicely did they organise”. So + adverb + did + subject + V1 + that... Quick Rule: So + adverb + did + subject + V1 + that...","Correct the highlighted part. Rarely I have a chance to read fiction. Answer: Rarely do I have Hinglish Explanation: Habitual simple present hai, so inversion do-support se: do I have. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “Rarely do I have”. Initial rarely + do/does + subject + V1. Quick Rule: Initial rarely + do/does + subject + V1.","Correct the highlighted part. No sooner did the watchman saw the chairman than he started saluting. Answer: did the watchman see Hinglish Explanation: “Did” ke baad V1 “see”. English Explanation: The highlighted part should use “did the watchman see”. After did, use base verb. Quick Rule: After did, use base verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=1", "t": "Coordinating conjunctions · FANBOYS and equal elements", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I don't like to run or swim . Answer: or Hinglish Explanation: “Run” aur “swim” do equal verb choices hain; “or” alternatives ko connect karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “or”. Use coordinating conjunctions to join grammatically equal words/phrases/clauses. Quick Rule: Use coordinating conjunctions to join grammatically equal words/phrases/clauses.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He was clever but lazy . Answer: but Hinglish Explanation: “Clever” aur “lazy” contrast kar rahe hain, isliye “but”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “but”. But shows contrast between equal elements. Quick Rule: But shows contrast between equal elements.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. The dog wagged his tail and panted excitedly . Answer: and Hinglish Explanation: Dono actions same subject “the dog” ke hain aur add ho rahe hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “and”. And joins similar/additional actions. Quick Rule: And joins similar/additional actions.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She usually studies in the library or at a cafe . Answer: or Hinglish Explanation: Do alternative places diye gaye hain, isliye “or”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “or”. Or shows alternatives. Quick Rule: Or shows alternatives.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I wanted to go shopping, but my friend wants to go to a movie. Answer: but Hinglish Explanation: Do independent clauses opposite preferences dikhate hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “but”. Use a coordinating conjunction such as but to join contrasting independent clauses. Quick Rule: Use a coordinating conjunction such as but to join contrasting independent clauses."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=2", "t": "Subordinating conjunctions · dependent clauses", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I will clean the house after the relatives are gone. Answer: after Hinglish Explanation: “After” time relation banata hai aur dependent clause introduce karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “after”. Subordinating conjunctions connect a dependent clause to a main clause. Quick Rule: Subordinating conjunctions connect a dependent clause to a main clause.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She wrote my article even though it is assigned to me. Answer: even though Hinglish Explanation: “Even though” concessive contrast dikhata hai—expected situation ke bawajood action hua. English Explanation: The correct answer is “even though”. Even though/although introduce a concessive dependent clause. Quick Rule: Even though/although introduce a concessive dependent clause.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. My father always inspires me because he believes in me. Answer: because Hinglish Explanation: Second clause reason batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “because”. Because introduces a reason clause. Quick Rule: Because introduces a reason clause.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. When he was washing my car, I went to the store. Answer: When Hinglish Explanation: “When” time clause introduce karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “When”. When is a subordinating conjunction of time. Quick Rule: When is a subordinating conjunction of time."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=3", "t": "Correlative conjunctions · paired structures", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He ate not only the ice cream but also the chocolate. Answer: not only ... but also Hinglish Explanation: Pair addition with emphasis dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “not only ... but also”. Correlative conjunctions work in fixed pairs and should connect parallel elements. Quick Rule: Correlative conjunctions work in fixed pairs and should connect parallel elements.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She likes either tea or coffee. Answer: either ... or Hinglish Explanation: Do choices me se ek ko show karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “either ... or”. Either ... or presents alternatives. Quick Rule: Either ... or presents alternatives.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Raja is both the CEO and Managing Director of the company. Answer: both ... and Hinglish Explanation: Dono roles ek saath true hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “both ... and”. Both ... and joins two positive parallel elements. Quick Rule: Both ... and joins two positive parallel elements."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=4", "t": "Although/Though · avoid but; exam-style yet", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Although he is well off, ___ he is greedy. Answer: yet Hinglish Explanation: Source ke competitive-exam pattern me “although/though ... yet” diya gaya hai. Natural English me “Although he is well off, he is greedy” bhi bilkul standard hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “yet”. Do not pair although/though with but. In traditional exam grammar, yet may be used. Quick Rule: Do not pair although/though with but. In traditional exam grammar, yet may be used.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Though he worked hard, ___ he did not succeed. Answer: yet Hinglish Explanation: Contrast already “though” se aa gaya; “but” avoid hota hai. Source “yet” ko pair karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “yet”. Although/though should not be followed by but. Quick Rule: Although/though should not be followed by but.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Although I am not so rich as you are, but I have the ability to donate more money than you can. Answer: yet Hinglish Explanation: “Although” ke saath “but” redundant hai; source correction “yet” hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “yet”. Competitive-exam rule: although/though ... yet, not although/though ... but. Quick Rule: Competitive-exam rule: although/though ... yet, not although/though ... but.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Although he has two big eyes, but he cannot see these types of bigger things even in daylight. Answer: yet Hinglish Explanation: “But” ko source ke rule ke hisaab se “yet” se replace kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “yet”. Do not use but after although/though. Quick Rule: Do not use but after although/though.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Although she was responsible for the accident, nobody blamed her . Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Sentence without “but” already correct hai; concessive meaning clear hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. Modern standard English normally uses although + clause, clause without yet/but. Quick Rule: Modern standard English normally uses although + clause, clause without yet/but."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=5", "t": "Supposing/If and Lest · condition & negative purpose", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Supposing if a number of ghosts meet us in the forest, will we be able to get rid of them easily? Answer: If Hinglish Explanation: “Supposing” aur “if” dono condition introduce karte hain; dono ko ek saath mat use karo. English Explanation: The correct answer is “If”. Use either supposing or if, not supposing if. Quick Rule: Use either supposing or if, not supposing if.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Supposing if you become a government officer in the next few months, what will you do next? Answer: If Hinglish Explanation: Double condition marker redundant hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “If”. Use one condition marker: supposing OR if. Quick Rule: Use one condition marker: supposing OR if.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Work hard, lest you would not fail . Answer: should fail Hinglish Explanation: “Lest” khud negative purpose deta hai (“aisa na ho ki”); isliye extra not nahi, aur exam pattern me should + V1. English Explanation: The correct answer is “should fail”. Lest + subject + should + V1; avoid not in this structure. Quick Rule: Lest + subject + should + V1; avoid not in this structure.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Run fast, lest you may not miss the train. Answer: should miss Hinglish Explanation: Lest already “so that ... not” ka sense deta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “should miss”. Use lest + should + base verb, without not. Quick Rule: Use lest + should + base verb, without not.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. A mother said to her children, “Walk carefully, lest you may fall down or hurt yourself.” Answer: should Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam construction me lest ke baad “should”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “should”. Lest commonly takes should + V1 in formal/competitive-exam grammar. Quick Rule: Lest commonly takes should + V1 in formal/competitive-exam grammar.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Make haste lest you should not miss the train. Answer: miss Hinglish Explanation: “Not” हटेगा, kyunki lest me negative sense already built-in hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “miss”. Lest + should + V1; do not add not. Quick Rule: Lest + should + V1; do not add not.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. You should not be so quick in giving advice, lest you will be perceived as interfering in others’ affairs. Answer: should be Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern: lest + should + V1/be. English Explanation: Source pattern: lest + should + V1/be. Rule: After lest, use should + base form in this exam pattern. Quick Rule: After lest, use should + base form in this exam pattern."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=6", "t": "If/Whether and Doubt/Doubtful · clause choice", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I am not sure that he will join the company. Answer: if / whether Hinglish Explanation: “Not sure” uncertainty dikhata hai; if/whether suitable hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “if / whether”. After not sure / not know / wonder, use if or whether for indirect yes-no questions. Quick Rule: After not sure / not know / wonder, use if or whether for indirect yes-no questions.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I don’t know that she has committed a crime. Answer: if / whether Hinglish Explanation: “Don’t know” ke baad yes/no uncertainty hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “if / whether”. Use if/whether after don’t know for an indirect yes-no clause. Quick Rule: Use if/whether after don’t know for an indirect yes-no clause.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I am not sure if she will join the company or not. Answer: whether Hinglish Explanation: “Or not” ke saath “whether” preferred/required in this rule. English Explanation: The correct answer is “whether”. Use whether ... or not, especially in formal exam grammar. Quick Rule: Use whether ... or not, especially in formal exam grammar.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I don’t know if she has committed a crime or not. Answer: whether Hinglish Explanation: Explicit “or not” pair ke saath whether. English Explanation: The correct answer is “whether”. Whether pairs naturally with or not. Quick Rule: Whether pairs naturally with or not.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I doubt if she will marry me. Answer: doubt if / whether Hinglish Explanation: Affirmative “doubt” me source if/whether use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “doubt if / whether”. Traditional exam rule: affirmative doubt/doubtful → if/whether. Quick Rule: Traditional exam rule: affirmative doubt/doubtful → if/whether.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I don’t doubt that she will marry me. Answer: don’t doubt that Hinglish Explanation: Negative “don’t doubt” certainty ke kareeb hai, isliye “that” clause. English Explanation: The correct answer is “don’t doubt that”. Traditional exam rule: negative/interrogative doubt → that. Quick Rule: Traditional exam rule: negative/interrogative doubt → that.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. It is doubtful if she will marry me. Answer: doubtful if / whether Hinglish Explanation: Affirmative doubtful uncertainty dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “doubtful if / whether”. Affirmative doubtful can take if/whether. Quick Rule: Affirmative doubtful can take if/whether.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Is it doubtful that she will marry me? Answer: Is it doubtful that Hinglish Explanation: Interrogative doubtful ke source-rule me “that”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Is it doubtful that”. Traditional exam convention: interrogative/negative doubtful → that. Quick Rule: Traditional exam convention: interrogative/negative doubtful → that.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Their director doubts that the new machinery is really necessary. Answer: if / whether Hinglish Explanation: Source treats affirmative “doubt” as taking if/whether. English Explanation: Source treats affirmative “doubt” as taking if/whether. Rule: Affirmative doubt → if/whether in this chapter’s exam convention. Quick Rule: Affirmative doubt → if/whether in this chapter’s exam convention.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Her father was doubtful that one of his colleagues was involved in the embezzlement. Answer: if / whether Hinglish Explanation: Affirmative “doubtful” uncertainty hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “if / whether”. Affirmative doubtful → if/whether in this source convention. Quick Rule: Affirmative doubtful → if/whether in this source convention.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Economists doubt if interest rates will fall in the near future or not. Answer: whether Hinglish Explanation: “Or not” ke saath “whether”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “whether”. Use whether ... or not. Quick Rule: Use whether ... or not.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Can you any longer be doubtful if you are mistaken? Answer: that Hinglish Explanation: Question form “doubtful” me source “that” prescribe karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “that”. Traditional exam convention: interrogative doubtful → that. Quick Rule: Traditional exam convention: interrogative doubtful → that.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. It began to appear equally doubtful if they should continue to slaughter one another or sign a peace treaty. Answer: whether Hinglish Explanation: Do alternatives (“continue ... or sign ...”) hain, isliye whether natural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “whether”. Use whether when explicit alternatives are presented. Quick Rule: Use whether when explicit alternatives are presented."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=7", "t": "Because/Since/As · do not add so/therefore/thus", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Because he came late, therefore he failed. Answer: remove “therefore” Hinglish Explanation: “Because” reason relation already establish karta hai; same sentence me therefore redundant/wrong in this exam pattern. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “therefore””. Do not use because/since/as together with so/therefore/thus for the same clause relation. Quick Rule: Do not use because/since/as together with so/therefore/thus for the same clause relation.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Because he has been bedridden for more than two months, therefore you must not force him to do much work now. Answer: remove “therefore” Hinglish Explanation: Because-clause ke baad direct main clause aata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “therefore””. Because ... , main clause — no therefore/so needed. Quick Rule: Because ... , main clause — no therefore/so needed.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. As everyone already knows each other, therefore there’s no need for introductions. Answer: remove “therefore” Hinglish Explanation: “As” reason introduce kar raha hai; therefore ko hatao. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “therefore””. Do not double-mark cause/result with as + therefore. Quick Rule: Do not double-mark cause/result with as + therefore.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Because he has been very good and supportive to all of us, so we must help him always. Answer: remove “so” Hinglish Explanation: Because ke saath same construction me so redundant hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “so””. Use either because-clause or so-result structure, not both together. Quick Rule: Use either because-clause or so-result structure, not both together.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Since everything can be done from home with computers and telephones, so there’s no need to dress up for work anymore. Answer: remove “so” Hinglish Explanation: “Since” yahan reason = because; “so” ko hatao. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “so””. Since (reason) + main clause; avoid adding so. Quick Rule: Since (reason) + main clause; avoid adding so.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Because breathing is something we do automatically, thus we rarely think about it. Answer: remove “thus” Hinglish Explanation: Because already cause relation introduce karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “remove “thus””. Avoid because + thus/therefore in one paired construction. Quick Rule: Avoid because + thus/therefore in one paired construction."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=8", "t": "Correlative pairs · parallel placement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Either the action was right or wrong. Answer: The action was either right or wrong. Hinglish Explanation: Either ko un dono parallel adjectives “right” aur “wrong” ke just before place karna best hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The action was either right or wrong.”. Place correlative conjunctions directly before parallel elements. Quick Rule: Place correlative conjunctions directly before parallel elements.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He neither visited Jaipur nor Udaipur. Answer: He visited neither Jaipur nor Udaipur. Hinglish Explanation: Neither/nor ko parallel objects “Jaipur/Udaipur” se align karo. English Explanation: The correct answer is “He visited neither Jaipur nor Udaipur.”. Neither ... nor should connect parallel elements. Quick Rule: Neither ... nor should connect parallel elements.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He is not only famous in India but also in abroad . Answer: He is famous not only in India but also abroad. Hinglish Explanation: Not only/but also dono parallel place phrases ko introduce kare. “Abroad” se pehle “in” nahi chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “He is famous not only in India but also abroad.”. Keep not only ... but also parallel. Quick Rule: Keep not only ... but also parallel.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He is both a writer as well as a teacher. Answer: and Hinglish Explanation: Fixed pair “both ... and” hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “and”. Use both ... and, not both ... as well as in this exam rule. Quick Rule: Use both ... and, not both ... as well as in this exam rule.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. We had not only helped them with money but also with new machinery and raw material . Answer: We had helped them not only with money but also with new machinery and raw material. Hinglish Explanation: Pair ko parallel “with money” / “with machinery...” se immediately pehle lagao. English Explanation: The correct answer is “We had helped them not only with money but also with new machinery and raw material.”. Place not only/but also before the parallel phrases they connect. Quick Rule: Place not only/but also before the parallel phrases they connect.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Not only did he criticise the party’s policies but also the leader’s personal matters. Answer: He criticised not only the party’s policies but also the leader’s personal matters. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan parallel objects hain, isliye not only ko first object ke just before rakho. English Explanation: The correct answer is “He criticised not only the party’s policies but also the leader’s personal matters.”. Correlative markers should introduce matching grammatical units. Quick Rule: Correlative markers should introduce matching grammatical units.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. In the fall, either Phillip will start classes at the community college or join the Navy. Answer: In the fall, Phillip will either start classes at the community college or join the Navy. Hinglish Explanation: Either ko two verb phrases “start...” and “join...” ke before align karo. English Explanation: The correct answer is “In the fall, Phillip will either start classes at the community college or join the Navy.”. Either ... or must join parallel alternatives. Quick Rule: Either ... or must join parallel alternatives.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Most of the popular tele-serials are not only illogical in their story line but also crude in their presentation . Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: Dono adjective phrases parallel hain: illogical... / crude.... English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. Not only ... but also can correctly join parallel adjective phrases. Quick Rule: Not only ... but also can correctly join parallel adjective phrases.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Money not only satisfies the basic physiological needs but also the higher psychological needs. Answer: Money satisfies not only the basic physiological needs but also the higher psychological needs. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan conjunction pair ko two noun objects se align karna chahiye. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Money satisfies not only the basic physiological needs but also the higher psychological needs.”. Put not only/but also immediately before the parallel objects. Quick Rule: Put not only/but also immediately before the parallel objects.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. The films were both enjoyable to watch and to discuss . Answer: The films were enjoyable both to watch and to discuss. Hinglish Explanation: “Both” ko matching infinitives “to watch / to discuss” se align karo. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The films were enjoyable both to watch and to discuss.”. Both ... and should connect parallel forms. Quick Rule: Both ... and should connect parallel forms.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He is not either good or wise . Answer: or Hinglish Explanation: Source ke pattern me “not either ... or ...” diya hai. (Natural English me “neither good nor wise” zyada idiomatic hai.) English Explanation: The correct answer is “or”. In not either ... or, pair or is used; neither ... nor is the common alternative. Quick Rule: In not either ... or, pair or is used; neither ... nor is the common alternative.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She was not either tall or short . Answer: or Hinglish Explanation: Same paired structure: not either ... or. English Explanation: Same paired structure: not either ... or. Rule: Use either ... or; or use neither ... nor. Quick Rule: Use either ... or; or use neither ... nor."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=9", "t": "Seldom/Never · fixed pairs and inversion", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Language policies for education are highly charged political issues and seldom or ever decided on educational grounds alone. Answer: seldom or never Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression source me “seldom or never”. English Explanation: Fixed expression source me “seldom or never”. Rule: Use seldom or never; use seldom if ever for “very rarely, if at all”. Quick Rule: Use seldom or never; use seldom if ever for “very rarely, if at all”.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Seldom if never there was any training or instructions in such tactics. Answer: Seldom, if ever, was there Hinglish Explanation: Phrase “seldom, if ever” hai; sentence-initial negative/restrictive adverb se inversion hoti hai: was there. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Seldom, if ever, was there”. Initial seldom triggers subject–auxiliary inversion; fixed phrase is seldom, if ever. Quick Rule: Initial seldom triggers subject–auxiliary inversion; fixed phrase is seldom, if ever.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. These goods are made for export and seldom if ever sold in the country. Answer: No error Hinglish Explanation: “Seldom if ever” = bahut hi kam, agar kabhi. Yahan auxiliary “are” pehle hi shared hai. English Explanation: No change is needed; the given form is acceptable. Seldom if ever is a standard idiomatic phrase. Quick Rule: Seldom if ever is a standard idiomatic phrase."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=10", "t": "Since · conjunction, preposition and adverb", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Rahul has had several different jobs since he left school . Answer: since = conjunction of time Hinglish Explanation: “Since” ke baad full clause “he left school” hai, so conjunction; starting point in time batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = conjunction of time”. Since + clause can mark the starting point of a continuing time period. Quick Rule: Since + clause can mark the starting point of a continuing time period.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. They haven’t seen each other since they graduated . Answer: since = conjunction of time Hinglish Explanation: “They graduated” clause hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = conjunction of time”. Since + subject + verb = conjunction. Quick Rule: Since + subject + verb = conjunction.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Since it’s raining , we should take an umbrella. Answer: since = because / reason Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “since” ka meaning “because” hai, time nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = because / reason”. Since can introduce a reason clause. Quick Rule: Since can introduce a reason clause.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Since there’s no more business , we can all go home. Answer: since = because / reason Hinglish Explanation: First clause main action ka reason deta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = because / reason”. Since may mean because. Quick Rule: Since may mean because.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I have been studying since morning . Answer: since = preposition Hinglish Explanation: “Morning” noun/time expression hai, clause nahi. Standard international English me “since this morning” aur clearer hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = preposition”. Since + noun/time point functions as a preposition. Quick Rule: Since + noun/time point functions as a preposition.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She hasn’t spoken to him since last year . Answer: since = preposition Hinglish Explanation: “Last year” noun phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = preposition”. Since + time noun phrase = preposition. Quick Rule: Since + time noun phrase = preposition.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. She left home in 1993 and hasn’t been seen since . Answer: since = adverb Hinglish Explanation: “Since” ke baad object/clause nahi; meaning “since then”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “since = adverb”. Since can stand alone as an adverb meaning from then until now. Quick Rule: Since can stand alone as an adverb meaning from then until now.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He started working for the company in 1995 and has been there ever since . Answer: ever since = adverbial phrase Hinglish Explanation: “Ever since” stand-alone = tab se ab tak continuously. English Explanation: The correct answer is “ever since = adverbial phrase”. Ever since can function adverbially without a following noun/clause. Quick Rule: Ever since can function adverbially without a following noun/clause."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=12.+Conjunctions&section=11", "t": "Unless/Until · condition vs time", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 12. Conjunctions", "x": ["Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. You can’t get a job unless you have experience. Answer: unless = except if / condition Hinglish Explanation: Job milne ki condition hai: experience na ho to job nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “unless = except if / condition”. Unless means except if and introduces a negative condition. Quick Rule: Unless means except if and introduces a negative condition.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Most wild animals won’t attack unless they are provoked. Answer: unless = condition Hinglish Explanation: Attack hone ki condition provocation hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “unless = condition”. Unless introduces condition, not time. Quick Rule: Unless introduces condition, not time.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. The audience listened carefully until he spoke. Answer: until = up to a point in time Hinglish Explanation: Listening ek time point “he spoke” tak chala. English Explanation: Listening ek time point “he spoke” tak chala. Rule: Until marks an endpoint in time. Quick Rule: Until marks an endpoint in time.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. No one is allowed to leave the classroom until the exam is over. Answer: until = time limit Hinglish Explanation: Leaving ka time-limit exam ke over hone tak hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “until = time limit”. Use until for time endpoint. Quick Rule: Use until for time endpoint.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. You can’t get a job unless and until you have experience. Answer: unless and until = emphatic condition/time Hinglish Explanation: Source emphatic pair use karta hai: condition + required point dono stress hote hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “unless and until = emphatic condition/time”. Unless and until may be used emphatically in Indian/competitive-exam English. Quick Rule: Unless and until may be used emphatically in Indian/competitive-exam English.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. No one is allowed to leave the classroom until and unless the exam is over. Answer: until and unless = emphatic restriction Hinglish Explanation: Source combined emphasis dikhata hai; ordinary English me simple “until” often enough hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “until and unless = emphatic restriction”. Combined until and unless is an emphatic exam-style construction. Quick Rule: Combined until and unless is an emphatic exam-style construction.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Until / Unless you are dedicated to your studies, you cannot expect success. Answer: Unless Hinglish Explanation: Meaning condition hai: dedication ke bina success expect nahi kar sakte. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Unless”. Use unless for condition; until is for time. Quick Rule: Use unless for condition; until is for time.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. We will wait for Tanu until / unless she arrives. Answer: until Hinglish Explanation: Waiting arrival ke time-point tak chalega. English Explanation: The correct answer is “until”. Use until for a time endpoint. Quick Rule: Use until for a time endpoint.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. He cannot withdraw all his money unless he does not give advance notice. Answer: gives Hinglish Explanation: “Unless” already negative condition hai; extra “not” meaning ulta kar deta. English Explanation: The correct answer is “gives”. Do not normally use an extra not in the unless-clause when the intended meaning is “if not”. Quick Rule: Do not normally use an extra not in the unless-clause when the intended meaning is “if not”.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. I lived with my parents until / unless I was 17. Answer: until Hinglish Explanation: Age 17 ek time endpoint hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “until”. Until = up to the time when. Quick Rule: Until = up to the time when.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. You can’t get admission in Delhi University unless / until you have scored good grades in the higher secondary examination. Answer: unless Hinglish Explanation: Grades admission ki condition hain, time point nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “unless”. Use unless for a necessary condition. Quick Rule: Use unless for a necessary condition.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Rahul can’t vote until / unless he turns 18. Answer: until Hinglish Explanation: 18 turn karna time/age threshold hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “until”. Use until for a time or age threshold. Quick Rule: Use until for a time or age threshold.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. Once Tanu starts playing badminton, she will continue until / unless she wins. Answer: until Hinglish Explanation: Action “wins” wale endpoint tak continue karega. English Explanation: Action “wins” wale endpoint tak continue karega. Rule: Continue until + event = continue up to that time/event. Quick Rule: Continue until + event = continue up to that time/event.","Choose the best answer for the highlighted part. No one can help you unless / until you tell us the problem. Answer: unless Hinglish Explanation: Help possible hone ki condition problem batana hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “unless”. Unless expresses the required condition. Quick Rule: Unless expresses the required condition."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=1", "t": "Basics · article position and specific/unspecific reference", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I saw ___ adorable puppy at the park. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Adorable” vowel sound /ə/ se start hota hai, isliye singular countable noun “puppy” se pehle “an”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Choose “a” or “an” according to the sound of the following word, not its spelling. Quick Rule: A/An ka choice spelling se nahi, next word ki sound se hota hai.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Naman sang ___ song. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Song” consonant sound /s/ se start hota hai, isliye “a song”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. This is ___ bag which contains the cash. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “which contains the cash” bag ko specific bana raha hai—kaunsa bag, ye clear hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the when the listener/reader can identify the specific noun. Quick Rule: Use the when the listener/reader can identify the specific noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ man in the black suit is my brother. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Black suit wala particular man identify ho raha hai, isliye definite article “the”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the for a definite or clearly identified person/thing. Quick Rule: Use the for a definite or clearly identified person/thing.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He is ___ singer. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Profession ke singular countable noun se pehle normally a/an lagta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an before a singular profession or role. Quick Rule: Use a/an before a singular profession or role.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He is ___ good singer. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Article adjective ke pehle aata hai, lekin noun “singer” ko determine karta hai: a + good + singer. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Article + adjective + singular countable noun. Quick Rule: Article + adjective + singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. It is ___ valuable skill. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Valuable” /v/ consonant sound se start hota hai, isliye “a valuable skill”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An depends on sound of the word immediately after the article. Quick Rule: A/An depends on sound of the word immediately after the article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He is ___ very good singer. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Adverb “very” aur adjective “good” beech me hone par bhi article noun phrase ke start me aata hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Article + adverb + adjective + singular countable noun. Quick Rule: Article + adverb + adjective + singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. This is ___ very valuable skill. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Very” consonant sound /v/ se start hota hai, so “a very valuable skill”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Choose a/an from the first pronounced sound after the article. Quick Rule: Choose a/an from the first pronounced sound after the article."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=2", "t": "A vs An · sound-based choice", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ book Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Book” /b/ consonant sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ uniform Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Uniform” ka sound /juː/ (“yoo”) se start hota hai, jo consonant sound hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ cat Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Cat” consonant sound /k/ se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ man Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Man” consonant sound /m/ se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ union Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Union” /juː/ (“yoo”) consonant sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ heir Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Heir” me h silent hai; pronunciation vowel sound /eə/ se start hoti hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ apple Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Apple” vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ umbrella Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Umbrella” vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ M.P. / M.L.A. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: Letter M ko “em” bola jata hai; vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ hour Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Hour” me h silent hai; pronunciation “our” jaisi vowel sound se start hoti hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a consonant sound and an before a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ adventurous traveller embarked on a journey to explore a mysterious island. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Adventurous” vowel sound se start hota hai, isliye “an adventurous traveller”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. An + vowel sound. Quick Rule: An + vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Canada has long been ___ U.S. ally. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “U.S.” ko “you-ess” bola jata hai; pehli sound /j/ consonant sound hai, isliye “a U.S. ally”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. With initialisms, choose the article according to the pronunciation of the first letter, not its written spelling. Quick Rule: Initialisms me letter ki pronunciation dekho, spelling nahi.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ S.P. has recently been appointed to combat crime in the city. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: S ko “ess” bola jata hai; vowel sound /e/ se start hoti hai, isliye “an S.P.” English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. The first spoken sound of an initialism determines whether to use “a” or “an”. Quick Rule: Initialism ka first spoken sound article decide karta hai."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=3", "t": "A/An · one, some/any, frequency and singular countables", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Pummy has ___ cat. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “a” ka sense “one” hai—ek cat. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An can mean one with a singular countable noun. Quick Rule: A/An can mean one with a singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ apple a day keeps the doctor away. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “An apple” = one apple; apple vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use an before a singular countable noun with vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use an before a singular countable noun with vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Mr Aggarwal was looking for you. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Naam se pehle “a” ka sense “some/a certain” ho sakta hai—koi Mr Aggarwal. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An can mean some or a certain unknown person. Quick Rule: A/An can mean some or a certain unknown person.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Miss Khiti wants to speak to you. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A Miss Khiti” ka sense hai koi person of that name, speaker ko fully identified nahi hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An may introduce an unknown person by name/title. Quick Rule: A/An may introduce an unknown person by name/title.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He did not give me ___ book. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Negative sentence me yahan “a book” ka sense “any single book” hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An can carry an “any one” sense with singular countables. Quick Rule: A/An can carry an “any one” sense with singular countables.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She does not like to read ___ novel. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A novel” yahan kisi bhi ek novel/class member ko represent karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an with a singular countable noun when speaking nonspecifically. Quick Rule: Use a/an with a singular countable noun when speaking nonspecifically.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I go there once ___ week. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Frequency expression “once a week” fixed pattern hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an in rate/frequency expressions: once a week, twice a month. Quick Rule: Use a/an in rate/frequency expressions: once a week, twice a month.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She earns two thousand rupees ___ month. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A month” = per month. English Explanation: “A month” = per month. Rule: A/An can mean “per” in frequency/rate expressions. Quick Rule: A/An can mean “per” in frequency/rate expressions.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ cat is a notorious animal. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Source ek representative singular member se class ko show karta hai: “A cat…”. Modern English me generic plural “Cats…” bhi common hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A singular countable noun can represent a class with a/an in formal examples. Quick Rule: A singular countable noun can represent a class with a/an in formal examples.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ man should be of principles. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Source me “a man” generic representative hai. Modern neutral wording often “a person” use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An may introduce a representative member of a class. Quick Rule: A/An may introduce a representative member of a class.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ student should be disciplined. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A student” kisi bhi representative student ko denote karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An can be used for a representative singular member. Quick Rule: A/An can be used for a representative singular member.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I need ___ cup of coffee. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Cup” singular countable noun hai aur /k/ consonant sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Singular countable noun normally needs a determiner such as a/an. Quick Rule: Singular countable noun normally needs a determiner such as a/an.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I am ___ astrologer. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: Profession “astrologer” vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use a/an with singular professions; choose by sound. Quick Rule: Use a/an with singular professions; choose by sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He bought ___ mansion. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Mansion” consonant sound se start hota hai aur first mention nonspecific hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an for first mention of an unspecified singular countable noun. Quick Rule: Use a/an for first mention of an unspecified singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She is ___ engineer. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Engineer” vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use an before a singular profession beginning with a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use an before a singular profession beginning with a vowel sound."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=4", "t": "First mention, repeated mention and counting expressions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Ayushi found ___ dog on the street. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Dog first time introduce ho raha hai, isliye indefinite article “a”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. First mention of an unspecified singular countable noun normally takes a/an. Quick Rule: First mention of an unspecified singular countable noun normally takes a/an.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Ayushi found a dog on the street. ___ dog seemed petrified. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Second sentence me wahi dog dobara refer ho raha hai, isliye “the”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. After first mention with a/an, use the when referring to the same noun again. Quick Rule: After first mention with a/an, use the when referring to the same noun again.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He bought ___ branded shirt. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Shirt first mention hai, specific identity pehle se known nahi. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. First mention → a/an for a singular countable noun. Quick Rule: First mention → a/an for a singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He bought a branded shirt. ___ shirt is very costly. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Same shirt second time mention ho rahi hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Repeated/specific reference → the. Quick Rule: Repeated/specific reference → the.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I have ___ dozen bananas. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Standard counting expression “a dozen” hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before fixed counting units such as a dozen, a hundred, a thousand, a million. Quick Rule: Use a before fixed counting units such as a dozen, a hundred, a thousand, a million.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He has ___ million dollars. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A million dollars” standard unit expression hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before million when it functions as an exact counting unit. Quick Rule: Use a before million when it functions as an exact counting unit.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. This smartphone costs ___ thousand dollars. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “A thousand” ek fixed numerical unit hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before hundred/thousand/million when meaning one such unit. Quick Rule: Use a before hundred/thousand/million when meaning one such unit.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. The railway station is only ___ hour away. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Hour” me h silent hai, so vowel sound se pehle “an”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use an before hour because the h is silent. Quick Rule: Use an before hour because the h is silent.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Sherlock is living in my neighbourhood. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Yahan proper name common-type comparison ban gaya: “a Sherlock” = Sherlock jaisa person. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. A/An can turn a proper name into a common-type label meaning “a person like…”. Quick Rule: A/An can turn a proper name into a common-type label meaning “a person like…”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. You are ___ Tendulkar. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Sense hai “Tendulkar jaisa cricketer/person”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an before a proper name when it means “a person like that famous person”. Quick Rule: Use a/an before a proper name when it means “a person like that famous person”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He proposed that we should go for ___ walk. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Fixed noun phrase “go for a walk”. English Explanation: Fixed noun phrase “go for a walk”. Rule: Some verbs/nouns form fixed expressions with a/an: have a rest, take a walk, pay a visit. Quick Rule: Some verbs/nouns form fixed expressions with a/an: have a rest, take a walk, pay a visit.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. You look tired. Why don’t you take ___ rest? Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression “take a rest”. English Explanation: Fixed expression “take a rest”. Rule: Use a in the fixed countable expression “a rest”. Quick Rule: Use a in the fixed countable expression “a rest”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I often go for ___ walk after dinner. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Go for a walk” standard phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a in the fixed expression “go for a walk”. Quick Rule: Use a in the fixed expression “go for a walk”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. My long-lasting friend paid me ___ visit last week. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Standard phrase “pay someone a visit”. English Explanation: Standard phrase “pay someone a visit”. Rule: Use a with the countable event noun in “pay a visit”. Quick Rule: Use a with the countable event noun in “pay a visit”."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=5", "t": "Omission of A/An · adjectives, uncountables and plurals", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She is ___ smart. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Smart” yahan adjective hai, noun nahi; adjective ke aage akela a/an nahi lagta. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. A/An cannot normally stand before a bare adjective without a singular countable noun. Quick Rule: A/An cannot normally stand before a bare adjective without a singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Rohan is ___ cunning. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Cunning” predicative adjective hai; “a cunning” incorrect hoga unless noun follows. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Do not use a/an before a lone predicative adjective. Quick Rule: Do not use a/an before a lone predicative adjective.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Gold is a precious metal. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Gold” material/uncountable noun general sense me hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. No a/an before an uncountable noun used generally. Quick Rule: No a/an before an uncountable noun used generally.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. We drink ___ water. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Water” uncountable noun general sense me hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use zero article with uncountable nouns in a general sense. Quick Rule: Use zero article with uncountable nouns in a general sense.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Children are playing. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Plural noun “children” general/indefinite sense me a/an nahi le sakta. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. A/An is only for singular countable nouns, not plural nouns. Quick Rule: A/An is only for singular countable nouns, not plural nouns.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Cows eat grass. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Generic plural “cows” ke saath zero article natural hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use zero article with generic plural nouns. Quick Rule: Use zero article with generic plural nouns."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=6", "t": "The · specific names, geographical names and unique reference", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Mount Everest is the world’s highest mountain above sea level. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Single mountain peak names normally article nahi lete: Mount Everest. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Do not normally use the before names of individual mountain peaks. Quick Rule: Do not normally use the before names of individual mountain peaks.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Nanda Devi is a famous Himalayan peak. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Individual peak name “Nanda Devi” se pehle “the” nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Individual mountain peak names usually take zero article. Quick Rule: Individual mountain peak names usually take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I am reading ___ Valmiki’s Ramayana. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Author ka possessive name already determiner ka kaam kar raha hai: “Valmiki’s Ramayana”. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Do not add the before a title already determined by a possessive author’s name. Quick Rule: Do not add the before a title already determined by a possessive author’s name.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. We discussed ___ Homer’s Iliad. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Possessive “Homer’s” ke saath extra “the” nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. A possessive determiner replaces the article. Quick Rule: A possessive determiner replaces the article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Pacific Ocean is the largest and deepest ocean on Earth. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Ocean names normally “the” lete hain. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the before oceans and seas. Quick Rule: Use the before oceans and seas.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Ganga is considered a holy river in India. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Traditional exam rule me river names se pehle “the” aata hai: the Ganga/Ganges. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the before river names in standard exam grammar. Quick Rule: Use the before river names in standard exam grammar."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=7", "t": "Languages, nationality words and group reference", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I have met ___ Indian in Australia. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Indian” yahan singular countable person hai aur vowel sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. When a nationality word is a singular countable noun for one person, use a/an. Quick Rule: When a nationality word is a singular countable noun for one person, use a/an.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Englishman was there in my college. Answer: an Hinglish Explanation: “Englishman” vowel sound /ɪ/ se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “an”. Use an before a singular nationality/person noun beginning with a vowel sound. Quick Rule: Use an before a singular nationality/person noun beginning with a vowel sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ English is spoken in England. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Language name “English” general sense me article nahi leta. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Language names normally take zero article. Quick Rule: Language names normally take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I know ___ Hindi, Urdu and Punjabi. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Language names list me zero article hota hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use no article before language names used by themselves. Quick Rule: Use no article before language names used by themselves.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Do you speak ___ English language? Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Jab “language” noun explicitly follows, “the English language” standard noun phrase hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the in the phrase “the + language name + language”. Quick Rule: Use the in the phrase “the + language name + language”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Do you speak ___ English? Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: English yahan language name akela hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Language name alone → zero article. Quick Rule: Language name alone → zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I know ___ German language. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Standard phrase “the German language”. English Explanation: Standard phrase “the German language”. Rule: Use the when the nationality adjective modifies the common noun “language”. Quick Rule: Use the when the nationality adjective modifies the common noun “language”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I know ___ German. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: German yahan language ka proper name hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Proper language names normally take zero article. Quick Rule: Proper language names normally take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Translate ___ Hindi into English. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Standard modern English me language names ke saath article nahi: “Translate Hindi into English.” Source ka marked “the Hindi/the English” exam-note standard usage se conflict karta hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use zero article with language names after translate into/from. Quick Rule: Use zero article with language names after translate into/from.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. This text was translated from ___ Sanskrit. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Sanskrit language name hai, isliye zero article. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Language names after from/into normally take zero article. Quick Rule: Language names after from/into normally take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. On my way to the UK, I met ___ Briton. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Briton” singular countable person noun hai aur /b/ sound se start hota hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an before one unspecified person described by nationality. Quick Rule: Use a/an before one unspecified person described by nationality.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I met a French woman who also speaks ___ Spanish. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Spanish language name hai, no article. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Language names used alone take zero article. Quick Rule: Language names used alone take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. English is the language of ___ English. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: “The English” collective expression English people ko denote karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Some nationality adjectives (the English, the French, the Dutch) take the when referring to the people as a group. Quick Rule: Some nationality adjectives (the English, the French, the Dutch) take the when referring to the people as a group.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. We Indians can learn and speak English fluently; ___ Indians after “we” is unnecessary. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Pronoun + nationality noun pattern “we Indians” hota hai, “we the Indians” tabhi jab special contrast/emphasis ho. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. After we/you, a nationality plural usually appears without an article in ordinary usage. Quick Rule: After we/you, a nationality plural usually appears without an article in ordinary usage."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=8", "t": "The · musical instruments, inventions, radio/TV", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He cannot play ___ guitar. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Traditional grammar/exam usage me “play the guitar”. English Explanation: Traditional grammar/exam usage me “play the guitar”. Rule: Use the before musical instruments when talking about the skill of playing them. Quick Rule: Use the before musical instruments when talking about the skill of playing them.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He wants to learn ___ tabla. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Instrument as skill: “learn/play the tabla”. English Explanation: Instrument as skill: “learn/play the tabla”. Rule: Use the with a musical instrument in the general skill sense. Quick Rule: Use the with a musical instrument in the general skill sense.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He has bought ___ piano. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Yahan ek physical countable piano kharida gaya hai, isliye “a piano”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. When an instrument means one physical item, use a/an as with any singular countable noun. Quick Rule: When an instrument means one physical item, use a/an as with any singular countable noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. My sister wants to learn ___ flute. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Learning the instrument as a skill → “the flute”. English Explanation: Learning the instrument as a skill → “the flute”. Rule: Use the with an instrument when referring to playing/learning it as an art or skill. Quick Rule: Use the with an instrument when referring to playing/learning it as an art or skill.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Ari bought ___ guitar for himself. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Ek physical guitar buy kiya, so indefinite article “a”. English Explanation: Ek physical guitar buy kiya, so indefinite article “a”. Rule: Use a/an when referring to one countable instrument as an object. Quick Rule: Use a/an when referring to one countable instrument as an object.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Who invented ___ telescope? Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Invention as a class/device is traditionally referred to with “the telescope”. English Explanation: Invention as a class/device is traditionally referred to with “the telescope”. Rule: Use the before names of inventions when referring to the invention as a type/class. Quick Rule: Use the before names of inventions when referring to the invention as a type/class.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He has bought ___ TV. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Ek physical television set = singular countable object. English Explanation: Ek physical television set = singular countable object. Rule: Use a/an for one physical device. Quick Rule: Use a/an for one physical device.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He has bought ___ radio. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Ek radio set countable object hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a/an for a singular countable device. Quick Rule: Use a/an for a singular countable device.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Listen to ___ radio. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Fixed media expression “listen to the radio”. English Explanation: Fixed media expression “listen to the radio”. Rule: Use the in the traditional media phrase “the radio”. Quick Rule: Use the in the traditional media phrase “the radio”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I heard the news on ___ radio. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Standard phrase “on the radio”. English Explanation: Standard phrase “on the radio”. Rule: Say on the radio, but on TV. Quick Rule: Say on the radio, but on TV.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. We watched the match on ___ TV. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression “on TV” me article nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use zero article in “on TV” and often “watch TV”. Quick Rule: Use zero article in “on TV” and often “watch TV”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. The children watch ___ TV after dinner. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Watch TV” fixed media expression hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Use zero article in the fixed expression “watch TV”. Quick Rule: Use zero article in the fixed expression “watch TV”."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=9", "t": "The · body parts, titles and apposition", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She hit me on ___ head. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Possessor already pronoun “me” se clear hai; English me body part ke saath often “the” use hota hai after hit/touch. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the with body parts in many prepositional expressions: hit someone on the head. Quick Rule: Use the with body parts in many prepositional expressions: hit someone on the head.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He looked her in ___ eye. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Standard pattern “look someone in the eye”. English Explanation: Standard pattern “look someone in the eye”. Rule: Use the in fixed body-part expressions such as in the eye, on the head. Quick Rule: Use the in fixed body-part expressions such as in the eye, on the head.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He was shot in ___ leg. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Standard idiom “shot in the leg”. English Explanation: Standard idiom “shot in the leg”. Rule: Use the with a body part in this passive/prepositional construction. Quick Rule: Use the with a body part in this passive/prepositional construction.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Cancer of ___ lung can be serious. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Source exam rule disease + body part pattern me “the lung”. In real medical English article varies with context, but this is the intended structure. English Explanation: Source exam rule disease + body part pattern me “the lung”. In real medical English article varies with context, but this is the intended structure. Rule: With certain disease + body-part phrases, the may identify the affected organ. Quick Rule: With certain disease + body-part phrases, the may identify the affected organ.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Inflammation of ___ throat can be painful. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Specific body part in this construction takes “the”. English Explanation: Specific body part in this construction takes “the”. Rule: Use the when a body part is treated as the identifiable affected part. Quick Rule: Use the when a body part is treated as the identifiable affected part.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. A cow has ___ mouth. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: Yahan body part ek singular countable noun hai: one mouth. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. When a body-part noun is a normal singular countable possession, use a/an. Quick Rule: When a body-part noun is a normal singular countable possession, use a/an.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. A cow has ___ nose. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: “Nose” consonant sound se start hota hai; one nose. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “a”. Use a before a singular countable body-part noun beginning with consonant sound. Quick Rule: Use a before a singular countable body-part noun beginning with consonant sound.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. A cow has ___ head. Answer: a Hinglish Explanation: One head = “a head”. English Explanation: One head = “a head”. Rule: Use a/an for one countable body part when it is not in a fixed the-expression. Quick Rule: Use a/an for one countable body part when it is not in a fixed the-expression.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. He travelled ___ foot. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Fixed expression “on foot” me article nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Some fixed prepositional phrases use zero article: on foot, at hand, in hand. Quick Rule: Some fixed prepositional phrases use zero article: on foot, at hand, in hand.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Help is ___ hand. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Fixed phrase “at hand” = near/available. Article nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Zero article in fixed phrase “at hand”. Quick Rule: Zero article in fixed phrase “at hand”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Keep your documents ___ hand. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Fixed phrase “in hand” me article nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Zero article in fixed phrase “in hand”. Quick Rule: Zero article in fixed phrase “in hand”.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Prime Minister wants to talk to you. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Ek specific official post ko refer karte waqt title se pehle “the”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the before a title/post when the office itself is meant and no personal name follows. Quick Rule: Use the before a title/post when the office itself is meant and no personal name follows.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Chief Minister comes here tomorrow. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Specified constitutional/official post → “the Chief Minister”. English Explanation: Specified constitutional/official post → “the Chief Minister”. Rule: Use the with a specific official title used as a common noun. Quick Rule: Use the with a specific official title used as a common noun.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. You should trust ___ director. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Context me identifiable director ki baat ho rahi hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the before a specific post-holder known from context. Quick Rule: Use the before a specific post-holder known from context.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Tagore, ___ poet, was famous. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Apposition me “the poet” Tagore ko identify/describe karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the before a common noun in identifying apposition: Tagore, the poet… Quick Rule: Use the before a common noun in identifying apposition: Tagore, the poet…","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Raghav, ___ dancer, is laborious. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Source pattern identifying apposition ka hai: “Raghav, the dancer”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the with an identifying common noun placed in apposition after a proper name. Quick Rule: Use the with an identifying common noun placed in apposition after a proper name."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=10", "t": "The · abstracted qualities and proper-name comparisons", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ patriot in him came alive. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Common noun “patriot” yahan abstract quality/side of a person ko represent kar raha hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. The can turn a common noun into a specific quality/aspect: the child in him, the patriot in him. Quick Rule: The can turn a common noun into a specific quality/aspect: the child in him, the patriot in him.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. When I found a baby, ___ father in me was aroused. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Source construction “the father in me” = paternal quality/aspect. Sentence stylistically unusual hai, but article pattern yahi hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the when a common noun denotes a specific inner role/quality in this construction. Quick Rule: Use the when a common noun denotes a specific inner role/quality in this construction.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ mother in his wife cried out. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “the mother” wife ki maternal side/quality ko denote karta hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. The can mark a specific abstracted role/quality in a person. Quick Rule: The can mark a specific abstracted role/quality in a person.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Mumbai is ___ London of India. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Proper name comparison me “the London of India” = London jaisa representative city. English Explanation: Proper name comparison me “the London of India” = London jaisa representative city. Rule: Use the before a proper noun used metaphorically/comparatively with an of-phrase. Quick Rule: Use the before a proper noun used metaphorically/comparatively with an of-phrase.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Kalidas is ___ Shakespeare of India. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Kalidas ko Shakespeare se representative comparison diya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the in “the + famous proper name + of …” comparison. Quick Rule: Use the in “the + famous proper name + of …” comparison.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Abdul Kalam is ___ Newton of India. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Metaphorical proper-name comparison → “the Newton of India”. English Explanation: Metaphorical proper-name comparison → “the Newton of India”. Rule: A proper name used as a unique comparison with of normally takes the. Quick Rule: A proper name used as a unique comparison with of normally takes the.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Shanghai is sometimes called ___ Paris of the East. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Established comparison phrase “the Paris of the East”. English Explanation: Established comparison phrase “the Paris of the East”. Rule: Use the before a proper name used as a comparative label with an of-phrase. Quick Rule: Use the before a proper name used as a comparative label with an of-phrase.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. These are ___ boons of science. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Source sentence specific set “boons of science” ko point out karta hai, isliye “the”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the when the plural noun is made definite by context or an identifying of-phrase. Quick Rule: Use the when the plural noun is made definite by context or an identifying of-phrase."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=13.+Articles&section=11", "t": "Zero article · general concepts and institutional use", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 13. Articles", "x": ["Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. ___ Man is mortal. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: “Man” yahan mankind/humanity ka old-fashioned generic sense hai, individual male nahi. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Abstract/general mass-like uses such as mankind, humanity, life may take zero article. Quick Rule: Abstract/general mass-like uses such as mankind, humanity, life may take zero article.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Sooner or later you will come to know the truth that ___ man is mortal. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Source correction ka point generic “man” se pehle “the” hatana hai. Modern inclusive English usually “humans are mortal” kahega. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Generic “man” meaning mankind takes zero article in this traditional expression. Quick Rule: Generic “man” meaning mankind takes zero article in this traditional expression.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I go to ___ school regularly. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: School apne primary institutional purpose—study—ke liye ja rahe ho, so zero article. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. In British/Indian exam English, school/college/church/bed/hospital/prison can take zero article when used for their primary institutional purpose. Quick Rule: In British/Indian exam English, school/college/church/bed/hospital/prison can take zero article when used for their primary institutional purpose.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I am going to ___ school to meet the principal. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Yahan school institution as study-purpose nahi, specific building/place visit ho raha hai. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the when referring to the specific building/place rather than its primary institutional purpose. Quick Rule: Use the when referring to the specific building/place rather than its primary institutional purpose.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. The driver was heavily injured in the accident, so he was rushed to ___ hospital. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: British/Indian exam usage me patient treatment ke institutional purpose se “to hospital” ho sakta hai. American English commonly says “to the hospital”. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. BrE institutional use: go/be taken to hospital; AmE usually uses the hospital. Quick Rule: BrE institutional use: go/be taken to hospital; AmE usually uses the hospital.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. The robbers were caught just as they were about to escape from ___ jail. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Prisoners/inmates ke institutional sense me BrE exam grammar zero article use karta hai. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Institutional use can take zero article: in jail/prison, at school, in hospital (BrE). Quick Rule: Institutional use can take zero article: in jail/prison, at school, in hospital (BrE).","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. The moment Karan was admitted to ___ hospital, the warden informed his parents. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Treatment purpose ke institutional BrE sense me “admitted to hospital”. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. BrE: admitted to hospital; AmE: admitted to the hospital is normal. Quick Rule: BrE: admitted to hospital; AmE: admitted to the hospital is normal.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. Several ministers visited ___ jail to see a number of prisoners. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: Ministers inmates nahi hain; woh specific jail building visit kar rahe hain. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Use the when the institution is visited as a particular place/building, not for its primary role as participant/inmate. Quick Rule: Use the when the institution is visited as a particular place/building, not for its primary role as participant/inmate.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. She never goes to ___ college to attend the lectures delivered by the head of the department. Answer: no article Hinglish Explanation: Student purpose—study/lectures—ke liye “go to college” zero article. English Explanation: No article is needed in this position. Primary institutional purpose → zero article in BrE/Indian exam grammar. Quick Rule: Primary institutional purpose → zero article in BrE/Indian exam grammar.","Choose the correct article for the highlighted blank. I am going to ___ mall to meet my friend. Answer: the Hinglish Explanation: “Mall” school/hospital jaisa institutional zero-article noun nahi; specific mall place ke liye “the mall”. English Explanation: The correct article choice is “the”. Ordinary specific places normally need a determiner such as the. Quick Rule: Ordinary specific places normally need a determiner such as the."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=1", "t": "Basic polarity", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. He is going to the party, ___ Answer: isn’t he? Hinglish Explanation: Positive statement ke saath tag negative hota hai. Main clause me “is” hai aur subject “he”, isliye “isn’t he?”. English Explanation: “isn’t he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Positive statement → negative tag; same auxiliary + pronoun. Quick Rule: Positive statement → negative tag; same auxiliary + pronoun.","Choose the correct question tag. He is not going to the party, ___ Answer: is he? Hinglish Explanation: Main statement negative hai (“is not”), isliye tag positive hoga: “is he?”. English Explanation: “is he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Negative statement → positive tag. Quick Rule: Negative statement → positive tag.","Choose the correct question tag. You are coming to the party, ___ Answer: aren’t you? Hinglish Explanation: Statement positive hai aur helping verb “are” hai; tag me “aren’t” + “you”. English Explanation: “aren’t you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Use the same auxiliary in the tag. Quick Rule: Use the same auxiliary in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. They have finished their homework, ___ Answer: haven’t they? Hinglish Explanation: “Have finished” present perfect hai, so auxiliary “have” tag me repeat hota hai. Positive statement ke liye negative tag. English Explanation: “haven’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Present perfect: have/has is repeated in the tag. Quick Rule: Present perfect: have/has is repeated in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. She isn’t feeling well, ___ Answer: is she? Hinglish Explanation: Statement already negative hai, so tag positive: “is she?”. English Explanation: “is she?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Negative main clause → positive tag. Quick Rule: Negative main clause → positive tag.","Choose the correct question tag. John didn’t eat lunch, ___ Answer: did he? Hinglish Explanation: “Didn’t” negative auxiliary hai; positive tag “did he?”. Noun “John” tag me pronoun “he” banega. English Explanation: “did he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Use a pronoun, not the noun, in the tag. Quick Rule: Use a pronoun, not the noun, in the tag."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=2", "t": "Special form · I am", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. I am writing a letter, ___ Answer: aren’t I? Hinglish Explanation: Standard question-tag exception: positive “I am …” ke saath “aren’t I?” use hota hai. English Explanation: “aren’t I?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. I am → aren’t I? Quick Rule: I am → aren’t I?","Choose the correct question tag. I am not writing a letter, ___ Answer: am I? Hinglish Explanation: Negative “I am not …” ke saath positive tag “am I?” hota hai. English Explanation: “am I?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. I am not → am I? Quick Rule: I am not → am I?"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=3", "t": "Do/Does/Did fallback", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. He writes a letter, ___ Answer: doesn’t he? Hinglish Explanation: Main clause me helping verb nahi hai; simple present singular “writes” ke liye tag auxiliary “does” use hota hai. Positive statement → “doesn’t he?”. English Explanation: “doesn’t he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. No auxiliary in simple present → do/does in the tag. Quick Rule: No auxiliary in simple present → do/does in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. They write a letter, ___ Answer: don’t they? Hinglish Explanation: Simple present plural subject “they” ke saath fallback auxiliary “do”; positive statement ke liye “don’t they?”. English Explanation: “don’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Simple present plural → do/don’t. Quick Rule: Simple present plural → do/don’t.","Choose the correct question tag. He wrote a letter, ___ Answer: didn’t he? Hinglish Explanation: Simple past “wrote” me separate helping verb nahi hai, so tag me “did”. Positive statement → “didn’t he?”. English Explanation: “didn’t he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Simple past without auxiliary → did/didn’t. Quick Rule: Simple past without auxiliary → did/didn’t."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=4", "t": "Pronoun replacement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. Rahul has completed the work, ___ Answer: hasn’t he? Hinglish Explanation: “Rahul” male singular noun hai, tag me “he” use hoga. “Has completed” ka auxiliary “has” repeat hoga. English Explanation: “hasn’t he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Nouns are replaced by suitable pronouns in tags. Quick Rule: Nouns are replaced by suitable pronouns in tags.","Choose the correct question tag. Rakhi was dancing in the room, ___ Answer: wasn’t she? Hinglish Explanation: “Rakhi” ko tag me “she” se replace karte hain; “was” positive hai, so “wasn’t she?”. English Explanation: “wasn’t she?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Keep the auxiliary; replace noun with pronoun. Quick Rule: Keep the auxiliary; replace noun with pronoun."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=5", "t": "Negative-sense words", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. They hardly talk to each other, ___ Answer: do they? Hinglish Explanation: “Hardly” negative sense deta hai, isliye sentence ko negative maana jata hai aur tag positive hota hai: “do they?”. English Explanation: “do they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Hardly/seldom/scarcely/rarely etc. make the clause negative in meaning. Quick Rule: Hardly/seldom/scarcely/rarely etc. make the clause negative in meaning.","Choose the correct question tag. She has seldom eaten chicken, ___ Answer: has she? Hinglish Explanation: “Seldom” negative sense deta hai; present perfect auxiliary “has” ka positive tag “has she?”. English Explanation: “has she?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Negative-sense word → positive tag. Quick Rule: Negative-sense word → positive tag.","Choose the correct question tag. A barking dog seldom bites, ___ Answer: does it? Hinglish Explanation: “Seldom” clause ko negative sense deta hai. Main verb simple present “bites” hai, so positive tag “does it?”. English Explanation: “does it?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Seldom + simple present → positive do/does tag. Quick Rule: Seldom + simple present → positive do/does tag.","Choose the correct question tag. I know little about you, ___ Answer: do I? Hinglish Explanation: “Little” = almost no, negative meaning. Simple present “know” ke saath positive fallback tag “do I?”. English Explanation: “do I?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Little (without a) is negative in meaning; a little is positive. Quick Rule: Little (without a) is negative in meaning; a little is positive."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=6", "t": "A little / A few", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. I have spent a little money, ___ Answer: haven’t I? Hinglish Explanation: “A little” positive meaning deta hai (some money). “Have spent” positive present perfect hai, so tag negative “haven’t I?”. English Explanation: “haven’t I?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. A little is positive in meaning, unlike little. Quick Rule: A little is positive in meaning, unlike little.","Choose the correct question tag. I know a few answers, ___ Answer: don’t I? Hinglish Explanation: “A few” means some, so clause positive hai. Simple present “know” ke liye negative fallback tag “don’t I?”. English Explanation: “don’t I?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. A few is positive in meaning, unlike few. Quick Rule: A few is positive in meaning, unlike few."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=7", "t": "Indefinite pronouns → they", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. Someone called me last night, ___ Answer: didn’t they? Hinglish Explanation: “Someone” singular-looking indefinite pronoun hai, lekin natural question tag me gender-neutral “they” use hota hai. Past “called” → “didn’t they?”. English Explanation: “didn’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Someone/somebody/anyone/everyone etc. normally take they in tags. Quick Rule: Someone/somebody/anyone/everyone etc. normally take they in tags.","Choose the correct question tag. Anyone can do this question, ___ Answer: can’t they? Hinglish Explanation: “Anyone” ke liye tag pronoun “they”; positive modal “can” ka negative tag “can’t they?”. English Explanation: “can’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Indefinite person pronoun → they; keep the modal. Quick Rule: Indefinite person pronoun → they; keep the modal.","Choose the correct question tag. Everybody loves me, ___ Answer: don’t they? Hinglish Explanation: “Everybody” ke liye tag pronoun “they”. Simple present “loves” ke liye “do” auxiliary; positive clause → “don’t they?”. English Explanation: “don’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Everybody/everyone → they in the tag. Quick Rule: Everybody/everyone → they in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. Nobody has seen God, ___ Answer: have they? Hinglish Explanation: “Nobody” itself negative hai, so tag positive. Indefinite-person pronoun ke liye “they”; present perfect plural pronoun ke saath “have they?”. English Explanation: “have they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Nobody/no one = negative meaning; tag is positive and usually uses they. Quick Rule: Nobody/no one = negative meaning; tag is positive and usually uses they."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=8", "t": "Imperatives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the pair of tags that can both be used with this positive imperative. For the positive imperative “Switch off the lights”, which tag pair is acceptable? Answer: will you? / won’t you? Hinglish Explanation: Positive imperatives ke saath “will you?” aur “won’t you?” dono request/command ke tone ke hisab se mil sakte hain. Source dono forms dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “will you? / won’t you?”. Positive imperative → will you? or won’t you? can both be used. Quick Rule: Positive imperative → will you? or won’t you? can both be used.","Choose the pair of tags that can both be used with this positive imperative. For the polite imperative “Please help me”, which tag pair is acceptable? Answer: will you? / won’t you? Hinglish Explanation: “Please help me” positive imperative/request hai; “will you?” aur “won’t you?” dono possible hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “will you? / won’t you?”. Positive/polite imperative → will you? / won’t you? Quick Rule: Positive/polite imperative → will you? / won’t you?","Choose the correct question tag. Don’t go there, ___ Answer: will you? Hinglish Explanation: Negative imperative ke saath standard tag “will you?” use hota hai. English Explanation: “will you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Negative imperative → will you? Quick Rule: Negative imperative → will you?","Choose the correct question tag. Don’t make a noise, ___ Answer: will you? Hinglish Explanation: “Don’t …” negative command hai, so question tag “will you?”. English Explanation: “will you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Negative imperative → will you? Quick Rule: Negative imperative → will you?"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=9", "t": "Let constructions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. Let’s party, ___ Answer: shall we? Hinglish Explanation: “Let’s” suggestion includes speaker + listener, so fixed tag “shall we?”. English Explanation: “Let’s” suggestion includes speaker + listener, so fixed tag “shall we?”. Rule: Let’s … → shall we? Quick Rule: Let’s … → shall we?","Choose the correct question tag. Let’s play Holi, ___ Answer: shall we? Hinglish Explanation: “Let’s” proposal/suggestion ka standard tag “shall we?”. English Explanation: “shall we?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Let’s … → shall we? Quick Rule: Let’s … → shall we?","Choose the correct question tag. Let me talk to her, ___ Answer: will you? Hinglish Explanation: “Let me …” request/permission construction hai; source aur exam grammar me tag “will you?”. English Explanation: “will you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Let me/him/her/them … → will you? Quick Rule: Let me/him/her/them … → will you?","Choose the correct question tag. Let her dance, ___ Answer: will you? Hinglish Explanation: “Let her …” ke saath tag “will you?” use hota hai. English Explanation: “will you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Let + object + verb → will you? Quick Rule: Let + object + verb → will you?"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=10", "t": "Special subjects", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. There is a pen on the table, ___ Answer: isn’t there? Hinglish Explanation: Existential “there” sentence me tag pronoun bhi “there” hi rehta hai. Positive “is” → negative “isn’t there?”. English Explanation: “isn’t there?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. There … → there in the tag. Quick Rule: There … → there in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. One cannot solve this question, ___ Answer: can one? Hinglish Explanation: Formal pronoun “one” subject hai, tag me bhi “one” repeat hota hai. Clause “cannot” negative hai, so tag positive “can one?”. English Explanation: “can one?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Pronoun one as subject → one in the tag. Quick Rule: Pronoun one as subject → one in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. This is your bag, ___ Answer: isn’t it? Hinglish Explanation: “This” demonstrative pronoun subject hai; question tag me “it” use hota hai. English Explanation: “isn’t it?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. This/that as pronoun subject → it in the tag. Quick Rule: This/that as pronoun subject → it in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. These are your books, ___ Answer: aren’t they? Hinglish Explanation: “These” plural demonstrative pronoun hai; tag me “they” use hota hai. English Explanation: “aren’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. These/those as pronoun subject → they in the tag. Quick Rule: These/those as pronoun subject → they in the tag."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=11", "t": "Demonstrative/determiner + noun", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. One boy can solve this question, ___ Answer: can’t he? Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “one” pronoun nahi, noun “boy” ko modify kar raha hai. Real subject “boy” hai, isliye tag pronoun “he”. English Explanation: “can’t he?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. When one/this/these modifies a noun, the tag follows the noun’s person/number/gender. Quick Rule: When one/this/these modifies a noun, the tag follows the noun’s person/number/gender.","Choose the correct question tag. This girl always loves me, ___ Answer: doesn’t she? Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “this” adjective/determiner hai; subject noun “girl” hai, so pronoun “she”. Simple present positive → “doesn’t she?”. English Explanation: “doesn’t she?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. This + noun → tag pronoun comes from the noun. Quick Rule: This + noun → tag pronoun comes from the noun.","Choose the correct question tag. These bags are yours, ___ Answer: aren’t they? Hinglish Explanation: Subject “these bags” plural hai, so tag pronoun “they”; positive “are” → “aren’t they?”. English Explanation: “aren’t they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Plural noun subject → they in the tag. Quick Rule: Plural noun subject → they in the tag."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=14.+Question+Tags&section=12", "t": "Group expressions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 14. Question Tags", "x": ["Choose the correct question tag. All of us can solve this question, ___ Answer: can’t we? Hinglish Explanation: “All of us” speaker ko include karta hai, so tag pronoun “we”. Positive “can” → “can’t we?”. English Explanation: “can’t we?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. All of us / some of us → we in the tag. Quick Rule: All of us / some of us → we in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. Either of you is guilty, ___ Answer: aren’t you? Hinglish Explanation: “Either of you” listener(s) ko refer karta hai, so exam-style tag “aren’t you?”. Main clause formal singular “is” hai, lekin tag pronoun “you” ke saath “are”. English Explanation: “aren’t you?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Either of you → you in the tag; agreement follows the tag pronoun. Quick Rule: Either of you → you in the tag; agreement follows the tag pronoun.","Choose the correct question tag. Neither of them is cool, ___ Answer: are they? Hinglish Explanation: “Neither” negative meaning deta hai, so tag positive. “Of them” ke reference ke liye natural plural pronoun “they” use hota hai. English Explanation: “are they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Neither of them → positive tag, usually they. Quick Rule: Neither of them → positive tag, usually they.","Choose the correct question tag. Some of us are intelligent, ___ Answer: aren’t we? Hinglish Explanation: “Some of us” speaker-inclusive group hai; tag pronoun “we”. Positive “are” → “aren’t we?”. English Explanation: “aren’t we?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. Some of us → we in the tag. Quick Rule: Some of us → we in the tag.","Choose the correct question tag. None of them has seen London, ___ Answer: have they? Hinglish Explanation: “None” negative meaning deta hai, so tag positive. “Them” ke reference ke liye “they”; tag auxiliary therefore “have they?”. English Explanation: “have they?” is the correct question tag for this sentence. None of them → positive tag, commonly they. Quick Rule: None of them → positive tag, commonly they."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=1", "t": "Voice basics", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Aditi writes a letter. Answer: A letter is written by Aditi. Hinglish Explanation: “writes” simple present hai. Object “a letter” ko subject bana kar “is + written (V3)” use hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “A letter is written by Aditi.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=2", "t": "Transitive vs intransitive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct analysis. He wants toys. Answer: Transitive verb — “toys” is the object. Hinglish Explanation: “wants” ke baad “what?” puchne par “toys” milta hai, isliye verb transitive hai aur passive ban sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Transitive verb — “toys” is the object.”. Transitive verbs take an object; passive voice normally needs an object. Quick Rule: Transitive verbs take an object; passive voice normally needs an object.","Choose the correct analysis. I adore her. Answer: Transitive verb — “her” is the object. Hinglish Explanation: “whom do I adore?” ka answer “her” hai, so object present hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Transitive verb — “her” is the object.”. Object present → transitive verb; passive possible. Quick Rule: Object present → transitive verb; passive possible.","Choose the correct analysis. I am going to Shimla. Answer: Intransitive use — no direct object. Hinglish Explanation: “to Shimla” prepositional phrase hai, direct object nahi. Isliye ordinary passive nahi banta. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Intransitive use — no direct object.”. Intransitive verbs do not take a direct object and normally have no passive. Quick Rule: Intransitive verbs do not take a direct object and normally have no passive.","Choose the correct analysis. He walks fast. Answer: Intransitive verb — “fast” is an adverb. Hinglish Explanation: “walks” ke baad object nahi hai; “fast” manner batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Intransitive verb — “fast” is an adverb.”. No direct object → no normal passive. Quick Rule: No direct object → no normal passive.","Choose the correct analysis. She has come to the office. Answer: Intransitive use — “to the office” is not an object. Hinglish Explanation: “come” yahan movement verb hai; “to the office” prepositional phrase hai, direct object nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Intransitive use — “to the office” is not an object.”. Come is normally intransitive, so this sentence has no normal passive. Quick Rule: Come is normally intransitive, so this sentence has no normal passive."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=3", "t": "Linking verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct analysis. The juice tastes delicious. Answer: Linking verb; “delicious” is a subject complement. Hinglish Explanation: “tastes” yahan action nahi kar raha; subject “juice” ki quality “delicious” batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Linking verb; “delicious” is a subject complement.”. Linking verbs connect the subject with a complement; they are not normally passivized. Quick Rule: Linking verbs connect the subject with a complement; they are not normally passivized.","Choose the correct analysis. He is handsome. Answer: “handsome” is a subject complement; no passive transformation. Hinglish Explanation: “is” linking/copular verb hai aur “handsome” subject ki quality batata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is ““handsome” is a subject complement; no passive transformation.”. Copular/linking structures do not form normal passives. Quick Rule: Copular/linking structures do not form normal passives.","Choose the correct analysis. She looks stunning. Answer: Linking verb; “stunning” is a subject complement. Hinglish Explanation: “looks” yahan appearance batata hai, deliberate action nahi. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Linking verb; “stunning” is a subject complement.”. Look/seem/appear/taste/smell etc. can be linking verbs and then do not form ordinary passive. Quick Rule: Look/seem/appear/taste/smell etc. can be linking verbs and then do not form ordinary passive."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=4", "t": "Object vs complement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct analysis. He became a musician. Answer: “a musician” is a subject complement. Hinglish Explanation: “became” linking verb hai; “a musician” subject “he” ko rename karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is ““a musician” is a subject complement.”. After a linking verb, a noun/adjective that describes or renames the subject is a subject complement. Quick Rule: After a linking verb, a noun/adjective that describes or renames the subject is a subject complement.","Choose the correct analysis. He married a musician. Answer: “a musician” functions as the object of “married”. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan “married” action verb hai aur “whom did he marry?” = “a musician”. English Explanation: The correct answer is ““a musician” functions as the object of “married”.”. An object receives/completes the action of a transitive verb. Quick Rule: An object receives/completes the action of a transitive verb."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=5", "t": "Tense-based passive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: People love cricket. Answer: Cricket is loved by people. Hinglish Explanation: Simple present “love” → “is loved”. English Explanation: Simple present “love” → “is loved”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Aditi is writing a letter. Answer: A letter is being written by Aditi. Hinglish Explanation: Present continuous me passive pattern “is/am/are + being + V3” hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “A letter is being written by Aditi.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He has bought the vegetables. Answer: The vegetables have been bought by him. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect me “has/have + been + V3” use hota hai; plural “vegetables” ke saath “have”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “The vegetables have been bought by him.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: You have been playing football since morning. Answer: Football has been being played by you since morning. Hinglish Explanation: Perfect continuous ka mechanical passive “has/have been being + V3” hota hai. Ye grammatically possible hai lekin real English me bahut awkward/rare hai; usually sentence recast kiya jata hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Football has been being played by you since morning.”. Perfect continuous passive exists mechanically as has/have been being + V3, but is very rare in natural English. Quick Rule: Perfect continuous passive exists mechanically as has/have been being + V3, but is very rare in natural English.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She stole my heart. Answer: My heart was stolen by her. Hinglish Explanation: Simple past “stole” → “was stolen”. English Explanation: Simple past “stole” → “was stolen”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The family was cooking a meal. Answer: A meal was being cooked by the family. Hinglish Explanation: Past continuous passive = was/were + being + V3. English Explanation: Past continuous passive = was/were + being + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He has composed a song. Answer: A song has been composed by him. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect passive = has/have + been + V3. English Explanation: Present perfect passive = has/have + been + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Swiggy will deliver the groceries. Answer: The groceries will be delivered by Swiggy. Hinglish Explanation: Future simple passive = will + be + V3. English Explanation: Future simple passive = will + be + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She will have studied the course. Answer: The course will have been studied by her. Hinglish Explanation: Future perfect passive = will have been + V3. English Explanation: Future perfect passive = will have been + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=6", "t": "Modals in passive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: They must complete the task. Answer: The task must be completed by them. Hinglish Explanation: Modal “must” ke baad passive me “be + V3”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “The task must be completed by them.”. Modal + be + V3. Quick Rule: Modal + be + V3."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=7", "t": "Yes/No questions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Does he cook a meal? Answer: Is a meal cooked by him? Hinglish Explanation: Simple present yes/no question me “do/does” passive me “is/am/are + V3” ban jata hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Is a meal cooked by him?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Do you watch a movie? Answer: Is a movie watched by you? Hinglish Explanation: Object singular hai, so “is … watched”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Is a movie watched by you?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Do you watch movies? Answer: Are movies watched by you? Hinglish Explanation: Object plural “movies” passive subject ban kar “are” leta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Are movies watched by you?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Am I catching the thief? Answer: Is the thief being caught by me? Hinglish Explanation: Present continuous question → is/am/are + object + being + V3. English Explanation: Present continuous question → is/am/are + object + being + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Was he beating me? Answer: Was I being beaten by him? Hinglish Explanation: Past continuous passive question = was/were + new subject + being + V3. English Explanation: Past continuous passive question = was/were + new subject + being + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Was she drinking juice? Answer: Was juice being drunk by her? Hinglish Explanation: “drink” ka V3 “drunk” hai; past continuous passive me “was being drunk”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Was juice being drunk by her?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Has she taught a class? Answer: Has a class been taught by her? Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect question me “has/have + subject + been + V3”. English Explanation: Present perfect question me “has/have + subject + been + V3”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Has she joined an NGO? Answer: Has an NGO been joined by her? Hinglish Explanation: “join” transitive hai; present perfect passive “has been joined”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Has an NGO been joined by her?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Have they collected the money? Answer: Has the money been collected by them? Hinglish Explanation: New subject “money” singular/uncountable hai, so auxiliary “has”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Has the money been collected by them?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Did she beat you? Answer: Were you beaten by her? Hinglish Explanation: Simple past “did + beat” passive question me “were + you + beaten”. English Explanation: Simple past “did + beat” passive question me “were + you + beaten”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Did he trouble you? Answer: Were you troubled by him? Hinglish Explanation: Simple past passive question = was/were + subject + V3. English Explanation: Simple past passive question = was/were + subject + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Did they abuse you? Answer: Were you abused by them? Hinglish Explanation: “you” passive subject hai; “were abused”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Were you abused by them?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=8", "t": "Modal questions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Can he manage the office? Answer: Can the office be managed by him? Hinglish Explanation: Modal question me modal same rehta hai: can + subject + be + V3. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Can the office be managed by him?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Should she eat pineapples? Answer: Should the pineapples be eaten by her? Hinglish Explanation: Should + object + be + V3. English Explanation: Should + object + be + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: May I get an estimate? Answer: May an estimate be obtained by me? Hinglish Explanation: Mechanical source form “be got” mil sakta hai, lekin standard natural English me “be obtained” zyada clear hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “May an estimate be obtained by me?”. With a modal: modal + subject + be + V3. Prefer natural lexical choices where needed. Quick Rule: With a modal: modal + subject + be + V3. Prefer natural lexical choices where needed."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=9", "t": "WH questions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Why have you borrowed the money? Answer: Why has the money been borrowed by you? Hinglish Explanation: WH-word “Why” front me rehta hai; baaki yes/no passive structure follow karta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Why has the money been borrowed by you?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: When will you read my book? Answer: When will my book be read by you? Hinglish Explanation: “When” unchanged; will + new subject + be + V3. English Explanation: “When” unchanged; will + new subject + be + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: When do you water the plant? Answer: When is the plant watered by you? Hinglish Explanation: Simple present do-question passive me “is + plant + watered”. English Explanation: Simple present do-question passive me “is + plant + watered”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Why are you breaking the chair? Answer: Why is the chair being broken by you? Hinglish Explanation: Present continuous passive = is/am/are + being + V3. English Explanation: Present continuous passive = is/am/are + being + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=10", "t": "Who → by whom", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who discovered gravity? Answer: By whom was gravity discovered? Hinglish Explanation: Active “who” agent hai; formal passive me “by whom” front me aata hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “By whom was gravity discovered?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who has discovered gravity? Answer: By whom has gravity been discovered? Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect passive: by whom + has + subject + been + V3. English Explanation: Present perfect passive: by whom + has + subject + been + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who cut this blooming tree? Answer: By whom was this blooming tree cut? Hinglish Explanation: Simple past passive: by whom + was/were + subject + V3. English Explanation: Simple past passive: by whom + was/were + subject + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who asked that question? Answer: By whom was that question asked? Hinglish Explanation: “asked” V3 bhi “asked” hai; formal agent question “by whom”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “By whom was that question asked?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who can decode this puzzle? Answer: By whom can this puzzle be decoded? Hinglish Explanation: Modal passive question: by whom + can + subject + be + V3. English Explanation: Modal passive question: by whom + can + subject + be + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Who will feed him? Answer: By whom will he be fed? Hinglish Explanation: Object “him” subject ban kar “he”; feed → fed. English Explanation: Object “him” subject ban kar “he”; feed → fed. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=11", "t": "Whom questions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Whom did he beat? Answer: Who was beaten by him? Hinglish Explanation: Active object “whom” passive me subject ban jata hai, isliye “who”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Who was beaten by him?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Whom has he beaten? Answer: Who has been beaten by him? Hinglish Explanation: Passive subject form “who”; present perfect = has been beaten. English Explanation: Passive subject form “who”; present perfect = has been beaten. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Whom did she love? Answer: Who was loved by her? Hinglish Explanation: Object pronoun question passive me subject “who” ban jata hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Who was loved by her?”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Whom has he tricked? Answer: Who has been tricked by him? Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect passive: who + has been + V3. English Explanation: Present perfect passive: who + has been + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=12", "t": "Imperatives", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the valid passive forms of the command. Shut the door. Answer: Let the door be shut. / You are ordered to shut the door. Hinglish Explanation: Object wala imperative do common passive patterns le sakta hai: “Let + object + be + V3” ya command sense me “You are ordered to + V1”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Let the door be shut. / You are ordered to shut the door.”. Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered/advised/requested to + V1. Quick Rule: Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered/advised/requested to + V1.","Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Work hard. Answer: You are ordered to work hard. Hinglish Explanation: Yahan direct object nahi hai, isliye “Let + object + be + V3” possible nahi. Command ko “You are ordered to…” se render karte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “You are ordered to work hard.”. Imperative without object → You are ordered/advised/requested to + V1. Quick Rule: Imperative without object → You are ordered/advised/requested to + V1.","Choose the valid passive forms of the command. Switch off the lights. Answer: Let the lights be switched off. / You are ordered to switch off the lights. Hinglish Explanation: “the lights” object hai, so both passive imperative patterns possible. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Let the lights be switched off. / You are ordered to switch off the lights.”. Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered to + V1. Quick Rule: Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered to + V1.","Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Do not go there. Answer: You are ordered not to go there. Hinglish Explanation: Negative imperative me “not” infinitive se pehle aata hai: ordered not to go. English Explanation: The correct answer is “You are ordered not to go there.”. Negative command → You are ordered not to + V1. Quick Rule: Negative command → You are ordered not to + V1.","Choose the valid passive forms. Bring a cup of coffee. Answer: Let a cup of coffee be brought. / You are ordered to bring a cup of coffee. Hinglish Explanation: Object present hai, so “Let + object + be + V3” and order form both possible. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Let a cup of coffee be brought. / You are ordered to bring a cup of coffee.”. Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered to + V1. Quick Rule: Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3; or You are ordered to + V1.","Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Do not go outside. Answer: You are ordered not to go outside. Hinglish Explanation: Negative order me “ordered not to + V1”. English Explanation: Negative order me “ordered not to + V1”. Rule: Negative imperative → ordered not to + V1. Quick Rule: Negative imperative → ordered not to + V1.","Choose the valid passive command. Complete the assignment. Answer: Let the assignment be completed. Hinglish Explanation: Object imperative ka direct passive “Let + object + be + V3”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “Let the assignment be completed.”. Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3. Quick Rule: Imperative with object → Let + object + be + V3."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=13", "t": "Advice", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the valid passive renderings. Help the needy woman. Answer: The needy woman should be helped. / You are advised to help the needy woman. Hinglish Explanation: Advice context me direct object ko subject bana kar “should be + V3” ya “You are advised to…” dono milte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The needy woman should be helped. / You are advised to help the needy woman.”. Advice → object + should be + V3; or You are advised to + V1. Quick Rule: Advice → object + should be + V3; or You are advised to + V1.","Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Get up early. Answer: You are advised to get up early. Hinglish Explanation: No direct object; advice ko “You are advised to…” se express karna natural hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “You are advised to get up early.”. Imperative without object used as advice → You are advised to + V1. Quick Rule: Imperative without object used as advice → You are advised to + V1.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Always obey the elders. Answer: The elders should always be obeyed. Hinglish Explanation: Advice sense me object “the elders” subject ban kar “should be obeyed” leta hai; adverb “always” retain hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “The elders should always be obeyed.”. Advice with object → object + should + adverb + be + V3. Quick Rule: Advice with object → object + should + adverb + be + V3.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Know your limitations. Answer: Your limitations should be known. Hinglish Explanation: Object “your limitations” subject banta hai; advice sense me “should be known”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Your limitations should be known.”. Advice with object → object + should be + V3. Quick Rule: Advice with object → object + should be + V3."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=14", "t": "Requests", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Please give me some money. Answer: You are requested to give me some money. Hinglish Explanation: “Please/kindly” request ko passive-style me “You are requested to + V1” se likhte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “You are requested to give me some money.”. Request → You are requested to + V1. Quick Rule: Request → You are requested to + V1.","Choose the correct passive-style rendering. Please hold the bag. Answer: You are requested to hold the bag. Hinglish Explanation: Polite imperative/request → “You are requested to + V1”. English Explanation: Polite imperative/request → “You are requested to + V1”. Rule: Please/kindly request → You are requested to + V1. Quick Rule: Please/kindly request → You are requested to + V1."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=15", "t": "Infinitive passive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He is to write a book. Answer: A book is to be written by him. Hinglish Explanation: “is to + V1” passive me “is to be + V3”. English Explanation: “is to + V1” passive me “is to be + V3”. Rule: be + to + V1 → be + to be + V3. Quick Rule: be + to + V1 → be + to be + V3.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He has to write a book. Answer: A book has to be written by him. Hinglish Explanation: “has to + V1” → “has to be + V3”. English Explanation: “has to + V1” → “has to be + V3”. Rule: has/have to + V1 → has/have to be + V3. Quick Rule: has/have to + V1 → has/have to be + V3.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I am to buy some groceries. Answer: Some groceries are to be bought by me. Hinglish Explanation: New subject plural hai, so “are to be bought”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Some groceries are to be bought by me.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I have to buy some groceries. Answer: Some groceries have to be bought by me. Hinglish Explanation: Plural subject “groceries” → “have to be bought”. English Explanation: Plural subject “groceries” → “have to be bought”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He has to visit all his relatives. Answer: All his relatives have to be visited by him. Hinglish Explanation: Object plural passive subject ban kar “have to be visited” leta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “All his relatives have to be visited by him.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The government is to take some strict actions against the conspirators. Answer: Some strict actions are to be taken against the conspirators by the government. Hinglish Explanation: “is to take” → plural passive “are to be taken”; other phrase retained. English Explanation: “is to take” → plural passive “are to be taken”; other phrase retained. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: You have to buy the smartwatch. Answer: The smartwatch has to be bought by you. Hinglish Explanation: New subject singular “smartwatch” → “has to be bought”. English Explanation: New subject singular “smartwatch” → “has to be bought”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The judge is to announce the verdict. Answer: The verdict is to be announced by the judge. Hinglish Explanation: “is to announce” → “is to be announced”. English Explanation: “is to announce” → “is to be announced”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: There are some groceries to buy. Answer: There are some groceries to be bought. Hinglish Explanation: Noun ke baad active infinitive “to buy” ko passive meaning me “to be bought” banaya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “There are some groceries to be bought.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: There is no question to ask. Answer: There is no question to be asked. Hinglish Explanation: Infinitive passive = to be + V3. English Explanation: Infinitive passive = to be + V3. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: There are chores to do. Answer: There are chores to be done. Hinglish Explanation: “to do” → “to be done”. English Explanation: “to do” → “to be done”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=16", "t": "Omitting vague agents", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The police are to catch the thieves. Answer: The thieves are to be caught. Hinglish Explanation: Passive me obvious/generic agent “by the police” often omit kiya ja sakta hai; focus action/result par hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “The thieves are to be caught.”. In passive voice, an obvious or unimportant agent may be omitted. Quick Rule: In passive voice, an obvious or unimportant agent may be omitted.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: People organise parties here. Answer: Parties are organised here. Hinglish Explanation: Generic “people” ko passive me normally omit karte hain. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Parties are organised here.”. Generic agents such as people/someone/they are often omitted in passive voice. Quick Rule: Generic agents such as people/someone/they are often omitted in passive voice.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Somebody has picked my pocket. Answer: My pocket has been picked. Hinglish Explanation: Unknown “somebody” ko “by somebody” repeat karna unnecessary hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “My pocket has been picked.”. Unknown/unimportant agent can be omitted. Quick Rule: Unknown/unimportant agent can be omitted."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=17", "t": "Double objects", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the pair in which both passive forms are correct. My brother gifted me a bicycle. Answer: I was gifted a bicycle by my brother. / A bicycle was gifted to me by my brother. Hinglish Explanation: Sentence me indirect object “me” aur direct object “a bicycle” dono hain; kisi ko bhi passive subject banaya ja sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I was gifted a bicycle by my brother. / A bicycle was gifted to me by my brother.”. Double-object verbs often allow two passives: person as subject, or thing as subject + to/for + person. Quick Rule: Double-object verbs often allow two passives: person as subject, or thing as subject + to/for + person.","Choose the pair in which both passive forms are correct. She has given me only bitter memories. Answer: I have been given only bitter memories by her. / Only bitter memories have been given to me by her. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect me dono objects me se kisi ko passive subject bana sakte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “I have been given only bitter memories by her. / Only bitter memories have been given to me by her.”. Double-object passive keeps tense and may use to/for before the remaining indirect object. Quick Rule: Double-object passive keeps tense and may use to/for before the remaining indirect object.","Choose the pair in which both passive forms are correct. The shopkeeper gave him a discount. Answer: He was given a discount by the shopkeeper. / A discount was given to him by the shopkeeper. Hinglish Explanation: Simple past “gave” → “was given”; person or thing can become passive subject. English Explanation: Simple past “gave” → “was given”; person or thing can become passive subject. Rule: Double-object verbs can yield two passive constructions. Quick Rule: Double-object verbs can yield two passive constructions."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=18", "t": "Fixed prepositions in passive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The magician amazes the children. Answer: The children are amazed at the magician. Hinglish Explanation: Certain participial adjectives/passive forms fixed preposition lete hain: amazed at. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “The children are amazed at the magician.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I annoy her. Answer: She is annoyed with me. Hinglish Explanation: “annoyed” ke saath person/cause context me source pattern “with” use karta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “She is annoyed with me.”. Some passive/participial expressions take fixed prepositions: annoyed with, surprised at, engulfed in, etc. Quick Rule: Some passive/participial expressions take fixed prepositions: annoyed with, surprised at, engulfed in, etc.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She surprised me. Answer: I was surprised at her. Hinglish Explanation: “surprised at” source ka fixed-preposition pattern hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was surprised at her.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Their nonchalance vexed me. Answer: I was vexed at their nonchalance. Hinglish Explanation: “vexed at” fixed collocation pattern. English Explanation: “vexed at” fixed collocation pattern. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The flood engulfed many houses. Answer: Many houses were engulfed in the flood. Hinglish Explanation: “engulfed in” fixed preposition retain hota hai; agent-style “by” yahan meaning ko natural nahi rakhta. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Many houses were engulfed in the flood.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=19", "t": "Let constructions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the passive-style form. Let me sing. Answer: I may be allowed to sing. Hinglish Explanation: “Let + person + V1” permission sense me passive-style “person + may be allowed to + V1”. English Explanation: “Let + person + V1” permission sense me passive-style “person + may be allowed to + V1”. Rule: Let + person + V1 (permission) → person + may be allowed to + V1. Quick Rule: Let + person + V1 (permission) → person + may be allowed to + V1.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Let her cook the meal. Answer: Let the meal be cooked by her. Hinglish Explanation: Let + object1 + V1 + object2 me action-object ko subject bana kar “Let + object2 + be + V3 + by object1”. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Let the meal be cooked by her.”. Let + person + V1 + thing → Let + thing + be + V3 + by + person. Quick Rule: Let + person + V1 + thing → Let + thing + be + V3 + by + person.","Choose the accepted passive-style rendering. Let him play. Answer: He may be allowed to play. / It is suggested that he should play. Hinglish Explanation: Context ke hisab se “let” permission ya suggestion ho sakta hai; source dono renderings dikhata hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “He may be allowed to play. / It is suggested that he should play.”. Let + person + V1 can be rendered with may be allowed to, or suggestion structure when that is the intended meaning. Quick Rule: Let + person + V1 can be rendered with may be allowed to, or suggestion structure when that is the intended meaning.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Let her bring a glass of water. Answer: Let a glass of water be brought by her. Hinglish Explanation: Action-object “a glass of water” passive subject banta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Let a glass of water be brought by her.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the accepted passive-style renderings. Let us dance. Answer: We may be allowed to dance. / It is suggested that we should dance. Hinglish Explanation: “Let us” permission/suggestion context ke hisab se dono ways me render ho sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “We may be allowed to dance. / It is suggested that we should dance.”. Let us + V1 → We may be allowed to + V1 / It is suggested that we should + V1. Quick Rule: Let us + V1 → We may be allowed to + V1 / It is suggested that we should + V1.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: Let him buy toys. Answer: Let toys be bought by him. Hinglish Explanation: Thing-object “toys” subject ban kar “be bought”. English Explanation: Thing-object “toys” subject ban kar “be bought”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=20", "t": "Causative / perception verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He saw me sleep. Answer: I was seen to sleep by him. Hinglish Explanation: See/watch/hear/notice/make + object + bare infinitive active me hota hai; passive me “to + V1” usually appear hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was seen to sleep by him.”. Perception/causative bare infinitive → passive usually uses to + V1 (except let). Quick Rule: Perception/causative bare infinitive → passive usually uses to + V1 (except let).","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She made him laugh. Answer: He was made to laugh by her. Hinglish Explanation: “make + object + V1” → passive “object + be + made + to + V1”. English Explanation: “make + object + V1” → passive “object + be + made + to + V1”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I have noticed her steal some money. Answer: She has been noticed to steal some money by me. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect passive me “has been noticed”; bare infinitive “steal” → “to steal”. English Explanation: Present perfect passive me “has been noticed”; bare infinitive “steal” → “to steal”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She saw me eat momos. Answer: I was seen to eat momos by her. Hinglish Explanation: “saw + object + V1” → “was seen to + V1”. English Explanation: “saw + object + V1” → “was seen to + V1”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: We noticed him loaf around. Answer: He was noticed to loaf around by us. Hinglish Explanation: “noticed + object + bare infinitive” → passive “was noticed to + V1”. English Explanation: “noticed + object + bare infinitive” → passive “was noticed to + V1”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I saw him ride a bicycle. Answer: He was seen to ride a bicycle by me. Hinglish Explanation: Perception verb “saw” → passive “was seen to ride”. English Explanation: Perception verb “saw” → passive “was seen to ride”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=21", "t": "Let exception", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: They let me play. Answer: I was let play by them. Hinglish Explanation: “let” exception hai: passive me bare infinitive hi rehta hai; “to play” nahi. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was let play by them.”. With let, the bare infinitive stays bare in this exam-style passive pattern. Quick Rule: With let, the bare infinitive stays bare in this exam-style passive pattern.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She let me speak. Answer: I was let speak by her. Hinglish Explanation: “let” ke saath source pattern me “to” add nahi hota. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was let speak by her.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He let me work. Answer: I was let work by him. Hinglish Explanation: Let exception: no “to” before work in the source pattern. English Explanation: Let exception: no “to” before work in the source pattern. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=22", "t": "Object complement", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: We made him a star. Answer: He was made a star by us. Hinglish Explanation: Object “him” passive subject banta hai; complement “a star” retain hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “He was made a star by us.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: They elected him captain. Answer: He was elected captain by them. Hinglish Explanation: Object complement “captain” passive me bhi subject ko describe karta rehta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “He was elected captain by them.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The committee appointed him chairman. Answer: He was appointed chairman by the committee. Hinglish Explanation: “chairman” complement hai, isliye “by” se pehle retain hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “He was appointed chairman by the committee.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=23", "t": "Phrasal verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The dogs fell upon me. Answer: I was fallen upon by the dogs. Hinglish Explanation: Phrasal/prepositional verb ka particle “upon” passive me verb ke saath retain hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was fallen upon by the dogs.”. In passive voice, keep the particle/preposition of a phrasal/prepositional verb. Quick Rule: In passive voice, keep the particle/preposition of a phrasal/prepositional verb.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: I will bear out your testimony. Answer: Your testimony will be borne out by me. Hinglish Explanation: “bear out” ka particle “out” retain hota hai; bear ka V3 “borne” yahan use hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “Your testimony will be borne out by me.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He can bring forth his case. Answer: His case can be brought forth by him. Hinglish Explanation: Modal + be + V3; particle “forth” retain. English Explanation: Modal + be + V3; particle “forth” retain. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: The company is laying off employees. Answer: Employees are being laid off by the company. Hinglish Explanation: Present continuous passive; phrasal particle “off” retain. English Explanation: Present continuous passive; phrasal particle “off” retain. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: He was looking for his wallet. Answer: His wallet was being looked for by him. Hinglish Explanation: Past continuous passive; “for” ko drop nahi karte. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “His wallet was being looked for by him.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=24", "t": "Verb + object + to-infinitive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: She advised me to prepare for UPSC. Answer: I was advised to prepare for UPSC by her. Hinglish Explanation: Advise/request/order/warn type verbs me object subject banta hai aur “to + V1” retained rehta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was advised to prepare for UPSC by her.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: My mother warned me not to play outside. Answer: I was warned not to play outside by my mother. Hinglish Explanation: Negative infinitive “not to play” passive me unchanged retain hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “I was warned not to play outside by my mother.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: My father ordered me to bring him a glass of water. Answer: I was ordered to bring him a glass of water by my father. Hinglish Explanation: Object “me” → “I”; ordered + to-infinitive retained. English Explanation: Object “me” → “I”; ordered + to-infinitive retained. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: My brother advised me to learn time management. Answer: I was advised to learn time management by my brother. Hinglish Explanation: “advised me to learn” → “I was advised to learn”. English Explanation: “advised me to learn” → “I was advised to learn”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=25", "t": "Sense verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the source-style passive equivalent. The samosa tastes spicy. Answer: The samosa is spicy when tasted. Hinglish Explanation: Taste/feel jaise linking sense verbs ordinary action-passive nahi banate; source quality ko “is + adjective + when + V3” me recast karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The samosa is spicy when tasted.”. Sense-linking sentence can be recast as subject + be + adjective + when + V3. Quick Rule: Sense-linking sentence can be recast as subject + be + adjective + when + V3.","Choose the source-style passive equivalent. The velvet feels smooth. Answer: The velvet is smooth when felt. Hinglish Explanation: “feels smooth” ko source “is smooth when felt” me recast karta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “The velvet is smooth when felt.”. Sense-linking sentence can be recast as subject + be + adjective + when + V3. Quick Rule: Sense-linking sentence can be recast as subject + be + adjective + when + V3."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=26", "t": "Reporting verbs", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the standard passive renderings. People believe that the government is judicious. Answer: It is believed that the government is judicious. / The government is believed to be judicious. Hinglish Explanation: Believe/think/know/report type clauses ke do common passive patterns hain: impersonal “It is believed that…” aur personal “subject is believed to…”. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is believed that the government is judicious. / The government is believed to be judicious.”. Reporting verb + that-clause → It + be + V3 + that-clause OR clause-subject + be + V3 + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: Reporting verb + that-clause → It + be + V3 + that-clause OR clause-subject + be + V3 + to-infinitive.","Choose the standard passive renderings. We believe that Sachin Tendulkar is the best. Answer: It is believed that Sachin Tendulkar is the best. / Sachin Tendulkar is believed to be the best. Hinglish Explanation: Reporting-clause ke impersonal aur personal passive dono valid hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is believed that Sachin Tendulkar is the best. / Sachin Tendulkar is believed to be the best.”. Reporting verbs allow impersonal and personal passive patterns. Quick Rule: Reporting verbs allow impersonal and personal passive patterns.","Choose the standard passive renderings. They think that the children are mischievous. Answer: It is thought that the children are mischievous. / The children are thought to be mischievous. Hinglish Explanation: “think” → “thought”; plural subject “children” + are thought to be. English Explanation: “think” → “thought”; plural subject “children” + are thought to be. Rule: Reporting verb + that-clause → impersonal It-passive or personal subject + be + V3 + to-infinitive. Quick Rule: Reporting verb + that-clause → impersonal It-passive or personal subject + be + V3 + to-infinitive."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=27", "t": "It is time + infinitive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: It is time to feed the kids now. Answer: It is time for the kids to be fed. Hinglish Explanation: Infinitive object “the kids” ko passive infinitive subject-like phrase me “for the kids to be fed” banaya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “It is time for the kids to be fed.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: It is time to reward him. Answer: It is time for him to be rewarded. Hinglish Explanation: “to reward him” → “for him to be rewarded”. English Explanation: “to reward him” → “for him to be rewarded”. Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: It is time to learn the guitar. Answer: It is time for the guitar to be learned. Hinglish Explanation: Ye source ka mechanical voice transformation hai; everyday English me “learn to play the guitar” zyada natural hota hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “It is time for the guitar to be learned.”. Source pattern: It is time to + V1 + object → It is time for + object + to be + V3. Quick Rule: Source pattern: It is time to + V1 + object → It is time for + object + to be + V3.","Choose the correct passive transformation. Change into passive voice: It is time to leave the company. Answer: It is time for the company to be left. Hinglish Explanation: Mechanical passive source pattern. Natural context me meaning ko preserve karne ke liye sentence recast bhi kiya ja sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct passive form is “It is time for the company to be left.”. Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense. Quick Rule: Passive voice: object becomes subject; use the correct form of be + V3; keep the original tense."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=15.+Voice&section=28", "t": "Duty / supposed to", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 15. Voice", "x": ["Choose the passive-style equivalent. It is your duty to meet the obligation now. Answer: You are supposed to meet the obligation now. Hinglish Explanation: “It is your duty to…” ko person-subject construction “You are supposed to…” me express kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “You are supposed to meet the obligation now.”. It is + possessive + duty + to V1 → person + be + supposed to + V1. Quick Rule: It is + possessive + duty + to V1 → person + be + supposed to + V1.","Choose the passive-style equivalent. It is our duty to give back to society. Answer: We are supposed to give back to society. Hinglish Explanation: Possessive “our” ka corresponding subject “we” hota hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “We are supposed to give back to society.”. It is our duty to… → We are supposed to… Quick Rule: It is our duty to… → We are supposed to…"]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=1", "t": "Direct vs Indirect basics", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Rahul says to me, “I want to be a singer.” Answer: Rahul tells me that he wants to be a singer. Hinglish Explanation: Reporting verb present (“says”) hai, isliye tense backshift nahi hota. “says to me” → “tells me”; “I” Rahul ke liye “he” hota hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Rahul tells me that he wants to be a singer.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Tanu said to me, “I am in love with you.” Answer: Tanu told me that she was in love with me. Hinglish Explanation: Past reporting verb “said” ke saath present “am” → past “was”. “I” = Tanu → she; “you” = me → me. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Tanu told me that she was in love with me.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=2", "t": "Sentence type identification", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Identify the sentence type. Sachin is playing cricket. Answer: Assertive (affirmative) Hinglish Explanation: Statement information de raha hai; question/order/exclamation nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive (affirmative)”. Before converting narration, first identify the sentence type of the reported speech. Quick Rule: Narration conversion se pehle reported speech ka sentence type identify karna zaroori hai.","Identify the sentence type. She is going to school. Answer: Assertive (affirmative) Hinglish Explanation: Simple positive statement hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive (affirmative)”. Before converting narration, first identify the sentence type of the reported speech. Quick Rule: Narration conversion se pehle reported speech ka sentence type identify karna zaroori hai.","Identify the sentence type. Sachin is not playing cricket. Answer: Assertive (negative) Hinglish Explanation: “not” ke bawajood ye statement hi hai, isliye negative assertive. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive (negative)”. Before converting narration, first identify the sentence type of the reported speech. Quick Rule: Narration conversion se pehle reported speech ka sentence type identify karna zaroori hai.","Identify the sentence type. She is not going to school. Answer: Assertive (negative) Hinglish Explanation: Negative statement hai, interrogative nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Assertive (negative)”. Before converting narration, first identify the sentence type of the reported speech. Quick Rule: Narration conversion se pehle reported speech ka sentence type identify karna zaroori hai.","Identify the sentence type. Are you hungry? Answer: Interrogative — Yes/No question Hinglish Explanation: Helping verb se start ho raha hai aur yes/no answer expect karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative — Yes/No question”. Yes/No questions are interrogative sentences. Quick Rule: Yes/No questions are interrogative sentences.","Identify the sentence type. What do you want to eat? Answer: Interrogative — WH-question Hinglish Explanation: “What” WH-word se question start ho raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Interrogative — WH-question”. WH-word + auxiliary + subject pattern → interrogative. Quick Rule: WH-word + auxiliary + subject pattern → interrogative.","Identify the sentence type. Stay quiet. Answer: Imperative — order/instruction Hinglish Explanation: Base verb “Stay” se sentence start hota hai; subject “you” understood hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Imperative — order/instruction”. Imperative sentences express an order, advice, request, or permission; the subject “you” is usually understood rather than stated. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences express order, advice, request or permission; subject “you” usually hidden hota hai.","Identify the sentence type. Let me go. Answer: Imperative — let construction Hinglish Explanation: “Let” se permission/request type imperative banta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Imperative — let construction”. Imperative sentences express an order, advice, request, or permission; the subject “you” is usually understood rather than stated. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences express order, advice, request or permission; subject “you” usually hidden hota hai.","Identify the sentence type. Do not go outside. Answer: Imperative — negative command Hinglish Explanation: “Do not + V1” negative imperative ka standard pattern hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Imperative — negative command”. Imperative sentences express an order, advice, request, or permission; the subject “you” is usually understood rather than stated. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences express order, advice, request or permission; subject “you” usually hidden hota hai.","Identify the sentence type. Please, don’t make a noise. Answer: Imperative — request Hinglish Explanation: “Please” request tone ko show karta hai; structure imperative hi hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Imperative — request”. Imperative sentences express an order, advice, request, or permission; the subject “you” is usually understood rather than stated. Quick Rule: Imperative sentences express order, advice, request or permission; subject “you” usually hidden hota hai.","Identify the sentence type. What a movie! Answer: Exclamatory Hinglish Explanation: “What a …!” strong feeling/reaction express karta hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory”. What/How + exclamation mark often signals exclamatory sentence. Quick Rule: What/How + exclamation mark often signals exclamatory sentence.","Identify the sentence type. Alas! My team lost the match. Answer: Exclamatory — sorrow Hinglish Explanation: “Alas!” sorrow/grief ka interjection hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Exclamatory — sorrow”. Interjections such as Alas!/Hurrah!/Wow! indicate exclamatory force. Quick Rule: Interjections such as Alas!/Hurrah!/Wow! indicate exclamatory force.","Identify the sentence type. May God bless you! Answer: Optative — blessing Hinglish Explanation: “May + subject + V1” blessing/wish express kar raha hai. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Optative — blessing”. Optative sentences express wish, blessing or curse; “May + subject + V1” is common. Quick Rule: Optative sentences express wish, blessing or curse; “May + subject + V1” is common.","Identify the sentence type. May you do well in life! Answer: Optative — wish/blessing Hinglish Explanation: Ye wish/blessing hai, factual statement nahi. English Explanation: The highlighted word or phrase functions as “Optative — wish/blessing”. May + subject + V1 commonly forms an optative sentence. Quick Rule: May + subject + V1 commonly forms an optative sentence."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=3", "t": "Reporting verb & tense", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. She says to me, “I like mangoes.” Answer: She tells me that she likes mangoes. Hinglish Explanation: Reporting verb present hai, so “like” ka tense same rehta hai. “I” → she, aur third-person singular ke saath “likes”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She tells me that she likes mangoes.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “I am a teacher.” Answer: She told me that she was a teacher. Hinglish Explanation: Past reporting verb ke saath present “am” normally backshift hokar “was” hota hai; “said to” → “told”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She told me that she was a teacher.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said, “I can decode this riddle.” Answer: He said that he could decode that riddle. Hinglish Explanation: can → could; this → that; I → he. Object absent so “said that”. English Explanation: can → could; this → that; I → he. Object absent so “said that”. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=4", "t": "Pronoun change", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “I have completed my task.” Answer: She told me that she had completed her task. Hinglish Explanation: First person reported speech reporting-verb ke subject ke according badalta hai: I → she, my → her. Present perfect → past perfect. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She told me that she had completed her task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to me, “I have completed my task.” Answer: He told me that he had completed his task. Hinglish Explanation: I/my speaker “he” ko refer karte hain, so he/his. “have completed” → “had completed”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He told me that he had completed his task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to her, “You have completed your task.” Answer: He told her that she had completed her task. Hinglish Explanation: Second person reporting verb ke object ke according badalta hai: you/your → she/her. Present perfect → past perfect. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He told her that she had completed her task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to them, “You have completed your task.” Answer: He told them that they had completed their task. Hinglish Explanation: Object “them” plural hai, so you → they, your → their. “have completed” → “had completed”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He told them that they had completed their task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “You have completed your task.” Answer: She said that I had completed my task. Hinglish Explanation: Source example hidden addressee “I/me” assume karta hai; isliye you → I, your → my. Reporting verb me object nahi hai, so “said that”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She said that I had completed my task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to me, “He has completed his task.” Answer: He told me that he had completed his task. Hinglish Explanation: Third-person pronouns “he/his” normally unchanged rehte hain; tense present perfect → past perfect. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He told me that he had completed his task.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “Avi, I am impressed with you.” Answer: She told Avi that she was impressed with him. Hinglish Explanation: Vocative “Avi” addressee/object ban jata hai; I → she, you → him; am → was. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She told Avi that she was impressed with him.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=5", "t": "Assertive narration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to her, “I do not want to play now.” Answer: He told her that he did not want to play then. Hinglish Explanation: Assertive sentence me “said to” → “told”; simple present → simple past; now → then; I → he. English Explanation: Assertive sentence me “said to” → “told”; simple present → simple past; now → then; I → he. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “Kids are sleeping now.” Answer: She said that kids were sleeping then. Hinglish Explanation: Present continuous → past continuous; now → then. Reporting verb ke object na hone par “said that”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She said that kids were sleeping then.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. I said to my brother, “I have bought a bicycle for you.” Answer: I told my brother that I had bought a bicycle for him. Hinglish Explanation: I reporting subject ke saath same rehta hai; you → him (my brother); present perfect → past perfect. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “I told my brother that I had bought a bicycle for him.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to him, “I have been looking for you for an hour.” Answer: She told him that she had been looking for him for an hour. Hinglish Explanation: Present perfect continuous → past perfect continuous; I → she; you → him. English Explanation: Present perfect continuous → past perfect continuous; I → she; you → him. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=6", "t": "Time & place words", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Piyush said, “He completed his Ph.D. a year ago.” Answer: Piyush said that he had completed his Ph.D. a year before. Hinglish Explanation: Simple past → past perfect; ago → before. Object absent hai, so “said that”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Piyush said that he had completed his Ph.D. a year before.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to Rohan, “My friend was looking for you last week.” Answer: She told Rohan that her friend had been looking for him the previous week. Hinglish Explanation: Past continuous → past perfect continuous; my → her; you → him; last week → the previous week. English Explanation: Past continuous → past perfect continuous; my → her; you → him; last week → the previous week. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “I shall visit you next week.” Answer: She told me that she would visit me the following week. Hinglish Explanation: shall/will future reporting past me normally would; I → she; you → me; next week → following week. English Explanation: shall/will future reporting past me normally would; I → she; you → me; next week → following week. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=7", "t": "Universal truth / no change", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. He said, “The sun rises in the east.” Answer: He said that the sun rises in the east. Hinglish Explanation: Universal truth hone ke kaaran tense backshift nahi hota; “rises” present me hi rehta hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He said that the sun rises in the east.”. Universal truths generally keep their original tense in reported speech. Quick Rule: Universal truths generally keep their original tense in reported speech.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “Humans are mortal.” Answer: She said that humans are mortal. Hinglish Explanation: General/universal truth hai, isliye “are” ko “were” me backshift karna necessary nahi. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She said that humans are mortal.”. Universal/general truths normally retain present tense in indirect speech. Quick Rule: Universal/general truths normally retain present tense in indirect speech.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said, “If I were a politician, I would eliminate corruption.” Answer: He said that if he were a politician, he would eliminate corruption. Hinglish Explanation: Hypothetical/unreal condition already remote form me hai; were/would ko source rule ke according change nahi kiya jata. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He said that if he were a politician, he would eliminate corruption.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “You must pass the examination.” Answer: She told me that I must pass the examination. Hinglish Explanation: Source/exam rule me “must” ko unchanged rakha gaya hai. Context-dependent modern reporting me obligation ke liye “had to” bhi possible ho sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She told me that I must pass the examination.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=8", "t": "Greetings / wishes", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Karan said to me, “Good morning, I am happy to see you.” Answer: Karan wished me good morning and said that he was happy to see me. Hinglish Explanation: Greeting ko “wished … good morning” me report kiya jata hai; baaki assertive clause normal backshift/pronoun change follow karta hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Karan wished me good morning and said that he was happy to see me.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=9", "t": "Interrogative narration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to me, “Have you completed these tasks?” Answer: He asked me if I had completed those tasks. Hinglish Explanation: Yes/No question me said to → asked; connector if/whether; question word order → statement order; have completed → had completed; these → those. English Explanation: Yes/No question me said to → asked; connector if/whether; question word order → statement order; have completed → had completed; these → those. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “Is he going to school?” Answer: She asked if he was going to school. Hinglish Explanation: Yes/No question me “if/whether” use hota hai; inversion khatam karke subject + verb order aata hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She asked if he was going to school.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. They said to him, “Do you teach in this school?” Answer: They asked him if he taught in that school. Hinglish Explanation: Do-question → if/whether + assertive order; teach → taught; this → that; you → he. English Explanation: Do-question → if/whether + assertive order; teach → taught; this → that; you → he. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to me, “Why have you broken this chair?” Answer: He asked me why I had broken that chair. Hinglish Explanation: WH-word “why” retained rehta hai; no if/whether. Present perfect → past perfect; this → that. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He asked me why I had broken that chair.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Salman asked, “Where do you sleep?” Answer: Salman asked where I slept. Hinglish Explanation: WH-question me WH-word retained; auxiliary “do” disappears; indirect speech me statement order. Source hidden addressee ko “I” assume karta hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Salman asked where I slept.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “Where will they go?” Answer: She asked me where they would go. Hinglish Explanation: WH-word retained; will → would; question order se assertive order “they would go”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She asked me where they would go.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He says, “How do we approach the problem?” Answer: He asks how they approach the problem. Hinglish Explanation: Reporting verb present hai, so tense same. “How” retained; question inversion hata kar statement order. Source context me “we” → “they”. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He asks how they approach the problem.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He says, “When do we play?” Answer: He asks when they play. Hinglish Explanation: Present reporting verb → no backshift; “when” retained; indirect question statement order me hota hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He asks when they play.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=10", "t": "Tag questions", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Simmi said to me, “You are going to visit Paris, aren’t you?” Answer: Simmi asked me if I was going to visit Paris. Hinglish Explanation: Tag question ko indirect me normal yes/no question ki tarah report kar sakte hain: asked + if/whether + statement order. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Simmi asked me if I was going to visit Paris.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “You didn’t beat my brother, did you?” Answer: She asked me if I had not beaten her brother. Hinglish Explanation: Negative main clause ka sense preserve kiya gaya; simple past → past perfect; my → her. Modern reporting me neutral “whether I had beaten…” context ke hisaab se possible hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She asked me if I had not beaten her brother.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=11", "t": "Yes/No response reporting", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Rohan said to me, “Did you approach her?” I said, “Yes.” Answer: Rohan asked me if I had approached her. I replied in the affirmative. Hinglish Explanation: Question part → asked + if + past perfect. “Yes” ko formal indirect speech me “replied/answered in the affirmative” kaha jata hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Rohan asked me if I had approached her. I replied in the affirmative.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=12", "t": "Imperative narration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Teacher said to me, “Complete your homework.” Answer: Teacher ordered me to complete my homework. Hinglish Explanation: Command me reporting verb sense ke hisaab se “ordered”; imperative V1 → to + V1; your → my. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Teacher ordered me to complete my homework.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Radha said, “Begin the meeting.” Answer: Radha ordered me to begin the meeting. Hinglish Explanation: Source hidden addressee “me” assume karta hai; command → ordered + object + to + V1. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Radha ordered me to begin the meeting.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Avi said to his wife, “Please do not take too much time in store.” Answer: Avi requested his wife not to take too much time in the store. Hinglish Explanation: “Please” request signal hai. Negative imperative → requested + object + not to + V1. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Avi requested his wife not to take too much time in the store.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to me, “Do not spend mindlessly.” Answer: He advised me not to spend mindlessly. Hinglish Explanation: Context advice jaisa hai, so “advised”; negative command/advice → not to + V1. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He advised me not to spend mindlessly.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to the boy, “Do not follow me.” Answer: She ordered the boy not to follow her. Hinglish Explanation: Negative command → ordered + object + not to + V1. Source alternate recast: “She prevented the boy from following her.” English Explanation: Negative command → ordered + object + not to + V1. Source alternate recast: “She prevented the boy from following her.” Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=13", "t": "Polite request", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. Aman says to me, “Sir, show me your ID card, please?” Answer: Aman respectfully requests me to show him my ID card. Hinglish Explanation: “please” aur respectful address request ko signal karte hain; present “says” ke context me “requests”; me → him, your → my. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Aman respectfully requests me to show him my ID card.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=14", "t": "Let’s / suggestion", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to his friends, “Let’s go to watch a movie.” Answer: He suggested to his friends that they should go to watch a movie. Hinglish Explanation: “Let’s” suggestion/proposal hota hai. Exam pattern: suggested/proposed + that + we/they + should + V1. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He suggested to his friends that they should go to watch a movie.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. He said to his girlfriend, “Let’s go on a museum date.” Answer: He proposed to his girlfriend that they should go on a museum date. Hinglish Explanation: “Let’s” → proposed/suggested + that + they should + V1; speaker + girlfriend = they. English Explanation: “Let’s” → proposed/suggested + that + they should + V1; speaker + girlfriend = they. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=15", "t": "Optative narration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. My mother said to me, “May God bless you.” Answer: My mother blessed me and prayed that God might bless me. Hinglish Explanation: Blessing ko “blessed/wished/prayed” sense me report kiya jata hai; may → might. Source form “blessed me that God might bless me” exam notes me milta hai; natural English me “blessed me and prayed…” clearer hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “My mother blessed me and prayed that God might bless me.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said to me, “May you burn in hell!” Answer: She cursed me, saying that I might burn in hell. Hinglish Explanation: Hostile optative curse hai, so reporting verb “cursed”; may → might. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She cursed me, saying that I might burn in hell.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Naman said, “You keep succeeding.” Answer: Naman wished that I might keep succeeding. Hinglish Explanation: Source context isko wish ke sense me leta hai; wish → wished that … might. Hidden addressee “I” assume kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Naman wished that I might keep succeeding.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “May you have a healthy life!” Answer: She wished that I might have a healthy life. Hinglish Explanation: Optative wish/blessing me “wished/blessed” use hota hai; may → might. Object context hidden ho to “wished that…” natural hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She wished that I might have a healthy life.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=16", "t": "Exclamatory → assertive", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Convert the exclamatory idea into an assertive sentence. What a sight! Answer: It is a very beautiful sight. Hinglish Explanation: “What a/an + noun!” ko sense-preserving assertive me “It is a very + suitable adjective + noun” ke form me recast kiya ja sakta hai. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is a very beautiful sight.”. When changing an exclamatory sentence into an assertive sentence, express the emotion with a suitable adjective or adverb and remove the exclamation mark. Quick Rule: Exclamatory → assertive: emotion ko suitable adjective/adverb se express karo; exclamation mark hatao.","Convert the exclamatory idea into an assertive sentence. What a beautiful girl! Answer: She is a very beautiful girl. Hinglish Explanation: “What a beautiful girl!” admiration hai; assertive form me “She is a very beautiful girl.” English Explanation: The correct answer is “She is a very beautiful girl.”. What + a/an + adjective + noun → subject + be + very + adjective + noun (sense-based). Quick Rule: What + a/an + adjective + noun → subject + be + very + adjective + noun (sense-based).","Convert the exclamatory idea into an assertive sentence. How sad! Answer: It is very sad. Hinglish Explanation: “How + adjective!” ko assertive me “It is very + adjective” bana sakte hain. English Explanation: The correct answer is “It is very sad.”. How + adjective! → It/Subject + be + very + adjective. Quick Rule: How + adjective! → It/Subject + be + very + adjective."]},{"f": "./Original%20Practice/English_Grammar_Complete_Practice.html?chapter=16.+Narration&section=17", "t": "Exclamatory narration", "b": "Original Practice / English Grammar / 16. Narration", "x": ["Choose the correct indirect narration. He said, “Oh! Damn, I have bruised my toe against the side of the table.” Answer: He exclaimed with grief that he had bruised his toe against the side of the table. Hinglish Explanation: Pain/grief reaction hai; exclamatory reporting verb “exclaimed with grief”; present perfect → past perfect; my → his. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “He exclaimed with grief that he had bruised his toe against the side of the table.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. She said, “Yay! I have cleared the exam.” Answer: She exclaimed with joy that she had cleared the exam. Hinglish Explanation: “Yay!” joy signal karta hai. Present perfect → past perfect; I → she. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “She exclaimed with joy that she had cleared the exam.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Anurag said, “Alas! I lost my wallet.” Answer: Anurag exclaimed with sorrow that he had lost his wallet. Hinglish Explanation: “Alas!” sorrow; simple past → past perfect; I/my → he/his. English Explanation: “Alas!” sorrow; simple past → past perfect; I/my → he/his. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Raju said, “Hurray! I am going to London.” Answer: Raju exclaimed with joy that he was going to London. Hinglish Explanation: “Hurray!” joy; am going → was going; I → he. English Explanation: “Hurray!” joy; am going → was going; I → he. Rule: Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Anita said, “Wow, what a place!” Answer: Anita exclaimed with surprise that it was a very beautiful place. Hinglish Explanation: “Wow” + “what a place!” surprise/admiration show karta hai; indirect me exclamation ko assertive meaning me recast kiya gaya hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Anita exclaimed with surprise that it was a very beautiful place.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Gaurav said, “How cool!” Answer: Gaurav exclaimed with joy that it was very cool. Hinglish Explanation: “How cool!” positive reaction/joy hai; indirect exclamatory → exclaimed with joy + assertive clause. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Gaurav exclaimed with joy that it was very cool.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao.","Choose the correct indirect narration. Sachin said, “Wow, what a stunning car!” Answer: Sachin exclaimed with surprise that it was a very stunning car. Hinglish Explanation: “Wow” + “what a stunning car!” surprise/admiration; reported clause assertive order me hota hai. English Explanation: The correct indirect form is “Sachin exclaimed with surprise that it was a very stunning car.”. Direct to indirect speech: change the reporting verb, connector, tense, pronouns, and time words according to context, and remove the quotation marks. Quick Rule: Direct → Indirect: reporting verb, connector, tense/pronoun/time-word ko context ke hisaab se change karo; inverted commas hatao."]}];
